const CONTENT={"synced":"2026-10-11 12:08","courses":{"BUS1103":{"file":"BUS1103_Financial_Accounting_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Financial Statements and Business Decisions","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.1 Why Study Financial Accounting?"},{"t":"li","x":"Provides fundamental financial information for business decision-making."},{"t":"li","x":"Ensures compliance with legal requirements, regulations, and business ethics."},{"t":"li","x":"Enhances career opportunities and professional development."},{"t":"li","x":"Improves personal financial investment and management skills."},{"t":"li","x":"Essential for students who want to launch their own start-ups."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.2 Understanding the Business — Who Uses Financial Statements?"},{"t":"p","x":"Businesses raise resources from two main groups, each expecting a different kind of return:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source of Financing","Who","Expected Return"],["Equity financing","Stockholders (owners)","Dividends and/or a higher future stock price"],["Debt financing","Creditors (lenders)","Interest"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both groups estimate the company's future performance partly from information in its financial statements."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Financial vs. Managerial Accounting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Financial Accounting Reports","Managerial Accounting Reports"],["Content","Periodic financial statements and related disclosures","Detailed plans and continuous performance reports"],["Audience","External decision makers (creditors, investors) — evaluate the company","Internal decision makers (managers) — run the company"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.3 The Four Basic Financial Statements"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Statement","What it reports"],["Balance Sheet (Statement of Financial Position)","The financial position — assets, liabilities, and stockholders' equity — of an entity at ONE point in time."],["Income Statement (Statement of Earnings/Operations)","Revenues less expenses (performance) DURING an accounting period."],["Statement of Stockholders' Equity","Changes in each stockholders' equity account during the period, including how net income and dividends change Retained Earnings."],["Statement of Cash Flows","Cash inflows and outflows during the period, split into Operating, Investing, and Financing activities."]]},{"t":"p","x":"\"The notes are an integral part of these financial statements\" — this line appears at the bottom of every statement: notes/footnotes give supplemental information needed to properly understand the numbers."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement heading — always include:"},{"t":"li","x":"Name of the entity (company name)"},{"t":"li","x":"Title of the statement (e.g., Balance Sheet)"},{"t":"li","x":"The specific date OR period covered (e.g., \"At December 31, 2026\" vs. \"For the Year Ended December 31, 2026\")"},{"t":"li","x":"Unit of measure (e.g., in millions of dollars)"},{"t":"p","x":"Statements can be prepared for any period: annual, quarterly, or monthly."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Balance Sheet in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Accounting equation: **Assets = Liabilities + Stockholders' Equity (A = L + SE) — must always balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity"],["Cash; Short-term investments; Accounts receivable; Notes receivable; Inventories; Supplies; Prepaid expenses; Long-term investments; Equipment; Buildings; Land; Intangibles","Accounts payable; Accrued expenses; Notes payable; Taxes payable; Unearned revenue; Bonds payable","Common stock; Retained earnings"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Income Statement in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Equation: **Revenues − Expenses = Net Income (also called \"profit\", \"net earnings\", or \"the bottom line\"). If expenses exceed revenues, the result is a net loss."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Revenues (examples)","Expenses (examples)"],["Sales Revenue; Service Revenue; Rental Revenue; Interest Revenue","Cost of Goods Sold; Wages Expense; Rent Expense; Depreciation Expense; Insurance Expense; Repair Expense; Income Tax Expense"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement of Stockholders' Equity in detail"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Common Stock roll-forward","Retained Earnings roll-forward"],["Beginning Common Stock + Stock Issuance = Ending Common Stock","Beginning Retained Earnings + Net Income − Dividends = Ending Retained Earnings"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Statement of Cash Flows in detail"},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **± CFO ± CFI ± CFF = Change in Cash; + Beginning Cash Balance = Ending Cash Balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Definition"],["Operating Activities (CFO)","Cash flows directly tied to earning income — e.g. cash collected from customers less cash paid for operating expenses (suppliers, employees)."],["Investing Activities (CFI)","Cash flows from buying/selling the company's plant, equipment, and investments."],["Financing Activities (CFF)","Cash flows tied to financing the enterprise — receiving/repaying money to investors and creditors (except suppliers)."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Formatting Conventions"},{"t":"li","x":"Put the $ sign beside the first amount in a group of items and beside each group total."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets are listed by ease of conversion to cash (most liquid first)."},{"t":"li","x":"Liabilities are listed by maturity/due date (soonest first)."},{"t":"li","x":"A single underline goes below the last item before a subtotal; a double underline goes below a final group total."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.4 GAAP, IFRS, and the Standard-Setting Process"},{"t":"li","x":"GAAP (Generally Accepted Accounting Principles) = the rules determining the content and measurement of financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"The U.S. SEC has legal authority over financial reporting for public companies, but has delegated the detailed rule-making to the FASB (Financial Accounting Standards Board)."},{"t":"li","x":"FASB's official pronouncements = the FASB Accounting Standards Codification."},{"t":"li","x":"The FASB uses a conceptual framework to guide the development of specific standards."},{"t":"li","x":"Internationally, IFRS (International Financial Reporting Standards), issued by the IASB, has been adopted by 147+ jurisdictions since 2002 — including the EU, UK, Australia, New Zealand, Hong Kong, Malaysia, Korea, Israel, Turkey, Brazil, Chile, Canada, and Mexico. The US SEC now also allows foreign private issuers listed in the US to report under IFRS."},{"t":"p","x":"Since Hong Kong follows IFRS-equivalent standards (HKFRS), this is directly relevant to companies you will see referenced in class (e.g., Fairwood, Café de Coral, AEON, listed HK property developers)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.5 Principles and Assumptions of Accounting (core definitions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These 9 terms recur throughout the whole course — know them cold. (Consolidated from the Chapter 1 slides and the \"Accounting Assumptions and Principles\" Q&A hand-out.)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Economic entity assumption (a.k.a. Business/Separate entity assumption)","The enterprise is accounted for separately from its owners and other entities."],["Monetary unit assumption","A common denominator — money (e.g., the dollar) — is used to express all transactions, with no adjustment for changes in purchasing power."],["Going-concern assumption (continuity assumption)","The entity will continue operating indefinitely / long enough to meet its commitments and plans."],["Periodicity assumption (time period assumption)","The life of an enterprise can be divided into artificial time periods (months, quarters, years) for reporting."],["Historical cost principle (measurement/cost principle)","Balance sheet elements are initially recorded at their original transaction (cash-equivalent) cost."],["Revenue recognition principle","Revenue is recognized only when it is earned — criteria are usually satisfied at the point of sale/delivery."],["Expense recognition principle (matching principle)","Expenses are recorded in the same period as the revenue they helped generate."],["Full-disclosure principle (fair/faithful disclosure principle)","All information that could affect users' decisions must be reported, even details behind the numbers."],["Materiality","Information is material if omitting or misstating it could influence a user's economic decisions; immaterial items don't need strict adherence to normal principles."],["Prudence / Conservatism","Don't overstate revenues or assets, and don't understate expenses or liabilities — when in doubt, report conservatively."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.6 Accounting Ethics"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"What is ethics / accounting ethics?"},{"t":"li","x":"Ethics: a branch of philosophy addressing morality — good/bad, right/wrong, justice, virtue — and the choices we make and resulting behaviour, using accepted standards."},{"t":"li","x":"Accounting ethics: a branch of professional ethics — the study of moral values and judgments as they apply specifically to accountants."},{"t":"li","x":"Why it matters: to understand your own choices, to handle ethical challenges skilfully, to act morally (for yourself and society), and because ethics is increasingly central to professional accounting services."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Steps in Ethical Decision-Making"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Identify the ethical issue(s) in the case."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Identify the stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Consider the likely effects of the decision on those stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"4. Identify the relevant (accounting) ethical principles."},{"t":"li","x":"5. Identify the alternative courses of action."},{"t":"li","x":"6. Choose the best course of action."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"HKICPA's Five Ethical Principles"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Principle","Meaning","Example"],["Integrity (誠信)","Be straightforward and honest in all professional/business relationships.","Do not associate with a materially false or misleading statement."],["Objectivity (客觀，公正)","Don't let bias, conflicts of interest, or undue influence override professional judgment.","Avoid any relationship that would impair objectivity."],["Professional Competence & Due Care (專業勝任能力 / 應有的關注)","Maintain the knowledge/skill needed for competent service and act diligently and thoroughly.","Keep up to date with auditing standards; don't forget to address all major parts of an audit."],["Confidentiality (保密)","Respect confidentiality of information gained through professional relationships; don't disclose or exploit it without proper authority.","Don't tip off a friend about investment opportunities learned while auditing a company."],["Professional Behaviour (良好的職業行為)","Comply with relevant laws/regulations and avoid discrediting the profession.","Don't encourage tax evasion; don't exaggerate the services you can provide."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ensuring Accuracy — Three Controls Beyond Personal Ethics"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Maintain a system of internal controls over records and assets."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Hire external independent auditors to audit the fairness of the financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Form a board committee (audit committee) to oversee the integrity of these safeguards."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked Ethics Case Study — Nebula Ltd."},{"t":"p","x":"You are the CPA/financial accountant of Nebula Ltd., a Hong Kong public company. The head of accounts wants to extend the depreciable life of the company's main plant assets from 5 to 8 years — even though nothing about the assets themselves has changed — because the company hasn't reached its profit targets and wants to reduce expenses (boosting reported earnings) to avoid a drop in share price."},{"t":"p","x":"**Discussion & suggested analysis**"},{"t":"li","x":"Ethical issue: deliberately changing an accounting estimate (useful life) with no economic justification, purely to manage/inflate reported earnings — this breaches the faithful-representation and historical-cost/consistency spirit of GAAP and misleads users."},{"t":"li","x":"Stakeholders: shareholders/investors (misled about true performance), creditors (misjudge credit risk), employees (job security tied to a false picture), regulators/auditors (reputational and legal exposure), the accountant personally (professional license at risk)."},{"t":"li","x":"HKICPA principles at stake: Integrity (don't be party to a misleading statement), Objectivity (resist pressure from the head of accounts), Professional Competence & Due Care (the depreciation estimate should reflect genuine economic useful life), Professional Behaviour (don't discredit the profession)."},{"t":"li","x":"What the accountant should do: refuse to make the unjustified change, document the reasoning, raise the issue with the audit committee/those charged with governance if pressure continues, and, if necessary, escalate to the external auditor or decline to certify the accounts. Changing an estimate must be driven by a genuine change in facts (e.g. new information about how the asset is actually used), never by a desired profit outcome."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 1"},{"t":"li","x":"I can name the four basic financial statements and state, from memory, exactly what each one reports and over what period."},{"t":"li","x":"I can state the accounting equation and explain why it must always balance."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list what a financial statement heading must contain (entity, title, date/period, unit of measure)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain how the Income Statement, Statement of Stockholders' Equity, Balance Sheet, and Statement of Cash Flows connect to one another (net income flows into retained earnings; ending retained earnings appears on the balance sheet; ending cash on the cash flow statement equals cash on the balance sheet)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can define and give an example of each of the 9 core assumptions/principles (entity, going concern, monetary unit, periodicity, historical cost, revenue recognition, expense recognition/matching, full disclosure, materiality) plus conservatism."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the roles of the SEC, FASB, and IASB, and name GAAP vs. IFRS."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list HKICPA's 5 ethical principles with an example of each, and walk through the 6-step ethical decision-making process on a case."},{"t":"li","x":"I can list the 3 controls (beyond personal ethics) that help ensure accurate financial reporting."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.7 Official Practice — Class Practice Questions & Solutions"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the exact exercises assigned for Chapter 1 in the Teaching Schedule (E1-11, E1-12, E1-13) plus the \"Accounting Assumptions and Principles\" matching hand-out."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Extraction gap: the original slide text for the E1-11/E1-12/E1-13 question prompts (\"Chapter 1 – Class Practice Questions.txt\") came through as blank/image-only pages — only the official suggested-solutions file was text-extractable. The completed financial statements below ARE the verbatim official answer key; the short task descriptions are reconstructed from what the solutions imply (each exercise gives you unsorted item amounts and asks you to organize them into the correct statement). Please cross-check the exact original wording against the Moodle PDF/textbook if you want the literal instructions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-11 — Prepare an Income Statement and Balance Sheet (reconstructed task: arrange the given items into properly classified statements)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["PAINTER CORPORATION — Income Statement, For the Month of January, Current Year",""],["Total revenues","$305,000"],["Less: Total expenses (excluding income tax)","189,000"],["Pretax income","116,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","25,000"],["Net income","$91,000"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["PAINTER CORPORATION — Balance Sheet, At January 31, Current Year",""],["Cash","$65,150"],["Receivables from customers","44,700"],["Merchandise inventory","94,500"],["Total assets","$204,350"],["Payables to suppliers","$25,950"],["Income taxes payable","25,000"],["Total liabilities","50,950"],["Common stock","62,400"],["Retained earnings (from income statement)","91,000"],["Total stockholders' equity","153,400"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$204,350"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-12 — Prepare an Income Statement and Balance Sheet"},{"t":"table","rows":[["ANALYTICS CORPORATION — Income Statement, For the Year Ended December 31, Current Year",""],["Total revenues","$299,000"],["Less: Total expenses (excluding income tax)","184,000"],["Pretax income","115,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","34,500"],["Net income","$80,500"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["ANALYTICS CORPORATION — Balance Sheet, At December 31, Current Year",""],["Cash","$70,150"],["Receivables from customers","34,500"],["Merchandise inventory","96,600"],["Total assets","$201,250"],["Payables to suppliers","$26,450"],["Income taxes payable","34,500"],["Total liabilities","60,950"],["Common stock","59,800"],["Retained earnings (from income statement)","80,500"],["Total stockholders' equity","140,300"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$201,250"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E1-13 — Prepare a Statement of Stockholders' Equity"},{"t":"table","rows":[["PLUMMER STONEWORK CORPORATION — Statement of Stockholders' Equity, For the Year Ended December 31, 2023","Common Stock","Retained Earnings"],["Balance, December 31, 2022*","$100,000","$16,800"],["Net income","","42,000"],["Dividends","","(18,700)"],["Balance, December 31, 2023","$100,000","$40,100"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*For 2022: Beginning RE $0 + Net income $31,000 − Dividends $14,200 = Ending RE $16,800, which becomes the beginning balance for 2023."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Accounting Assumptions and Principles — Matching Q&A (E1-9 style)"},{"t":"p","x":"Match each accounting term (List A) with its correct description (List B):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["#","List A — Term","List B — Correct Description"],["1","Expense recognition","Record expenses in the period the related revenue is recognized."],["2","Periodicity assumption","The life of an enterprise can be divided into artificial time periods."],["3","Historical cost principle","The original transaction value upon acquisition."],["4","Materiality","Concerns the relative size of an item and its effect on decisions."],["5","Revenue recognition","Criteria usually satisfied for products at point of sale."],["6","Going concern assumption","The entity will continue indefinitely."],["7","Monetary unit assumption","A common denominator is the dollar."],["8","Economic entity assumption","The enterprise is separate from its owners and other entities."],["9","Full-disclosure principle","All information that could affect decisions should be reported."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.1 (MCQ) — Financial statements"},{"t":"p","x":"Which financial statement would you check to find out how much cash a company generated from its day-to-day operations during the year?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Balance Sheet"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Income Statement"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Statement of Cash Flows"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Statement of Stockholders' Equity"},{"t":"a","x":"C. The Statement of Cash Flows reports cash inflows/outflows split into Operating, Investing and Financing activities; \"cash generated from day-to-day operations\" is precisely Cash Flow from Operating Activities (CFO)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.2 (MCQ) — Accounting assumptions"},{"t":"p","x":"A sole proprietor records his personal car purchase separately from his shop's accounting records, even though legally the business and the owner are the same person. This best illustrates:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Going-concern assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Monetary unit assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Economic entity (separate entity) assumption"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Full-disclosure principle"},{"t":"a","x":"C. The economic/separate entity assumption requires that a business's activities be accounted for separately from the personal activities of its owner(s), regardless of the legal form of the business."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.3 (Short problem) — Building the accounting equation"},{"t":"p","x":"At the start of the year, Nova Traders Ltd. had total assets of $420,000 and total liabilities of $150,000. During the year, net income was $60,000, the company issued additional common stock for $25,000 cash, and paid dividends of $18,000. No other stockholders' equity transactions occurred."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Compute beginning stockholders' equity. (b) Compute ending stockholders' equity. (c) If ending total liabilities are $165,000, compute ending total assets."},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) Beginning SE = Assets − Liabilities = $420,000 − $150,000 = $270,000.\n• (b) Ending SE = Beginning SE + Net income + Stock issued − Dividends = $270,000 + $60,000 + $25,000 − $18,000 = $337,000.\n• (c) Ending Assets = Ending Liabilities + Ending SE = $165,000 + $337,000 = $502,000."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.4 (Short problem) — Classify accounting concepts"},{"t":"p","x":"For each situation below, name the single accounting assumption/principle it best illustrates:"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) A company reports its assets and results in HK dollars, without adjusting for inflation over the years."},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) A retailer records a laptop bought for $8,000 at $8,000 on the balance sheet, even though a similar laptop now sells for $9,500."},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) A company does not bother separately disclosing a $40 stationery expense in a multi-million-dollar set of financial statements."},{"t":"li","x":"(iv) Even though a company operates continuously, it produces a full set of financial statements every 3 months."},{"t":"a","x":"• (i) Monetary unit assumption.\n• (ii) Historical cost (measurement) principle.\n• (iii) Materiality.\n• (iv) Periodicity (time period) assumption."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C1.5 (Statement-preparation / journal-entry style problem) — Put it all together"},{"t":"p","x":"Harbor Consulting Corp. had the following account balances at the end of its first year of operations, listed in random order (all amounts in $): Cash 38,000; Accounts receivable 12,500; Service revenue 96,000; Salaries expense 41,000; Rent expense 18,000; Common stock 20,000; Accounts payable 9,500; Income tax expense 7,400; Retained earnings (beginning) 0; Dividends declared 5,000; Equipment 8,000; Income taxes payable 7,400; Insurance expense 3,000."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Prepare a classified Income Statement for the year. (b) Compute ending Retained Earnings. (c) Prepare a Balance Sheet at year-end and confirm it balances."},{"t":"table","rows":[["HARBOR CONSULTING CORP. — Income Statement",""],["Service revenue","$96,000"],["Less: Salaries expense","41,000"],["Less: Rent expense","18,000"],["Less: Insurance expense","3,000"],["Pretax income","34,000"],["Less: Income tax expense","7,400"],["Net income","$26,600"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(b) Retained earnings: **$0 (beginning) + $26,600 (net income) − $5,000 (dividends) = $21,600 (ending)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["HARBOR CONSULTING CORP. — Balance Sheet (at year-end)",""],["Cash","$38,000"],["Accounts receivable","12,500"],["Equipment","8,000"],["Total assets","$58,500"],["Accounts payable","$9,500"],["Income taxes payable","7,400"],["Total liabilities","16,900"],["Common stock","20,000"],["Retained earnings","21,600"],["Total stockholders' equity","41,600"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$58,500"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Check: Total assets $58,500 = Total liabilities $16,900 + Total stockholders' equity $41,600. Balances ✓ — always foot both sides of the balance sheet as a final check before moving on."}]},"2":{"title":"Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.1 Recognition and Measurement Concepts"},{"t":"p","x":"Three assumptions and one measurement concept underlie the balance sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Concept","Meaning"],["Separate entity assumption (business entity assumption)","Each business's activities must be accounted for separately from the personal activities of its owners, other persons, and other entities."],["Going concern assumption (continuity assumption)","The business is assumed to continue operating into the foreseeable future, long enough to meet its contractual commitments and plans."],["Monetary unit assumption","Each business reports its results primarily in the national monetary unit (e.g. HKD, USD), without adjusting for changes in purchasing power."],["Historical cost (measurement concept)","Balance sheet elements are initially recorded at cost — the cash-equivalent value on the date of the transaction."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.2 Elements of the Balance Sheet"},{"t":"p","x":"**A = L + SE**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity"],["Economic resources owned/controlled by the company, with measurable value, expected to produce future cash inflows or reduce future cash outflows.","Debts/obligations (claims to company resources) from past transactions; settled by transferring assets or providing services.","The financing provided by the owners and by the operations (earnings) of the business."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.3 What Is a Business Transaction?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Definition","Example"],["External events","Exchanges of assets/goods/services for assets/services/promises to pay, between the business and another party.","Purchasing a machine from a supplier."],["Internal events","Events not involving an exchange with another party but with a direct, measurable effect on the entity.","Using up insurance that was previously paid in advance."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.4 Typical Account Titles (memorize these families)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assets","Liabilities","Stockholders' Equity","Revenues","Expenses"],["Cash; Short-Term Investments; Accounts Receivable; Notes Receivable; Inventory; Supplies; Prepaid Expenses; Long-Term Investments; Equipment; Buildings; Land; Intangibles","Accounts Payable; Accrued Expenses Payable; Notes Payable; Taxes Payable; Unearned Revenue; Bonds Payable","Common Stock; Additional Paid-in Capital; Retained Earnings","Sales Revenue; Fee Revenue; Interest Revenue; Rent Revenue; Service Revenue","Cost of Goods Sold; Wages Expense; Rent Expense; Interest Expense; Depreciation Expense; Advertising Expense; Insurance Expense; Repair Expense; Income Tax Expense"]]},{"t":"li","x":"\"Receivable\" in the title → always an ASSET (owed TO the company by others)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Payable\" in the title → always a LIABILITY (owed BY the company to others)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Unearned\" in the title → always a LIABILITY (cash received in advance for goods/services not yet delivered)."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Prepaid ...\" → always an ASSET (cash paid in advance for future benefit)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expense accounts are named after what was incurred/used + \"Expense\", EXCEPT inventory sold, which is titled Cost of Goods Sold."},{"t":"li","x":"Revenue accounts are named after their source + \"Revenue\"."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.5 Transaction Analysis — the Dual-Effects Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"Every transaction has at least two effects on the accounting equation (\"dual effects\")."},{"t":"li","x":"Every transaction affects at least two accounts — correctly identifying which accounts, and whether each increases or decreases, is critical."},{"t":"li","x":"The accounting equation (A = L + SE) must remain in balance after every single transaction."},{"t":"p","x":"External-party exchanges involve the business (1) receiving something and (2) giving something in return. Example: Chipotle buys supplies and pays cash → receives supplies (+A), gives up cash (−A). If bought on credit instead, there are two separate transactions at two points in time (purchase on account, then later payment)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3-Step Transaction Analysis Method"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — What was received? (account name, type, amount, direction of effect)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — What was given?"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 3 — Verify the accounting equation still balances: A = L + SE."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.6 Worked Example — Chipotle's Quarterly Investing & Financing Transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"All amounts in millions of dollars unless noted."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","17",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","1"],["","Additional Paid-in Capital (+SE)","","16"],["","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]","Issued 100 shares of common stock, $0.01 par, at $0.17 market price → $17 cash from investors. [Financing]"],["(b)","Cash (+A)","4",""],["","Notes Payable, noncurrent (+L)","","4"],["","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]","Borrowed $4 from the bank, 3-year note (noncurrent liability). [Financing]"],["(c)","Equipment (+A)","26",""],["","Intangible Assets (+A)","5",""],["","Cash (−A)","","31"],["","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]","Purchased $26 equipment and $5 intangible assets, for cash. [Investing]"],["(d)","Land (+A)","20",""],["","Buildings (+A)","40",""],["","Operating Lease Assets (+A)","86",""],["","Cash (−A)","","29"],["","Current Operating Lease Liabilities (+L)","","6"],["","Long-Term Operating Lease Liabilities (+L)","","111"],["","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]","Acquired $20 land, $40 buildings, $86 right-of-use lease assets; paid $29 cash, signed $6 current + $111 long-term operating lease liabilities. [Investing]"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","19",""],["","Short-Term Investments (−A)","","19"],["","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]","Sold $19 of short-term investments for $19 cash. [Investing]"],["(f)","Notes Payable (−L)","1",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1"],["","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]","Paid $1 on the note payable from (b) (ignore interest). [Financing]"],["(g)","Treasury Stock (−SE)","103",""],["","Cash (−A)","","103"],["","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]","Repurchased common stock (treasury stock) from investors for $103 cash. [Financing]"],["(h)","Retained Earnings (−SE)","2",""],["","Dividends Payable (+L)","","2"],["","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]","Board declared $2 cash dividend, payable next quarter. [Financing]"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Tip: use +A/−A, +L/−L, +SE/−SE labels next to each account while learning — it forces you to check the accounting equation stays balanced after every line."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.7 Debits and Credits"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account Type","Increase (+)","Decrease (−)"],["Assets","Debit","Credit"],["Dividends Declared","Debit","Credit"],["Liabilities","Credit","Debit"],["Stockholders' Equity (Common Stock, APIC, Retained Earnings)","Credit","Debit"],["Revenues","Credit","Debit"],["Expenses","Debit","Credit"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Journal entry convention: **debited accounts listed first (amount on the left); credited accounts listed below, usually indented (amount on the right). Debits must always equal credits for each entry."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.8 T-Accounts and the Accounting Cycle"},{"t":"li","x":"General Journal — a chronological list of every transaction's effects."},{"t":"li","x":"General Ledger / T-accounts — a record of the effects on, and balances of, each individual account."},{"t":"li","x":"The Accounting Cycle (recurring each period): analyze transactions → record in the journal → post to the ledger (T-accounts) → prepare a trial balance → make adjusting entries → prepare financial statements → close the books."},{"t":"p","x":"T-account mechanics: start with the beginning balance on the correct side, post each transaction's debit/credit, draw a total line, and put the ending balance on the side matching its normal balance."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cash (Asset)","Cash (Asset)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. 561",""],["(a) 17",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ending balance = 561 + 17 = 578 (on the debit side, since Cash is an asset)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.9 Trial Balance"},{"t":"li","x":"Lists every T-account balance in financial-statement order: assets, liabilities, stockholders' equity, revenues, expenses."},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose: check that total debits = total credits."},{"t":"li","x":"Limitation: a trial balance can still balance even if the WRONG account or WRONG amount was used, or if a transaction was omitted entirely — it only catches debit/credit imbalances, not every type of error."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.10 Classified Balance Sheet"},{"t":"li","x":"Prepared from the trial balance."},{"t":"li","x":"A good heading: company name, statement title, date, and the dollar unit (thousands/millions) if rounded."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets and liabilities are grouped into Current and Noncurrent (long-term)."},{"t":"li","x":"Often shows comparative data for two periods (e.g., current year-end and prior year-end)."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ IFRS note: IFRS uses the same underlying recognition/measurement logic as GAAP, but financial statement FORMATTING can differ (e.g., balance sheet order, terminology) — always check the statement's own headings rather than assuming a US layout."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.13 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 2"},{"t":"li","x":"I can state the 3 assumptions + 1 measurement concept underlying the balance sheet."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish external events from internal events."},{"t":"li","x":"I can classify any given account as Asset/Liability/SE and state its normal balance (debit or credit)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the 3-step transaction analysis method (received / given / verify) to any transaction."},{"t":"li","x":"I can write a correctly formatted journal entry (debit first, credit indented, debits = credits)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can post journal entries to T-accounts and compute ending balances."},{"t":"li","x":"I can prepare a trial balance and explain what it does and does NOT prove."},{"t":"li","x":"I can prepare a simple classified balance sheet (current vs. noncurrent) with a correct heading."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.11 Official Practice — Worksheet P2-1, P2-2, P2-3, P2-5 (with Suggested Solutions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the exact class practice questions for Chapter 2 (per the Teaching Schedule), taken from the blank worksheet distributed in class, followed by the official suggested solutions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-1 — Classify Balance Sheet Items"},{"t":"p","x":"Classify each item as a Current Asset (CA), Noncurrent Asset (NCA), Current Liability (CL), Noncurrent Liability (NCL), or Stockholders' Equity (SE), and state whether its normal balance is a debit or credit:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Classification","Debit or Credit"],["(1) Notes and Loans Payable (short-term)","CL","Credit"],["(2) Materials and Supplies","CA","Debit"],["(3) Common Stock","SE","Credit"],["(4) Intangible Assets","NCA","Debit"],["(5) Income Taxes Payable","CL","Credit"],["(6) Long-Term Debt","NCL","Credit"],["(7) Property, Plant, and Equipment","NCA","Debit"],["(8) Retained Earnings","SE","Credit"],["(9) Notes and Accounts Receivable (short-term)","CA","Debit"],["(10) Investments (long-term)","NCA","Debit"],["(11) Cash and Cash Equivalents","CA","Debit"],["(12) Accounts Payable and Accrued Liabilities","CL","Credit"],["(13) Crude Oil, Products, and Merchandise","CA","Debit"],["(14) Additional Paid-in Capital","SE","Credit"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-2 — East Hill Home Healthcare Services: transaction analysis"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 1: Was East Hill organized as a corporation, partnership, or sole proprietorship?"},{"t":"a","x":"A corporation — only a corporation issues shares of common stock to owners in exchange for investment, as happens in transaction (a)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 2 — Transaction analysis table (beginning balances then 7 transactions a–g; dollars):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Cash","ST Invest.","Notes Rec.","Land","Buildings","Equip.","ST Notes Pay.","LT Notes Pay.","Common Stock","APIC","Ret. Earnings"],["Beg.","50,000","500,000","100,000","50,000","—","—","100,000","100,000","20,000","80,000","400,000"],["(a)","+90,000","","","","","","","","+9,000","+81,000",""],["(b)","−9,000","","","+14,000","+60,000","+15,000","","+80,000","","",""],["(c)","+3,500","","−3,500","","","","","","","",""],["(d)","−18,000","+18,000","","","","","","","","",""],["(e)","No effect*","","","","","","","","","",""],["(f)","−5,000","","+5,000","","","","","","","",""],["(g)","+8,000","","","","","","+8,000","","","",""],["End.","$119,500","$18,000","$5,000","$510,500*","$160,000*","","$108,000","$180,000","$29,000","$161,000","$400,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*Event (e) is a transaction between the two owners personally — it has no effect on the business (separate entity assumption). Ending Land/Buildings combine beginning balances with (b); exact per-column figures follow directly from summing beginning + each transaction column above."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3: The transaction between the two stockholders (Event e) is NOT recorded in the business's books — it occurs between the owners personally, so there is no effect on the business itself (separate/economic entity assumption)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 4 — Summary answers:"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Total assets = $119,500 + $18,000 + $5,000 + $510,500 + $160,000 + $65,000 = $878,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Total liabilities = $108,000 + $180,000 = $288,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Total stockholders' equity = Total assets − Total liabilities = $878,000 − $288,000 = $590,000"},{"t":"li","x":"(d) Cash balance = $50,000 + $90,000 − $9,000 + $3,500 − $18,000 − $5,000 + $8,000 = $119,500"},{"t":"li","x":"(e) Total current assets = Cash $119,500 + Short-term investments $18,000 + Notes receivable $5,000 = $142,500"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-3 — Jaguar Plastics Company: T-accounts, Trial Balance, Balance Sheet"},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 3: No effect was recorded for transaction (d) — it involved no exchange or receipt of cash, goods, or services, so it is not a recordable transaction (e.g., merely signing an agreement with no economic exchange yet)."},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 4 — Trial Balance:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Jaguar Plastics Company — Trial Balance, At December 31","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","12,000",""],["Investments (short-term)","13,000",""],["Accounts receivable","3,000",""],["Inventory","20,000",""],["Notes receivable (long-term)","6,000",""],["Equipment","49,000",""],["Factory building","114,000",""],["Operating lease assets","158,000",""],["Intangible assets","8,000",""],["Accounts payable","","15,000"],["Accrued liabilities payable","","4,000"],["Notes payable (short-term)","","16,000"],["Notes payable (long-term)","","103,000"],["Long-term operating lease liabilities","","76,000"],["Common stock","","11,000"],["Additional paid-in capital","","127,000"],["Retained earnings","","31,000"],["Totals","$383,000","$383,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Req. 5 — Classified Balance Sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Jaguar Plastics Company — Balance Sheet, At December 31",""],["Cash","$12,000"],["Investments","13,000"],["Accounts receivable","3,000"],["Inventory","20,000"],["Total Current Assets","48,000"],["Notes receivable","6,000"],["Equipment","49,000"],["Operating lease assets","158,000"],["Factory building","114,000"],["Intangible assets","8,000"],["Total Assets","$383,000"],["Accounts payable","$15,000"],["Accrued liabilities payable","4,000"],["Notes payable","16,000"],["Total Current Liabilities","35,000"],["Notes payable (long-term)","103,000"],["Long-term operating lease liabilities","76,000"],["Total Liabilities","214,000"],["Common stock","11,000"],["Additional paid-in capital","127,000"],["Retained earnings","31,000"],["Total Stockholders' Equity","169,000"],["Total Liabilities & Stockholders' Equity","$383,000"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P2-5 — Apple Inc.: Journal Entries, T-accounts, Trial Balance, Balance Sheet (dollars in millions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","18,266",""],["","Long-term debt (+L)","","18,266"],["(b)","Long-term investments (+A)","4,200",""],["","Short-term investments (+A)","16,800",""],["","Cash (−A)","","21,000"],["(c)","Property, plant, and equipment (+A)","10,981",""],["","Cash (−A)","","9,571"],["","Short-term debt (+L)","","1,410"],["(d)","Cash (+A)","1,469",""],["","Common stock (+SE)","","1"],["","Additional paid-in capital (+SE)","","1,468"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","18,810",""],["","Short-term investments (−A)","","18,810"],["(f)","Retained earnings (−SE)","11,126",""],["","Dividends payable (+L)","","11,126"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Trial Balance (from posting the above to beginning balances):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Apple Inc. — Trial Balance, At September 28, 2024 (in millions)","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","37,939",""],["Short-term investments","29,580",""],["Accounts receivable","60,985",""],["Inventories","6,331",""],["Other current assets","14,695",""],["Long-term investments","104,744",""],["Property, plant, and equipment","54,696",""],["Other noncurrent assets","64,758",""],["Accounts payable","","62,611"],["Accrued expenses","","58,829"],["Unearned revenue","","8,061"],["Short-term debt","","17,217"],["Dividends payable","","11,126"],["Long-term debt","","113,547"],["Other noncurrent liabilities","","49,848"],["Common stock","","2"],["Additional paid-in capital","","75,279"],["Accumulated deficit and other comprehensive loss","22,792",""],["Totals","$396,520","$396,520"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Classified Balance Sheet:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Apple Inc. — Balance Sheet, At September 28, 2024 (in millions)",""],["Cash","$37,939"],["Short-term investments","29,580"],["Accounts receivable","60,985"],["Inventories","6,331"],["Other current assets","14,695"],["Total current assets","149,530"],["Long-term investments","104,744"],["Property, plant, and equipment, net","54,696"],["Other noncurrent assets","64,758"],["Total assets","$373,728"],["Accounts payable","$62,611"],["Accrued expenses","58,829"],["Unearned revenue","8,061"],["Short-term debt","17,217"],["Dividends payable","11,126"],["Total current liabilities","157,844"],["Long-term debt","113,547"],["Other noncurrent liabilities","49,848"],["Total liabilities","321,239"],["Common stock","2"],["Additional paid-in capital","75,279"],["Accumulated deficit and other comprehensive loss","(22,792)"],["Total stockholders' equity","54,489"],["Total liabilities and stockholders' equity","$373,728"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.12 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.1 (MCQ) — Dual effects"},{"t":"p","x":"A company purchases equipment for $12,000, paying $4,000 cash and signing a note payable for the rest. What is the effect on total assets?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Increase by $12,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Increase by $8,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. No change"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Decrease by $4,000"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Equipment (+A) $12,000 and Cash (−A) $4,000 → net change in assets = +$8,000 (matched by Notes Payable (+L) $8,000, so the equation still balances)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.2 (MCQ) — Trial balance limitations"},{"t":"p","x":"A bookkeeper recorded a $500 cash purchase of supplies as a debit to Equipment and a credit to Cash. Will the trial balance still balance?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. No — debits won't equal credits"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Yes — because both sides used the correct dollar amount, even though the wrong account was debited"},{"t":"li","x":"C. It depends on when the error occurred"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Only if the amount was under $1,000"},{"t":"a","x":"B. A trial balance only checks that total debits equal total credits; it cannot detect an error where the correct dollar amount was posted to the wrong account."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.3 (Short problem) — Classify and normal balance"},{"t":"p","x":"For each account, state whether it is an Asset, Liability, or Stockholders' Equity account, and whether its normal balance is a debit or credit: (a) Unearned Subscription Revenue; (b) Prepaid Rent; (c) Notes Payable (long-term); (d) Additional Paid-in Capital; (e) Notes Receivable."},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) Liability, normal balance credit (cash received before it is earned).\n• (b) Asset, normal balance debit (cash paid in advance for a future benefit).\n• (c) Liability, normal balance credit.\n• (d) Stockholders' equity, normal balance credit.\n• (e) Asset, normal balance debit (amount owed TO the company)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.4 (Journal entry / T-account problem) — Full transaction cycle"},{"t":"p","x":"Bright Design Studio Ltd. began operations on 1 March with $0 balances. During March it completed the following transactions:"},{"t":"li","x":"(1) Issued common stock for $50,000 cash."},{"t":"li","x":"(2) Borrowed $20,000 from a bank on a 2-year note."},{"t":"li","x":"(3) Purchased equipment for $30,000 cash."},{"t":"li","x":"(4) Purchased $4,000 of supplies on account."},{"t":"li","x":"(5) Paid $1,500 of the accounts payable from (4)."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) Journalize each transaction. (b) Post to T-accounts for Cash and Accounts Payable. (c) Prepare a trial balance."},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Journal entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","50,000",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","50,000"],["(2)","Cash (+A)","20,000",""],["","Notes Payable (+L)","","20,000"],["(3)","Equipment (+A)","30,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","30,000"],["(4)","Supplies (+A)","4,000",""],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","4,000"],["(5)","Accounts Payable (−L)","1,500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,500"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(b) T-accounts**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cash","Cash"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. 0","(3) 30,000"],["(1) 50,000","(5) 1,500"],["(2) 20,000",""]]},{"t":"a","x":"Ending Cash balance = 50,000 + 20,000 − 30,000 − 1,500 = $38,500 (debit balance)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Accounts Payable","Accounts Payable"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["(5) 1,500","Beg. 0"],["","(4) 4,000"]]},{"t":"a","x":"Ending Accounts Payable balance = 4,000 − 1,500 = $2,500 (credit balance).\n**(c) Trial balance**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Bright Design Studio Ltd. — Trial Balance, At 31 March","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","38,500",""],["Supplies","4,000",""],["Equipment","30,000",""],["Accounts payable","","2,500"],["Notes payable","","20,000"],["Common stock","","50,000"],["Totals","$72,500","$72,500"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C2.5 (Short problem) — Reconstruct a missing figure"},{"t":"p","x":"Delta Freight Co. reports total assets of $940,000 and total stockholders' equity of $560,000 at year-end. During the year, the only liability transactions were: issuing a $150,000 long-term note and paying off a $40,000 short-term note that existed at the start of the year. If beginning total liabilities were $270,000, what are ending total liabilities, and does the accounting equation hold?"},{"t":"a","x":"• Ending liabilities = Beginning $270,000 + New note $150,000 − Repayment $40,000 = $380,000.\n• Check: Assets = Liabilities + SE → $380,000 + $560,000 = $940,000, which matches the given total assets. The equation holds. ✓"}]},"3":{"title":"Operating Decisions and the Accounting System","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.1 The Operating Cycle & Time Period Assumption"},{"t":"p","x":"A business's operating cycle repeats continuously until it ceases activities. To measure income for a specific period, accountants apply the time period assumption (a company's long life is divided into shorter periods: months, quarters, years). This raises two issues:"},{"t":"li","x":"Recognition issue — WHEN should the effects of operating activities be recorded?"},{"t":"li","x":"Measurement issue — WHAT AMOUNT should be recorded?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.2 Elements of the Income Statement"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Definition","Examples"],["Operating Revenues","Amounts earned/recorded from day-to-day business activities (the central focus of the business).","Sales Revenue, Service Revenue"],["Operating Expenses","Costs of operating the business incurred to generate revenue during the period.","Depreciation, Repairs, Supplies, Utilities, Wages, Insurance, Rent, G&A expense, Losses on disposal"],["Other Items","Revenues/expenses/gains/losses NOT central to ongoing operations.","Interest/Dividend Revenue, Interest Expense, Gains/Losses on Sale of Investments"]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Expenditure ≠ Expense! An expenditure is any cash outlay (buying equipment, repaying a loan, paying for a repair). An expense is specifically a cost incurred to generate revenue. So NOT all cash expenditures are expenses (e.g. buying equipment is a cash expenditure but creates an ASSET, not an immediate expense)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Typical (multi-step) Income Statement Format"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Line",""],["Operating Revenues",""],["Less: Operating Expenses",""],["= Income from Operations",""],["+/− Other Items",""],["= Income before Income Taxes (Pretax Income)",""],["− Income Tax Expense (Provision for Income Taxes)",""],["= Net Income",""]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.3 Cash Basis vs. Accrual Basis Accounting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Cash Basis","Accrual Basis (required by GAAP)"],["Revenue recorded when...","cash is received","goods/services are delivered (earned) — regardless of when cash is received"],["Expense recorded when...","cash is paid","incurred in the same period as the revenue it relates to — regardless of when cash is paid"],["Allowed under GAAP?","No","Yes — required"],["Main weakness","Can badly distort the picture of performance (e.g., a big upfront cash outlay makes a healthy company look like it's failing)","Faithfully matches effort (expense) with the resulting benefit (revenue)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Example of the distortion: a company with $60,000/year of sales on credit shows negative cash flow of $(25,000) in Year 1 under the cash basis purely because of upfront insurance and low collections — even though the underlying business is healthy and collects the same $180,000 total over 3 years."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Revenue Recognition Principle — 5-Step Model"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Identify the contract between the company and the customer."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Identify the seller's performance obligations (promised goods/services)."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Determine the transaction price."},{"t":"li","x":"4. Allocate the transaction price to the performance obligations."},{"t":"li","x":"5. Recognize revenue when each performance obligation is satisfied (or over time, if the service is delivered over time)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Critical point: **revenue recognition is triggered by DELIVERY of goods/services, not by receipt of cash."},{"t":"p","x":"**Three revenue-recognition timing scenarios**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","On $100 cash deposit received: Cash (+A)","100",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","100"],["","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery.","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery.","Cash received BEFORE delivery → record a liability (Unearned Revenue); recognize revenue only on delivery."],["","On delivery: Unearned Revenue (−L)","100",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","100"],["(2)","Cash (+A)","12",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","12"],["","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly.","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly.","Cash received in the SAME period as delivery → record revenue directly."],["(3)","On delivery of $50 on account: Accounts Receivable (+A)","50",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","50"],["","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery.","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery.","Cash received AFTER delivery → record an asset (Accounts Receivable) at the point of delivery."],["","On later cash receipt: Cash (+A)","50",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","50"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**In-class practice — ABC Ltd. unearned revenue, worked in full**"},{"t":"p","x":"On Jan 2, ABC Ltd. receives $2,700 cash for services of $900/month for 3 months, starting in January."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Jan 2","Cash (+A)","2,700",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","2,700"],["","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability.","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability.","No service has been delivered yet on Jan 2, so the full amount is a liability."],["Jan 31","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue.","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue.","One month of service (Jan) has now been delivered — recognize $900 of revenue."],["Feb 28","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["Mar 31","Unearned Revenue (−L)","900",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Expense Recognition (Matching) Principle"},{"t":"p","x":"Costs incurred to generate revenue must be recognized in the same period as that revenue — regardless of when cash is paid. Common costs: salaries/wages worked, utilities used, supplies used, facilities/equipment used during the period."},{"t":"p","x":"**Three expense-recognition timing scenarios**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","On $200 cash paid for supplies: Supplies (+A)","200",""],["","Cash (−A)","","200"],["","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used.","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used.","Cash paid BEFORE the expense is incurred → record an asset first (e.g., Supplies), then expense it as used."],["","On using half the supplies: Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","100",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","100"],["(2)","Repairs Expense (+E, −SE)","275",""],["","Cash (−A)","","275"],["","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly.","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly.","Cash paid in the SAME period the expense is incurred → record the expense directly."],["(3)","On using $400 of employee services: Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","400",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","400"],["","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later.","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later.","Cash paid AFTER the expense is incurred → record a liability first, then pay it later."],["","On paying cash later: Wages Payable (−L)","400",""],["","Cash (−A)","","400"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**In-class practice — ABC Ltd. accrued utilities expense, worked in full**"},{"t":"p","x":"On Jan 31, ABC Ltd. receives a $1,800 utility bill for the month; it plans to pay it on Feb 6."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Jan 31","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","1,800",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","1,800"],["","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid).","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid).","The utilities were used/enjoyed during January, so the expense is incurred now (even though unpaid)."],["Feb 6","Utilities Payable (−L)","1,800",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,800"],["","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense.","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense.","Paying the bill is a settlement of the liability, not a new expense."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.4 Expanded Transaction Analysis Model & Analysis Steps"},{"t":"p","x":"Stockholders' equity splits into Contributed Capital (Common Stock + APIC — increases with stock issuance) and Earned Capital (Retained Earnings — increases with Net Income, decreases with Dividends Declared and with Net Loss)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Net income vs. net loss: **Revenues > Expenses → Net Income → increases Retained Earnings and SE. Expenses > Revenues → Net Loss → decreases Retained Earnings and SE."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Steps for Analyzing Any Transaction (Exhibit 3.5)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — Ask: Was a revenue earned by delivering goods/services? → debit what was received (+A/−L), credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."},{"t":"li","x":"OR was an expense incurred to generate revenue this period? → debit Expense (+E, −SE), credit what was given (−A/+L)."},{"t":"li","x":"OR neither → debit what was received (+A/−L), credit what was given (−A/+L)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — Verify: does A = L + SE still hold? Do debits = credits?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.5 Worked Example — Chipotle's Q1 Operating Transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"(All amounts in millions; T-accounts begin from the Ch.2 trial balance.) Note how each entry marks (+R,+SE) for revenues and (+E,−SE) for expenses:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","1,315",""],["","Accounts Receivable (+A)","44",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","1,359"],["","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash.","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash.","Sold food/beverages for $1,359 total; $44 on account to universities, rest in cash."],["(2)","Supplies (+A)","459",""],["","Cash (−A)","","379"],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","80"],["","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account.","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account.","Purchased $459 of supplies; paid $379 cash, rest on account."],["(3)","Prepaid Expenses (+A)","207",""],["","Cash (−A)","","207"],["","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses).","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses).","Paid $207 cash for rent/insurance/advertising to be used in the future (Prepaid Expenses)."],["(4)","Training Expense (+E, −SE)","65",""],["","Cash (−A)","","65"],["","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period).","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period).","Paid $65 cash for management training expense (incurred and paid same period)."],["(5)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","342",""],["","Wages Payable (−L)","47",""],["","Cash (−A)","","389"],["","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter.","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter.","Paid employees $342 for this quarter's work and $47 owed from last quarter."],["(6)","Cash (+A)","12",""],["","Loss on Sale of Land (+E, −SE)","9",""],["","Land (−A)","","21"],["","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal.","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal.","Sold land costing $21 for $12 cash — a $9 loss on disposal."],["(7)","Cash (+A)","61",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","61"],["","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance.","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance.","Received $61 cash from customers paying down their receivable balance."],["(8)","Accounts Payable (−L)","66",""],["","Utilities Payable (−L)","25",""],["","Cash (−A)","","91"],["","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter).","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter).","Paid $66 accounts payable to suppliers and $25 utilities payable (both from last quarter)."],["(9)","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","117",""],["","Repairs Expense (+E, −SE)","35",""],["","Cash (−A)","","152"],["","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs.","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs.","Paid $117 utilities used this quarter and $35 for building/equipment repairs."],["(10)","Cash (+A)","35",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","35"],["","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet.","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet.","Sold gift cards for $35 cash — a liability, since no food has been delivered yet."],["(11)","Cash (+A)","3",""],["","Interest Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3"],["","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue).","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue).","Received $3 cash as interest revenue earned this quarter (an \"Other Item\", not operating revenue)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.6 Unadjusted (Classified) Income Statement"},{"t":"p","x":"An unadjusted income statement — built straight from the trial balance before adjusting entries — is NEVER presented to external users, because it omits necessary period-end adjustments (covered in Chapter 4)."},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Current development (context only, not examinable in detail): HKFRS 18 will replace HKAS 1 for periods beginning on/after 1 Jan 2027, mandating a more structured profit-or-loss statement with 5 defined categories of income/expense. Good general knowledge for Hong Kong-based accounting students."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 3"},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish operating revenues/expenses from \"Other Items\" and place each correctly on a multi-step income statement."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain expenditure vs. expense with an example."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why GAAP requires accrual accounting and can give an example of how cash-basis accounting distorts performance."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the 5-step revenue recognition model and identify the correct timing (before/during/after cash receipt) for any scenario, with the correct journal entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can apply the expense recognition (matching) principle and identify the correct timing for any scenario, with the correct journal entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can work through a full quarter of operating transactions (like the Chipotle example) confidently, tagging each account with +A/−A/+L/−L/+R/+E/±SE."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why an unadjusted income statement is not suitable for external users."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.7 Official Practice — Worksheet E3-2, E3-3, E3-7, AP3-6 (with Suggested Solutions)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-2 — Identify revenue recognized in September (13 activities)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Activity","Revenue Account / Amount Recognized in September"],["a.","None — earnings process not yet complete."],["b.","Interest revenue $125 (= $15,000 × 10% × 1/12)."],["c.","Sales revenue $74,000."],["d.","None — only an exchange of promises has occurred, no transaction yet."],["e.","Sales revenue $21,000 (= 1,000 shirts × $21) — revenue earned when goods are delivered."],["f.","None — this cash receipt relates to revenue already recorded in (e) when delivered."],["g.","None — recorded instead as the liability Unearned Revenue (deferred revenue)."],["h.","None — issuing stock is a financing activity, not revenue."],["i.","None — recorded as Unearned Revenue (deferred revenue)."],["j.","Ticket sales revenue $2,940,000 (= $20,580,000 ÷ 7 games)."],["k.","None — earnings process not yet complete."],["l.","Sales revenue $60,000."],["m.","Sales revenue $300."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-3 — Identify expense incurred in January (17 activities)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Activity","Expense Account / Amount Incurred in January"],["a.","Utilities expense $3,800."],["b.","Advertising expense $450 (= $1,350 × 1/3 months); remainder is a Prepaid Expense (asset) for Feb/Mar."],["c.","Salaries expense $201,500 for January (the other half was incurred/expensed in December)."],["d.","None yet — recorded as the asset Prepaid Expense until the related revenue is earned."],["e.","None yet — recorded as Inventory when received; expensed later as Cost of Goods Sold."],["f.","Cost of goods sold $84,000 (= 400 books × $210)."],["g.","None — this was a December expense paid in January; not a January expense."],["h.","Commissions expense $55,560."],["i.","None yet — expensed later as Depreciation over the grill's useful life."],["j.","Supplies expense $4,700 (= $3,500 + $2,600 − $1,400)."],["k.","Wages expense $184 (= 8 hours × $23)."],["l.","Insurance expense $400 (= $4,800 ÷ 12); remainder is Prepaid Insurance."],["m.","Repairs expense $600."],["n.","Utilities expense $154."],["o.","Consulting expense $2,034."],["p.","None — December expense paid in January."],["q.","Cost of goods sold $9,450 (= 450 shirts × $21)."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E3-7 — Journal Entries and Accounts Receivable T-account"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Cash (+A)","2,300,000",""],["","Short-term note payable (+L)","","2,300,000"],["b.","Equipment (+A)","98,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","98,000"],["c.","Inventory (+A)","35,000",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","35,000"],["d.","Repairs expense (+E, −SE)","62,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","62,000"],["e.","Cash (+A)","390,000",""],["","Unearned pass revenue (+L)","","390,000"],["f.","Accounts receivable (+A)","700",""],["","Ski shop sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","700"],["g.","Cost of goods sold (+E, −SE)","400",""],["","Inventory (−A)","","400"],["h.","Cash (+A)","320,000",""],["","Lift revenue (+R, +SE)","","320,000"],["i.","Cash (+A)","3,500",""],["","Unearned rent revenue (+L)","","3,500"],["j.","Accounts payable (−L)","17,500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","17,500"],["k.","Cash (+A)","400",""],["","Accounts receivable (−A)","","400"],["l.","Wages expense (+E, −SE)","245,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","245,000"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Accounts Receivable","Accounts Receivable"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["Beg. bal. 1,000","(k) 400"],["(f) 700",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ending Accounts Receivable balance = 1,000 + 700 − 400 = $1,300."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AP3-6 — Exxon Mobil Corporation: Journal Entries, T-accounts, Income Statement (in millions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["a.","Property and equipment (+A)","1,610",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","1,610"],["b.","Cash (+A)","3,100",""],["","Accounts receivable (−A)","","3,100"],["c.","Utilities expense (+E, −SE)","3",""],["","Cash (−A)","","3"],["d.","Accounts receivable (+A)","39,780",""],["","Sales revenue (+R, +SE)","","39,780"],["e.","Cost of sales (+E, −SE)","5,984",""],["","Inventories (−A)","","5,984"],["f.","Wages expense (+E, −SE)","1,238",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,238"],["g.","Income taxes payable (−L)","1,185",""],["","Cash (−A)","","1,185"],["","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash.","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash.","1,185 = 3/4 × $1,580 income taxes payable settled in cash."],["h.","Inventories (+A)","23",""],["","Accounts payable (+L)","","23"],["i.","Prepaid expenses (+A)","82",""],["","Cash (−A)","","82"],["j.","Other long-term debt (−L)","10",""],["","Interest expense (+E, −SE)","1",""],["","Cash (−A)","","11"],["k.","Other assets and intangibles (+A)","6",""],["","Cash (−A)","","6"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Exxon Mobil Corporation — Income Statement (unadjusted), For the Month Ended January 31 (in millions)",""],["Sales revenue","$39,780"],["Less: Cost of sales","5,984"],["Less: Wages expense","1,238"],["Less: Utilities expense","3"],["Total expenses","7,225"],["Operating income","32,555"],["Other items — Interest expense","1"],["Net Income","$32,554"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.1 (MCQ) — Cash vs. accrual"},{"t":"p","x":"A landscaping company mows a client's lawn on 28 June but is not paid until 5 July. Under accrual accounting, in which month is the revenue recognized?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. June — when the service was performed"},{"t":"li","x":"B. July — when the cash was received"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Split 50/50 between June and July"},{"t":"li","x":"D. Neither — revenue is never recognized for services"},{"t":"a","x":"A. Under accrual accounting, revenue is recognized when the service is delivered/earned, not when cash changes hands."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.2 (MCQ) — Matching principle"},{"t":"p","x":"A company pays a full year of insurance, $12,000, on 1 October. How much insurance EXPENSE should be recognized for the quarter ended 31 December?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $12,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. $3,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"D. $0"},{"t":"a","x":"B. $12,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct, Nov, Dec) = $3,000 expensed; the remaining $9,000 stays as Prepaid Insurance (an asset) at year-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.3 (Short problem) — Classify revenue/expense timing"},{"t":"p","x":"For each event, state whether cash is received/paid BEFORE, DURING (same period as), or AFTER the related revenue/expense, and name the balance sheet account created (if any):"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) A gym sells a $1,200 one-year membership on 1 Nov, cash up front."},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) A law firm bills a client $5,000 for work finished this month; the client will pay next month."},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) A shop pays $2,000 cash to a cleaning company for cleaning done this month."},{"t":"a","x":"• (i) Cash received BEFORE the service is delivered → Unearned Revenue (liability) of $1,200, recognized as revenue at $100/month over the year.\n• (ii) Cash received AFTER the service is delivered → Accounts Receivable (asset) of $5,000; revenue is recognized now, in the month the work was done.\n• (iii) Cash paid DURING the same period as the expense → Cleaning Expense recorded directly for $2,000; no balance sheet account created."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.4 (Journal-entry problem) — Multi-scenario adjustments-style transactions"},{"t":"p","x":"Summit Tutors Ltd. completed these transactions in November:"},{"t":"li","x":"(1) Received $6,000 cash on Nov 1 for a 3-month tutoring package (Nov–Jan), $2,000/month."},{"t":"li","x":"(2) Tutored a walk-in student for $150 cash, paid immediately."},{"t":"li","x":"(3) Delivered $900 of tutoring to a corporate client, to be billed and collected in December."},{"t":"li","x":"(4) Received the electricity bill for November, $340, to be paid in December."},{"t":"li","x":"(5) Paid $500 cash for October's rent (rent was already recorded as an expense/payable in October)."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: journalize each transaction for November."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(1)","Cash (+A)","6,000",""],["","Unearned Tutoring Revenue (+L)","","6,000"],["","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned.","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned.","Only $2,000 (November's portion) would be recognized as revenue via a period-end adjustment (Chapter 4) — as of the receipt date, the full amount is unearned."],["(2)","Cash (+A)","150",""],["","Tutoring Revenue (+R, +SE)","","150"],["(3)","Accounts Receivable (+A)","900",""],["","Tutoring Revenue (+R, +SE)","","900"],["(4)","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","340",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","340"],["(5)","Rent Payable (−L)","500",""],["","Cash (−A)","","500"],["","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle.","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle.","This is a settlement of a liability from October, NOT a November expense — the expense was already recognized in October under the matching principle."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C3.5 (Comprehensive short problem) — Build a mini income statement"},{"t":"p","x":"Zenith Ltd.'s December trial balance (unadjusted, partial) shows: Sales revenue $85,000; Interest revenue $600; Cost of goods sold $38,000; Wages expense $19,500; Rent expense $6,000; Utilities expense $1,400; Interest expense $900; Income tax rate 20% of pretax income."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: prepare a classified income statement for December, down to net income."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Zenith Ltd. — Income Statement, For the Month Ended December 31",""],["Sales revenue","$85,000"],["Less: Cost of goods sold","38,000"],["Less: Wages expense","19,500"],["Less: Rent expense","6,000"],["Less: Utilities expense","1,400"],["Income from operations","20,100"],["Other items: Interest revenue","600"],["Other items: Interest expense","(900)"],["Income before income taxes","19,800"],["Income tax expense (20% × $19,800)","3,960"],["Net income","$15,840"]]}]},"4":{"title":"Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.1 Purpose of Adjustments"},{"t":"li","x":"Revenues are recorded when EARNED (revenue recognition principle)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expenses are recorded when INCURRED to generate revenue (expense recognition principle)."},{"t":"li","x":"Assets are reported at amounts representing probable future benefits remaining at period-end."},{"t":"li","x":"Liabilities are reported at amounts representing probable future sacrifices owed at period-end."},{"t":"p","x":"Adjusting entries are required every time a company prepares financial statements. Companies wait until period-end to adjust because doing so daily/weekly would be too costly and time-consuming."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.2 The Four Types of Adjustments — Master Table"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","What happened","Adjusting entry needed"],["Deferred (Unearned) Revenue","Cash was received in the PAST, before goods/services were delivered — recorded as a liability.","If earned by period-end: Debit Unearned Revenue (−L); Credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."],["Accrued Revenue","Revenue was EARNED but cash will be received in the FUTURE — nothing recorded yet.","Debit a Receivable (+A); Credit Revenue (+R, +SE)."],["Deferred Expense","Cash was paid in the PAST for an asset (e.g. Supplies, Prepaid Rent) that is used up over time.","Debit Expense (+E, −SE); Credit the Asset (−A) [Prepaid Expense / Supplies]."],["Accrued Expense","An expense was INCURRED but cash will be paid in the FUTURE — nothing recorded yet.","Debit Expense (+E, −SE); Credit a Payable (+L)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Key exam tip: notice the Cash account is NEVER touched by an adjusting entry — cash has already moved (deferred items) or will move later (accrued items). Every adjusting entry always includes at least one Income Statement account AND at least one Balance Sheet account."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.3 Worked AJEs — Chipotle's Quarter-End Adjustments (all in $ millions)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 1 — Deferred (Unearned) Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"Unadjusted Unearned Revenue balance = $245 (gift card sales). During the quarter, customers redeemed $52 of gift cards for food."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Unearned Revenue (−L)","52",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","52"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 2 — Accrued Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"Short-term investments earned $1 of additional interest this quarter; cash will be received next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Interest Receivable (+A)","1",""],["","Interest Revenue (+R, +SE)","","1"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 3 — Deferred Expense: Supplies"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Computation of Supplies Expense",""],["Beginning balance — Supplies","$39"],["+ Purchases during the quarter","459"],["Unadjusted balance at end of quarter","498"],["− Amount on hand (physical count) at end of quarter","(36)"],["Supplies used during the quarter (= expense)","$462"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","462",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","462"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 4 — Deferred Expense: Prepaid Expenses (rent, insurance, advertising)"},{"t":"p","x":"Unadjusted Prepaid Expenses balance = $324, made up of: $128 rent (3 months, $32/month), $96 insurance (6 months from Jan 1), $41 advertising for the quarter. By quarter-end, three months of each has been used."},{"t":"li","x":"Rent used: $128 (all 3 months of prepayment used) = $128 expensed."},{"t":"li","x":"Insurance used: $96 × 3/6 months = $48 expensed."},{"t":"li","x":"Advertising used: $41 (all used during the quarter) = $41 expensed."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","128",""],["","Insurance Expense (+E, −SE)","48",""],["","Advertising Expense (+E, −SE)","41",""],["","Prepaid Expenses (−A)","","217"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 5 — Depreciation (Deferred Expense on Buildings & Equipment)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Depreciation **is the systematic allocation of the cost of buildings/equipment (NOT land — land does not depreciate) over their estimated useful lives."},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **Depreciation expense for the quarter = Annual depreciation × (months in the quarter ÷ 12). Here: $232/year × 3/12 = $58."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","58",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA, a contra-asset)","","58"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset: it has a CREDIT normal balance (opposite of a normal asset) and is subtracted from the related asset's cost on the balance sheet to show net book value."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 6 — Accrued Expense: Wages"},{"t":"p","x":"Employees worked 2 days at quarter-end, earning $26/day, but won't be paid until next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","52",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","52"]]},{"t":"p","x":"52 = 2 days × $26/day."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 7 — Accrued Expense: Interest on Debt"},{"t":"p","x":"Principal balance on long-term notes payable = $151; annual interest rate = 2.65%; interest will be paid in the future."},{"t":"p","x":"**Formula: **Interest = Principal × Annual rate × (time outstanding this period ÷ 12). Quarterly interest ≈ $151 × 2.65% × 3/12 ≈ $1."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Interest Expense (+E, −SE)","1",""],["","Interest Payable (+L)","","1"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 8 — Accrued Expense: Utilities"},{"t":"p","x":"Utility bill of $15 received for usage during the quarter; will be paid next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Utilities Expense (+E, −SE)","15",""],["","Utilities Payable (+L)","","15"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AJE 9 — Accrued Expense: Income Taxes (always the LAST adjusting entry)"},{"t":"p","x":"Compute adjusted pretax income (after AJE 1–8), then apply the tax rate (here 20.0%) to determine Income Tax Expense; taxes are due next quarter."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Income Tax Expense (+E, −SE)","15",""],["","Income Taxes Payable (+L)","","15"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.4 Summary — Applying the Adjustment Logic (Decision Tree)"},{"t":"li","x":"Was revenue earned that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WAS received in the past? → Deferred Revenue → increase Revenue, decrease Liability (Unearned Revenue)."},{"t":"li","x":"Was revenue earned that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WILL be received in the future? → Accrued Revenue → increase Revenue, increase Asset (a Receivable)."},{"t":"li","x":"Was an expense incurred that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WAS paid in the past? → Deferred Expense → increase Expense, decrease Asset."},{"t":"li","x":"Was an expense incurred that is NOT yet recorded, and cash WILL be paid in the future? → Accrued Expense → increase Expense, increase Liability (a Payable)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.5 Preparing the Financial Statements"},{"t":"p","x":"The interrelationships flow in this order: (1) Adjusted Trial Balance → (2) Income Statement (Revenues − Expenses = Net Income) → (3) Statement of Stockholders' Equity (Beg. Retained Earnings + Net Income − Dividends = End. Retained Earnings) → (4) Balance Sheet (Ending Retained Earnings appears in Stockholders' Equity, and the balance sheet must balance: A = L + SE)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.6 The Closing Process"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Permanent vs. Temporary Accounts"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Permanent Accounts","Temporary Accounts"],["Balance Sheet accounts (Assets, Liabilities, SE). Retain their balances from one period to the next.","Revenue, Expense, Gain, and Loss accounts (Income Statement accounts) — and Dividends Declared. Balances accumulate during the period then are reset to ZERO to start the next period."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Why We Close the Books"},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose 1: transfer the balances in temporary accounts to Retained Earnings."},{"t":"li","x":"Purpose 2: reset each temporary account to a zero balance to start the next period."},{"t":"p","x":"The closing entry is dated the last day of the period. Temporary accounts with debit balances are credited (closed out); those with credit balances are debited. The net effect equals net income (or loss) and flows into Retained Earnings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Method — Closing Using an \"Income Summary\" Account (4 steps)"},{"t":"li","x":"Step 1 — Close credit balances in Revenue accounts to Income Summary (debit each Revenue, credit Income Summary for the total)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 2 — Close debit balances in Expense accounts to Income Summary (credit each Expense, debit Income Summary for the total)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 3 — Close Income Summary to Retained Earnings (the balance in Income Summary after steps 1–2 = net income/loss)."},{"t":"li","x":"Step 4 — Close the Dividends (Declared) account to Retained Earnings."},{"t":"p","x":"**Fully worked example — FastForward, Adjusted Trial Balance at Dec 31, 2026**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Account","Debit","Credit"],["Cash","$4,350",""],["Accounts receivable","1,800",""],["Supplies","8,670",""],["Prepaid insurance","2,300",""],["Equipment","26,000",""],["Accumulated depreciation—Equipment","","$375"],["Accounts payable","","6,200"],["Salaries payable","","210"],["Unearned consulting revenue","","2,750"],["Share capital","","30,000"],["Retained earnings","","0"],["Dividends","200",""],["Consulting revenue","","7,850"],["Rental revenue","","300"],["Depreciation expense—Equipment","375",""],["Salaries expense","1,610",""],["Insurance expense","100",""],["Rent expense","1,000",""],["Supplies expense","1,050",""],["Utilities expense","230",""],["Totals","$47,685","$47,685"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 1 — Close Revenue accounts to Income Summary**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Consulting revenue","7,850",""],["","Rental revenue","300",""],["","Income Summary","","8,150"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Consulting Revenue","Consulting Revenue"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["7,850","7,850 (Beg.)"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income Summary (running)","Income Summary (running)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["","8,150"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 2 — Close Expense accounts to Income Summary**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Income Summary","4,365",""],["","Depreciation expense—Equipment","","375"],["","Salaries expense","","1,610"],["","Insurance expense","","100"],["","Rent expense","","1,000"],["","Supplies expense","","1,050"],["","Utilities expense","","230"]]},{"t":"p","x":"4,365 = 375 + 1,610 + 100 + 1,000 + 1,050 + 230, the sum of all expense balances."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income Summary (after steps 1 & 2)","Income Summary (after steps 1 & 2)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["4,365","8,150"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Net Profit (balance in Income Summary) = 8,150 − 4,365 = $3,785.**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 3 — Close Income Summary to Retained Earnings**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Income Summary","3,785",""],["","Retained Earnings","","3,785"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Retained Earnings (after step 3)","Retained Earnings (after step 3)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["","3,785"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Step 4 — Close Dividends to Retained Earnings**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Dec 31","Retained Earnings","200",""],["","Dividends","","200"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Retained Earnings (final)","Retained Earnings (final)"],["Debit (+)","Credit (−)"],["200","3,785"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Final Retained Earnings balance = 3,785 − 200 = $3,585.**"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Alternative Method for Dividends Declared"},{"t":"p","x":"Instead of debiting Retained Earnings directly when a dividend is declared, a company may use a separate \"Dividends Declared\" account:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Direct method","Alternative (Dividends account) method"],["Dr. Retained Earnings\nCr. Dividends Payable","Dr. Dividends (Declared)\nCr. Dividends Payable"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Either way, the Dividends account (if used) is a temporary account eventually closed to Retained Earnings at period-end (Step 4 above)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Post-Closing Trial Balance"},{"t":"li","x":"Prepared as a final check: debits still equal credits, and all temporary (income statement + dividends) accounts have been reduced to zero."},{"t":"li","x":"Retained Earnings is now fully up to date and matches the balance sheet; it becomes the beginning balance for the next period."},{"t":"li","x":"This marks the end of the accounting cycle for the period."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.9 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 4"},{"t":"li","x":"I can name and distinguish the 4 types of adjustments (deferred revenue, accrued revenue, deferred expense, accrued expense) and write the correct generic journal entry for each."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute supplies expense from beginning balance + purchases − ending count."},{"t":"li","x":"I can allocate a prepaid expense (rent/insurance/advertising) across the months it covers."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute a period's depreciation from an annual figure (pro-rate by months) and I know Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute accrued wages, accrued interest, and accrued utilities at period-end."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain why Cash is never touched in an adjusting entry."},{"t":"li","x":"I can distinguish permanent from temporary accounts."},{"t":"li","x":"I can perform all 4 steps of closing using an Income Summary account, and explain the 2 purposes of closing."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the alternative \"Dividends Declared\" account method and how/when it gets closed."},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain what a post-closing trial balance checks for."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.7 Official Practice — Worksheet E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2"},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Extraction gap: unlike Chapters 1–3, no \"Suggested-solutions\" file for Chapter 4's exercises (E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2) was found among the files shared on Moodle at the time of extraction, and the blank worksheet files (\"Chapter4-Worksheet...\") only contain the answer TEMPLATE (empty debit/credit/statement tables), not the original transaction data itself. This means the underlying numbers for these four exercises could not be recovered from the source material. Please obtain the official answer key from your instructor or Connect/Moodle once posted, and use it alongside the structure below and the fully worked FastForward closing-entries example above as your primary study tool for this chapter."},{"t":"p","x":"What each blank worksheet tells us about the required task (from its template structure):"},{"t":"li","x":"E4-8 and E4-9: each require 7 adjusting journal entries (a–g), in the standard Debit/Credit two-column format — practice recognizing which of the 4 adjustment types (deferred revenue, accrued revenue, deferred expense, accrued expense) applies to each letter."},{"t":"li","x":"P4-7 (Tunstall, Inc.): requires (1) 6 adjusting entries (a–f); (2) a full classified Income Statement (down to Net Income) and Balance Sheet; (3) a closing entry."},{"t":"li","x":"COMP4-2 (Furniture Refinishers, Inc.): a comprehensive problem — adjusting entries (a–l), further entries (m–q), a full Income Statement, Statement of Stockholders' Equity, and Balance Sheet, a cash-flow effect classification table, and closing entries done BOTH without and WITH an Income Summary account, all posted to T-accounts."},{"t":"p","x":"Use the complementary exercises below (C4.4 and C4.5 in particular) as a stand-in worked example covering the same skills — they are built to the same pattern as E4-8/E4-9/P4-7/COMP4-2, with a full answer key."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.8 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 4 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.1 (MCQ) — Identify the adjustment type"},{"t":"p","x":"A gym collected $12,000 cash on 1 December for a 12-month membership. By 31 December, one month of service has been provided. What adjustment type applies, and what is the December 31 adjusting entry?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Accrued revenue — Debit Cash, Credit Revenue $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"B. Deferred revenue — Debit Unearned Revenue $1,000, Credit Revenue $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"C. Accrued expense — Debit Expense, Credit Payable $1,000"},{"t":"li","x":"D. No adjustment is needed"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Cash was received in the past before the service was delivered = deferred (unearned) revenue. One month's worth ($12,000 ÷ 12 = $1,000) has now been earned, so $1,000 moves from the liability to revenue."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.2 (MCQ) — Accumulated depreciation"},{"t":"p","x":"Accumulated Depreciation is best described as:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. A liability account with a normal credit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"B. A contra-asset account with a normal credit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"C. An expense account with a normal debit balance"},{"t":"li","x":"D. A contra-liability account with a normal debit balance"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Accumulated Depreciation offsets (reduces) a related asset's cost, so it is a contra-asset — even though assets normally carry debit balances, a contra-asset carries the OPPOSITE (credit) balance."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.3 (Short problem) — Compute 4 adjusting entries"},{"t":"p","x":"At 31 December (year-end), Coral Bay Resort Ltd. has the following unadjusted facts:"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Supplies account shows $2,400; a physical count finds $900 still on hand."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Prepaid Insurance of $6,000 was paid on 1 October for 12 months of coverage."},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Equipment has estimated annual depreciation of $9,600; none has been recorded yet for the year."},{"t":"li","x":"(d) Employees earned $2,100 of wages in the last week of December, to be paid in January."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: prepare the 4 adjusting entries."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","1,500",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","1,500"],["","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used.","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used.","$2,400 − $900 on hand = $1,500 used."],["(b)","Insurance Expense (+E, −SE)","1,500",""],["","Prepaid Insurance (−A)","","1,500"],["","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used.","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used.","$6,000 ÷ 12 months × 3 months (Oct–Dec) = $1,500 used."],["(c)","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","9,600",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA)","","9,600"],["","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year.","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year.","Full annual amount, since none was recorded yet this year."],["(d)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","2,100",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","2,100"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.4 (Comprehensive problem) — Adjusting entries → Income Statement → Balance Sheet (in the style of P4-7)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: the account list below is a partial extract (only the accounts needed for this exercise are shown) — it is not a full trial balance, so its balances are not expected to foot to Assets = Liabilities + SE on their own. Palmtree Café, Inc.'s unadjusted account balances at 31 December include: Cash $9,000; Accounts receivable $4,000; Supplies $3,200; Prepaid rent $6,000 (paid 1 Sep for 6 months); Equipment $40,000; Accumulated depreciation $8,000; Accounts payable $2,500; Unearned revenue $5,000 (2/3 earned by year-end); Common stock $20,000; Retained earnings (beginning) $7,000; Service revenue $58,000; Wages expense $22,000; Utilities expense $1,600. Additional year-end facts: (i) supplies on hand $900; (ii) annual depreciation on equipment is $4,000; (iii) 2/3 of the unearned revenue has now been earned; (iv) employees are owed an additional $1,300 of unpaid wages; assume no income tax for simplicity."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (1) Journalize the 4 adjusting entries. (2) Prepare a classified Income Statement. (3) Prepare the asset and liability sections that would appear on a classified Balance Sheet (an SE section is not required since not all SE-affecting accounts were given)."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Adjusting entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(i)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","2,300",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","2,300"],["","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used.","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used.","$3,200 − $900 on hand = $2,300 used."],["(ii)","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","2,000",""],["","Prepaid Rent (−A)","","2,000"],["","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000.","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000.","$6,000 ÷ 6 months × 2 months (Nov–Dec) used = $2,000."],["(iii)","Depreciation Expense (+E, −SE)","4,000",""],["","Accumulated Depreciation (+xA)","","4,000"],["(iv)","Unearned Revenue (−L)","3,333",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,333"],["","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned.","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned.","$5,000 × 2/3 ≈ $3,333 now earned."],["(v)","Wages Expense (+E, −SE)","1,300",""],["","Wages Payable (+L)","","1,300"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(2) Classified Income Statement**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Palmtree Café, Inc. — Income Statement, For the Year Ended December 31",""],["Service revenue ($58,000 + $3,333)","$61,333"],["Less: Wages expense ($22,000 + $1,300)","23,300"],["Less: Utilities expense","1,600"],["Less: Supplies expense","2,300"],["Less: Rent expense","2,000"],["Less: Depreciation expense","4,000"],["Total expenses","33,200"],["Net income","$28,133"]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(3) Balance Sheet — asset and liability sections**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Palmtree Café, Inc. — Balance Sheet (partial), At December 31",""],["Cash","$9,000"],["Accounts receivable","4,000"],["Supplies","900"],["Prepaid rent","4,000"],["Total current assets","17,900"],["Equipment","40,000"],["Less: Accumulated depreciation","(12,000)"],["Equipment, net","28,000"],["Total assets (shown accounts only)","$45,900"],["Accounts payable","$2,500"],["Wages payable","1,300"],["Unearned revenue ($5,000 − $3,333)","1,667"],["Total current liabilities","5,467"]]},{"t":"a","x":"For reference, ending Retained Earnings (which would appear in the Stockholders' Equity section together with Common Stock $20,000) = Beginning RE $7,000 + Net income $28,133 − Dividends $0 = $35,133."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C4.5 (Closing entries problem, in the style of COMP4-2)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using Palmtree Café, Inc.'s adjusted figures from C4.4 (Service revenue $61,333; Wages expense $23,300; Utilities expense $1,600; Supplies expense $2,300; Rent expense $2,000; Depreciation expense $4,000; no dividends declared), prepare the closing entries using the Income Summary method."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Step 1","Service Revenue","61,333",""],["","Income Summary","","61,333"],["","Close the revenue account.","Close the revenue account.","Close the revenue account."],["Step 2","Income Summary","33,200",""],["","Wages Expense","","23,300"],["","Utilities Expense","","1,600"],["","Supplies Expense","","2,300"],["","Rent Expense","","2,000"],["","Depreciation Expense","","4,000"],["","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200).","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200).","Close the expense accounts (total = 23,300+1,600+2,300+2,000+4,000 = 33,200)."],["Step 3","Income Summary","28,133",""],["","Retained Earnings","","28,133"],["","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings.","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings.","Close Income Summary (balance = 61,333 − 33,200 = 28,133 = Net Income) to Retained Earnings."],["Step 4","— (not applicable)","",""],["","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared).","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared).","No Dividends account to close this period (none declared)."]]}]},"6":{"title":"Sales Revenue, Receivables, and Cash: Sales Revenue","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Note: only \"Part 1: Sales Revenue\" was shared as a lecture for this course at the time of extraction (per the Teaching Schedule, Receivables and Cash are separate weeks — Week 6 and Week 7 — with their own class practice questions E6-15/E6-21/E6-22/P6-3 and E6-28/P6-5/P6-6, but no lecture files for those weeks were found among the extracted materials). This section therefore covers Sales Revenue only."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.1 Accounting for Net Sales Revenue"},{"t":"p","x":"The revenue recognition principle requires revenue to be recorded (1) when the company transfers goods/services to customers, and (2) in the amount it expects to be entitled to receive."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"When Does Title Transfer? (Shipping Terms)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Title changes hands...","Who normally pays shipping"],["FOB Destination","On delivery to the buyer","Seller"],["FOB Shipping Point","At the point of shipment","Buyer"]]},{"t":"li","x":"FOB = \"Free On Board\"."},{"t":"li","x":"Service companies generally record revenue when the service has been provided."},{"t":"li","x":"Direct-to-consumer sales are recognized at point of sale (retail stores) or upon shipment (website sales)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.2 Motivating Sales & Collections"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies use three main tools to encourage sales and prompt payment:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Allowing customers to pay by credit/debit card."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Offering business customers direct credit with early-payment discounts."},{"t":"li","x":"3. Allowing returns under certain conditions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Credit (and Debit) Card Sales"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies accept card payments to: increase customer traffic, avoid the cost of extending credit directly, lower bad-cheque risk, avoid fraud losses, and receive money faster. The same accounting applies to contactless mobile payments (Apple Pay, Google Pay)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key rule: **the card company's fee (the \"credit card discount\") is recorded as part of Selling, General, and Administrative (SG&A) Expense — NOT as a reduction of sales revenue."},{"t":"p","x":"Example: Skechers has $3,000 of online credit-card sales on Jan 2, with a 3% card fee:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["","Cash (+A)","2,910",""],["","Selling, General & Administrative Expense (+E, −SE)","90",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"2,910 = 3,000 × (1 − 3%); 90 = 3,000 × 3%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sales Discounts to Businesses"},{"t":"p","x":"Companies selling to other businesses on open account may offer an early-payment (\"cash\") discount, written in shorthand like 2/10, n/30:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Notation","Meaning"],["2/10, n/30","2% discount if paid within 10 days; otherwise the full (net) amount is due within 30 days."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Example: credit sales of $1,000, terms 2/10, n/30. If the customer pays within the discount period: $1,000 × 0.98 = $980 is collected, and net sales of $980 are ultimately reported for this sale. If payment is made AFTER the discount period, the full $1,000 is reported as net sales instead."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key rule: **Sales Discounts is recorded as a contra-revenue account (it reduces gross sales revenue on the income statement), unlike the credit card discount above."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sales Returns and Allowances"},{"t":"p","x":"If merchandise is unsatisfactory or damaged, a customer may: (i) return it for a refund (a return), or (ii) receive a bill reduction (an allowance). Both are recorded in Sales Returns and Allowances — always a contra-revenue account."},{"t":"p","x":"Example: Skechers sells 40 pairs of shoes to Dick's Sporting Goods for $2,000 on account; before paying, 10 pairs (wrong color) are returned."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Sale","Accounts Receivable (+A)","2,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","2,000"],["Return","Sales Returns and Allowances (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","500",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","500"],["","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair).","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair).","10 of 40 pairs returned = 1/4 of the sale = $500 (10 pairs × $50/pair)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["",""],["Sales revenue","$2,000"],["Less: Sales returns and allowances (10 pairs × $50)","500"],["Net sales (reported on the income statement)","$1,500"]]},{"t":"p","x":"The related Cost of Goods Sold for the returned units would also be reversed (covered in Chapter 7) — not required for this course's Chapter 6 coverage."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.3 Reporting Net Sales — the Formula"},{"t":"table","rows":[["",""],["Sales revenue","$6,000"],["Less: Sales discounts (a contra-revenue)","(20)"],["Less: Sales returns and allowances (a contra-revenue)","(500)"],["Net Sales (the first line reported on the income statement)","$5,480"]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ The Credit Card Discount is NOT part of this formula — it is an SG&A expense, so it never reduces the \"Net Sales\" line itself. Sales Discounts and Sales Returns and Allowances ARE contra-revenues and DO reduce Net Sales. This distinction is the single most commonly tested trap in this chapter."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.4 Worked Concept Checks (with computed answers)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Concept Check MC#1"},{"t":"p","x":"Pro-Tree's gross sales total $280,000: credit sales $160,000, credit card sales $90,000, cash sales $30,000. Sales returns and allowances of $16,000 apply to the credit sales; a 3% sales discount was taken on all of the resulting net credit sales; credit card sales of $90,000 were subject to a 4% credit card discount. What is net sales?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $256,080  B. $259,680  C. $264,000  D. $271,600  E. $280,000"},{"t":"p","x":"**Worked answer: B, $259,680. **Net credit sales after returns = $160,000 − $16,000 = $144,000. Sales discount = 3% × $144,000 = $4,320. Net sales = Gross sales $280,000 − Returns $16,000 − Discount $4,320 = $259,680. The 4% credit card discount ($3,600) is EXCLUDED from this calculation — it is recorded as SG&A expense, not as a reduction of net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Concept Check MC#2"},{"t":"p","x":"Playgroup Corporation records an increase in sales returns and allowances, and more customers take the 2% sales discount. What happens to net income, all else equal?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. No change  B. Increases  C. Decreases  D. Either (a) or (b)  E. Either (b) or (c)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Worked answer: C, decreases. **Both sales returns/allowances and sales discounts are contra-revenues; increasing either one reduces Net Sales, which (with expenses unchanged) reduces net income."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.7 Exam-Style Review Checklist — Chapter 6 (Part 1)"},{"t":"li","x":"I can explain the difference between FOB shipping point and FOB destination, and when title/revenue recognition occurs under each."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a credit card sale, correctly treating the card fee as SG&A expense (not a reduction of sales revenue)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can interpret credit terms like \"2/10, n/30\" and compute the discount when payment falls within the discount window."},{"t":"li","x":"I can journalize a sales return/allowance as a contra-revenue."},{"t":"li","x":"I can compute Net Sales = Sales Revenue − Sales Discounts − Sales Returns and Allowances (excluding the credit card discount)."},{"t":"li","x":"I can work a multi-step problem combining a credit sale, a partial return, and a discounted collection."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.5 Official Practice — Worksheet E6-3 and P6-7"},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ Extraction gap: no \"Suggested-solutions\" file for E6-3 or P6-7 was found among the Moodle materials, and the underlying transaction amounts for E6-3's Nov 20/25/28 events and for P6-7's transactions (a)–(m) were not present in the extracted text (the worksheet files only contain the blank answer template). The task requirements below are taken directly from the official instructions that WERE extracted."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"E6-3 (with additional requirement) — official instructions"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Give the appropriate journal entry for each transaction (a credit sale on Nov 20, a credit card sale on Nov 25, and a further event on Nov 28). Do not record cost of goods sold."},{"t":"li","x":"2. Assuming Sales Returns and Allowances and Sales Discounts are contra-revenues, and Credit Card Discounts are part of SG&A expense, compute net sales for the two months ended December 31."},{"t":"p","x":"Since the exact dollar amounts for the Nov 20/25/28 events were not recoverable from the extracted files, use the fully worked example in section 6.2 above (Skechers credit card sale, and the sales-returns example) as your template for the journal entry mechanics, and see complementary exercise C6.4 below for an equivalent fully-solved version of this exact exercise type."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"P6-7 (refers to P6-1, parts a–k) — official instructions"},{"t":"li","x":"Req. 1: prepare journal entries for transactions (a) through (m), including: (a) cash sales for the year; (b) a $11,500 credit sale; and further sales/collections/returns/discounts/card-fee transactions (c)–(m)."},{"t":"li","x":"Req. 2: prepare the \"Income statement (extracted)\" showing Sales revenue, less contra-revenues, Net sales revenue, and SG&A expenses including Bad debt expense."},{"t":"p","x":"Bad debt expense (part of Req. 2's SG&A section) is a receivables topic covered in the following week's lecture (\"Receivables\", not shared in the extracted materials) — flagged here so you know to revisit this specific line once you've studied that topic."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.6 Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6 Part 1 (original, with full answer key)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.1 (MCQ) — FOB terms"},{"t":"p","x":"A seller ships goods FOB shipping point. When does the seller recognize sales revenue (assuming all other revenue-recognition criteria are met)?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. When the buyer receives the goods"},{"t":"li","x":"B. When the goods are shipped"},{"t":"li","x":"C. When the buyer pays for the goods"},{"t":"li","x":"D. When the order is placed"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Under FOB shipping point, title (and the risk) transfers to the buyer at the point of shipment, so that is when revenue is recognized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.2 (MCQ) — Discount terms"},{"t":"p","x":"Credit terms of \"3/15, n/45\" mean:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. 3% discount if paid within 45 days, full amount due in 15 days"},{"t":"li","x":"B. 15% discount if paid within 3 days, full amount due in 45 days"},{"t":"li","x":"C. 3% discount if paid within 15 days, full amount due within 45 days"},{"t":"li","x":"D. 45% discount if paid within 3 days, full amount due in 15 days"},{"t":"a","x":"C."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.3 (Short problem) — Card fee vs. sales discount"},{"t":"p","x":"Cedar Retail Ltd. had the following for June: cash sales $10,000; credit card sales $25,000 (2.5% card fee); credit sales (on account) $40,000 with terms 1/10, n/30 — of this, $30,000 was collected within the discount period and $10,000 after. There were no returns."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: (a) journalize the credit card sales. (b) journalize the collection of the $30,000 within the discount period. (c) compute June's net sales."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","24,375",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","625",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","25,000"],["","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625.","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625.","625 = 25,000 × 2.5%; 24,375 = 25,000 − 625."],["(b)","Cash (+A)","29,700",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","300",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","30,000"],["","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300.","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300.","300 = 30,000 × 1%; 29,700 = 30,000 − 300."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(c) Net sales for June: **Gross sales = 10,000 (cash) + 25,000 (card) + 40,000 (credit) = $75,000. Less: Sales discounts $300 (only on the $30,000 paid within the discount period; the other $10,000 was paid after the discount period and stays at full value). Net sales = $75,000 − $300 = $74,700. (The $625 credit card discount is SG&A expense and does NOT reduce net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.4 (Journal entry problem, mirrors the official E6-3 structure) — Original numbers"},{"t":"p","x":"Ridgeline Traders Ltd. completed these November transactions:"},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 20 — Sold merchandise on account (credit sale) for $8,000, terms 2/10, n/30. (Do not record cost of goods sold.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 25 — Sold merchandise for $3,000 to a walk-in customer who paid by credit card; the card company charges a 3% fee."},{"t":"li","x":"Nov 28 — Received payment in full from the Nov 20 customer, within the discount period."},{"t":"p","x":"Required: (1) journalize all three transactions. (2) Compute net sales for November, assuming Sales Discounts and Sales Returns/Allowances are contra-revenues and the credit card fee is SG&A expense."},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Journal entries"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Nov 20","Accounts Receivable (+A)","8,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","8,000"],["Nov 25","Cash (+A)","2,910",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","90",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","3,000"],["","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90.","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90.","90 = 3,000 × 3%; 2,910 = 3,000 − 90."],["Nov 28","Cash (+A)","7,840",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue, +xR, −SE)","160",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","8,000"],["","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160.","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160.","160 = 8,000 × 2% (paid within the 10-day discount window); 7,840 = 8,000 − 160."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**(2) Net sales for November: **Sales revenue = 8,000 + 3,000 = $11,000. Less: Sales discounts $160. Net sales = $10,840. (The $90 card fee is SG&A expense, not a reduction of net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"C6.5 (Comprehensive short problem) — Sales returns & discount interacting"},{"t":"p","x":"On 1 May, Alpine Sports sells $5,000 of goods on account, terms 2/10, n/30. On 4 May, the customer returns $1,000 of defective goods. On 9 May, the customer pays the remaining balance in full."},{"t":"li","x":"Required: journalize the sale, the return, and the collection, and state the net sales recognized."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["May 1","Accounts Receivable (+A)","5,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","5,000"],["May 4","Sales Returns and Allowances (contra-revenue)","1,000",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","1,000"],["May 9","Cash (+A)","3,920",""],["","Sales Discounts (contra-revenue)","80",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","4,000"],["","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80.","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80.","The discount period runs from the sale date, and payment on day 9 is within the 10-day window. The discount applies to the remaining balance owed after the return: (5,000 − 1,000) × 2% = 80."]]},{"t":"a","x":"**Net sales recognized: **$5,000 − $1,000 (return) − $80 (discount) = $3,920."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS1103**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Financial Accounting**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Notes & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Chapters 1–4 and Chapter 6 (Part 1: Sales Revenue) — 1st Term 2026/27, Lingnan University"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all course materials shared on Moodle, with original complementary practice questions and full worked answer keys."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"**Course Overview**"},{"t":"p","x":"1. Course Identification · 2. Instructor(s) · 3. Assessment Structure · 4. Textbook & Connect Access"},{"t":"p","x":"5. Weekly Teaching Schedule · 6. Chapter 5 Gap · 7. Textbook Exclusions/Additions · 8. Learning Outcomes · 9. Policies"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 1 — Financial Statements and Business Decisions**"},{"t":"p","x":"Financial statements, GAAP/IFRS, accounting assumptions & principles, accounting ethics (HKICPA), official practice (E1-11/12/13), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 2 — Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System**"},{"t":"p","x":"Balance sheet concepts, transaction analysis, journal entries & T-accounts, trial balance, official practice (P2-1/2/3/5), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 3 — Operating Decisions and the Accounting System**"},{"t":"p","x":"Accrual vs. cash accounting, revenue & expense recognition, worked transaction sets, official practice (E3-2/3/7, AP3-6), complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 4 — Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process**"},{"t":"p","x":"4 types of adjustments, depreciation, the closing process (Income Summary method, full worked example), official practice notes, complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"(Chapter 5 is confirmed excluded from this course's syllabus — see Course Overview §6)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Chapter 6 (Part 1) — Sales Revenue**"},{"t":"p","x":"FOB terms, credit card sales, sales discounts, sales returns & allowances, net sales, official practice notes, complementary exercises, review checklist"},{"t":"p","x":"**Mixed Exam-Style Practice Set — All Chapters**"},{"t":"p","x":"10 mixed questions (MCQ, short answer, journal entries, comprehensive problems) spanning Ch.1–4 and 6, with a full answer key"},{"t":"p","x":"**Appendix — Source Material Index & Known Gaps**"},{"t":"p","x":"Every source file mapped to its section, plus a consolidated list of content gaps in the Moodle materials"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"p","x":"This section summarizes the course administration information from the Course Syllabus, Teaching Schedule, Week 1 introduction slides, and the Connect/textbook purchase notices shared on Moodle. It is here for reference — the study content itself starts in Chapter 1."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Course Identification"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Details"],["Course Title / Code","Financial Accounting — BUS1103"],["Institution","Lingnan University, Department of Accountancy"],["Term","1st Term 2026/27"],["Credits / Contact Hours","3 credits — 42 hours total (3-hour lecture per week)"],["Category","Foundation Core"],["Recommended Study Year","Year 1 / Year 2"],["Prerequisite / Co-requisite / Exclusion","NIL / NIL / NIL"],["Mode","Sectional approach (this document follows Section 4)"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Instructor(s)"},{"t":"p","x":"The course runs in several parallel sections, each with its own instructor. Section 4 (the section these lecture slides come from) is taught by:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Name","Contact"],["Dr. Tracy H. Y. Yeung (Section 4)","Email: tracyhyyeung@ln.edu.hk · Phone: 2616-8187 · Office: SEK 201/11, Simon and Eleanor Kwok Building · Consultation: Mon 2–4pm, Wed 10am–12noon (book by email in advance)"],["Other sections' instructors","Mr. Asamoah, Joseph Maxwell · Prof. Wang Rui Erik · Mr. Xie Yinjie"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Assessment Structure"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight","Notes"],["Class Participation","5%","Attendance, contribution to discussion, preparedness (see rubric)"],["Online Assignments (Connect)","10%","Weekly; 2 attempts allowed (best score counts); need ≥40% and on-time submission for credit; no credit for late submission"],["Computerized Accounting Project (MYOB)","5%","Full accounting-cycle project; max 3 students/group; recommended to start after the midterm"],["Ethical Dilemma Analysis","5%","Given together with the midterm; based on HKICPA ethical principles"],["Mid-term Examination","25%","25 Oct (Sun), 12:00–14:30, Venue: Sin Cho Ming Function Hall (LCHLG01). MCQ + long problems. Covers Ch.1–4, Ch.6 (Sales Revenue), and Accounting Ethics"],["Final Examination","50%","Cumulative — Ch.1–12 excluding Ch.5. Long problems only. Closed book/notes"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Total: 100%. **Both exams are closed-book and closed-notes. Students absent from an exam receive zero unless valid documentary proof of exceptional circumstances is provided."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Textbook & Connect Access"},{"t":"li","x":"Required textbook: Financial Accounting, McGraw-Hill Education, 12th International Student Edition, by Libby, Libby & Hodge."},{"t":"li","x":"Students must purchase ONE option: (i) E-edition = Connect access code only (with e-book access), or (ii) Bundle = printed book + Connect access code."},{"t":"li","x":"Connect access is required because online assignments (10% of the grade) are completed through it."},{"t":"li","x":"Connect license validity: 180 days from registration; a 14-day free trial access is available in week 1 while waiting for the purchased code to arrive (results carry over once the code is redeemed)."},{"t":"li","x":"Ordering: through SUP Bookshop (Google Form, Visa/Mastercard only) or A&P Bookshop — see the purchase e-mail for the exact links; access-code lead time is 5–7 working days, so order in week 1."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Using Connect (quick reference)"},{"t":"li","x":"Register once via your section's Class URL using your Lingnan student e-mail, student ID, and English full name, then redeem your 20-digit access code (or start the 14-day free trial)."},{"t":"li","x":"The Connect e-book includes a search tool, adjustable font size, highlighting/notes/placemarks, a translation tool, and an \"AI Reader\" that can simplify text, explain graphs/tables, and check your understanding — useful for reviewing dense chapters."},{"t":"li","x":"Assignments: note the due date, number of attempts, and time limit before starting; \"Save & Exit\" preserves progress (but a timed assignment's clock keeps running)."},{"t":"li","x":"Tech support: +80-881-39190 (Asia) or international-asia@mheducation.com."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Weekly Teaching Schedule"},{"t":"p","x":"(Subject to revision at the instructor's discretion based on course progress.)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Week","Ch.","Topic","Class Practice Questions","Online Assignment Due"],["1","1","Financial Statements and Business Decisions","E1-11, E1-12, E1-13","18 Sep"],["2","2","Investing and Financing Decisions and the Accounting System","P2-1, P2-2, P2-3, P2-5","2 Oct"],["3","3","Operating Decisions and the Accounting System","E3-2, E3-3, E3-7, AP3-6","—"],["4","4","Adjustments, Financial Statements, and the Closing Process","E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2","9 Oct"],["5","6","Sales Revenue","E6-3, P6-7","16 Oct"],["6","6","Receivables","E6-15, E6-21, E6-22, P6-3","—"],["7","6","Cash","E6-28, P6-5, P6-6","30 Oct"],["—","—","Midterm Test — 25 Oct (Sun), 12:00–14:30, Sin Cho Ming Function Hall. Covers Ch.1–4, Ch.6 (Sales Revenue), and Ethics","",""],["8","7","Cost of Goods Sold and Inventory","E7-1, E7-13, E7-22","6 Nov"],["9","8","Property, Plant, and Equipment; Intangibles; Natural Resources","E8-5, E8-10, E8-16, E8-19, E8-21, E8-24","13 Nov"],["10","9 & 10","Liabilities & Bond Securities","Ch.9: E9-4, E9-9, P9-2; Ch.10: P10-2","20 Nov"],["11","11","Stockholders' Equity","E11-15, E11-21, E11-22, P11-2, P11-5, P11-8","27 Nov"],["12","12","Statement of Cash Flows","E12-1, E12-4, E12-10, E12-16, AP12-2","30 Nov"],["13","—","Review — Computerized project due 12:00 noon, 2 Dec (Wed)","",""]]},{"t":"p","x":"⚠ This document covers Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, and 6 (Part 1 — Sales Revenue only), matching the lecture files that were shared on Moodle. Chapters 6 (Receivables & Cash), 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 12 were not shared as lecture files at the time of extraction and are NOT covered here."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Chapter 5 — Confirmed Gap"},{"t":"p","x":"The official \"Course Learning Objectives: Exclusions/Additions\" table in the Teaching Schedule explicitly states for Chapter 5: \"All excluded.\" No Chapter 5 lecture exists because the whole chapter is dropped from this course's syllabus — this is not a missing file, it is intentional. The teaching schedule jumps directly from Chapter 4 to Chapter 6."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Textbook Content Exclusions/Additions (relative to the standard textbook)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Chapter","Excluded","Added"],["1","N/A","HKICPA code of ethics"],["2","2.5 (current ratio), 2.6","—"],["3","3.6, 3.7","—"],["4","4.2 (earnings per share), 4.3","—"],["5","All excluded","—"],["6","6.3","—"],["7","7.5, 7.6","—"],["8","8.1 (fixed asset turnover ratio), 8.7","—"],["9","9.2, 9.3 (time value of money), 9.5, 9.7, 9.8","—"],["10","10.3 – 10.8","—"],["11","11.2, 11.5, 11.9","—"],["12","12.3, 12.5","—"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Financial ratios and ratio analysis are generally not covered in this course."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8. Learning Outcomes (from the Syllabus)"},{"t":"p","x":"On completion of the course, students should be able to:"},{"t":"li","x":"Discuss the role of accounting in business organizations and capital markets."},{"t":"li","x":"Make judgments on the ethical issues involved in financial reporting."},{"t":"li","x":"Discuss the accounting assumptions, principles and limitations underlying the preparation of financial statements, and apply them to different circumstances."},{"t":"li","x":"Prepare the four essential financial statements, explain their uses and inter-relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"Manually work through the accounting cycle and use accounting software (MYOB) to prepare financial statements from a given set of transactions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9. Course Policies Worth Remembering"},{"t":"li","x":"Visit Moodle at least 3 times a week — lecture notes, suggested solutions, and announcements are posted there regularly."},{"t":"li","x":"Read the relevant chapter(s) before each class; this is when \"in-class practice\" and \"class practice questions\" get reviewed."},{"t":"li","x":"Homework/online assignments: 2 attempts, higher score counts, unlimited time before the deadline, save-and-exit supported."},{"t":"li","x":"Both exams are closed-book, closed-notes; the final exam is cumulative."},{"t":"li","x":"Academic integrity: written assignments go through Turnitin; plagiarism (including AI-generated text presented as one's own without following the university's GAI usage guidelines) is a serious disciplinary matter."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Exam-Style Practice Set — All Chapters"},{"t":"p","x":"10 questions spanning Chapters 1–4 and 6 (Part 1), mixing multiple choice, short answer, journal entries, and one comprehensive problem — similar in mix and difficulty to the midterm (which covers exactly this material: Ch.1–4, Ch.6 Sales Revenue, and Ethics). Attempt every question before checking the answer key."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Questions"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following would NOT appear on a classified balance sheet?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Accounts Payable   B. Cost of Goods Sold   C. Retained Earnings   D. Prepaid Insurance"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company receives $9,000 cash on 1 October for services to be rendered evenly over the next 6 months. Under accrual accounting, how much revenue should be recognized by 31 December of the same year?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $0   B. $1,500   C. $4,500   D. $9,000"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which statement about Accumulated Depreciation is correct?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. It is an expense account closed at year-end.   B. It is a contra-asset account with a normal credit balance.   C. It appears on the income statement.   D. It increases with a debit."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Fern Co. had gross sales of $50,000, sales returns and allowances of $2,000, sales discounts of $800, and credit card discounts of $500 (credit card discounts are SG&A expense). What is net sales?"},{"t":"li","x":"A. $46,700   B. $47,200   C. $48,000   D. $49,500"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the accounting assumption or principle illustrated in each: (a) A Hong Kong company reports all figures in HKD without adjusting for inflation. (b) A firm records a machine at the $200,000 it paid, even though it is now believed to be worth $230,000. (c) A firm delays recognizing a $50,000 sale until the goods are actually shipped to the customer, even though the order was placed and paid for earlier."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Journal entries)"},{"t":"p","x":"Journalize the following independent transactions for Willow Corp.: (a) Issued 500 shares of $1 par common stock for $12 cash per share. (b) Purchased $3,000 of supplies on account. (c) Paid $2,000 cash for the current month's rent. (d) Provided $6,000 of services on account. (e) Received $4,500 cash from a customer as an advance for services to be performed next month."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q7 (Adjusting entries)"},{"t":"p","x":"At year-end, Willow Corp. (from Q6) has these additional facts: (i) $800 of the $3,000 supplies purchased in (b) remain on hand. (ii) One month has passed on the advance received in (e) — no services have yet been performed. (iii) The company estimates $250 of interest has accrued on a bank loan, to be paid next year. Prepare the 3 adjusting entries. (Hint for (ii): re-read the deferred revenue rule carefully before answering.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q8 (Comprehensive problem)"},{"t":"p","x":"Cascade Bikes, Inc. — adjusted trial balance extract at 31 December: Service revenue $84,000; Rental revenue $2,000; Wages expense $31,000; Rent expense $9,600; Depreciation expense $4,400; Utilities expense $1,900; Dividends declared $3,000; Retained Earnings (beginning) $18,500. Required: (a) Compute net income. (b) Prepare the 4 closing journal entries using the Income Summary method. (c) Compute ending Retained Earnings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q9 (Sales revenue comprehensive)"},{"t":"p","x":"Granite Hardware Ltd. had these June transactions: sold $20,000 of goods on account, terms 3/10, n/30; the customer returned $2,000 of goods 2 days later; the customer paid the remaining balance within the discount period. Separately, the store had $6,000 of credit card sales, with a 2.8% processing fee. Required: (a) journalize all events. (b) compute June net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q10 (Ethics — short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A junior accountant is told by their manager to record a large customer order as a completed sale in December, even though the goods will not ship until January, in order to hit the year's sales target. Identify (a) the accounting principle being violated, and (b) the most relevant HKICPA ethical principle(s) at stake."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set — Full Answer Key"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A1"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Cost of Goods Sold is an income-statement (expense) account, not a balance ssheet item. The other three are all balance sheet accounts (liability, stockholders' equity, and asset, respectively)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A2"},{"t":"a","x":"C, $4,500. $9,000 ÷ 6 months = $1,500/month earned. From 1 Oct to 31 Dec = 3 months (Oct, Nov, Dec) → 3 × $1,500 = $4,500 recognized as revenue; the remaining $4,500 stays as Unearned Revenue (a liability) at year-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A3"},{"t":"a","x":"B. Accumulated Depreciation is a contra-asset — it is a permanent balance sheet account (not closed at year-end, unlike expenses) that carries a normal CREDIT balance, the opposite of a regular asset."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A4"},{"t":"a","x":"B, $47,200. Net sales = Gross sales $50,000 − Sales returns and allowances $2,000 − Sales discounts $800 = $47,200. The $500 credit card discount is excluded because it is recorded as SG&A expense, not as a reduction of net sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A5"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Monetary unit assumption."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) Historical cost (measurement) principle."},{"t":"li","x":"(c) Revenue recognition principle (revenue is recognized on delivery/transfer of goods, not on order or cash receipt)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A6"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(a)","Cash (+A)","6,000",""],["","Common Stock (+SE)","","500"],["","Additional Paid-in Capital (+SE)","","5,500"],["","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC.","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC.","500 shares × $12 = $6,000 total; par value 500 × $1 = $500; the remainder $5,500 is APIC."],["(b)","Supplies (+A)","3,000",""],["","Accounts Payable (+L)","","3,000"],["(c)","Rent Expense (+E, −SE)","2,000",""],["","Cash (−A)","","2,000"],["(d)","Accounts Receivable (+A)","6,000",""],["","Service Revenue (+R, +SE)","","6,000"],["(e)","Cash (+A)","4,500",""],["","Unearned Revenue (+L)","","4,500"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A7"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["(i)","Supplies Expense (+E, −SE)","2,200",""],["","Supplies (−A)","","2,200"],["","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used.","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used.","3,000 − 800 on hand = 2,200 used."],["(ii)","— No adjusting entry required.","",""],["(iii)","Interest Expense (+E, −SE)","250",""],["","Interest Payable (+L)","","250"]]},{"t":"p","x":"(ii) explanation: this is a Deferred (Unearned) Revenue. An adjusting entry moving it into revenue is only needed once the service has actually been performed/earned. The scenario explicitly states no services have yet been performed, so the full $4,500 correctly remains as Unearned Revenue (a liability) — no entry is required yet, even though a month has passed. This tests whether you distinguish \"time has passed\" from \"the performance obligation has been satisfied\"."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A8"},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) Net income: **Revenues (84,000 + 2,000) − Expenses (31,000 + 9,600 + 4,400 + 1,900) = 86,000 − 46,900 = $39,100."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Step 1","Service Revenue","84,000",""],["","Rental Revenue","2,000",""],["","Income Summary","","86,000"],["Step 2","Income Summary","46,900",""],["","Wages Expense","","31,000"],["","Rent Expense","","9,600"],["","Depreciation Expense","","4,400"],["","Utilities Expense","","1,900"],["Step 3","Income Summary","39,100",""],["","Retained Earnings","","39,100"],["Step 4","Retained Earnings","3,000",""],["","Dividends Declared","","3,000"]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Ending Retained Earnings: **$18,500 + $39,100 − $3,000 = $54,600."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A9"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date/Ref","Account","Debit","Credit"],["Sale","Accounts Receivable (+A)","20,000",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","20,000"],["Return","Sales Returns and Allowances","2,000",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","2,000"],["Collection","Cash (+A)","17,460",""],["","Sales Discounts","540",""],["","Accounts Receivable (−A)","","18,000"],["","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540.","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540.","Discount on remaining balance: (20,000 − 2,000) × 3% = 540."],["Card sales","Cash (+A)","5,832",""],["","SG&A Expense — credit card discount (+E, −SE)","168",""],["","Sales Revenue (+R, +SE)","","6,000"],["","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168.","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168.","Fee = 6,000 × 2.8% = 168."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) June net sales: **Sales revenue (20,000 + 6,000) − Sales returns and allowances 2,000 − Sales discounts 540 = $23,460. (The $168 card fee is SG&A expense and does not reduce net sales.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A10"},{"t":"li","x":"(a) Principle violated: the revenue recognition principle — revenue must be recognized when goods/services are actually transferred to the customer, not before. Recording the sale in December when shipment happens in January overstates December's revenue and net income."},{"t":"li","x":"(b) HKICPA principles at stake: Integrity (being party to a materially misleading statement) and Objectivity (resisting pressure from the manager to bend the numbers to hit a target); Professional Behaviour is also relevant if it becomes a pattern that could discredit the profession. The correct action is to decline to record the sale early, explain the applicable principle to the manager, and escalate if the pressure continues."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Appendix — Source Material Index & Known Gaps"},{"t":"p","x":"For transparency and so you can go back to the original Moodle files if needed, here is what every one of the 23 source files became in this document, and a consolidated list of every content gap flagged throughout."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Source File Index"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Original Moodle file","Where it went in this document"],["#Week1 Introduction 1st-Term 2026-27 (S4).txt","Course Overview (instructor contact, class arrangement, assessment overview)"],["(a) [BUS1103 Textbook E-edition Purchase]...txt","Course Overview §4 (Textbook & Connect Access)"],["(b) [User Guide] Connect FDOC LU_BUS1103...txt","Course Overview §4 (Connect quick reference)"],["Course Syllabus and Rubrics...txt","Course Overview §1, §3, §8, §9"],["BUS1103 Teaching Schedule_1st term 2026-27.txt","Course Overview §5, §6, §7 (schedule, Ch.5 gap, exclusions/additions)"],["Lecture-Chapter1.txt","Chapter 1 — full notes + ethics case study"],["Accounting Assumptions and Principles [Q and A].txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (matching Q&A)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_E1-11_E1-12_E1-13.txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (flagged as blank/unextractable)"],["Suggested-solutions_E1-11_E1-12_E1-13.txt","Chapter 1 §1.7 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter2 (after class).txt","Chapter 2 — full notes"],["Ch2-Worksheet (P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5).txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (official question template)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5.txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (flagged as blank/unextractable)"],["Suggested-solutions_P2-1_P2-2_P2-3_P2-5.txt","Chapter 2 §2.11 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter3 (after class).txt","Chapter 3 — full notes"],["Ch3-Worksheet (E3-2_E3-3_E3-7_AP3-6).txt","Chapter 3 §3.7 (official question template)"],["Suggested-solutions_E3-2_E3-3_E3-7_AP3-6.txt","Chapter 3 §3.7 (official answer key)"],["Lecture-Chapter4 (after class).txt","Chapter 4 — full notes"],["[Note] Closing entries using Income summary.txt","Chapter 4 §4.6 (FastForward worked example)"],["Chapter4-Worksheet (E4-8_E4-9_P4-7).txt","Chapter 4 §4.7 (flagged — no solution found)"],["Chapter4-Worksheet (COMP4-2).txt","Chapter 4 §4.7 (flagged — no solution found)"],["Lecture-Ch6-Part1-Sales Revenue.txt","Chapter 6 (Part 1) — full notes"],["Ch6-Part1_Sales Revenue_Worksheet (E6-3_P6-7).txt","Chapter 6 §6.5 (flagged — no data/solution found)"],["ClassPracticeQuestions_E6-3 (add-requirement).txt","Chapter 6 §6.5 (official instructions)"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Consolidated List of Content Gaps"},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 5 is entirely absent by design — the official \"Exclusions/Additions\" table states \"Chapter 5: All excluded\" from this course's syllabus. This is confirmed, not a missing file."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapters 6 (Receivables & Cash — the rest of Chapter 6 beyond Sales Revenue), 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, and 12 were listed in the Teaching Schedule but no lecture slide files for them were found among the 23 extracted files — only Chapter 6 Part 1 (Sales Revenue) was shared. This document therefore stops after Chapter 6 Part 1, matching exactly the midterm's coverage (Ch.1–4, Ch.6 Sales Revenue, and Ethics), but does NOT cover material needed for the final exam beyond that point."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 1's official class practice questions (E1-11, E1-12, E1-13): the original question-prompt text extracted as blank/image-only pages. Only the official suggested-solutions file was usable, and its content is presented in full in §1.7; the task descriptions there are reconstructed from what the solutions imply."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 4's official class practice questions (E4-8, E4-9, P4-7, COMP4-2): no suggested-solutions file was found on Moodle, and the worksheet files contain only blank answer templates (no transaction data). Flagged clearly in §4.7; complementary exercises C4.3–C4.5 were built to cover the identical skills with full worked solutions instead."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6 Part 1's official class practice questions (E6-3, P6-7): no suggested-solutions file was found, and the underlying transaction amounts were not present in the extracted worksheet (template only). Flagged clearly in §6.5; complementary exercises C6.3–C6.5 (built to the same pattern, with original numbers) supply full worked solutions instead."},{"t":"li","x":"Chapter 6's Concept Check MC#1 and MC#2 (in the lecture slides) were posed without an answer key in the source file — this document computes and shows the correct answer with full reasoning in §6.4; cross-check against the instructor's own key if one is later released."},{"t":"li","x":"Several source files were OCR-extracted from scanned/photographed content (notably the textbook purchase e-mail and the Accounting Assumptions Q&A) and contained minor garbling (broken words, stray characters). Where this affected substance, the content was reconstructed using standard accounting knowledge and cross-checked against the parallel clean sources (e.g., the Chapter 1 lecture slides, which cover the same 9 assumptions/principles cleanly)."},{"t":"p","x":"If your instructor releases official solutions for the Chapter 4 and Chapter 6 exercises flagged above, it is worth updating this document (or your own notes) with the real numbers — the skills and method shown here are correct and exam-ready, but the specific dollar figures for those particular textbook problems could not be verified against an official key."}]},"BUS2108":{"file":"BUS2108_Global_Business_Environment_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Globalization","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.1 What is Globalization?"},{"t":"p","x":"Globalization: the shift toward a more integrated and interdependent world economy — away from a world of largely self-contained national economies and toward one with far lower barriers to the cross-border flow of goods, services, capital, people and ideas."},{"t":"p","x":"Evidence: trade and foreign investment have grown much faster than world output for over fifty years, alongside the rise of supra-national coordinating bodies (WTO, IMF, World Bank)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two Facets of Globalization"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Globalization of markets","The merging of historically distinct national markets into one global marketplace, as consumer tastes converge on some global norm. Splits into B2C (e.g. Coca-Cola, McDonald's, IKEA, Starbucks, Apple) and B2B. Most global of all are markets for undifferentiated industrial commodities: oil, aluminium, wheat."],["Globalization of production","Sourcing goods/services from wherever in the world offers the best combination of cost and quality of factors of production (labour, energy, land, capital) — e.g. Boeing's Dreamliner is built from components sourced worldwide. Impediments: formal/informal trade barriers, FDI barriers, transport costs, political/economic risk, and the managerial challenge of coordinating a dispersed supply chain."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Emergence of Global Institutions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Institution","Role"],["WTO","Polices the world trading system; enforces WTO treaty rules; successor to GATT. Covered ~164 nations / 98% of world trade (2017)."],["IMF","Maintains order in the international monetary system; acts as lender of last resort, typically requiring policy reform in return for loans."],["World Bank","Promotes economic development through low-interest loans; generally seen as less politically controversial than the IMF."],["UN","Maintains international peace/security, develops friendly relations among nations, promotes human rights and cooperation; 193 member states."],["G20","Finance ministers/central bank governors of the 19 largest economies + the EU (~90% of global GDP); became the main forum for coordinated crisis response after 2008–09."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two Drivers of Globalization"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Declining trade and investment barriers**"},{"t":"p","x":"International trade = exporting goods/services to another country's consumers; FDI = investing resources to build/control business activities abroad. 1920s–30s protectionism gave way, after WWII, to progressive liberalization (GATT → Uruguay Round → WTO), and world trade has since grown faster than world production."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Technological change**"},{"t":"li","x":"Communications: the microprocessor drove explosive, low-cost computing power (Moore's Law) and modern telecommunications/the internet, making it far easier for buyers and sellers worldwide to find each other."},{"t":"li","x":"Transportation: commercial jets, containerization and \"superfreighters\" collapsed shipping costs and times."},{"t":"p","x":"Implications: cheaper transport makes geographically-dispersed production economical (globalization of production); cheap communication + transport create an electronic global marketplace, reduce effective \"cultural distance\", and converge consumer tastes (globalization of markets)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.2 The Changing Demographics of the Global Economy"},{"t":"p","x":"As the world has globalized, the share of world production and trade has spread more evenly — multinationals from developing economies (and even \"mini-multinationals\") are now common, not just Western giants."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1.3 The Globalization Debate: Pros and Cons"},{"t":"p","x":"There is no single right answer on whether globalization is, on balance, good or bad — both sides of the argument contain real truth, and the honest answer depends heavily on which stakeholder's perspective is being considered."},{"t":"p","x":"**Arguments FOR globalization**"},{"t":"li","x":"Allows country specialization → most efficient use of the world's resources (comparative advantage; developed fully in Chapter 6)"},{"t":"li","x":"Has helped lift very large numbers of people out of poverty and hunger, especially in export-oriented developing economies"},{"t":"li","x":"Gives consumers access to a wider range of higher-quality, cheaper goods"},{"t":"li","x":"Faster technology/capital diffusion to developing countries; faster spread of information and cultural exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"**Arguments AGAINST globalization**"},{"t":"li","x":"Environmental harm; the \"race to the bottom\" concern on environmental and labour standards"},{"t":"li","x":"Takes advantage of low-cost labour in developing countries while displacing higher-cost labour in developed ones — the Forbes article ties this to U.S. deindustrialization, a persistent U.S. trade deficit, and estimated job losses (e.g. ~3.2 million U.S. jobs linked to China's most-favoured-nation trading status, per one cited estimate)"},{"t":"li","x":"Erodes national sovereignty; creates unelected supranational \"elites\" (WTO/IMF) with real influence over domestic policy"},{"t":"li","x":"Widens pay inequality — CEOs of global firms see rising pay while many domestic workers face downward wage pressure or job loss"},{"t":"li","x":"Multinationals accused of exploiting tax havens, weak labour/safety standards, and — in some cases — using political influence in ways that raise sovereignty concerns"},{"t":"li","x":"Currency manipulation complaints: some countries are accused of holding their currency artificially low to gain an unfair trade advantage (this becomes a recurring theme in Chapters 10–11)"},{"t":"li","x":"Can spread communicable disease faster via increased travel; creates unrealistic consumption expectations in developing economies; associated with a perceived \"Americanization\" of culture and taste"},{"t":"p","x":"Conclusion (as taught): there is no absolute right or wrong answer — evaluate each claim critically, and recognise both sides have some truth to them."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A company sources its raw cotton from India, has the fabric woven in Vietnam, and has the finished garments sewn in Bangladesh before selling worldwide. This is best described as an example of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the globalization of markets"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the globalization of production"},{"t":"p","x":"C) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) an optimal currency area"},{"t":"p","x":"E) trade diversion"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSourcing goods/services from the locations that offer the best combination of cost and quality of factors of production is, by definition, the globalization of production."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which institution is most accurately described as the \"lender of last resort\" in the global monetary system, typically requiring policy reform in exchange for loans?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) World Bank"},{"t":"p","x":"B) WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"C) IMF"},{"t":"p","x":"D) G20"},{"t":"p","x":"E) United Nations"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nThe IMF's core role is maintaining order in the international monetary system and lending to countries in balance-of-payments difficulty, usually attaching policy conditions."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following is most likely to be cited by critics as a COST of globalization, rather than a benefit?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) wider consumer choice of cheaper goods"},{"t":"p","x":"B) faster diffusion of technology to poorer countries"},{"t":"p","x":"C) downward pressure on wages for import-competing workers in developed countries"},{"t":"p","x":"D) specialization according to comparative advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"E) more people lifted out of poverty in export-oriented developing economies"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nOptions A, B, D and E are standard pro-globalization arguments from the study guide/Forbes article; wage pressure on import-competing domestic labour is a standard anti-globalization argument."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Using the EY gaming-industry report discussed in the Lesson 1 participation exercise, explain (with two supporting facts) why the gaming industry is a strong illustration of BOTH the globalization of markets and the globalization of production.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Globalization of markets: gaming content and platforms (e.g. Fortnite, Roblox) attract a converging, worldwide audience of billions of players across mobile, PC and console — a genuinely global consumer market, reinforced by global metaverse ambitions and cross-border in-game payment/asset systems (NFTs).\n• Globalization of production: gaming companies source talent, technology and partnerships across borders (e.g. cross-industry partnerships cited by 43% of surveyed executives) and rely on globally dispersed supply chains for hardware (chips, consoles) — the same underlying forces (falling trade/tech barriers) that drive globalization generally."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A Hong Kong exporter argues: \"Globalization has been unambiguously good for our business, so it must be good for everyone.\" Using material from this chapter, explain why this reasoning is flawed.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Globalization creates both winners and losers, and their identity depends on whose perspective is taken — a Hong Kong exporter (or a developing-country factory worker) may gain from wider market access and FDI inflows, while an import-competing worker in a developed economy may lose their job to lower-cost overseas production.\n• The course explicitly teaches that there is no single right answer on globalization's net effect; students should evaluate claims from multiple stakeholder perspectives (consumers, workers, governments, the environment) rather than generalising from one party's experience."}]},"2":{"title":"National Differences in Political, Economic and Legal Systems","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"\"Political economy\" refers to a country's political, economic and legal systems together — they are interdependent, and each is shaped by (and shapes) national culture. Never present one country's system as objectively \"better\" than another's; each country has made different choices based on what its government believes benefits its citizens."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.1 Political Systems"},{"t":"p","x":"Assessed along two dimensions: (a) collectivism vs. individualism, and (b) democracy vs. totalitarianism."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Collectivism vs. Individualism"},{"t":"li","x":"Collectivism (traced to Plato): the good of society as a whole is emphasised; individual rights may be sacrificed for social harmony/distribution. Socialism (traced to Karl Marx) advocates state ownership of the means of production; split in the early 20th century into Communism (violent revolution, totalitarian dictatorship — in worldwide retreat since the mid-1990s) and Social democracy (same collectivist goals pursued by democratic means)."},{"t":"li","x":"Individualism (traced to Aristotle, refined by Hume, Smith, Mill): individual economic and political freedom is paramount; government's role is limited (chiefly stopping one individual harming another); associated with democratic systems and free markets."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Democracy vs. Totalitarianism"},{"t":"li","x":"Democracy: government by the people, directly or via elected representatives (most common form = representative democracy). Features: free expression/media, regular elections, universal suffrage, limited terms, fair courts, a non-political bureaucracy/police/military, free access to state information. (Case: the U.S. — three branches, legislative/executive/judicial; federal/state/local government layers.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Totalitarianism: one person, party or group exercises absolute control over all spheres of life; opposition parties banned. Features: political repression, no free/fair elections, censored media, denial of civil liberties. Four forms: Communist totalitarianism, Theocratic totalitarianism (religious principles), Tribal totalitarianism (one tribe monopolises power), Right-wing totalitarianism (economic freedom allowed, political freedom restricted to block a communist path)."},{"t":"li","x":"Pseudo-democracies: hybrids where authoritarian elements capture much of the machinery of a nominally democratic state (example given in lecture: Russia under Vladimir Putin)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.2 Legal Systems"},{"t":"p","x":"A country's legal system regulates business practice, defines how transactions are executed, and sets the rights/obligations of the parties involved."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Common law","Based on tradition, precedent and custom; judiciary has flexibility to interpret law based on prior cases. Found in the UK and most former British colonies (incl. the U.S.). Contracts tend to be long and highly detailed, spelling out every contingency, because courts rely heavily on what was actually agreed."],["Civil law","Based on a detailed, codified set of laws. Found in 80+ countries (Germany, France, Japan, Russia, etc.). Contracts can be shorter/less specific because the code itself, not case-by-case precedent, settles most disputes."],["Theocratic law","Based on religious teachings; Islamic law is the most widely practised example."]]},{"t":"li","x":"The UN Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods (CISG) creates a uniform set of rules for cross-border commercial contracts; adopting it signals that a country treats the Convention's rules as part of its own law."},{"t":"p","x":"**Property rights and corruption**"},{"t":"li","x":"Property rights: the legal rights over the use of a resource and any income derived from it. Threatened by private action (theft, piracy, blackmail) and public action (officials extorting income/assets — e.g. excessive taxation, forced licensing costs, uncompensated expropriation)."},{"t":"li","x":"Higher corruption is associated with lower FDI, lower trade, and lower economic growth."},{"t":"p","x":"**Intellectual property protection**"},{"t":"li","x":"Patents (exclusive rights to manufacture/use/sell an invention), Copyrights (rights of authors/artists/publishers over their work), Trademarks (protected names/designs distinguishing a firm's products)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Product safety & product liability**"},{"t":"li","x":"Product safety laws set required safety standards; product liability holds a firm/its officers responsible for injury, death or damage caused by a product. Liability laws are usually least extensive in less-developed countries, forcing firms to choose between home-country and host-country standards."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2.3 Illustrative Cases from the Lecture"},{"t":"li","x":"US political structure: three branches (legislative/Congress, executive/President, judicial/Federal courts) plus federal, state and local government layers; elections every two years (federal) and four years (presidential)."},{"t":"li","x":"North Korea: a highly centralised communist totalitarian state (Korean Workers' Party) — one of the poorest countries in the world yet sustains one of the largest militaries and an illicit nuclear-weapons programme."},{"t":"li","x":"Political factors affecting firms: e.g. President Trump threatening that Canada's Bombardier cannot sell aircraft in the U.S. unless it manufactures there (2026) — a direct example of political risk shaping market access."},{"t":"li","x":"Legal-environment cases: U.S. restrictions on Huawei telecoms/chip technology (national-security-driven legal barriers); the TikTok ban saga in the U.S. (legal/political risk to a foreign-owned platform); product liability cases (IKEA dresser recalls, Samsung Galaxy Note 7 recall, unsafe toys on Amazon)."},{"t":"li","x":"Corruption cases: Hong Kong's ICAC as a comparatively low-corruption benchmark; Singapore's rare political scandals (Action Party) vs. other political scandal examples discussed (Bill Clinton) as illustrations of how corruption/scandal risk varies and is monitored across political systems."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A country's legal system relies heavily on codified statutes rather than case-by-case judicial precedent, and contracts in that country tend to be relatively short because the code itself resolves most disputes. This country most likely has a:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) common law system"},{"t":"p","x":"B) civil law system"},{"t":"p","x":"C) theocratic law system"},{"t":"p","x":"D) totalitarian system"},{"t":"p","x":"E) collectivist economic system"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the defining feature of civil law systems (e.g. Germany, France, Japan), in contrast to common law's reliance on precedent and highly detailed contracts."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the following is a defining feature of a totalitarian (rather than democratic) political system?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) universal adult suffrage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a free and independent media"},{"t":"p","x":"C) opposition political parties are prohibited"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a non-political state bureaucracy"},{"t":"p","x":"E) regular, fair elections"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nBanning opposition parties and concentrating absolute power in one person/party/group is the core defining feature of totalitarianism."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A foreign investor finds that local officials in a host country routinely demand unofficial payments before approving standard business licences. This is best described as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a common law contract dispute"},{"t":"p","x":"B) public-action corruption threatening property rights"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a tariff-rate quota"},{"t":"p","x":"D) an optimal currency area problem"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nPublic officials extorting payment for services they should provide as of right is the textbook definition of public-action threats to property rights."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Explain why a common law contract for an international sale of goods is typically much longer and more detailed than an equivalent civil law contract, and why this matters for a manager negotiating that contract.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• In common law systems, courts rule largely on precedent and the specific wording the parties agreed to, so anything not explicitly written into the contract may not be enforceable in the way a party expects; the contract itself must anticipate and spell out every likely contingency.\n• In civil law systems, an extensive statutory code already governs most contractual situations by default, so the contract can be shorter because the code — not the document — fills the gaps.\n• Managerial implication: a manager used to civil law norms who signs a \"thin\" contract in a common law jurisdiction risks being unprotected on issues the contract is silent on; conversely, over-specifying a civil law contract wastes negotiation time on points already settled by the code."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A multinational is deciding whether to expand into two countries: Country X has weak property-rights protection and moderate corruption but very low costs and fast growth; Country Y has strong property-rights protection and low corruption but higher costs and slower growth. Using the framework from this chapter, outline the trade-off the firm faces.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Country Y is the classically \"attractive\" business environment (well-established legal system, protected property rights) but, precisely because it is attractive, is likely to already have strong competition and lower growth potential (a mature market).\n• Country X offers greater growth/first-mover upside precisely because it is less developed and less protected, but the firm faces materially higher political, legal and economic risk (weaker enforcement of contracts/IP, corruption exposure, possible expropriation).\n• The correct answer is not one absolute choice: the firm must weigh the opportunity (growth, lower costs) against the specific risks in Country X, and can partially manage those risks (e.g. joint ventures, insurance, staged investment) rather than avoiding the market outright."}]},"3":{"title":"National Differences in Economic Development","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"A country's political economy choices (Chapter 2) strongly shape its level of economic development and growth — and thus its attractiveness as a trading partner or FDI location."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.1 Measuring Economic Development"},{"t":"table","rows":[["GDP (Gross Domestic Product)","Total value of goods/services produced within a country in a year, usually expressed per capita in US$. Reflects a country's productivity and (roughly) its citizens' living standards and purchasing power. High-productivity societies (strong access to resources, technology, education, infrastructure) produce higher-value goods/services (chips, pharma, banking) and so have higher GDP per capita."],["GNI (Gross National Income)","The measure the textbook favours: total income earned by a country's residents/government, including income earned abroad (distinct from GDP, which measures output produced within the country's borders)."],["PPP adjustment","Corrects for cross-country differences in local prices. Example: a Hong Kong pizza-maker selling 5,000 pizzas at US$15 each contributes US$75,000 to GDP; a Filipino pizza-maker selling the same 5,000 pizzas at US$5 each (due to lower local prices/incomes) contributes only US$25,000 — despite identical real output. Table 3.1 in the textbook shows the U.S. is ~6× richer than China by nominal GDP but under 4× richer once adjusted for PPP."]]},{"t":"li","x":"Growth rate matters more than the static level: a high-GDP-growth country is far more attractive to international business (rising sales/profit opportunity) than a high-but-flat-GDP country."},{"t":"li","x":"Limitations of GDP: does not capture pollution, health, life expectancy or leisure time. Economist Amartya Sen argued development should be judged by people's capabilities and opportunities, not just material output; the UN's Human Development Index (HDI) blends economic measures with broader \"lifestyle\" indicators."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.2 Government Revenue Sources (Political Economy of Taxation)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Direct taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"Personal income tax (progressive: higher earners pay a higher rate); Corporate income tax (on net profits); Capital gains tax (on profit from asset sales); Property tax; Inheritance/estate/gift taxes."},{"t":"p","x":"**Indirect taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"VAT/GST (charged at each stage of the supply chain); Sales tax (single point-of-sale tax, e.g. Hong Kong's hotel-stay tax); Excise tax (alcohol, tobacco, fuel — raises revenue and discourages consumption); Customs/import duties (also a trade-policy tool, covered in Chapter 7)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Environmental/resource taxes**"},{"t":"li","x":"Carbon and environmental taxes on activities/emissions that damage the environment."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.3 What Drives Economic Growth? — The Three I's"},{"t":"li","x":"Incentives: people only work harder, take risks and innovate if they expect to keep the rewards. Free-market, individualist systems (where individuals retain their own returns) are more conducive to innovation and growth than command/collectivist systems where gains flow to the state."},{"t":"li","x":"Institutions: a strong, independent legal system with well-enforced property rights lets innovators protect and profit from their investment (without it being copied or expropriated, directly or via corruption)."},{"t":"li","x":"Innovation: growth ultimately requires people willing to try new processes/products and take on risk and investment — incentives and institutions are what make that risk-taking rational."},{"t":"p","x":"Consensus view: economic growth is highest in market-based economies with strong property rights; \"states in transition\" (e.g. China privatising state-owned enterprises and strengthening its legal system) illustrate countries moving toward this model."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.4 Democracy and Growth: An Unsettled Relationship"},{"t":"p","x":"Observing that the world's richest countries (Japan, the U.S., much of Europe) are democracies does not prove democracy causes growth — it may instead be that development creates pressure for democratization. Some of the fastest-growing economies in modern history (China today; historically Taiwan and South Korea) grew fastest under authoritarian rule. The link between democracy and growth remains genuinely contested, not settled."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.5 Country Attractiveness"},{"t":"p","x":"Countries that open markets and protect property rights are attractive to international business through high growth; but many already-developed economies (with these features already in place) have comparatively low growth, while developing \"catch-up\" markets offer higher growth potential — at the cost of higher political, economic and legal risk. Businesses should not automatically avoid less-developed markets, but must price in and manage that additional risk."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3.6 Illustrative Cases"},{"t":"li","x":"Canada's \"per-capita recession\" (2024): GDP can be technically flat/positive while GDP per capita falls if population is growing faster than output — a distinct concept from a technical recession (two consecutive quarters of negative GDP growth)."},{"t":"li","x":"Australia's Great Barrier Reef: government-managed as a national economic + environmental asset — supports 60,000+ tourism-dependent jobs and 2+ million visitors/year, illustrating how political-economy choices (regulation + investment) shape a natural resource's economic contribution."},{"t":"li","x":"U.S. corn agriculture: high domestic GDP contribution and federal subsidies (insurance, income support) support large-scale corn production, processing and export — a live example of government policy shaping a nation's comparative position in a specific product category."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Country A and Country B have identical nominal GDP per capita, but the cost of a typical basket of goods is 40% lower in Country B. Once adjusted for purchasing power parity, Country B's citizens:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) have a lower real standard of living than Country A's"},{"t":"p","x":"B) have a higher real standard of living than Country A's"},{"t":"p","x":"C) have exactly the same real standard of living"},{"t":"p","x":"D) cannot be compared without knowing the exchange rate regime"},{"t":"p","x":"E) have a lower nominal GDP than Country A"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe same nominal income buys more in a lower-price economy, so Country B's PPP-adjusted (real) living standard is higher, even though nominal GDP per capita is identical."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the \"three I's\" of economic growth is most directly undermined when a country has a weak, corrupt judiciary that cannot reliably enforce patents?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Incentives only"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Institutions"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Innovation only, unrelated to institutions"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Indulgence (Hofstede dimension)"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Interest rates"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA weak/corrupt judiciary that cannot enforce property rights (like patents) is a direct institutional failure, which in turn undermines the incentive to innovate — but the textbook categorises the enforcement mechanism itself under \"institutions\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which statement best reflects the course's position on democracy and economic growth?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Democracy is a proven precondition for economic growth"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Authoritarian government always produces faster growth than democracy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) The direction of causality between democracy and growth is unsettled and contested"},{"t":"p","x":"D) GDP growth and democratization are unrelated phenomena with no historical overlap"},{"t":"p","x":"E) The Human Development Index proves democracy causes growth"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nThe study guide explicitly frames this relationship as \"more difficult, and more contentious\" — citing counterexamples on both sides (democracies that are rich; fast-growing authoritarian states) rather than asserting one direction of causality."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A government advisor claims: \"GDP per capita is all we need to compare living standards between countries.\" Give two reasons, drawn from this chapter, why this claim is incomplete.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• GDP does not adjust for differences in local price levels, so two countries with the same nominal GDP per capita can have very different real purchasing power (this is exactly what the PPP adjustment corrects for).\n• GDP is a purely material measure and ignores quality-of-life factors such as pollution, health/lifespan, and leisure time — which is why Amartya Sen argued for judging development by capabilities/opportunities, and why the UN created the broader Human Development Index."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain, using the \"three I's\" framework, why a resource-rich but institutionally weak developing country might still grow more slowly than a resource-poor country with strong institutions.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Resources alone do not generate sustained growth without the incentives to use them productively and the institutions to protect the returns from doing so.\n• In an institutionally weak country, an entrepreneur who innovates or invests risks having the gains expropriated (via corruption, weak property-rights enforcement, or an unpredictable legal system) — this removes the incentive to take the risk of innovating in the first place.\n• A resource-poor but institutionally strong country instead channels its more limited resources through reliable incentives and enforceable property rights, allowing accumulated innovation and investment to compound over time — which is why the course frames strong institutions/property rights as close to a precondition for sustained growth."}]},"4":{"title":"Differences in Culture","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Culture: \"a system of values and norms shared among a group of people and, when taken together, constitute a design for living\" (Hill). Society: a group of people sharing a common set of values and norms."},{"t":"li","x":"Values provide the underlying beliefs that give context to a society's norms (e.g. a belief in individual free expression, or in filial duty to family)."},{"t":"li","x":"Norms are the social rules governing behaviour, split into folkways (etiquette/manners) and mores (core rules central to how society functions)."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture is learned, not genetic — differences arise from environment, not biology — and it evolves over time (it is not static)."},{"t":"li","x":"Cross-cultural literacy: the ability to understand and respect other cultures without slipping into cultural stereotyping (assuming everyone from a culture behaves identically). Multinationals can themselves be agents of cultural change."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.1 Determinants of Culture"},{"t":"p","x":"Culture is shaped by — and in turn shapes — a society's prevailing political/economic philosophy, social structure, religion, language and education (a bi-directional relationship). Nation-states do not always map 1:1 onto a single culture (e.g. Canada has English- and French-official-language provinces); societies also contain sub-cultures (e.g. within a religion: Catholic vs. Protestant vs. Orthodox norms on clergy marriage or divorce)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.2 Cultural Distance"},{"t":"p","x":"Cultural distance = the difference between the home and host country's cultures, an important consideration for market-entry strategy. Firms typically enter culturally/geographically closer markets first, and expand to more distant markets as they gain international experience."},{"t":"li","x":"Higher cultural distance → greater difficulty and \"liability of foreignness\", due to weaker knowledge of how the host country works and greater collaboration barriers."},{"t":"li","x":"Effect on entry mode is debated: some studies associate high cultural distance with joint ventures (collaborative, shared-control entry); others find it associated with wholly-owned subsidiaries (full-control entry) — no consensus exists."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.3 Hofstede's Cultural Dimensions Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Developed by Geert Hofstede from a large 1960s–70s survey of IBM employees (100,000+ respondents across 40–50+ countries, refined with Bond and Minkov); widely used in international business, diplomacy and cross-cultural management, despite methodological criticisms (below). Originally 4 dimensions, later extended to 6."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"1. Power Distance (PDI)"},{"t":"p","x":"The extent to which less-powerful members of a society accept and expect unequal power distribution."},{"t":"li","x":"High PDI: hierarchy accepted without need for justification; superiors/subordinates not seen as equals; higher-ups decide without soliciting employee input; centralised organisations, large status gaps (examples given: many Asian societies; Malaysia scores 100)."},{"t":"li","x":"Low PDI: flatter organisations; managers consult and expect to be asked for input; more consultative/egalitarian relations (many Western societies)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"2. Individualism vs. Collectivism (IDV)"},{"t":"li","x":"Individualist: self-image = \"I\"; stresses personal achievement and rights; communication tends to be direct (example: an individual celebrity accepting a solo award)."},{"t":"li","x":"Collectivist: self-image = \"We\"; loyalty/harmony with the in-group prioritised over individual needs; communication tends to be more indirect, avoiding public loss of face (example: a group act's member crediting the team on receiving an award). Asian societies tend toward collectivism; Western societies toward individualism."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3. Uncertainty Avoidance (UAI)"},{"t":"li","x":"High UAI: low tolerance for ambiguity/risk; societies and organisations rely on strict rules/regulations to minimise the unknown; can be more emotionally expressive (example: France/Greece cited as higher-UAI; Greece scores 100)."},{"t":"li","x":"Low UAI: comfortable with ambiguity and change; fewer formal rules; more openness/flexibility. Hong Kong and Singapore are both cited as very low-UAI — reflected in a flexible workforce and nimble, quickly-adapting organisations (Singapore scores 8)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"4. Masculinity vs. Femininity (MAS)"},{"t":"li","x":"Masculine societies: value assertiveness, competition, toughness, achievement — expected of both men and women in the workplace (example: Japan, the highest-scoring country at 95; no reigning queens allowed under Japan's monarchy rules is cited as a cultural illustration)."},{"t":"li","x":"Feminine societies: value cooperation, nurturing, quality of life and modesty (example: Sweden, the lowest-scoring country at 5). A society's parental-leave generosity is cited as one femininity indicator (Hong Kong fathers get only 5 days of paternity leave, versus more generous norms in higher-femininity countries)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"5. Long-Term vs. Short-Term Orientation (LTO)"},{"t":"li","x":"Long-term orientation: pragmatism, thrift, perseverance, patience for results to materialise — a future focus that delays short-term gratification."},{"t":"li","x":"Short-term orientation: focus on the near future, quicker results, and stronger emphasis on tradition/principles — can result in comparatively higher present-day spending and lower saving (example given: contrasting pension/savings behaviour in Hong Kong vs. the US/UK/Canada/Singapore)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"6. Indulgence vs. Restraint (IVR)"},{"t":"p","x":"Added later (with Minkov); not covered in the textbook, but taught in this course."},{"t":"li","x":"Indulgent societies: allow relatively free gratification of desires and leisure — more optimistic, more spending on leisure/luxuries."},{"t":"li","x":"Restrained societies: suppress gratification via social norms — more pessimistic, more thrift and focus on practical needs (example given: cannabis is legal in Canada but illegal in Hong Kong)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Hofstede's Country Scores — Selected Countries (Textbook Table 4.1)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Country","Power Distance","Uncertainty Avoidance","Individualism","Masculinity","Long-Term Orientation"],["Australia","36","51","90","61","31"],["Brazil","69","76","38","49","65"],["Canada","39","48","80","52","23"],["Germany (F.R.)","35","65","67","66","31"],["United Kingdom","35","35","89","66","25"],["India","77","40","48","56","61"],["Japan","54","92","46","95","80"],["Netherlands","38","53","80","14","44"],["New Zealand","22","49","79","58","30"],["Pakistan","55","70","14","50","0"],["Philippines","94","44","32","64","19"],["Singapore","74","8","20","48","48"],["Sweden","31","29","71","5","33"],["Thailand","64","64","20","34","56"],["United States","40","46","91","62","29"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: This table reproduces the textbook's classic scores as presented in class; Hofstede's published scores have been periodically updated since, so verify against the live Hofstede Insights country-comparison tool for a current group project."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Criticisms of Hofstede's Model"},{"t":"li","x":"Assumes a clean one-to-one mapping between nation and culture, though many nations are genuinely multicultural"},{"t":"li","x":"Original survey respondents all worked in a single industry, within a single company (IBM) — limiting generalisability"},{"t":"li","x":"Certain social classes were excluded from the research; respondents were largely male, white-collar, and highly educated"},{"t":"li","x":"Takes a static view of culture and ignores individual personality/life experience, and how culture changes over time"},{"t":"p","x":"Despite these limits, the model remains one of the most widely used and empirically-supported tools for comparing national cultures, and is respected in practice by international managers."},{"t":"li","x":"Other frameworks (self-study, not detailed in class): GLOBE (9 dimensions incl. power distance, uncertainty avoidance, humane/institutional/in-group collectivism, assertiveness, gender egalitarianism, future and performance orientation); the World Values Survey (WVS)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.4 Cultural Relativism vs. Cultural Universalism"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cultural relativism","The belief that ethics are culturally determined and firms should adopt the ethics of the culture they operate in — \"when in Rome, do as the Romans do.\" (The textbook presents this view; it does not present a counter-argument for universalism.)"],["Cultural universalism","The belief that the same ethical standards should apply everywhere a firm does business, regardless of local culture — considered by the instructor to be an idealistic standard that is rare to find genuinely practised by real firms."]]},{"t":"p","x":"In practice, most multinationals today adopt formal \"Inclusion and Cultural Diversity\" policies that selectively adapt to local norms rather than applying either pure extreme — e.g. HSBC allows nap time and adjusts service hours in Vietnam (respecting local lunch/nap norms) while HSBC Hong Kong keeps branches open through lunch, reflecting different local expectations."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.5 Cultural Change"},{"t":"p","x":"Culture evolves — often slowly, and sometimes with social turmoil — and change tends to accelerate as countries grow economically richer (a shift from \"traditional\" to \"secular-rational\" values). Globalization is associated with a convergence hypothesis (cultures becoming more similar), but there is a counter-trend of some societies deliberately re-emphasising their cultural uniqueness."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4.6 Applying the Framework — Group Project Template"},{"t":"p","x":"For the group project, apply Hofstede's dimensions systematically: score Hong Kong and each of your two candidate countries on all six dimensions, identify the largest gaps, and translate each meaningful gap into a concrete managerial implication (e.g. a high power-distance gap implies a Hong Kong manager may need to adapt to a more directive style if managing a low-PDI workforce, or vice versa). Support the analysis with current, reliable data rather than definitions alone — the instructor stresses that \"definitions cannot get any marks\"."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 4"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A manager notices that in Country X, employees rarely question a supervisor's decision and expect instructions rather than consultation, while pay gaps and status differences between ranks are large and unremarkable. Country X is most likely:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) high in power distance"},{"t":"p","x":"B) high in femininity"},{"t":"p","x":"C) low in uncertainty avoidance"},{"t":"p","x":"D) high in indulgence"},{"t":"p","x":"E) low in long-term orientation"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nAcceptance of unequal, hierarchical power and unquestioning deference to superiors is the defining behaviour of a high power-distance culture."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. A firm launching a marketing campaign in a strongly collectivist culture should generally:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) emphasise individual achievement and personal rewards"},{"t":"p","x":"B) emphasise group belonging, harmony and benefits to the community"},{"t":"p","x":"C) use direct, blunt criticism of competitor products"},{"t":"p","x":"D) assume the same appeal that works in an individualist market will work unchanged"},{"t":"p","x":"E) avoid any reference to family or community"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nCollectivist cultures place a person's self-image and motivation in the context of the group (\"We\"), so campaigns emphasising community/group benefit and harmony tend to resonate better than individual-achievement appeals."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following is a documented methodological criticism of Hofstede's original cultural dimensions research?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) It surveyed a representative cross-section of every social class worldwide"},{"t":"p","x":"B) It was based on employees of a single company (IBM) in one industry"},{"t":"p","x":"C) It has never been replicated or tested since 1980"},{"t":"p","x":"D) It excludes any dimension related to individualism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) It was conducted after the year 2000, making it too recent to be tested"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA frequently-cited limitation is that Hofstede's respondents were all IBM employees (one company, one industry, largely white-collar and male) — limiting how representative the national scores are of the whole society."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Your group project assigns you Hong Kong (home) and Sweden (a candidate manufacturing location). Sweden scores very low on Masculinity (5) versus a higher Hong Kong score. Explain one concrete managerial adjustment a Hong Kong manager might need to make when managing a Swedish workforce, and justify it using the Masculinity/Femininity dimension.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Sweden's very low Masculinity score signals a feminine culture that values cooperation, consensus, quality of life and work-life balance over overt competitiveness and status display.\n• A Hong Kong manager used to a more assertive, target-driven, hierarchical management style may need to shift toward a more participative, consensus-based decision-making style, and place real weight on work-life balance policies (e.g. flexible hours) rather than relying on competitive incentives or long-hours culture, which would likely be poorly received and could hurt morale/retention in a low-Masculinity workforce."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Distinguish cultural relativism from cultural universalism and explain, using the HSBC Vietnam/Hong Kong nap-time example, which approach HSBC is actually following in practice.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Cultural relativism holds that ethics/practice should adapt to fit local culture (\"when in Rome...\"); cultural universalism holds that the same standard should apply everywhere regardless of local culture.\n• HSBC allowing nap time and adjusted service hours in its Vietnam branches, while keeping Hong Kong branches open through lunch with no nap policy, shows HSBC adapting its internal practices to local cultural norms in each market — this is an example of (a moderate, business-practical form of) cultural relativism, not universalism, even though HSBC likely maintains universal standards on other matters (e.g. core ethics/anti-bribery policy) across all its branches."}]},"5":{"title":"Ethics, Corporate Social Responsibility, Sustainability & Supply Chain","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.1 Why Business Ethics Matters More Today"},{"t":"li","x":"The internet: unethical behaviour anywhere is quickly exposed and spread worldwide; protest groups have a far more powerful voice than before"},{"t":"li","x":"High-profile scandals: Enron and WorldCom wiped out shareholder value and heightened scrutiny of corporate conduct"},{"t":"li","x":"Tougher legislation: e.g. the U.S. Sarbanes-Oxley Act makes individual company officers personally responsible for ethical misdeeds within their organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"Ethics: accepted principles of right/wrong governing the conduct of a person, profession, or organisation. Business ethics: the same, applied to business conduct. Ethical strategy: a course of action that does not violate these accepted principles. Ethics is a culturally-bound concept (echoing Chapter 4) — what is \"accepted\" varies by country, which is precisely what makes ethical decision-making complex for a multinational."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.2 Four Common Ethical Issue Areas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Employment practices","Wages, benefits, working conditions, hiring/discrimination, health & safety. Example: Hong Kong firms relocating factories to mainland China accused of exploiting weaker local labour protections and inadequate dormitory/safety conditions for staff."],["Human rights","Should a multinational continue operating where basic human rights (association, speech, assembly, movement) are denied? Precedent: the effective withdrawal of multinationals from apartheid-era South Africa is often cited as contributing to that system's collapse. Difficulty: \"human rights\" itself is a partly culturally-bound term."],["Environmental pollution","The \"tragedy of the commons\": a shared resource owned by no one gets over-used/degraded because no individual user bears its full cost. Should an MNC pollute in a developing country if doing so does not break local law? In practice, MNCs sometimes hold HIGHER environmental standards than local competitors, so the picture is not purely one-sided."],["Corruption","Public-level (bribes for licences/market access) vs. private-level (side payments to secure a business contract). Debate: some argue bribery can be welfare-enhancing where it lets business happen despite broken institutions; most economies and firms now reject this — the OECD Anti-Bribery Convention and many MNCs' zero-tolerance policies reflect the view that corruption reduces investment returns and growth."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.3 Making Ethical Decisions Internationally — Seven Managerial Actions"},{"t":"li","x":"1. Hiring & promotion: favour candidates with a well-grounded sense of personal ethics; consider ethical conduct, not just performance, in promotion decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"2. Organisational culture & leadership: articulate and repeatedly reinforce ethical values (codes of conduct); leaders model the behaviour themselves"},{"t":"li","x":"3. Decision-making processes: explicitly build ethical criteria into decisions (e.g. considering human rights when choosing where to invest, or supplier sustainability when choosing who to buy from). A practical three-question test for whether a decision is ethically acceptable: (i) does it fall within the organisation's accepted values/code of ethics? (ii) would I be comfortable if it were publicised (news, social media)? (iii) would people I respect — family, trusted colleagues — approve?"},{"t":"li","x":"4. Ethics officers: an internal \"ombudsperson\" role to monitor compliance with ethical standards"},{"t":"li","x":"5. Moral courage: the willingness to walk away from a profitable-but-unethical decision, and to support employees who speak up about wrongdoing"},{"t":"li","x":"6. Make CSR a cornerstone of enterprise policy"},{"t":"li","x":"7. Pursue sustainable strategies"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.4 Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)"},{"t":"p","x":"Dahlsrud (2008): a CSR is the concept that a firm is accountable for its impact on all relevant stakeholders — a continuing commitment to behave responsibly and contribute to economic development while improving the quality of life of the workforce, their families, and the local society and environment."},{"t":"p","x":"Beal (2013) adds practical characteristics: firms try to minimise the risk of negative CSR-related publicity; CSR addresses both internal stakeholders (employees, investors) and external stakeholders (suppliers, retailers, wholesalers, partners, consumers); it should be a long-term, ongoing commitment (not a one-off gesture), often delivered through dedicated foundations/funding; it builds long-term relationships and customer loyalty; and it is typically kept relatively low-profile rather than aggressively self-promoted."},{"t":"p","x":"\"Noblesse oblige\": the idea that those with power/influence have an obligation to use their position to help others — the philosophical underpinning often cited for CSR."},{"t":"p","x":"**Case studies**"},{"t":"li","x":"Disney Conservation Fund and the Disney x Avatar \"Keep Our Oceans Amazing\" campaign"},{"t":"li","x":"Disney's The Lion King \"Protect the Pride\" campaign: movie-goer donations feed the Lion Recovery Fund; funds conservation-expert training in Africa and grants, with employee/expert involvement"},{"t":"li","x":"Rolex.org (the CSR/environmental arm) is deliberately kept distinct from Rolex's commercial marketing website — illustrating that a genuine CSR effort should not be conflated with product marketing"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.5 Sustainability"},{"t":"p","x":"UN definition (1983): sustainability means meeting our own needs without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. A sustainable strategy helps an MNC make profit without harming the environment, while acting responsibly toward all its stakeholders (fairly compensating suppliers so they can keep providing quality inputs; treating employees fairly so the firm can keep attracting talent)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.6 Global Supply Chain Management"},{"t":"p","x":"A global supply chain runs from production/manufacturing/packaging through shipping/delivery to the end consumer."},{"t":"p","x":"**Packaging categories**"},{"t":"li","x":"Primary packaging (holds the product itself); Secondary packaging (bundles several primary packages); Transit packaging (used to physically move primary + secondary packages, e.g. courier boxes) — each layer raises environmental/waste questions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sourcing Terminology"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Meaning"],["Outsourcing","Buying products/services from an outside supplier (domestic or global)"],["Insourcing","Bringing a previously-outsourced activity back in-house"],["Offshoring","Buying from the firm's own operations located abroad"],["Offshore outsourcing","Buying from a third-party supplier located in a different country from where the product is made/used"],["Nearshoring","Shifting sourcing to a nearby country (often a bordering one)"],["Co-sourcing","Using a mix of in-house staff and an external supplier together"]]},{"t":"li","x":"Five levels of global purchasing maturity: (I) domestic purchasing only → (II) international purchasing as needed → (III) international purchasing as part of overall supply-chain strategy → (IV) global purchasing integrated across the firm's worldwide locations → (V) global purchasing integrated across worldwide locations AND functional groups."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Pillars of Managing a Global Supply Chain"},{"t":"li","x":"Just-in-time (JIT) inventory: materials arrive just as needed, cutting warehousing/holding costs and helping firms spot defects early — but removes the buffer stock that would otherwise absorb supply disruptions"},{"t":"li","x":"Information technology: systems (EDI, and increasingly blockchain) that optimise production scheduling and traceability"},{"t":"li","x":"Coordination: shared decision-making across the chain targeting responsiveness, variance reduction, inventory reduction, shipment consolidation, quality and life-cycle support"},{"t":"li","x":"Inter-organisational relationships: trust and commitment between supply-chain partners (including the choice between self-owned vs. outsourced transport/logistics)"},{"t":"p","x":"The lecture used Amazon's supply chain as a recurring illustrative (not separately examinable) case bundle spanning unsold-inventory destruction, warehouse-worker injury rates, sourcing from unsafe overseas factories, and privacy/DEI controversies — with the explicit lesson that a company's own website/marketing gives one-sided, positive information, and that independent journalism (WSJ, Reuters, CNN, BBC, etc.) should be used to critically assess a firm's real ethical performance for group-work analysis."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5.7 Official In-Class Ethical Case Discussion (Suggested Answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: The original case prompt text was not present in the extracted files — only the instructor's \"suggested solution\" was provided. The scenario below is reconstructed from the structure of that answer; please verify the exact wording of the original case against the Moodle file if you need it verbatim."},{"t":"p","x":"Reconstructed scenario: an accountant/finance team member is pressured by a superior to change the useful-life assumption used to calculate depreciation on long-term assets — without any genuine change in facts, events or circumstances — purely to make reported earnings look better."},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) Is this ethical?**"},{"t":"li","x":"No. Depreciation periods must be based on an asset's actual expected useful life; changing the assumption without a real underlying reason understates depreciation expense and overstates net income, producing misleading financial statements."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Who is harmed / which stakeholders are affected?**"},{"t":"li","x":"Investors and potential investors, who may hold or buy shares based on artificially inflated (misleading) performance; also banks, creditors, the government, the accounting profession generally, individual accountants, competitors, and the finance director/management themselves (reputational and legal exposure)."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Which professional/ethical values are engaged?**"},{"t":"li","x":"Integrity, Objectivity, Due care, and Professional behaviour."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Recommended course of action**"},{"t":"li","x":"(i) Refuse the request; explain the reasoning and ask the person requesting it to reconsider"},{"t":"li","x":"(ii) If ignored, escalate the matter to an ethics officer or a higher authority (e.g. the board)"},{"t":"li","x":"(iii) If still ignored, resign"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 5"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A factory dumps untreated waste into a river that is legally owned by no one and used by many local fishing communities. This scenario is the classic illustration of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the tragedy of the commons"},{"t":"p","x":"C) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Dunning's eclectic theory"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA shared resource (the river) owned by no one being over-used/degraded by individual users, with no one bearing the full cost, is precisely the tragedy of the commons."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which of the following is an example of Just-in-Time (JIT) inventory management's main documented WEAKNESS?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) higher warehousing costs"},{"t":"p","x":"B) reduced ability to catch defective parts"},{"t":"p","x":"C) lack of a buffer stock, making the firm vulnerable to supply disruptions"},{"t":"p","x":"D) inability to use information technology"},{"t":"p","x":"E) requiring more employees on the factory floor"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nJIT deliberately minimises held inventory; its trade-off is that firms lack a buffer to absorb unexpected disruptions in the supply chain."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. According to Beal's (2013) characterisation of CSR discussed in class, which of the following best fits genuine CSR practice?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a one-off donation heavily promoted in a single ad campaign"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a long-term, relatively low-profile commitment addressing both internal and external stakeholders"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a policy that focuses exclusively on shareholders"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a marketing campaign indistinguishable from the firm's main commercial website"},{"t":"p","x":"E) a policy reviewed once and never updated"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nBeal's framework stresses long-term (not one-off) commitment, attention to both internal and external stakeholders, and typically a lower-profile approach than aggressive self-promotion — as illustrated by Rolex keeping its CSR site distinct from its commercial site."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A multinational discovers a supplier factory abroad has working conditions that are legal locally but would be illegal in the firm's home country. Applying the ethics framework from this chapter, outline the key considerations the firm's management should weigh before deciding what to do.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Recognise that \"employment practices\" is one of the four core ethical issue areas — legality in the host country does not automatically make a practice ethically acceptable by the firm's own (or its home country's) standards.\n• Apply the three-question ethical decision test: would this fall within the firm's own code of conduct? Would management be comfortable if the conditions were reported in the media? Would people they respect approve?\n• Consider stakeholder impact broadly (workers, local community, the firm's reputation, consumers) and whether continuing to source there without changes could expose the firm to reputational and, potentially, legal risk if reported by independent media.\n• A firm with genuine moral courage and a CSR/sustainability commitment would use its leverage as a customer to require improved conditions (an ethical decision-making process, per action 3 above) rather than simply accepting the host country's minimum legal standard."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain the difference between 'outsourcing' and 'offshoring', and give an example of a firm decision that would be BOTH offshore AND outsourced at the same time.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Outsourcing means buying a product/service from an external supplier rather than producing it in-house — the supplier can be domestic or foreign.\n• Offshoring means the firm's OWN operations are relocated/established abroad — the activity stays inside the firm, just in another country.\n• A decision is 'offshore outsourcing' when a firm buys from a third-party supplier located in a different country than where the product is made or used — e.g. a US clothing brand contracting an independent Bangladeshi factory (not its own subsidiary) to manufacture garments for sale in the US."}]},"6":{"title":"International Trade Theory","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"This is described by the instructor as probably the single most important chapter of the course, because it lays the theoretical foundation (comparative advantage) for everything that follows on trade policy, FDI and regional integration. Classical theories explain WHY trade happens and why it is beneficial; modern theories explain WHAT specific goods a given country ends up trading."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.1 Classical Trade Theories — \"Trade Is Good\""},{"t":"p","x":"**Mercantilism (pre-1800 orthodoxy)**"},{"t":"p","x":"The belief that exports are good and imports are bad — a country should accumulate wealth by pushing exports while restricting imports. Many people still intuitively think this way. Historical illustration: the Opium Wars, fought partly over Britain's attempt to force China to accept British imports, led to Britain's acquisition of Hong Kong (1842/1847)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Absolute Advantage (Adam Smith, The Wealth of Nations, 1776)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A country should specialise in producing the goods for which it is more efficient than any other country (has an absolute advantage), and trade for everything else. Under free trade, both trading partners gain — imports are not inherently \"bad\"."},{"t":"p","x":"**Comparative Advantage (David Ricardo)**"},{"t":"p","x":"Ricardo's key extension: even a country with NO absolute advantage in anything still gains from specialising in the good(s) where it is relatively least inefficient (i.e., has the lowest opportunity cost) and trading for the rest. This is the intellectual foundation of modern international business: ALL countries can gain from trade, not only the most efficient ones. There is strong empirical evidence linking trade openness to growth (e.g. China's growth trajectory before vs. after opening to trade)."},{"t":"li","x":"Counter-view (Paul Samuelson): for a rich country trading with a poor country, the domestic job losses caused by that trade might outweigh the benefit of the lower prices trade delivers to consumers — a genuine qualification to the pure \"trade is always good\" framing."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.2 Modern Trade Theories — Explaining WHAT Countries Trade"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor endowments (Heckscher–Ohlin)","Countries are relatively better-endowed with different factors of production (labour, land, capital); different goods use these factors in different intensities (clothing = labour-intensive, timber = land-intensive, electronics = capital-intensive). A country should export goods intensive in the factor it holds relatively more of."],["The Leontief paradox","Economist Wassily Leontief tested Heckscher–Ohlin against real U.S. trade data and found the capital-abundant U.S. actually exported (and imported) a MIX of capital- and labour-intensive goods, not the pure pattern the theory predicted — showing factor endowments alone do not fully explain trade patterns."],["International Product Life-Cycle (Raymond Vernon, 1960s)","New products are invented and first produced in developed countries and exported worldwide; as the product matures/standardises, production shifts to developing countries, which then export back to the developed world. Now considered largely outdated: many innovations originate in developing economies today, and production shifts to lower-cost locations almost immediately."],["New Trade Theory (Paul Krugman)","In industries that can only support a small number of global competitors (large economies of scale/experience), whichever country produces first — often simply a matter of historical luck — can lock in lasting dominance, because it is hard for rivals to catch up. Implies a possible role for government support (subsidies, import protection) of strategic emerging industries — \"strategic trade policy\" — though this logic applies only to a limited set of industries."],["Porter's Diamond (Michael Porter, 1990)","A country becomes internationally competitive in an industry when it has a supporting \"diamond\" of four factors: (1) Factor conditions — basic AND advanced factors (infrastructure, education); (2) Demand conditions — sophisticated, demanding domestic buyers that push firms to innovate; (3) Related & supporting industries — competitive local suppliers/complementary industries; (4) Firm strategy, structure & rivalry — intense domestic competition drives innovation. Plus two external forces: government policy and chance."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6.3 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"li","x":"New Trade Theory suggests a firm should invest aggressively to build first-mover dominance in industries with strong scale/experience economies."},{"t":"li","x":"Porter's Diamond is a practical tool for deciding WHERE to locate a new plant or business — the same tool used later in the course's Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix exercise."},{"t":"li","x":"The theory of comparative advantage underlies essentially all of the next three chapters (government trade policy, FDI, and regional economic integration) — \"trade is good\" is the course's foundational premise."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 6"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Country A can produce both wine and cloth more efficiently than Country B in absolute terms, but is relatively MORE efficient at wine than cloth. According to Ricardo's theory of comparative advantage, Country A should:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) refuse to trade with Country B at all, since it has an absolute advantage in everything"},{"t":"p","x":"B) specialise in cloth, its area of relative disadvantage"},{"t":"p","x":"C) specialise in wine (its comparative advantage) and trade for cloth"},{"t":"p","x":"D) produce equal amounts of both goods for self-sufficiency"},{"t":"p","x":"E) impose a tariff on Country B's cloth exports"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nEven though Country A is absolutely more efficient at both goods, comparative advantage says both countries gain if each specialises in the good where it has the greatest RELATIVE efficiency (lowest opportunity cost) and trades for the rest."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. A researcher tests the Heckscher-Ohlin theory on a capital-abundant country's actual trade data and finds it exports a mix of both capital- and labour-intensive goods, contrary to the theory's prediction. This finding is known as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"p","x":"B) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the optimal currency area problem"},{"t":"p","x":"D) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Krugman's new trade theory"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nThis is precisely Wassily Leontief's famous empirical finding that contradicted the Heckscher-Ohlin factor-endowments prediction for U.S. trade."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A commercial aircraft manufacturer benefits from being one of only two global producers, having entered the market first and built up decades of scale and experience that make it very hard for a new entrant to match. This scenario best illustrates:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) absolute advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the international product life cycle theory"},{"t":"p","x":"C) New Trade Theory / strategic trade theory"},{"t":"p","x":"D) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nFirst-mover advantage and durable dominance in an industry that can only support a small number of global competitors is the central mechanism described by Krugman's New Trade Theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Using Porter's Diamond, briefly analyse why Hong Kong has historically been internationally competitive in trade-related financial and logistics services (you may use general knowledge of Hong Kong to support each point).**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Factor conditions: Hong Kong has advanced factor conditions — world-class port/airport infrastructure, a deep pool of finance/trade professionals, and strong rule of law/property rights supporting financial contracting.\n• Demand conditions: sophisticated local and regional demand for trade-finance and logistics services from firms trading with mainland China and across Asia pushes continual service innovation.\n• Related & supporting industries: Hong Kong has a dense cluster of banks, insurers, shipping firms, and legal/accounting professional services that reinforce each other.\n• Firm strategy, structure & rivalry: intense competition among Hong Kong's many banks/trading houses/logistics firms drives ongoing efficiency and innovation, reinforced by a historically open, pro-competition government policy stance."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A government minister argues: \"Comparative advantage theory is outdated — my country has no absolute advantage in anything, so free trade will only hurt us.\" Correct this misunderstanding using material from this chapter.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• This confuses absolute advantage with comparative advantage. Ricardo's key insight is that a country with NO absolute advantage in any good STILL gains from trade, as long as it specialises in whichever good it produces with the lowest opportunity cost relative to its other goods, and trades for the rest.\n• The empirical record strongly supports opening to trade being associated with faster growth (e.g. China's growth acceleration after opening to international trade) — though the minister is right to note (per Samuelson's qualification) that domestic job losses in specific import-competing sectors are a real, non-trivial cost that policy should try to address, even while the aggregate national gain from trade remains real."}]},"7":{"title":"Government Policy and International Trade","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Despite the strong theoretical and empirical case that free trade raises welfare (Chapter 6), virtually every government intervenes in trade to some degree — what mercantilists once did openly is today called \"protectionism.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.1 Instruments of Trade Policy"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Instrument","How it works"],["Tariffs","A tax on imports (ad valorem = % of value, or a fixed amount per unit); protects local producers and raises government revenue, at consumers' expense."],["Subsidies","Government payments/benefits to local producers so they can compete with imports or export more competitively; ultimately funded by taxpayers; especially common in agriculture, where it can hurt poorer countries with a genuine comparative advantage in farming."],["Quotas","A numerical limit on imports of a good; a tariff-rate quota allows a lower tariff up to a threshold, then a higher tariff above it; a Voluntary Export Restraint (VER) is the exporting country \"agreeing\" (usually under pressure) to limit its own exports."],["Local content requirements","Require a set % of a product's value to be produced locally; common in developing countries seeking both import substitution and technology transfer."],["Administrative policies","Bureaucratic/regulatory hurdles that make importing costly or slow without technically breaking trade-agreement commitments — a form of \"hidden\" protectionism."],["Antidumping policies","Countermeasures against \"dumping\" (selling below production cost to seize market share, often subsidised); legitimate when dumping is real, but frequently misused as disguised protectionism — e.g. EU anti-dumping tariffs on Chinese TVs were later overturned by the WTO, which found no dumping had occurred."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.2 Why Governments Intervene"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Political reasons (the dominant real-world driver)"},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting jobs & industries — by far the most common real motive: a small, concentrated group of workers/firms benefits visibly and lobbies/votes accordingly, while the (much larger) cost to the general public is diffuse and largely invisible — even though studies consistently show such tariffs cost the wider economy more than they \"save\" for the protected group."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting national security — e.g. maintaining domestic aerospace/defence capacity; U.S. restrictions on Huawei telecoms equipment over cybersecurity concerns."},{"t":"li","x":"Retaliation — projects political strength, even though economists show retaliatory barriers make both countries worse off."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting consumers — e.g. banning unsafe imports (British beef during mad-cow disease); genuine safety justifications and pretextual protectionism can be hard to distinguish."},{"t":"li","x":"Furthering foreign policy objectives — e.g. U.S. sanctions penalising any firm (domestic or foreign) doing business with Iran."},{"t":"li","x":"Protecting human rights — e.g. the historical international trade embargo on apartheid-era South Africa."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Economic reasons (less common, more contested)"},{"t":"li","x":"Infant industry argument: temporary protection lets young domestic industries in developing countries grow to a scale/competence where they can compete internationally."},{"t":"li","x":"Strategic trade policy: following New Trade Theory (Chapter 6), government support for industries with major first-mover/scale advantages — but risky, since it invites retaliation and applies to only a limited set of industries."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.3 A Brief History of the World Trading System"},{"t":"li","x":"Mercantilism dominant until the mid-19th century, when major powers (influenced by Smith and Ricardo) opened to trade, producing a \"first wave of globalization\" (~1850–1910)."},{"t":"li","x":"Trade rebounded after WWI through the 1920s (a boom era, especially for the U.S.), but the 1929 Wall Street Crash triggered the U.S. Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act (1930), which raised tariffs sharply; other countries retaliated, world trade collapsed, and the resulting economic pain helped drive both the Great Depression and the rise of nationalist movements that led to WWII."},{"t":"li","x":"Post-WWII, the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT, 1947) was created precisely to prevent a repeat of that collapse — committing signatories to gradually negotiate down trade barriers; trade and growth expanded strongly for decades."},{"t":"li","x":"By the 1980s, protectionist pressure resurfaced (partly driven by Japan's rise as a competitor), often via the less-visible \"administrative\" barriers described above — exposing GATT's lack of an effective enforcement mechanism."},{"t":"li","x":"This led to the creation of the World Trade Organization (WTO), with real adjudication/enforcement power. The WTO has since struggled: a persistent developed-vs-developing-country split, a post-2008-financial-crisis backlash against free trade, and — during the Trump administration — the U.S. blocking new appointments to the WTO's appellate body, undermining its ability to resolve disputes. Free trade is described in the study guide as \"at a crossroads\", with meaningful risk of a return to greater protectionism/trade wars."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7.4 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"li","x":"Firms must map exactly which barriers apply on each of their trade routes before committing to a strategy."},{"t":"li","x":"\"Re-shoring\": the U.S.-China trade war's tariffs have, in some cases, made pulling manufacturing back onshore cheaper than staying in China despite higher domestic production costs."},{"t":"li","x":"Firms actively lobby governments: export-oriented firms generally lobby FOR free trade agreements/lower barriers; domestically-oriented, import-competing firms generally lobby FOR protection."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 7"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A government imposes a tariff that raises domestic steel prices, protecting a relatively small number of steelworker jobs while raising costs for the much larger number of firms and consumers that buy steel. Economically, this outcome is best explained by:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the infant industry argument"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a concentrated-benefit, diffuse-cost political dynamic favouring protection despite a net welfare loss"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the Leontief paradox"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Porter's Diamond theory"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an optimal currency area"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the classic political-economy explanation for protectionism: a small, organised group (steelworkers) has a strong incentive to lobby, while the much larger, diffuse group of consumers bears a smaller individual cost and has little incentive to organise against it — even though the aggregate cost to the economy exceeds the benefit to the protected group."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. An exporting country agrees, under pressure from the importing country, to voluntarily limit how much of a good it will ship there. This is an example of:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a tariff-rate quota only"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a Voluntary Export Restraint (VER)"},{"t":"p","x":"C) an antidumping duty"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a local content requirement"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an administrative policy"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA VER is defined precisely as an agreement, usually made under duress from the importing country, in which the EXPORTING country limits its own exports of a good."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. The U.S. Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act of 1930 is significant in trade history chiefly because it:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) created the WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"B) triggered retaliatory tariffs that helped collapse world trade and deepen the Great Depression"},{"t":"p","x":"C) eliminated all U.S. import tariffs"},{"t":"p","x":"D) established the Bretton Woods system"},{"t":"p","x":"E) was the first free trade agreement between the U.S. and Canada"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSmoot-Hawley sharply raised U.S. tariffs after the 1929 crash; other countries retaliated, world trade collapsed, and the resulting economic damage is widely seen as having deepened the Great Depression and fuelled the nationalism that contributed to WWII."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A trade minister proposes an antidumping duty on imported solar panels, claiming the exporting country is \"dumping\" them. Explain what genuine dumping is, and outline one risk that antidumping policy is often misused.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Dumping is selling a good in a foreign market below its cost of production, typically to seize market share and drive out local competitors (sometimes enabled by government subsidies in the exporting country).\n• Antidumping measures are legitimate when used to counter genuine dumping, but the tool is frequently misused as disguised protectionism against foreign producers who are simply more efficient/lower-cost — not actually dumping. The EU's anti-dumping tariffs on Chinese TVs, later overturned by the WTO after it found no dumping had occurred, is the course's own example of this risk."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain why the WTO was created as a successor to GATT, and describe one specific challenge the WTO has faced in recent years.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• GATT lacked an effective enforcement mechanism: when countries flouted their commitments (especially via harder-to-detect 'hidden' barriers like administrative policies), there was no strong way to compel correction or punish offenders.\n• The WTO was created with real judicial/enforcement power — employing judges to adjudicate trade disputes on set timelines.\n• Recent challenges include: a persistent split between developed and developing member priorities (e.g. over agricultural vs. manufactured-goods/services liberalisation); intensified anti-globalisation protest after the 2008 financial crisis; and the Trump-era U.S. blocking of new appellate-body judge appointments, which has hampered the WTO's ability to resolve disputes."}]},"8":{"title":"Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"FDI is when a firm invests capital earned in its home operations to establish or control a business activity in another (\"host\") country; the home-country headquarters retains management control over the foreign subsidiary and expects to eventually repatriate profits. This distinguishes FDI from portfolio investment (passive cross-border investment, e.g. buying foreign shares, without management control) — the UN/textbook threshold is that a stake above 10% ownership qualifies as FDI."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.1 Measuring FDI"},{"t":"li","x":"FDI flow: the net amount of NEW investment from one country to another in a given year — the figure most often quoted in news headlines (\"FDI up X% this year\")."},{"t":"li","x":"FDI stock: the cumulative total value of all FDI investment made to date — arguably the more meaningful long-run measure."},{"t":"li","x":"Production realised from FDI: the total value of goods/services produced by FDI-funded operations — globally, this measure typically EXCEEDS the total value of international trade."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.2 Growth and Direction of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"FDI is more recent than trade (limited to the very largest firms before ~1950) but has grown far faster than both world trade and world GDP over the past fifty years, driven by the same forces as globalization (Chapter 1): falling trade/investment barriers and technological change (especially telecommunications, which now lets HQs manage/integrate foreign subsidiaries into a genuinely global supply chain)."},{"t":"li","x":"Traditionally, most FDI flowed between developed countries (the U.S., Japan and Britain have historically been the largest outward investors, and the U.S. the largest recipient)."},{"t":"li","x":"Since Deng Xiaoping opened China to FDI in the late 1970s, China and other developing economies have become both major FDI recipients AND, more recently, major outward investors themselves."},{"t":"li","x":"The theoretical link to comparative advantage: e.g. Nike keeps design/marketing (its comparative advantage) in the U.S. but manufactures shoes in Indonesia/Vietnam (their comparative advantage) — FDI, like trade, can raise world efficiency by matching activities to each country's relative strengths."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.3 Types of Outward FDI"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Greenfield investment","Building an entirely new foreign operation from scratch. Time-consuming and can be difficult to establish against already-entrenched local competitors."],["Acquisition (M&A)","Buying an existing foreign firm and taking over its operations. The most common form of FDI by volume — fast to establish, but can be costly to acquire."],["Joint venture / joint development","Partnering with a local firm. Lowers the initial capital outlay and eases entry into an unfamiliar market, but requires profit-sharing and can create partner-management friction."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.4 Why Does FDI Happen? Dunning's Eclectic (OLI) Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Because a foreign firm competing in an unfamiliar environment suffers a \"liability of foreignness\", FDI could in theory be an inefficient entry mode compared to simply buying from, or licensing to, local firms. Dunning's OLI theory explains why FDI happens anyway: it occurs when all three conditions hold simultaneously."},{"t":"li","x":"Ownership advantage: the firm can do or produce something more efficiently than local competitors"},{"t":"li","x":"Location advantage: producing in the foreign location makes more sense than producing at home"},{"t":"li","x":"Internalization advantage: there is a good reason to keep the activity INSIDE the firm rather than licensing it to a local partner (most often, to protect proprietary technology/know-how from leaking to a potential competitor)"},{"t":"p","x":"Nike example: Nike's ownership advantage (design/brand/marketing capability) stays in the U.S.; Indonesia/Vietnam offer the location advantage (lower-cost, appropriately-skilled manufacturing labour); Nike chooses FDI/tightly-controlled contract manufacturing (an internalization choice) rather than simply licensing its designs to unrelated local factories."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.5 Government Policy Toward FDI"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Free-market view","FDI benefits the world economy via comparative advantage; firms and governments should be free to invest/receive investment without restriction."],["Radical view","FDI is inherently extractive/harmful to the host economy — coming only to exploit cheap labour, the local market, or natural resources — and should be discouraged."],["Pragmatic nationalism (most common today)","Governments evaluate each FDI proposal case-by-case on its net benefits vs. costs, considering: (1) Jobs created and their quality; (2) Technology transfer potential; (3) Local impact (does it use local suppliers and boost local competitiveness, or displace local firms?); (4) Balance-of-payments impact (capital inflow use; subsequent import/export effects); (5) Sovereignty/national-security concerns."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Host country responses range along a spectrum: active encouragement (tax holidays, free land, dedicated investment-promotion agencies) → conditional acceptance (requiring local content or export commitments) → outright rejection for unattractive proposals."},{"t":"p","x":"Home country perspective: generally supportive of outward FDI (extends national influence, revenue and opportunities for domestic firms), but may restrict it when it causes domestic job losses, transfers strategically sensitive technology to a rival, or is seen as a wasteful use of foreign currency."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.6 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"FDI is costly, in both capital and managerial attention (a real opportunity cost); most firms therefore use lower-commitment entry modes (exporting, licensing) first, and only \"graduate\" to FDI once the market/relationship is proven."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8.7 In-Class Case: Taiwan FDI Proposal Ranking (Adapted)"},{"t":"p","x":"Three FDI proposals were presented to Taiwan's (fictional) Department of Trade and Industry:"},{"t":"li","x":"A. Ford Motor Co. — $100m greenfield investment for a state-of-the-art assembly plant using advanced Japanese robotics, producing luxury cars for export worldwide."},{"t":"li","x":"B. A small Malaysian computer firm — $4m low-tech assembly plant using local component suppliers, selling mostly to the region (not for export outside Asia)."},{"t":"li","x":"C. A German steel firm — proposes to acquire the loss-making, state-owned Taiwan Steel (the only steelmaker left in the country) for $40m, roughly half of its ~$80m asset value, citing years of losses; output sold almost entirely in the domestic market."},{"t":"p","x":"Applying the pragmatic-nationalism criteria (jobs, technology, local impact, BoP, sovereignty), Proposal A is generally the most attractive: high-value jobs, advanced technology transfer, and export-oriented (strongly positive BoP effect). Proposal B is moderate: modest job creation and some local-supplier benefit, but low-tech and limited to a regional (not export) market. Proposal C is the most complex: it rescues a failing state asset and could bring new technology/management, but also implies job losses/restructuring, sovereignty sensitivities (foreign control of a formerly state-owned strategic industry), and a purely domestic-market focus with no BoP export benefit — a government would likely want conditions attached (e.g. minimum employment guarantees, technology-transfer commitments) before approval."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 8"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A US investment fund buys a 3% passive stake in a French company's publicly-traded shares, with no management involvement. This is best classified as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Portfolio investment"},{"t":"p","x":"C) A Greenfield investment"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Offshore outsourcing"},{"t":"p","x":"E) A Joint venture"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA passive stake below the ~10% FDI ownership threshold, with no management control, is portfolio investment rather than FDI."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Under Dunning's OLI (eclectic) theory, a firm choosing to manufacture in-house abroad (rather than licensing its technology to a local partner) to prevent that technology being copied is exercising which advantage?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Ownership advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Location advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Internalization advantage"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Comparative advantage only"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Absolute advantage"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nChoosing to keep an activity inside the firm rather than licensing it out — specifically to protect proprietary technology — is the internalization advantage in the OLI framework."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. A host government reviews an incoming FDI proposal by weighing job creation, technology transfer, local-supplier impact, balance-of-payments effects, and sovereignty concerns on a case-by-case basis. This approach is known as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the radical view of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the free-market view of FDI"},{"t":"p","x":"C) pragmatic nationalism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) strategic trade policy"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nCase-by-case weighing of costs and benefits, rather than a blanket welcome (free-market view) or blanket rejection (radical view), defines pragmatic nationalism."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Distinguish FDI flow from FDI stock, and explain which one better answers the question: \"How large is total American economic presence, built up over decades, inside Vietnam today?\"**"},{"t":"a","x":"• FDI flow measures only the NET NEW investment made in a single year (e.g. this year's inflow minus repatriated capital) — a snapshot of recent momentum.\n• FDI stock measures the CUMULATIVE total of all FDI ever made and still held — the running total built up over time.\n• The question asks about total accumulated American economic presence built up over decades, so FDI stock is the correct measure — FDI flow would only show this year's incremental change, not the accumulated total."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. A government official says: \"We should welcome all incoming FDI without exception, since foreign investment always helps our economy.\" Using the free-market vs. radical vs. pragmatic-nationalism framework, evaluate this statement.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• The official's statement reflects the pure free-market view, which does have real support (comparative advantage logic suggests mutual gains from unrestricted investment) but ignores genuine host-country risks the radical view highlights — e.g. an FDI project that displaces local competitors, repatriates most profits with little local benefit, or compromises a strategically sensitive sector.\n• Most real-world governments (and the course's own preferred framework) instead apply pragmatic nationalism: evaluating each proposal on jobs, technology transfer, local impact, balance-of-payments effects and sovereignty/security concerns, encouraging clearly beneficial projects (e.g. via tax incentives) while attaching conditions to or rejecting less beneficial ones — a more balanced position than either extreme."}]},"9":{"title":"Regional Economic Integration","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Note: Per the official Study Guide: this chapter's scope is deliberately narrower than the others. Students do NOT need to memorise the specific details/history of individual regional agreements (NAFTA, EU, ASEAN, etc.) for the mid-term or final exam — only general familiarity with their membership and FORM. The exam-relevant focus is the general framework of integration levels, the economic/political rationale, trade creation vs. trade diversion, and the costs/benefits of a common currency."},{"t":"p","x":"Why do regional/bilateral deals keep proliferating? The WTO's multilateral \"single undertaking\" approach (\"nothing is agreed until everything is agreed\") has largely stalled, because it must reconcile too many divergent national interests at once. It is far easier for a smaller group of geographically-close countries to reach agreement among themselves."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.1 Levels of Economic Integration"},{"t":"p","x":"Each level nests inside the next — a deeper level includes all the features of the levels beneath it, and each step represents a significant loss of national sovereignty."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level","Defining feature"],["Free Trade Area","Tariff-free trade between members; each member independently sets its own tariffs on non-members."],["Customs Union","Free trade area + a common external tariff — allows border checks on origin to be eliminated."],["Common Market","Customs union + free movement of capital AND labour between members (no work permits needed)."],["Economic Union","Common market + shared macroeconomic policy — e.g. a common currency, harmonised tax rates, common monetary policy."],["Political Union","Economic union + political merger — common social policy and a shared defence policy."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.2 Why Integrate? Economic and Political Rationale"},{"t":"li","x":"Economic: removing internal trade barriers lets the region specialise via comparative advantage, giving consumers wider/cheaper/higher-quality choice and giving firms lower input costs and greater economies of scale, raising international competitiveness."},{"t":"li","x":"Political: reduces tension between historic rivals (part of the EU's founding motivation, after two European wars) and gives small countries greater collective bargaining power than they would have alone."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.3 Impediments to Deeper Integration"},{"t":"li","x":"Even if aggregate/medium-term gains are positive, some sectors face real short-term job losses as internal competition intensifies — a politically painful, immediate cost."},{"t":"li","x":"Each deeper level requires giving up more national sovereignty/decision-making autonomy — a key driver of the UK's vote for Brexit (resentment at \"being told what to do\" by EU institutions)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.4 Is Regional Integration Good for the World Economy? Trade Creation vs. Trade Diversion"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Trade creation","More efficient, LOW-COST producers WITHIN the new trade area replace less-efficient domestic producers — a net welfare GAIN."],["Trade diversion","More efficient producers OUTSIDE the trade area are replaced by less-efficient producers INSIDE the area, purely because of the area's preferential tariffs — a net welfare LOSS."]]},{"t":"p","x":"A regional trade agreement benefits the world economy overall only when the trade creation it generates exceeds the trade diversion it causes — this exact question is directly tested in the official Chapter 9 quiz below."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.5 Costs and Benefits of a Common Regional Currency"},{"t":"li","x":"Benefits: eliminates the transaction costs and exchange-rate uncertainty of cross-border trade/investment within the region (different currencies act like an invisible trade barrier); increases transparency, further boosting cross-border trade and investment."},{"t":"li","x":"Costs: member states lose national monetary autonomy — interest rates and money supply are set jointly (e.g. by the European Central Bank), not independently. This works well only when the members form an \"optimal currency area\" (their economies move in sync and share similar monetary-policy needs). When needs diverge, this becomes a serious problem — the Eurozone crisis arose largely because Greece needed higher interest rates/tighter policy at a time Germany needed the opposite. This is precisely why the UK, Denmark and Sweden chose to stay outside the euro."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.6 Regional Agreements in Asia (light-touch, per Study Guide)"},{"t":"li","x":"ASEAN: the oldest, most established regional agreement in Asia, though still limited in coverage (many goods/services excluded); deepening cooperation with mainland China and moving toward more tariff-free movement."},{"t":"li","x":"APEC: functions mostly as a talking forum, though it has proposed a free trade area of the Asia-Pacific."},{"t":"li","x":"RCEP (Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership): ASEAN + China, Japan, South Korea, Australia and New Zealand (see also the Illustrative Readings section for two contemporary news articles on RCEP's signing)."},{"t":"li","x":"TPP → CPTPP: the U.S.-led Trans-Pacific Partnership was abandoned by President Trump; the remaining 11 countries (Australia, Canada, Japan, Mexico, New Zealand, Vietnam, Singapore, etc.) ratified the narrower Comprehensive and Progressive Agreement for Trans-Pacific Partnership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.7 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"Regional agreements let firms serve a whole harmonised economic region rather than country-by-country (lower compliance costs, harmonised standards, free internal movement of goods) — but can also act as \"fortresses\", easing business within the bloc while raising effective barriers against outside firms. The resulting complexity of managing many overlapping regional/bilateral deals is itself one of the strongest arguments for renewed progress at the (single, universal) WTO level."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9.8 Official Chapter 9 Quiz (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Canada and the United States agree to remove all barriers to the trade of goods between them. However, the countries agree that each would be allowed to determine its own trade policies with regard to nonmembers. The economic integration between these countries is called a(n)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) free trade area."},{"t":"p","x":"B) customs union."},{"t":"p","x":"C) common market."},{"t":"p","x":"D) economic union."},{"t":"p","x":"E) political union."},{"t":"a","x":"A"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Which political factor resulted in the establishment of the European Union (EU)?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) spectacular success of NAFTA"},{"t":"p","x":"B) trade impasse following the 1970s oil crisis"},{"t":"p","x":"C) devastation of Western Europe during two world wars and the desire for lasting peace"},{"t":"p","x":"D) emergence of Japan as an economic/industrial superpower"},{"t":"p","x":"E) rise of communism in Europe"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. After the formation of a free trade area, the US found its high-cost furniture producers in North Carolina replaced by low-cost producers from another member country. This is an example of**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) value creation."},{"t":"p","x":"B) strategic pricing."},{"t":"p","x":"C) trade creation."},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade diversion."},{"t":"p","x":"E) economic exposure."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A regional free trade agreement will benefit the world only if**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) it raises the standard of living in one of the member countries."},{"t":"p","x":"B) the amount of trade it creates exceeds the amount it diverts."},{"t":"p","x":"C) the currencies of the nations involved appreciate."},{"t":"p","x":"D) the balance-of-trade situation remains stable in the region."},{"t":"p","x":"E) it creates a trade surplus for one of the countries involved."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Iceland and Norway form a free trade area. Iceland begins importing wool from Norway instead of from England, which had produced wool more cheaply than either Iceland or Norway. This is an example of**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) trade creation"},{"t":"p","x":"B) strategic pricing"},{"t":"p","x":"C) synergy"},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"E) protectionism"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**6. What was a change proposed by the Single European Act?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) establish frontier controls among EC countries"},{"t":"p","x":"B) increase the resources required for complying with trade bureaucracy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) place barriers to competition in retail banking and insurance"},{"t":"p","x":"D) apply the principle of \"mutual recognition\" to product standards"},{"t":"p","x":"E) reduce costs by not allowing lower-cost suppliers into national economies"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. A key advantage of adopting the euro is that it**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) helps reduce competition in Europe."},{"t":"p","x":"B) has prevented a highly liquid pan-European capital market."},{"t":"p","x":"C) lowers foreign exchange and hedging costs in Europe."},{"t":"p","x":"D) insulates Europe from international competition."},{"t":"p","x":"E) increases investment options open to institutions only."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. Great Britain, Denmark, and Sweden have stayed out of the euro zone because of the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) dollar peg advocated by some EU members."},{"t":"p","x":"B) implied loss of national sovereignty to the European Central Bank."},{"t":"p","x":"C) volatility of the euro."},{"t":"p","x":"D) reluctance to compete directly against the U.S. dollar."},{"t":"p","x":"E) reluctance to be considered an optimal currency area."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. Two neighbouring countries have the same wage rates, tax regimes and business cycles, and have reacted similarly to past economic shocks. These two countries can adopt a common currency because they form a(n)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) managed currency zone."},{"t":"p","x":"B) open exchange regime."},{"t":"p","x":"C) optimal currency area."},{"t":"p","x":"D) free trade area."},{"t":"p","x":"E) advanced monetary zone."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. An expected consequence of the implementation of NAFTA was that**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) low-skilled jobs would move to Mexico, lowering average wage rates in the U.S. and Canada."},{"t":"p","x":"B) increased imports from Mexico would reduce the U.S./Canada trade deficit."},{"t":"p","x":"C) lower Mexican incomes would decrease demand for U.S./Canadian goods."},{"t":"p","x":"D) many Mexican firms would hire low-skilled U.S. workers."},{"t":"p","x":"E) some U.S. and Canadian firms would move production to Mexico for lower labour costs."},{"t":"a","x":"E"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 9"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Which level of regional economic integration is the FIRST to require free movement of labour between member countries, without work permits?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Free trade area"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Customs union"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Common market"},{"t":"p","x":"D) A simple tariff-rate quota"},{"t":"p","x":"E) An antidumping agreement"},{"t":"a","x":"C\nFree movement of labour (alongside capital) is the defining addition at the Common Market level, beyond the customs union's shared external tariff."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Why might a regional trade bloc's common currency work well for some members but poorly for others?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) because tariffs always rise under a common currency"},{"t":"p","x":"B) because members lose independent monetary policy, which only works smoothly if they form an optimal currency area with synchronised economic needs"},{"t":"p","x":"C) because a common currency always causes trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"D) because a common currency abolishes the WTO"},{"t":"p","x":"E) because common currencies are illegal under GATT"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe core cost of a common currency is lost national monetary autonomy; this only works well when members' economies move together (an optimal currency area) — otherwise, as in the Eurozone crisis, one common interest rate cannot suit members with very different needs."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. ASEAN member states are considering deepening from a free trade area to a full common market. Identify one specific new benefit and one specific new sovereignty cost this deepening would bring.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• New benefit: free movement of labour and capital across ASEAN would let firms allocate workers/investment to wherever they are most productive within the region, and let workers move to where wages/opportunities are best, raising regional efficiency beyond what a free trade area alone can achieve.\n• New sovereignty cost: individual governments would lose the ability to independently control immigration/labour-market policy and cross-border capital flows — a bigger sovereignty concession than simply agreeing on tariffs, and the kind of concession that, in the EU's case, fed resentment that contributed to Brexit."}]},"10":{"title":"The Foreign Exchange Market","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"The instructor's own framing (not in the textbook, but taught explicitly in class): think of a unit of currency as a measure of VALUE. This reframing makes it much easier to understand what \"overvalued\" and \"undervalued\" actually mean, and why exchange rates move the way they do."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.1 The Foreign Exchange (\"Forex\") Market"},{"t":"p","x":"Operates across multiple financial centres (mainly London, New York, Tokyo), serving the currency-exchange needs of travellers, and of firms/governments engaged in trade and investment. In practice, however, the overwhelming majority of daily FX trading volume comes from short-term currency speculators, not underlying trade/investment activity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.2 Three Types of Foreign Exchange Risk"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Transaction risk","The exchange rate moves between agreeing a deal and settling payment — e.g. a firm that agrees to pay in a foreign currency in six months may end up paying more (in its own currency) if that currency appreciates in the meantime; a seller who is due to receive a foreign currency later may receive less (in its own currency) if that currency depreciates."],["Translation risk","Consolidating a multinational's foreign revenue/profit into home-currency financial statements can distort the picture — if the HOME currency appreciates, the same foreign earnings translate into a smaller home-currency figure, even though nothing changed in the actual foreign business; this can hurt stock price and the ability to raise capital."],["Economic risk","If a customer country's currency depreciates sharply, demand for the foreign firm's (now relatively more expensive) imported products falls; the firm must choose between accepting lower sales or cutting its price/margin to stay competitive there."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Hedging with forward contracts: a spot rate is today's market exchange rate; a forward rate is a rate locked in TODAY for a currency exchange that will happen at a FUTURE date. A forward contract removes exchange-rate uncertainty for the customer, in exchange for a premium/spread the bank charges for bearing that risk itself."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.3 Explaining Exchange Rate Movements"},{"t":"p","x":"**The Law of One Price / Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A given basket of goods should cost the same everywhere, once converted at the market exchange rate. When it doesn't, the exchange rate should adjust until it does; the equilibrium rate where prices equalise is called the PPP rate. Because relative inflation drives these price gaps, a country with persistently higher inflation should see its currency depreciate against lower-inflation countries. Since future inflation is hard to observe directly, relative interest rates are used as a proxy: the International Fisher Effect links interest-rate differences between two countries to the expected movement in their exchange rate. Non-economic factors (market sentiment, the \"bandwagon effect\") also matter, especially in the short run — but PPP remains the most credible tool for MEDIUM- to LONG-term exchange-rate movements."},{"t":"p","x":"**The Big Mac Index (The Economist)**"},{"t":"p","x":"A well-known, tongue-in-cheek but genuinely instructive real-world application of PPP: because a Big Mac is produced almost identically in 100+ countries, comparing its price (converted to USD) across countries gives a simple PPP-based valuation of each currency. A currency is \"undervalued\" if its Big Mac converts to fewer USD than the US price, and \"overvalued\" if it converts to more. The Economist has found that currencies flagged as undervalued by the index tend, ON AVERAGE, to appreciate over the following years — but short (e.g. two-year) back-tests of a naive \"buy the cheapest currency\" strategy have a genuinely mixed record, partly because poorer countries structurally have lower price levels even without currency misvaluation (higher-income countries simply have higher wages/prices even in less-tradable parts of the economy) — which is why The Economist also publishes a GDP-adjusted version of the index."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.4 Can Short-Term Exchange Rate Movements Be Predicted?"},{"t":"p","x":"A genuinely contested question. The efficient-market view (e.g. as expressed by former U.S. Federal Reserve chair Alan Greenspan, quoted in class materials as saying no model beats a coin flip) holds that today's exchange rate already reflects all available information, so short-term moves are essentially unpredictable. The alternative view holds markets are imperfect and can be \"beaten\" using either:"},{"t":"li","x":"Fundamental analysis: regression models built on macroeconomic variables — weakness: correlation is not causation, and any model is necessarily calibrated on past (backward-looking) data."},{"t":"li","x":"Technical analysis: chart patterns/trend-following — has little logical/theoretical grounding, though it remains popular among some traders."},{"t":"p","x":"Course conclusion: short-term currency speculation/day-trading is NOT recommended for individuals or non-specialist firms — many participants lose money (illustrated by the retail-forex boom described in the Newsweek article \"Making Money on Money\" and the U.S. CFTC's long record of fraud-enforcement actions against forex-trading schemes)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.5 Managerial Implications"},{"t":"p","x":"Recognise which of the three risk types apply to a given transaction/investment; use lead/lag strategies or forward-contract hedging to manage exposure; understand PPP and the Fisher effect as the fundamental drivers of exchange rates rather than relying on short-term forecasting, which is unreliable even for specialists."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.6 Official In-Class FX Exercises (worked examples, October 2017 rates as originally set)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are the instructor's own in-class numerical exercises, reproduced here as worked examples (illustrative rates only — always use current live rates for any real calculation):"},{"t":"li","x":"Currency conversion: converting a EUR, GBP or HKD amount into another currency at a given cross-rate is simply multiplying by the appropriate exchange rate — e.g. EUR 800 at a given EUR/USD rate converts directly to USD."},{"t":"li","x":"Forward-contract hedge decision: a Hong Kong importer owes a Thai supplier THB 2,400,000 in six months. If the Thai Baht is expected to strengthen, the importer compares (a) waiting and converting HKD to THB at the future spot rate (risky — costs more if the Baht strengthens as expected) against (b) locking in a forward contract today at a fixed HKD cost. If the forward-contract HKD cost is lower than the expected future spot-rate cost, the hedge is worthwhile — it removes the risk of the Baht appreciating further."},{"t":"li","x":"Big Mac / PPP valuation: given the US Big Mac price and the actual foreign-currency Big Mac price in another country, the implied PPP exchange rate is (foreign price) ÷ (US price); comparing this implied PPP rate to the ACTUAL market exchange rate reveals whether that currency is over- or under-valued, and (per PPP theory) which direction it should be expected to move over the medium/long term."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10.7 Official Chapter 10 Quiz (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Steven converted $1,000 to ¥105,000 for a trip to Japan. He spent only ¥50,000. During this period the dollar weakened against the yen. Using a current exchange rate of $1 = ¥100, how many dollars does Steven have left?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) $550"},{"t":"p","x":"B) $523"},{"t":"p","x":"C) $450"},{"t":"p","x":"D) $600"},{"t":"p","x":"E) $500"},{"t":"a","x":"A\n¥105,000 − ¥50,000 = ¥55,000 remaining; at $1 = ¥100, ¥55,000 = $550."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. Currency speculation takes place when**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) there is short-term movement of funds from one currency to another hoping to profit from shifts in exchange rates."},{"t":"p","x":"B) the exchange rate a dealer will convert one currency at differs on a particular day."},{"t":"p","x":"C) there is a simultaneous purchase and sale of a given amount of FX for two different value dates."},{"t":"p","x":"D) securities bought in one market are immediately resold in another to profit from a price discrepancy."},{"t":"p","x":"E) money supply growth exceeds output growth, leading to inflation."},{"t":"a","x":"A"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Currency A trades at 6 to the US$, currency B at 40 to the US$, and currency C at 120 to the US$. Which statement is true?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Of A, B and C, country A has the best economy."},{"t":"p","x":"B) Of A, B and C, currency A is the strongest."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Of A, B and C, currency C is the most undervalued."},{"t":"p","x":"D) A), B) and C) are all true."},{"t":"p","x":"E) It is impossible to say if any of the above are true."},{"t":"a","x":"E\nThe nominal exchange rate alone (how many units per US$) says nothing about which economy is 'best' or which currency is under/overvalued — that requires a PPP-style price comparison, not just the exchange-rate level."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Saturn Tide, a US energy firm, enters an agreement to exchange currency and execute the deal in eighteen months. What is Saturn Tide using to insure itself against foreign exchange risk?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency speculation"},{"t":"p","x":"B) carry trade"},{"t":"p","x":"C) hedging"},{"t":"p","x":"D) currency swap"},{"t":"p","x":"E) arbitrage"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. FutureForm (US) imports microprocessors from Japan, owing ¥150,000 per unit at a spot rate of $1 = ¥110, reselling at $1,600 each once the funds from resale arrive. What happens if the rate after 30 days is $1 = ¥90?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Profit of approx. $236 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"B) Profit of approx. $67 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Loss of approx. $236 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"D) Loss of approx. $67 per unit."},{"t":"p","x":"E) Loss of approx. $90 per unit."},{"t":"a","x":"D\nFutureForm must actually pay ¥150,000 to the supplier once it has the resale cash in hand. At $1=¥90, that costs $150,000/90 = $1,666.67; reselling at $1,600 leaves a loss of about $1,666.67 − $1,600 ≈ $67 per microprocessor."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. The current market exchange rate for a currency — the price right now — is called:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) The forward rate"},{"t":"p","x":"B) The constant rate"},{"t":"p","x":"C) The current rate"},{"t":"p","x":"D) The spot rate"},{"t":"p","x":"E) The PPP rate"},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. The idea that currencies will naturally return to the rate at which equal value can be acquired in each country is called:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency swap rule"},{"t":"p","x":"B) anti-speculation agenda"},{"t":"p","x":"C) law of one price"},{"t":"p","x":"D) fisher law"},{"t":"p","x":"E) arbitrage equilibrium"},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. The yen/dollar rate quoted in London at 3:00pm is ¥115=$1; the rate quoted in New York at 10:00am (=3:00pm London time) is ¥135=$1. Rinaldo buys yen in New York and sells it in London. Rinaldo is engaging in**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) currency swapping."},{"t":"p","x":"B) currency speculation."},{"t":"p","x":"C) carry trade."},{"t":"p","x":"D) arbitrage."},{"t":"p","x":"E) hedging."},{"t":"a","x":"D"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. In countries where inflation is expected to be high, interest rates will also be high, because investors want compensation for the decline in the value of their money. This relationship is called the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) purchasing power parity puzzle."},{"t":"p","x":"B) lead strategy."},{"t":"p","x":"C) Fisher effect."},{"t":"p","x":"D) bandwagon effect."},{"t":"p","x":"E) international Fisher effect."},{"t":"a","x":"C"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. A company's translation exposure is based on the**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) long-run effect of exchange-rate changes on future prices, sales and costs."},{"t":"p","x":"B) impact of currency exchange-rate changes on a company's reported financial statements."},{"t":"p","x":"C) extent to which future international earning power is affected by exchange-rate changes."},{"t":"p","x":"D) extent to which income from individual transactions is affected by FX fluctuations."},{"t":"p","x":"E) obligations for the purchase/sale of goods/services at previously agreed prices."},{"t":"a","x":"B"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 10"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. A Hong Kong firm will receive USD 100,000 in three months from a US customer, and is worried the US dollar might weaken against the HKD before then (reducing what it receives in HKD terms). Which of the three FX risk types does this best illustrate, and what could the firm do about it?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Translation risk; the firm should simply restate its financial statements"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Transaction risk; the firm could lock in today's rate using a forward contract"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Economic risk; the firm should raise its prices immediately"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Translation risk; there is no way to manage this risk"},{"t":"p","x":"E) None of the FX risk types apply here"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA single future cash receipt exposed to exchange-rate movement between now and settlement is transaction risk, and the standard tool to remove it is a forward contract locking in today's exchange rate."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. The Big Mac costs the equivalent of $6.00 in Country Z but $5.00 in the US at current market exchange rates. According to Big Mac index / PPP logic, Country Z's currency is:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) undervalued"},{"t":"p","x":"B) overvalued"},{"t":"p","x":"C) at exact PPP equilibrium"},{"t":"p","x":"D) impossible to assess from this information"},{"t":"p","x":"E) pegged to the US dollar"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nIf the identical product costs MORE once converted to USD, the local currency is buying 'too much' in USD terms relative to the US price — it is overvalued relative to PPP, similar to the Swiss franc example in the 2019 Big Mac index article."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Which of the following best explains why short-term exchange-rate forecasting is described in this course as unreliable even for professionals?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) exchange rates never actually move in the short term"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the efficient-market view holds that today's price already reflects available information, making short-term moves close to a coin flip"},{"t":"p","x":"C) PPP theory perfectly predicts hourly exchange-rate moves"},{"t":"p","x":"D) governments ban short-term currency trading everywhere"},{"t":"p","x":"E) technical analysis is proven to reliably beat the market"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThis is the efficient-market argument (attributed to Alan Greenspan in the lecture) for why short-term FX forecasting is unreliable — a view the course explicitly favours in recommending against short-term speculation."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. A US Big Mac costs $5.50. In Country Q, a Big Mac costs 220 units of the local currency, and the actual market exchange rate is 50 units per USD. (a) What is the PPP-implied exchange rate? (b) Is Country Q's currency over- or under-valued, and by roughly how much?**"},{"t":"a","x":"• (a) PPP-implied rate = local price ÷ US price = 220 ÷ 5.50 = 40 units per USD.\n• (b) The ACTUAL market rate (50 units per USD) is weaker for the local currency than the PPP-implied rate (40 units per USD) — it takes MORE local currency to buy a dollar than PPP suggests it should — so Country Q's currency is undervalued.\n• Approximate degree of undervaluation = (50 − 40) / 50 ≈ 20% undervalued against the US dollar."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Explain, using the concept of translation risk, why a European company with large profitable operations in the UK could report a FALL in overall group profit (in euros) even though its UK business had an excellent year in pound-sterling terms.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Translation risk arises because the parent company must convert (translate) its foreign subsidiary's local-currency results into the parent's reporting currency for consolidated financial statements.\n• If the euro strengthens significantly against the pound during the year, the same (or even higher) pound-denominated UK profit converts into FEWER euros when translated — so consolidated group profit in euros can fall even though the underlying UK business performed well in its own currency.\n• This is a purely accounting/translation effect, distinct from transaction risk (which affects actual cash flows) and economic risk (which affects competitiveness/demand) — but it can still affect the parent's reported earnings, stock price and ability to raise capital."}]},"11":{"title":"The Global Monetary System","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"This chapter has three components: (1) the history and development of the global monetary system (the gold standard and its successors); (2) fixed vs. floating exchange-rate regimes (plus pegged systems/currency boards); and (3) the IMF's origins, purpose, and its role in major monetary crises. The instructor stresses that the central focus for revision is the fixed-vs-floating comparison — the historical detail mainly provides context."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.1 Fixed vs. Floating Exchange Rate Systems"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Fixed exchange rate system","Pros: discourages currency speculation; imposes monetary discipline on governments (they cannot print money freely without breaking the peg). Cons: removes a tool for correcting chronic trade imbalances via currency adjustment; removes national monetary-policy autonomy (interest rates cannot be set independently of defending the peg)."],["Floating exchange rate system","Pros: allows chronic trade imbalances to self-correct through currency movements; preserves national monetary-policy autonomy. Cons: exposed to currency speculation and volatility; provides less automatic fiscal/monetary discipline."]]},{"t":"p","x":"There is no single consensus on which system is superior overall — the right choice depends on a country's specific circumstances. This directly connects back to Chapter 9's discussion of the euro: for its members, the euro functions as an extreme, effectively-permanent fixed-exchange-rate arrangement, which delivered real early benefits (see the euro-area readings below) but also produced the structural vulnerabilities that surfaced in the Eurozone debt crisis when member economies' needs diverged."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.2 The Mundell-Fleming \"Impossible Trinity\" (Trilemma)"},{"t":"p","x":"A country can have, at most, TWO of the following three at the same time — never all three simultaneously:"},{"t":"li","x":"1. A fixed exchange rate"},{"t":"li","x":"2. Independent monetary policy"},{"t":"li","x":"3. Free (unrestricted) international capital flows"},{"t":"p","x":"This trilemma is a key organising concept: e.g. a country with a fixed exchange rate and free capital flows (like Hong Kong) must give up independent monetary policy (HK effectively imports US monetary policy via its peg); a country wanting both a fixed rate and independent monetary policy (like China, historically) must restrict free capital flows."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.3 Pegged Systems and Currency Boards — Hong Kong's Own System"},{"t":"p","x":"Hong Kong operates a currency board arrangement pegging the HKD to the USD — a STRONGER commitment mechanism than a simple, adjustable peg (the HKMA is obliged to exchange HKD for USD at the fixed rate on demand, backed 1:1 by USD reserves). Removing this peg would have major implications for Hong Kong's monetary autonomy, credibility, and the pricing of HK assets/trade — a natural essay topic given the course's Hong Kong setting (see the official Study Guide self-assessment question on exactly this point, and the illustrative reading on the HKD peg's 30-year history)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.4 The International Monetary Fund (IMF)"},{"t":"p","x":"Introduced already in Chapter 1 as a \"global institution\": created (at Bretton Woods) to maintain order in the global monetary system and act as lender of last resort to countries in balance-of-payments difficulty, typically attaching policy-reform conditions (\"conditionality\") to its loans."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"11.5 Course Connections and Historical Threads"},{"t":"p","x":"The Illustrative Real-World Readings section below carries several directly relevant threads for this chapter: the end of the gold standard, the Renminbi's managed currency-basket system, the 30-year history of the HKD peg, and the Eurozone's structural weaknesses 20 years after launch. Students should be able to explain WHY the euro is, in effect, an extreme/permanent fixed-exchange-rate arrangement among its members, and why that explains both its early stability benefits and its debt-crisis vulnerabilities."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 11"},{"t":"p","x":"**1. Which of the following is a genuine ADVANTAGE of a fixed exchange rate system, per this chapter?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) it allows persistent trade imbalances to correct automatically"},{"t":"p","x":"B) it discourages currency speculation and imposes monetary discipline on governments"},{"t":"p","x":"C) it guarantees independent national monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"D) it eliminates the need for a central bank"},{"t":"p","x":"E) it always produces faster GDP growth than floating rates"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nDiscouraging speculation and forcing monetary discipline (since the government can't simply print money without breaking the peg) are the textbook's cited advantages of a fixed system."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. According to the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, a country that wants BOTH a fixed exchange rate AND full freedom of international capital flows must give up:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) its central bank entirely"},{"t":"p","x":"B) independent monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) all foreign trade"},{"t":"p","x":"D) its currency altogether"},{"t":"p","x":"E) its membership in the IMF"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nThe trilemma states a country can have at most two of (fixed rate, monetary autonomy, free capital flows) — choosing fixed rate + free capital flows means giving up independent monetary policy, exactly as Hong Kong effectively does under its USD peg."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. Hong Kong's currency board arrangement with the US dollar is best described as:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a purely floating exchange rate"},{"t":"p","x":"B) a stronger, reserve-backed version of a fixed peg"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a form of portfolio investment"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a customs union"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an example of trade diversion"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nA currency board is a stricter commitment than an ordinary adjustable peg: the HKMA must exchange HKD for USD on demand at the fixed rate, backed by full USD reserves."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. Explain why removing Hong Kong's USD peg would create both a potential benefit and a potential risk, using the fixed-vs-floating framework from this chapter.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Potential benefit: floating would restore Hong Kong's monetary-policy autonomy, letting the HKMA set interest rates suited to Hong Kong's own economic cycle instead of importing US Federal Reserve policy via the peg, and would let the HKD exchange rate itself absorb/correct any persistent trade or capital-flow imbalances.\n• Potential risk: Hong Kong would lose the discipline and credibility benefits of the peg (which discourages currency speculation and anchors inflation expectations), and — given Hong Kong's very open capital account — would become directly exposed to currency speculation and volatility that the peg currently insulates it from."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. Using the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, classify China's historical approach (fixed/managed exchange rate, capital controls, independent monetary policy) and explain which of the three trilemma elements China has traditionally sacrificed.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• China has historically combined (1) a managed/quasi-fixed exchange rate for the Renminbi with (2) an independent domestic monetary policy — the two elements the trilemma says CAN be combined only by giving up the third.\n• The element China has sacrificed is FREE (unrestricted) international capital flows: China has maintained significant capital controls, which is precisely what allows it to keep both a managed exchange rate and independent monetary policy without the trilemma's contradictions forcing a choice."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2108**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Global Business Environment**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Department of Marketing and International Business"},{"t":"p","x":"Instructor: Prof. Hilda Keung  •  Textbook: Charles Hill, Global Business Today (Evergreen release), McGraw-Hill"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all course materials shared on Moodle (lecture slides, study guides, official quizzes and readings), synthesised into structured revision notes with original complementary practice questions."},{"t":"p","x":"Prepared for personal exam revision — not an official course document."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"(Right-click this area in Word and choose \"Update Field\" to generate/refresh the table of contents.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"p","x":"This section gathers all course-administration material from Moodle: class rules, the participation/gaming warm-up exercise, and the official course outline (Section 4, Wednesday 1:00 p.m., Room LBY G06, Semester 1, 2026-27)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Course Description & Learning Outcomes"},{"t":"p","x":"BUS2108 helps students understand the international business world and the importance of international business to their careers. The course has five parts, each mapped to chapters of the textbook covered later in this document:"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 1 — defines globalization, its drivers, and debates its merits/drawbacks (Chapter 1)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 2 — national differences in political economy, culture and ethics, and their implications for ethical decision-making (Chapters 2-5)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 3 — international trade theory and the trade/investment environment (Chapters 6-9)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 4 — foreign exchange background and the global monetary system (Chapters 10-11)"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 5 — market entry strategy: which markets to enter, and how, when and on what scale (later in the course; only lightly touched in the extracted materials)"},{"t":"p","x":"**On completion, students should be able to:**"},{"t":"li","x":"Understand globalization's impact on jobs, income, labour policy, growth, environment and sovereignty"},{"t":"li","x":"Assess country attractiveness, corruption risk, IP issues and ethical dilemmas across cultures"},{"t":"li","x":"Apply trade/FDI theory and understand government influence on these activities"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain fixed vs floating exchange-rate theories and their relative merits"},{"t":"li","x":"Formulate an international market-entry strategy (which markets, how, when, what scale)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Assessment Structure"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight"],["Attendance & Participation","10%"],["Individual In-class Assessment (Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix exercise)","10%"],["Mid-term Test (45 MCQs, closed book, covers Chapters 1–6)","15%"],["Group Project & Presentation","25%"],["Continuous Assessment subtotal","60%"],["Final Examination (essay format, covers entire course)","40%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Lecture 1's slides mention participation as \"5% of the total assessment\", while the official course outline lists Attendance & Participation together at 10%. The course outline is the authoritative document for grading weights — please verify the current split with the instructor if in doubt."},{"t":"p","x":"**Key dates:**"},{"t":"li","x":"In-class individual assessment (WCAM exercise): Wednesday 16 September 2026"},{"t":"li","x":"Mid-term test: covers Chapters 1–6, 45 MCQs, closed book, at MBG22. The outline lists it twice — Thursday 22 October, 8:00–9:00 p.m. (evening) and Wednesday 28 October, 8:00–9:00 a.m. (morning)."},{"t":"li","x":"Group presentations: Wednesday 18 November 2026 (PPT uploaded to Turnitin before 11:00 a.m. that day)"},{"t":"li","x":"Group written report due: Sunday 22 November 2026, 5:00 p.m. (via Turnitin, ~4,000 words)"},{"t":"p","x":"Note: There appear to be two different dates/times listed for the mid-term in the source outline (22 Oct evening vs 28 Oct morning) — please confirm which single sitting applies to Section 4 with the instructor."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Weekly Schedule (Section 4 — Wednesday)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Topic","In-class activity","Ch."],["2/9","Course Introduction; The Meaning of Globalization","—","1"],["9/9","National Differences in Political, Economic & Legal Systems (per Study Guide numbering)","Group formation; Globalization Debate; group country/industry selection","2"],["16/9","National Differences in Economic Development","In-class individual assessment: Weighted Country Attractiveness Matrix (WCAM)","3"],["23/9","Differences in Culture","Culture group exercise (Hofstede application)","4"],["30/9","Ethics, CSR & Sustainability","ICAC visit — avoiding corruption in HK and overseas","5"],["7/10","International Trade Theory","New Trade Theory discussion","6"],["14/10","Government Policy and International Trade","Trade wars and impact on international business","7"],["21/10","NO CLASS — mid-term preparation","—","—"],["22 or 28/10","MID-TERM TEST (Chapters 1–6)","—","—"],["28/10","Foreign Direct Investment","Investment proposals discussion (Taiwan FDI case)","8"],["4/11","Regional Economic Integration","Project preparation — researching the global business environment","9"],["11/11","The Foreign Exchange Market","Foreign exchange exercises","10"],["18/11","GROUP PRESENTATIONS","—","—"],["22/11","Group written report due (5:00 p.m.)","—","—"],["25/11","Entering Developed & Emerging Markets; End-of-course review","Return of in-class assessments; classes finish","(12/13)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: The Study Guides used as the backbone of this document number chapters as: Ch.2 = Political/Economic/Legal Systems, Ch.3 = National Differences in Economic Development — the reverse of one row in the course outline table above (which lists 9/9 as \"Economic Development\" = Ch.2). This document follows the Study Guide / textbook numbering consistently throughout, since the Study Guides and the official quizzes both use it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Class Rules & Protocol"},{"t":"li","x":"Breaks of ~10–15 minutes during each class; classes finish around xx:15–xx:20"},{"t":"li","x":"Phones on vibrate only; step outside to take calls and return quietly"},{"t":"li","x":"Attendance taken every class; minimum 70% attendance required to be allowed to sit the final exam"},{"t":"li","x":"Persistent lateness/absence affects the participation grade; medical/legitimate absences need documentation"},{"t":"li","x":"Generative AI (ChatGPT, etc.) is NOT recommended for assignments; declaration forms required for individual and group work; detected AI use can mean a zero score and referral to the disciplinary committee"},{"t":"li","x":"Plagiarism is strictly prohibited per university regulations; a mandatory online \"Plagiarism Awareness\" tutorial is available; written work must be submitted via Turnitin"},{"t":"li","x":"Tips for doing well: develop a \"business-to-business\" sense; follow current world news; use reliable, evidence-based sources; apply theories/concepts rather than memorising definitions; do not choose China as one of the two comparison countries for the group project; complete every assessment component — missing even one component is very likely to result in failing the course"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Participation / Gaming Warm-Up Exercise"},{"t":"p","x":"The Lesson 1 \"warm-up\" participation activity used the gaming/metaverse industry as a live case of the global business environment, counting toward the participation grade. Students read an EY report, \"What's possible for the gaming industry in the next dimension?\" (Gaming Industry Survey 2022), and answered: \"Could you please share your opinion whether you support a favourable global business environment for the gaming industry in the near future? Explain your reasons.\""},{"t":"p","x":"**Key facts from the EY report (useful supporting evidence for that discussion):**"},{"t":"li","x":"Global gaming revenue exceeded $193 billion in 2021 (2.9 billion players worldwide), growing at a 15.6% CAGR from 2016–2021; forecast to reach $211 billion by 2025 (mobile gaming: $116bn)."},{"t":"li","x":"Key drivers: faster processors/graphics, new hardware, rising global internet access, and the rollout of 5G (enabling cloud gaming and VR/AR)."},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive pressure is seen as healthy: 72% of surveyed executives called increased competition an opportunity; 83% said the industry is under constant pressure to innovate. M&A activity was large ($36.3bn in deals in 2021, incl. Microsoft's proposed $68.7bn Activision Blizzard acquisition) but most executives prioritised organic growth (R&D, new tech) over M&A."},{"t":"li","x":"Data & cyber-security risk is a rising concern: 47% cite cyber risk as a key challenge today, rising to 58% looking three years out."},{"t":"li","x":"The metaverse is seen as gaming's natural next stage (97% of respondents believe gaming is central to the metaverse); NFTs and \"play-to-earn\" models are an emerging but still-unproven revenue source (90% of executives say they lack a viable business model to monetise digital assets yet)."},{"t":"li","x":"Recommended strategic responses: develop a clear metaverse vision, update the operating model (incl. new talent/skills), and design the customer experience around what users want to do in the metaverse (not simply port \"reality\" into it)."},{"t":"p","x":"This exercise links directly into the Chapter 1 globalization debate (see below): the gaming industry is a clear case of the globalization of markets (converging tastes for gaming content worldwide) and, through global supply chains and remote/distributed development teams, the globalization of production."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Group Project Brief"},{"t":"p","x":"Your team works as management consultants for a Hong Kong manufacturing company (40% of sales in Asia, 30% North America, 30% Europe) that currently has one HK factory and wants to build a larger, more modern plant (~1,500 workers) abroad, exporting worldwide (not for the local market). You must recommend which of two candidate countries is the better location."},{"t":"p","x":"**Factors to evaluate for each candidate country:**"},{"t":"li","x":"Wage levels and other economic background"},{"t":"li","x":"Tax rates and investment incentives"},{"t":"li","x":"Availability of appropriately skilled labour"},{"t":"li","x":"Natural factor conditions, infrastructure, and supporting industries"},{"t":"li","x":"Legal environment (labour law, environmental law, etc.)"},{"t":"li","x":"Membership of regional trade agreements and other trade considerations"},{"t":"li","x":"Cultural considerations / ability of the HK management team to work with local staff"},{"t":"li","x":"Political risk"},{"t":"p","x":"Deliverables: a 20-minute group presentation and a ~4,000-word written report, with a clear final recommendation and justification. Do not use China as one of your two comparison countries."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Illustrative Real-World Readings"},{"t":"p","x":"The Moodle materials include a batch of OCR'd newspaper and magazine clippings (mostly from The Economist and the Wall Street Journal, plus one Newsweek and one Financial Times piece) used in class as real-world illustrations of the currency, trade and globalization concepts covered above — chiefly for Chapters 1, 9, 10 and 11. Rather than reproducing the full text of each article (which would make this document unwieldy), each is summarised below with its relevance to the course. The full original OCR'd text of every article remains available in the source files on Moodle / in the extracted materials folder if you want to re-read one in full."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. \"Stronger Euro Good News for Hong Kong Traders\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"South China Morning Post-style report, early 2000s (\"03-04\")"},{"t":"p","x":"Reports that a firmer euro reduced the pressure Hong Kong/China exporters (priced in USD via the HKD peg) faced from European buyers to switch to euro pricing, and gave manufacturers a margin cushion — though the article notes export VOLUMES to Europe were not expected to rise much while the German/French economies stayed weak. A direct, concrete illustration of transaction/economic FX risk for Hong Kong exporters (Chapter 10) and of the HKD's USD-peg mechanics (Chapter 11)."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. \"Making Money on Money\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Newsweek, 15 March 2004"},{"t":"p","x":"Describes the 2000s boom in retail/individual forex (\"day-trading\") speculation, enabled by high leverage (up to 100:1) and made accessible by new online platforms. Highlights the serious risk of this activity (the U.S. CFTC pursued 57 enforcement cases involving $250m in consumer losses) and quotes Fed Chair Alan Greenspan's view that no model beats a coin flip at predicting currency moves. Directly supports Chapter 10's conclusion that short-term FX speculation is not recommended for non-specialists."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. \"HK Central Banker Says Currency Union Far Off in Asia\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Asian Wall Street Journal, 22 March 2005"},{"t":"p","x":"HKMA chief Joseph Yam argues an Asian monetary union (modelled on the euro) remains far off, citing much lower real economic convergence among Asian economies than existed in pre-euro Europe, and much greater political diversity. Useful counterpoint for Chapter 9's discussion of common-currency costs/benefits and \"optimal currency areas\" applied to Asia."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. \"Nought to Worry About\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 28 August 2004"},{"t":"p","x":"Covers a wave of currency redenominations (stripping zeroes from inflated currencies) in Turkey, Romania, Bulgaria and Afghanistan, driven by past high inflation. A light illustration of how chronic inflation erodes a currency's practical usability — reinforcing the Chapter 10/11 link between inflation and currency valuation."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. \"Japan's Currency Wonupmanship\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 3 March 2012"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains how a weakening yen against the South Korean won helped Korean firms (Samsung, Hyundai) undercut Japanese rivals (Sony, Honda, and DRAM-chip maker Elpida, which went bankrupt) on price after the 2008 financial crisis. A concrete case of exchange-rate movements directly shaping international competitiveness (Chapter 10's \"economic risk\")."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. \"Currencies' Fair Value Open to Debate\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Wall Street Journal, 25 February 2013"},{"t":"p","x":"Compares several models (PPP/\"Starbucks latte index\", the IMF's own models, capital-flow-based models) for valuing currencies, showing they often disagree — the euro, yen and Swedish krona are each judged over/undervalued differently by different models. A good companion/contrast to the Big Mac Index for Chapter 10, showing PPP is one tool among several, each imperfect."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. \"World Trade: The Other Conclave\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 16 March 2013"},{"t":"p","x":"Reports on the WTO's stalled Doha Round and the parallel boom in regional/bilateral deals (TPP, TTIP, RCEP-era predecessors) as major economies pursue \"pragmatic\" regional liberalisation while multilateral talks stagnate. Directly illustrates Chapter 7's account of WTO difficulties and Chapter 9's rationale for regional integration."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. \"Trading Places\" (Banyan column)**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 27 January 2018"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains why free trade remains popular in Asia even as the U.S. under President Trump turned protectionist — covering the revival of the TPP as the CPTPP after the U.S. withdrew, and arguing that at Asia's stage of development, trade mostly creates winners (unlike in the West, where it visibly creates concentrated losers too). Reinforces Chapter 1's globalization debate and Chapter 9's regional-integration material."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. \"Watch Your BMI\" (\"Burgernomics\")**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 14 July 2018"},{"t":"p","x":"A Big Mac Index update showing every tracked currency had moved in value; highlights the British pound as ~23% undervalued post-Brexit and notes the U.S. dollar strengthened against nearly every currency as the Fed raised rates. A worked, real-data example of the Big Mac Index / PPP theory for Chapter 10."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. \"The Euro at 20: Undercooked Union\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 5 January 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"A major retrospective on the euro's first 20 years: it survived the 2009-12 debt crisis but remains structurally incomplete — insufficient banking/fiscal integration, low labour mobility, and persistent north-south imbalances (Germany's surpluses vs. Greek/Italian/Portuguese/Spanish debt). Directly supports Chapters 9 and 11's discussion of common-currency costs, optimal currency areas, and the Eurozone crisis."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. \"Burgernomics: Pick of the Menu\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 12 January 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"Shows the U.S. dollar near a 30-year high on the Big Mac Index basis — almost every currency (rich and emerging) looked undervalued against it — and notes that Big-Mac-undervalued currencies have historically tended to appreciate over the following decade, on average. A strong data-driven Chapter 10 companion piece to the theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**12. \"The Meaning of Seven\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 10 August 2019"},{"t":"p","x":"Explains why the Chinese yuan crossing 7-per-dollar in August 2019 triggered a global market sell-off and a formal U.S. Treasury currency-manipulation accusation against China — even though 7.01 vs. 6.99 is a trivial economic difference — because markets had assigned huge symbolic importance to that round-number threshold amid the US-China trade war. Useful for Chapters 7 (trade war/tariffs) and 10 (FX market psychology, \"bandwagon effect\")."},{"t":"p","x":"**13. \"McJobbers and Brokers\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 19 September 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"A rigorous back-test of a naive \"buy the most Big-Mac-undervalued currency\" trading strategy: it LOST money in 10 of 15 completed two-year bets on the standard index, though a GDP-adjusted version of the index fared better. Directly nuances Chapter 10's PPP/Big Mac Index material — a good example of why short-term application of a sound long-run theory can still fail."},{"t":"p","x":"**14. \"Asia-Pacific Nations Sign Historic Accord in 'Victory' for Free Trade\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"Financial Times, 16 November 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"News report on the signing of RCEP (Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership) by 15 Asia-Pacific countries — the first trade deal linking China, Japan and South Korea, covering ~30% of world population/output, expected to add ~$200bn to global GDP by 2030. Directly supports Chapter 9's regional-integration material."},{"t":"p","x":"**15. \"RCEP: Big Deal\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 21 November 2020"},{"t":"p","x":"A more analytical companion to the FT article above: explains RCEP's main practical benefit (a single set of \"rules of origin\" replacing ASEAN's patchwork of separate bilateral deals), notes it is shallower than the TPP/CPTPP (no labour/SOE/environmental disciplines), and flags that India's absence leaves China with outsized influence over the bloc's future rule-setting."},{"t":"p","x":"**16. \"The Big Mac Index: The Happiest Meal\"**"},{"t":"p","x":"The Economist, 24 July 2021"},{"t":"p","x":"A further Big Mac Index update, highlighting Vietnam, Switzerland and Taiwan as flagged by the U.S. Treasury for potentially unfair currency practices, and Lebanon as the cheapest Big Mac in the world amid a currency/economic collapse. Reinforces the PPP/Big Mac Index material and shows real-world currency-manipulation disputes in action (linking Chapters 1, 10 and 11)."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist (Chapters 1–11)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this checklist as a final pass before the mid-term (Chapters 1–6) and the final exam (whole course). For each item, you should be able to explain it in your own words AND apply it to a concrete country/company example — the instructor repeatedly stresses \"application of theories and concepts, no memorization.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 1 — Globalization"},{"t":"li","x":"Define globalization and distinguish the globalization of markets from the globalization of production"},{"t":"li","x":"Name and describe the role of the five global institutions (WTO, IMF, World Bank, UN, G20)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the two macro drivers of globalization (declining barriers; technological change) and give an example of each"},{"t":"li","x":"Give at least 3 arguments FOR and 3 arguments AGAINST globalization, from different stakeholder perspectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 2 — Political, Economic & Legal Systems"},{"t":"li","x":"Place a named country on the collectivism↔individualism and democracy↔totalitarianism spectra"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish common law, civil law and theocratic law, and their effect on contract length/detail"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain how corruption and weak property rights affect FDI, trade and growth"},{"t":"li","x":"Define patents, copyrights and trademarks"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 3 — National Differences in Economic Development"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain GDP vs. GNI vs. PPP-adjusted GDP, with a worked numeric example"},{"t":"li","x":"List the \"three I's\" of growth (Incentives, Institutions, Innovation) and connect each to political-economy choices"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why the democracy–growth relationship is contested, with an example on each side"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why growth RATE (not just level) matters for country attractiveness"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 4 — Differences in Culture"},{"t":"li","x":"Define culture, values, norms (folkways vs. mores)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain all 6 Hofstede dimensions, with a country example for each"},{"t":"li","x":"State at least 2 criticisms of Hofstede's methodology"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish cultural relativism from cultural universalism with a business example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain \"cultural distance\" and its effect on market-entry mode"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 5 — Ethics, CSR, Sustainability & Supply Chain"},{"t":"li","x":"List the four common ethical issue areas (employment practices, human rights, environmental pollution, corruption)"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 7 managerial actions for ethical decision-making, and the 3-question ethical test"},{"t":"li","x":"Define CSR (Dahlsrud/Beal) and sustainability (UN definition), with a company example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain JIT inventory's core benefit and core risk"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish outsourcing / insourcing / offshoring / offshore outsourcing / nearshoring / co-sourcing"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 6 — International Trade Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain mercantilism and why it's now considered flawed"},{"t":"li","x":"State absolute advantage (Smith) and comparative advantage (Ricardo), and why comparative advantage matters even with no absolute advantage"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the Leontief paradox and why it challenged Heckscher-Ohlin"},{"t":"li","x":"State the four elements of Porter's Diamond and apply them to a real country/industry"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain what New Trade Theory implies for government policy"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 7 — Government Policy and International Trade"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 6 instruments of trade policy (tariffs, subsidies, quotas, local content requirements, administrative policies, antidumping) with a one-line definition of each"},{"t":"li","x":"List the political AND economic reasons governments intervene in trade, and identify the single MOST common real-world reason"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain trade creation vs. this chapter's protectionism history (Smoot-Hawley → GATT → WTO) in sequence"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 8 — Foreign Direct Investment"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish FDI from portfolio investment (with the 10% threshold)"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish FDI flow, FDI stock, and production realised from FDI"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 3 types of outward FDI (Greenfield, Acquisition, Joint venture) with a trade-off for each"},{"t":"li","x":"State Dunning's OLI theory's three conditions and apply them to a real company"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish free-market, radical, and pragmatic-nationalism views of FDI, and list the 5 pragmatic-nationalism evaluation criteria"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 9 — Regional Economic Integration"},{"t":"li","x":"List the 5 levels of integration in order (FTA → customs union → common market → economic union → political union) and what each ADDS"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish trade creation from trade diversion, and state the rule for when integration benefits the world economy"},{"t":"li","x":"List one benefit and one cost of a common regional currency, and define \"optimal currency area\""},{"t":"li","x":"Name the major Asian regional agreements (ASEAN, APEC, RCEP, CPTPP) at a high level only — no need for historical detail"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 10 — The Foreign Exchange Market"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish transaction, translation and economic FX risk with an example of each"},{"t":"li","x":"Distinguish spot rate from forward rate, and explain how a forward contract hedges risk"},{"t":"li","x":"State the Law of One Price / PPP theory and apply it to a Big Mac-style numeric example"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the International Fisher Effect (inflation/interest-rate link to exchange rates)"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain why short-term FX forecasting is unreliable (efficient market view) and what fundamental vs. technical analysis are"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 11 — The Global Monetary System"},{"t":"li","x":"Compare fixed vs. floating exchange rate systems: 2 pros and 2 cons of each"},{"t":"li","x":"State the Mundell-Fleming trilemma and classify a real country's choice of two-out-of-three"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain Hong Kong's currency board / USD peg and what removing it would imply"},{"t":"li","x":"Explain the IMF's core role and link it back to Chapter 1"},{"t":"li","x":"Connect the euro (Ch.9) to this chapter's fixed-vs-floating framework"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set — All Chapters (with Full Answer Key)"},{"t":"p","x":"A final, cumulative practice set mixing chapters, in the style of the final examination's essay/application format alongside MCQs. Attempt every question before checking the answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. [Ch.1/6] A firm sources semiconductors from Taiwan (best factor conditions for chip fabrication), assembles phones in Vietnam (lower labour costs), and sells worldwide via converging consumer tastes for smartphones. Which TWO concepts together best describe this?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Mercantilism and the gold standard"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Globalization of production and globalization of markets"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Trade diversion and an optimal currency area"},{"t":"p","x":"D) The Leontief paradox and cultural relativism"},{"t":"p","x":"E) A customs union and a common market"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSourcing components/assembly from wherever offers the best cost/quality (globalization of production) while selling into a converging worldwide consumer market (globalization of markets) are the two Chapter 1 concepts this describes."},{"t":"p","x":"**2. [Ch.2/3] A country has a civil law system, strong property-rights enforcement, and a free-market economic system. According to the course framework, this country is most likely to:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) have low FDI and low growth"},{"t":"p","x":"B) be an attractive destination for international business, though possibly a mature/competitive market"},{"t":"p","x":"C) automatically become a totalitarian state"},{"t":"p","x":"D) have no legal system at all"},{"t":"p","x":"E) be immune to political risk"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nStrong property rights + free-market economics + a functioning legal system are the classic conditions for an attractive investment/trade destination, though (Chapter 3) such countries are often already developed/competitive, limiting first-mover growth upside."},{"t":"p","x":"**3. [Ch.4] A Hong Kong manager negotiating with a supplier in a high-power-distance, high-collectivism country should most likely expect:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the supplier's junior staff to openly challenge their manager's decisions in the meeting"},{"t":"p","x":"B) decisions to be made by senior figures, with a preference for maintaining group harmony over direct public disagreement"},{"t":"p","x":"C) no hierarchy at all in the supplier organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"D) supplier staff to prioritise individual bonuses over group loyalty"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the supplier to insist on a fully detailed common-law-style contract"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nHigh power distance implies decisions flow from senior figures; high collectivism implies a strong preference for group harmony and avoiding direct public disagreement — together, exactly this expectation."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. [Ch.5] Which of the following actions is NOT one of the seven managerial actions for ethical decision-making covered in class?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) hiring/promoting people with strong personal ethics"},{"t":"p","x":"B) installing ethics officers"},{"t":"p","x":"C) developing moral courage"},{"t":"p","x":"D) maximising short-term profit regardless of stakeholder impact"},{"t":"p","x":"E) making CSR a policy cornerstone"},{"t":"a","x":"D\nThe seven actions are all about embedding ethics into hiring, culture, decision processes, ethics officers, moral courage, CSR and sustainability — maximising short-term profit regardless of stakeholders is the opposite of this framework."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. [Ch.7] A government subsidises its domestic solar panel makers so they can sell below cost abroad and capture global market share. If detected, the importing country could legitimately respond with:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) a Voluntary Export Restraint"},{"t":"p","x":"B) an antidumping duty"},{"t":"p","x":"C) a currency board"},{"t":"p","x":"D) a common market agreement"},{"t":"p","x":"E) an FDI ban only"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nSelling below the cost of production abroad (often subsidy-enabled) is the definition of dumping, and antidumping duties are the legitimate policy response — though as noted in Chapter 7, this tool is sometimes misused."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. [Ch.8/9] A German automaker builds a wholly-new factory in a Central European country that is also an EU member, benefiting from free movement of capital and parts across EU borders. Which TWO concepts does this best combine?**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) Greenfield FDI and Common Market-level regional integration"},{"t":"p","x":"B) Portfolio investment and a Free Trade Area"},{"t":"p","x":"C) Trade diversion and mercantilism"},{"t":"p","x":"D) Acquisition FDI and a Political Union"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Joint venture FDI and an optimal currency area"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nBuilding an entirely new plant from scratch is Greenfield FDI; benefiting from free movement of capital (and, within the EU, goods/labour) across member borders is a Common Market-level (or deeper) feature of regional integration."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. [Ch.10/11] A country's currency is pegged to the US dollar, and the country also allows fully free cross-border capital flows. Per the Mundell-Fleming trilemma, this country has necessarily given up:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) all foreign trade"},{"t":"p","x":"B) independent monetary policy"},{"t":"p","x":"C) its currency entirely"},{"t":"p","x":"D) WTO membership"},{"t":"p","x":"E) the ability to hedge with forward contracts"},{"t":"a","x":"B\nFixed rate + free capital flows means the third leg (independent monetary policy) must be sacrificed, exactly as the trilemma predicts (and as Hong Kong's own system illustrates)."},{"t":"p","x":"**8. [Ch.9/11] The main reason often given for the euro's structural stress during the 2009-12 debt crisis is that:**"},{"t":"p","x":"A) the Eurozone was not, in practice, an optimal currency area, so a single monetary policy could not suit members with very different needs"},{"t":"p","x":"B) the euro was a floating exchange-rate system"},{"t":"p","x":"C) the WTO banned the euro during the crisis"},{"t":"p","x":"D) trade creation exceeded trade diversion"},{"t":"p","x":"E) Hofstede's cultural dimensions predicted the crisis"},{"t":"a","x":"A\nThe course repeatedly links the euro crisis to the Eurozone failing to function as a true optimal currency area — e.g. Greece needing higher rates while Germany needed lower ones, a problem a single, shared monetary policy cannot solve for both simultaneously."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Mixed Set — Short-Answer / Case Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. A Hong Kong manufacturer is deciding between Vietnam and Poland as the site for a new export-oriented factory (per the group project brief). Name ONE relevant concept from each of three DIFFERENT chapters covered in this document that should inform the decision, and briefly explain why each matters.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Chapter 4 (Culture): Hofstede's cultural-distance framework — comparing Hong Kong's scores against Vietnam's and Poland's on all six dimensions reveals how much management-style adaptation will be needed in each location.\n• Chapter 6 (Trade Theory) / Porter's Diamond: assessing each country's factor conditions, related/supporting industries, and domestic rivalry indicates which location has a stronger underlying competitive base for manufacturing.\n• Chapter 9 (Regional Integration): Poland's EU membership gives tariff-free access to the whole EU common market, while Vietnam's membership of agreements like RCEP/CPTPP gives different regional access — this materially affects where finished goods can be shipped tariff-free.\n• (Any three well-justified, chapter-grounded concepts are acceptable — political/legal risk (Ch.2), economic development/growth rate (Ch.3), and FDI entry-mode choice (Ch.8) are equally valid alternatives.)"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. Using the Big Mac Index / PPP theory (Chapter 10) AND the concept of an optimal currency area (Chapter 9), explain why a country might resist joining a currency union even if doing so would reduce its transaction costs with its main trading partners.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• PPP/Big Mac Index logic shows that a country's currency may currently be systematically under- or over-valued relative to its trading partners' — joining a currency union would lock in a SINGLE conversion rate permanently, removing the ability to let the exchange rate adjust toward its \"fair\" PPP value over time.\n• If the country's economy does not move in sync with the currency union's other members (i.e. it is not part of a genuine optimal currency area), a single shared monetary policy may be poorly suited to its specific needs during an economic shock — exactly the problem that produced the Eurozone debt crisis for Greece and other southern European members.\n• The reduced transaction-cost benefit must therefore be weighed against the loss of monetary-policy autonomy and exchange-rate flexibility — which is precisely why countries like the UK, Denmark and Sweden chose to remain outside the euro despite the EU's common market benefits."},{"t":"p","x":"**11. A multinational is criticised in the press for both (a) using contract manufacturers in a country with weak labour law enforcement, and (b) contributing to environmental damage via its supply chain. Using material from Chapters 5 and 7, outline TWO concrete actions the firm could take, and explain the reasoning behind each.**"},{"t":"a","x":"• Action 1 (ethical decision-making process): embed supplier labour standards into sourcing decisions — e.g. requiring supplier audits against the firm's own code of conduct, going beyond the host country's legal minimum, directly following the Chapter 5 principle that ethics is not defined solely by local legality.\n• Action 2 (sustainability strategy): adopt a formal sustainable-sourcing policy addressing the \"tragedy of the commons\" risk in its supply chain (e.g. commitments on emissions, waste, and fair supplier compensation) — both because it reduces real environmental harm and because, per Chapter 5, unaddressed ethical issues are now quickly exposed via the internet/independent media, creating reputational and (per Chapter 7's national-security/consumer-protection logic) potential regulatory risk in the firm's home and host markets alike."}]},"BUS2205":{"file":"BUS2205_Marketing_Management_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Creating Customer Relationships and Value through Marketing","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 1 · Learning outcome: Define what marketing is and discuss its role in society; understand the importance of creating value and managing profitable customer relationships."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is Marketing?"},{"t":"p","x":"You are already a \"marketing expert\" in the sense that you make thousands of buying decisions and some selling decisions — but doing marketing well is not easy: it affects individuals, organizations, industries, and countries alike."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"AMA definition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing (AMA definition)\n• \"Marketing is the activity, set of institutions, and processes for creating, communicating, delivering, and exchanging offerings that have value for customers, clients, partners, and society at large.\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"Two core objectives sit behind this definition:"},{"t":"li","x":"Discover the needs and wants of customers."},{"t":"li","x":"Satisfy those needs and wants."},{"t":"p","x":"The mechanism that achieves both is exchange."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"Exchange is the trade of things of value between a buyer and a seller so that each is better off after the trade. For marketing (exchange) to occur, four conditions must hold:"},{"t":"li","x":"Two or more parties with unsatisfied needs."},{"t":"li","x":"A desire and ability on their part to have those needs satisfied."},{"t":"li","x":"A way for the parties to communicate."},{"t":"li","x":"Something to exchange."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. How Marketing Discovers Consumer Needs"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Needs vs. wants"},{"t":"li","x":"**Needs **— necessities: basic requirements a person must satisfy to survive (food, shelter, safety)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Wants **— the form a need takes as shaped by culture and personality (e.g., \"I need food\" becomes \"I want sushi\")."},{"t":"p","x":"A market consists of people with both the desire and the ability to buy a specific offering. Because consumers often cannot articulate what they need, firms use crowdsourcing and innovation tournaments to surface ideas. The challenge is real: it takes roughly 3,000 raw ideas to generate one commercial success, and about 40% of new products still fail. The remedy is to focus relentlessly on the consumer benefit and to learn from past product failures."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. How Marketing Satisfies Consumer Needs: The Marketing Mix and Environmental Forces"},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing programs are built from two very different sets of factors — one the firm controls, one it does not."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Four P's (controllable marketing mix)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Element","What it covers"],["Product","The good, service, or idea offered — including features, quality, branding, packaging."],["Price","What is exchanged for the product — list price, discounts, payment terms."],["Promotion","Communication between buyer and seller — advertising, personal selling, sales promotion, PR, social media."],["Place","How the product gets to the consumer — distribution channels, retailers, logistics."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Uncontrollable environmental forces"},{"t":"p","x":"These sit largely outside marketing's control and can act as accelerators or brakes on a marketing program:"},{"t":"li","x":"Social forces (demographics, culture)"},{"t":"li","x":"Economic forces (macroeconomic conditions, consumer income)"},{"t":"li","x":"Technological forces (innovation, AI, e-commerce)"},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive forces (structure of competition, rival actions)"},{"t":"li","x":"Regulatory forces (laws and regulations)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Exam-ready distinction\n• Marketing mix (4Ps) = controllable — the firm decides Product, Price, Promotion, Place.\n• Environmental forces = uncontrollable — Social, Economic, Technological, Competitive, Regulatory (mnemonic: \"SET-CR\").\n• A classic exam trap is to list an environmental force as if it were a 4P element, or vice versa — always ask \"can the firm directly set this?\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"The 3M Post-it® Flag case (slides) shows both elements working together: 3M used the SAME core technology but built two different marketing programs (product, price, promotion, place) for two segments — college students and office workers — because their needs differed."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Building Customer Relationships and Value"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Customer value strategies"},{"t":"p","x":"Firms typically compete on gaining loyal customers by emphasizing one dominant value driver:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Best price **— e.g., Target."},{"t":"li","x":"**Best product **— e.g., Starbucks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Best service **— e.g., Nordstrom."},{"t":"p","x":"**Customer value **is the unique combination of benefits received by targeted buyers — quality, convenience, on-time delivery, and both before-sale and after-sale service — at a specific price."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Relationship marketing, CRM, and customer experience"},{"t":"li","x":"**Relationship marketing **links the organization to its individual customers, employees, suppliers, and other partners for mutual long-term benefit. Easy to understand, hard to execute well."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer Relationship Management (CRM) **is the process of identifying prospective buyers, understanding them intimately, and developing long-term favorable perceptions so they choose the firm and become advocates after purchase."},{"t":"li","x":"**Customer experience **is the internal response customers have to all aspects of an organization and its offering — note the gap that often exists between \"what firms think they offer\" and \"what customers say they receive.\""},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. How Marketing Became So Important"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Evolution toward a market orientation (4 eras)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Era","Period","Dominant logic"],["Production era","Up to 1920s","Goods were scarce — focus on making enough product."],["Sales era","1920s–1960s","More goods were being produced than demanded — focus shifted to aggressive selling."],["Marketing concept era","Mid-20th century onward","Meet the needs of the consumer WHILE achieving the organization's own goals — gives the firm a market orientation."],["Customer relationship era","1980s to today","Use new technologies (CRM, data, digital) to increase value for consumers and build long-term relationships."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Definitions to know cold\n• Marketing concept: an organization should (1) strive to satisfy the needs of consumers, (2) while also trying to achieve the organization's goals.\n• Market orientation: an organization (1) continuously collects information about customers' needs, (2) shares this information across departments, and (3) uses it to create customer value."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ethics and social responsibility"},{"t":"p","x":"Marketing has shifted emphasis from producers' interests to consumers' interests. Firms build codes of ethics, policies, and guidelines (ethics), and are increasingly expected to be accountable to society at large (social responsibility) — including new questions raised by AI and machine learning. This underlies the societal marketing concept: organizations should satisfy consumer needs in a way that also provides for society's well-being."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Breadth and Depth of Marketing"},{"t":"li","x":"**What is marketed? **Products (goods), services (intangible), and ideas (concepts)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Who buys/uses it? **Ultimate consumers (people buying for personal/household use) vs. organizational buyers (manufacturers, wholesalers, retailers, service firms, nonprofits, government agencies buying for their own use or resale)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Who benefits? **Buying consumers, selling organizations, and society as a whole."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Utility — how consumers benefit"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of utility","Meaning"],["Form utility","Value created by converting raw materials/components into a finished, desired product."],["Place utility","Value created by making a product available where customers want it."],["Time utility","Value created by making a product available when customers want it."],["Possession utility","Value created by transferring ownership/facilitating the exchange (e.g., financing, delivery)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 1"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(B). A government regulation is an uncontrollable regulatory force. (A), (C) and (D) are all decisions the firm makes itself — Price, Product, and Place respectively — so they are controllable marketing mix elements."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(B). This is the textbook definition of market orientation: continuously collecting customer information, sharing it across departments, and using it to create value. The production and sales eras predate this customer-information-driven approach, and pure monopoly describes a market structure, not a management philosophy."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"A need is a basic, universal necessity (e.g., the need for nourishment); a want is the culturally and personally shaped form that need takes when a person decides how to satisfy it. Example: the need for nourishment might become a want for \"dim sum with family\" in Hong Kong, but a want for \"a quick protein shake\" in a fitness-focused Western market — same underlying need, very different wants driven by culture and lifestyle."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"Form utility — the app aggregates restaurant menus into a ready-to-order digital \"product.\" Place utility — food is delivered to the customer's home/office rather than requiring a trip to the restaurant. Time utility — on-demand delivery within a chosen time window. Possession utility — in-app payment and account management make the transaction/ownership transfer seamless."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"The marketing concept is the underlying philosophy — the idea that a firm should satisfy consumer needs while also achieving its own goals. Market orientation is that philosophy put into organizational practice: it describes the concrete behaviors (continuous customer-information gathering, cross-department sharing, and using the information to create value) that make a firm actually live out the marketing concept. In short: marketing concept = the \"why\"; market orientation = the \"how,\" operationalized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Product — a reusable, durable stainless-steel cup with a sustainability story; Price — likely a premium versus disposable cups but positioned as saving money over time; Promotion — direct-to-consumer digital marketing, community messaging, possibly influencer/sustainability partnerships; Place — online D2C plus pop-up kiosks in office districts for convenience and trial. (b) Social forces (growing environmental consciousness/value consciousness among HK consumers) could accelerate growth; Regulatory forces (e.g., government single-use plastics restrictions) could also accelerate growth; Economic forces (a downturn reducing discretionary spend on a premium reusable cup) could act as a brake. (c) Kumu could build a \"brand community\" or loyalty app that tracks each customer's environmental impact (e.g., \"cups of waste avoided\"), turning a single sale into an ongoing relationship anchored in shared values — consistent with relationship marketing's goal of mutual, long-term benefit rather than a one-off exchange."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 1"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is the best example of an uncontrollable environmental force affecting a bubble-tea chain, as opposed to an element of its marketing mix?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) The chain lowers its price for a limited-time promotion."},{"t":"li","x":"(B) The government introduces a new sugar-tax regulation on beverages."},{"t":"li","x":"(C) The chain redesigns its cup packaging."},{"t":"li","x":"(D) The chain opens three new outlets in shopping malls."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company that continuously collects information about customer needs, shares it across departments, and uses it to create customer value is demonstrating:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) The production era mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) A market orientation"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) The sales era mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Distinguish between a \"need\" and a \"want,\" and give one original example (not from the slides) showing how the same underlying need can produce two very different wants in two different cultures."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the four types of utility and, for a food-delivery app such as Deliveroo or Foodpanda in Hong Kong, give one concrete way the app creates each type."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between the marketing concept and market orientation. Why are they related but not identical?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: \"Kumu\" reusable coffee cups\n• A Hong Kong startup, Kumu, sells stainless-steel reusable coffee cups direct-to-consumer online and through pop-up kiosks in office districts. Its founder says: \"We don't want to just sell cups — we want to build a community of people who choose reusable habits.\""]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Identify Kumu's likely marketing mix (4Ps) at a basic level. (b) Identify two environmental forces (uncontrollable) that could accelerate or brake Kumu's growth. (c) Using the concept of relationship marketing, explain one action Kumu could take to build long-term customer relationships rather than one-off sales."}]},"2":{"title":"Developing Successful Organizational and Marketing Strategies","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 2 · Learning outcome: Discuss the major decisions involved in developing the marketing mix, the implementation challenges, and control activities."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Kinds of Organizations and Levels of Strategy"},{"t":"p","x":"Organizations are legal entities of people who share a common mission and develop offerings (goods, services, or ideas) that create value. Three kinds exist:"},{"t":"li","x":"For-profit organizations (business firms) — exist to earn a profit."},{"t":"li","x":"Nonprofit organizations — pursue a social mission rather than profit."},{"t":"li","x":"Government agencies."},{"t":"p","x":"An industry is a group of companies with similar offerings. Inside an organization, the board of directors oversees three levels of strategy:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level","Focus"],["Corporate level","Top management (including the CMO) sets overall direction across all businesses/SBUs."],["Strategic Business Unit (SBU) level","Each self-contained business/division with its own mission, competitors, and strategy."],["Functional level","Groups of specialists (e.g., marketing, finance) that create value; cross-functional teams pull departments together around a shared goal."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Strategy in Visionary Organizations"},{"t":"p","x":"Visionary organizations build (1) a foundation, then (2) set a direction, then (3) choose strategies to execute successfully."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(1) Organizational foundation — Why does it exist?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational purpose: **why the organization exists, what problems it wants to solve, who it wants to be to those it touches."},{"t":"li","x":"**Core values: **fundamental, passionate, enduring principles that guide conduct over time and inspire stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"**Mission (often used interchangeably with vision): **a statement of the organization's function in society — should be clear, concise, and meaningful; often identifies customers, markets, products, technologies."},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational culture: **the shared values, ideas, attitudes, and norms of behavior learned and shared among members."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(2) Organizational direction — What will it do?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Business: **the clear, broad, underlying industry or market sector of the organization's offering — \"what business are we really in?\" Business models can and do change over time (e.g., Uber)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Goals/Objectives: **statements of an accomplishment to be achieved, often by a specific time — profit, sales, market share, quality, customer satisfaction, employee welfare, social responsibility, or (for nonprofits) efficiency."},{"t":"li","x":"**Key Performance Indicators (KPIs): **demonstrate how effectively goals are being achieved."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"(3) Organizational strategies — How will it do it?"},{"t":"p","x":"Strategy — an organization's long-term course of action designed to deliver a unique customer experience while achieving its goals — can vary by level (corporate, SBU, functional) and by offering (product, service, idea). The marketing plan is the road map for the marketing actions that implement strategy."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Tracking Performance: Dashboards and Metrics"},{"t":"p","x":"\"You can't manage what you don't measure.\" Data analytics enables data-driven decisions through:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing dashboard: **the visual computer display of essential information related to achieving a marketing objective (graphs, tables in one place)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing metric: **a measure of the quantitative value or trend of a marketing action or result."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Key formulas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Marketing metrics you must be able to calculate\n• Dollar Sales ($) = Average price × Quantity sold\n• Dollar Market Share (%) = Firm's sales ($) ÷ Total industry sales ($)\n• Market share = the ratio of a firm's sales revenue to total industry sales revenue (including the firm itself)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Setting Strategic Direction: Where Are We Now? Where Do We Want to Go?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"A look around: where are we now?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Core competencies **— what the organization does best."},{"t":"li","x":"**Competitive advantage **— a unique strength relative to competitors."},{"t":"li","x":"Understanding customer preferences."},{"t":"li","x":"Scanning the (global) competitive landscape."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Growth strategies: the BCG (Business Portfolio) Matrix"},{"t":"p","x":"Business portfolio analysis quantifies performance measures and growth targets, treating a firm's SBUs/products like a portfolio of investments, plotted on market growth rate (vertical) vs. relative market share (horizontal):"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Quadrant","Market growth","Market share","Description / typical action"],["Stars","High","High","Leaders in fast-growing markets — invest to maintain/grow position."],["Question marks","High","Low","Uncertain potential in attractive markets — invest selectively or divest."],["Cash cows","Low","High","Generate large amounts of cash with little new investment needed — \"milk\" them to fund stars/question marks."],["Dogs","Low","Low","Weak position in an unattractive market — typically harvest or divest."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Diversification analysis (the Ansoff-style growth matrix)"},{"t":"p","x":"A second growth framework asks whether to pursue current or new products, in current or new markets:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Products","Markets","Example"],["Market penetration","Current","Current","Increase sales of a current product in a current market (e.g., more promotion, loyalty programs)."],["Market development","Current","New","Sell current products to new markets (e.g., new geography, new segment)."],["Product development","New","Current","Sell new products to current, existing markets (e.g., a line extension)."],["Diversification","New","New","Develop new products to sell in new markets — highest risk, potentially highest reward."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. The Strategic Marketing Process"},{"t":"p","x":"The strategic marketing process is the approach by which an organization allocates its marketing mix resources to reach its target markets. It rests on four guiding principles: customers are different; customers change; competitors change and react; and organizational resources are limited. It has three phases: Planning → Implementation → Evaluation."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 1 — Planning (3 steps)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Conduct a situation (SWOT) analysis. **A situation analysis takes stock of where the firm/product has been, is now, and is headed. A SWOT analysis appraises internal Strengths and Weaknesses and external Opportunities and Threats. For each cell, the generic action is: build on a strength, correct a weakness, exploit an opportunity, avoid a disaster-laden threat."},{"t":"li","x":"**Develop a market-product focus, customer value proposition, and goals. **This step uses market segmentation (aggregating buyers into groups sharing common needs and similar responses to marketing action) and defines a customer value proposition (the cluster of benefits an organization promises to satisfy customer needs) built around clear points of difference (characteristics that make the product superior to competitive substitutes). Customers are increasingly willing to pay a premium for experiences built on efficiency, convenience, knowledge, friendly service, and easy payment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Design a marketing program **— blend the 4Ps (product, price, promotion, place strategies) into one cohesive program."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 2 — Implementation (4 components)"},{"t":"li","x":"Obtaining resources (people, money, time)."},{"t":"li","x":"Designing the marketing organization."},{"t":"li","x":"**Defining precise tasks, responsibilities, and deadlines **— via an action item list (task / person responsible / deadline / deliverable) and a Gantt chart (a graph of a program schedule showing sequential and concurrent tasks)."},{"t":"li","x":"Executing the marketing program — turning marketing strategy (the means to achieve a goal) into marketing tactics (day-to-day operational actions)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Phase 3 — Evaluation"},{"t":"p","x":"The evaluation phase keeps the marketing program on track by comparing actual results with the plan to identify deviations — the difference between planned and actual results is the planning gap. Managers then act: exploit a positive deviation (things are going better than planned — capitalize on it) or correct a negative deviation (things are going worse than planned — fix it)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 2"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(D) Dog. Low market share + low/slow market growth defines a \"dog\" in the BCG matrix. Stars and question marks both sit in high-growth markets; cash cows have high share but low growth."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Market development. Current product, new market = market development. (Current product + current market = penetration; new product + current market = product development; new product + new market = diversification.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Average price = Dollar sales ÷ Quantity sold = $4,500,000 ÷ 150,000 units = $30 per unit. (b) Dollar market share = Firm's sales ÷ Total industry sales = $4,500,000 ÷ $30,000,000 = 15%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"Marketing strategy is the overall means by which a marketing goal will be achieved — e.g., \"target time-pressed students during their Friday afternoon free period with a value-priced combo to lift footfall.\" Marketing tactics are the specific, day-to-day operational actions that execute that strategy — e.g., \"post an Instagram Story every Friday at 1pm advertising a $5 discount on the classic milk tea from 3–5pm.\" Strategy sets the direction; tactics are the concrete steps that carry it out."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"A planning gap is the difference between the goals set in the marketing plan and the actual results achieved. The two responses are: (1) exploit a positive deviation — e.g., if sales exceed target because of unexpected viral social-media buzz, the firm could increase production and extend the promotion; (2) correct a negative deviation — e.g., if sales fall short because a new competitor undercut price, the firm could adjust its own pricing or intensify promotion to close the gap."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Strengths — strong same-day delivery technology, loyal niche customer base; Weaknesses — limited cash, low brand recognition outside its niche; Opportunities — growing HK demand for convenient, healthy grocery delivery, potential to expand into adjacent districts; Threats — HKTVmall's scale/brand dominance, risk of being undercut on price or selection. (b) FreshNow's Kowloon niche would likely be a \"question mark\": it sits in an attractive, fast-growing market (grocery delivery/health-conscious segment) but currently has low relative market share versus the dominant HKTVmall — it needs further investment to decide whether it can become a star or should be scaled back. (c) Market development is a reasonable recommendation for the next 12 months: FreshNow should extend its proven same-day delivery model and reputation among health-conscious professionals into adjacent, demographically similar districts (e.g., other white-collar neighborhoods) rather than attempting a completely new product line (diversification) while cash is limited — this leverages its core competency without stretching scarce resources too thin. (Accept product development or penetration if well justified against the resource constraint.)"}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 2"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A product with a low share of a slow-growth market, in the BCG matrix, is classified as a:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Star"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Cash cow"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Question mark"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Dog"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm that sells its existing skincare product line, unchanged, into a brand-new overseas market for the first time is pursuing:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Market penetration"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Market development"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Product development"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Diversification"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm reports dollar sales of $4,500,000 this quarter, having sold 150,000 units. The total industry sold $30,000,000 in the same quarter. Calculate (a) the firm's average price and (b) its dollar market share. Show your formulas."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between marketing strategy and marketing tactics, and give one example of each for a university bubble-tea kiosk trying to boost Friday afternoon sales."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is a \"planning gap,\" and what are the two possible managerial responses to it? Give an example of each response."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: HKTVmall vs. a new grocery-delivery entrant\n• A new HK-based grocery-delivery startup, \"FreshNow,\" is deciding how to compete against the dominant incumbent, HKTVmall. FreshNow has a small but loyal customer base, a strong same-day delivery technology, limited cash, and no brand recognition outside its current niche of health-conscious young professionals in Kowloon."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Conduct a brief SWOT analysis for FreshNow (one point per quadrant is enough). (b) Where would FreshNow's Kowloon niche business likely sit on the BCG matrix relative to a hypothetical larger, slower-growing division — justify your placement. (c) Recommend ONE of the four diversification-analysis strategies for FreshNow's next 12 months, with a one-sentence justification."}]},"3":{"title":"Scanning the Marketing Environment","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 3 · Learning outcome: Examine the various environmental forces in which a company operates."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Environmental Scanning"},{"t":"p","x":"**Environmental scanning **is the process of continually acquiring information on events occurring outside the organization to identify and interpret potential trends. It covers five environmental forces — the same five \"uncontrollable\" forces from Chapter 1:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Force","Illustrative current trends (from course slides)"],["Social","Rise of micro-influencers; conscious consumerism; growing focus on inclusion and social equity."],["Economic","Creator economy from digital video; shift to subscription business models (Google, Apple, Microsoft); circular economy (reuse over disposal)."],["Technological","AI personalizing customer experience; social commerce on Facebook/Instagram; the Fourth Industrial Revolution (5G, AI, IoT, robotics)."],["Competitive","Sustainability-driven innovation; shift from transactions to ongoing relationships/experiential journeys; machine learning (chatbots, voice search) as competitive strategy."],["Regulatory","Stricter privacy/data laws (GDPR, CPRA, KOSA); antitrust scrutiny of Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Meta, Microsoft; evolving rules for ridesharing, investing, healthcare."]]},{"t":"p","x":"This chapter is essentially the source of the \"PESTLE-style\" environmental scan referenced in your Group Project brief (Political/Regulatory, Economic, Social, Technological + Competitive)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Social Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Demographics"},{"t":"li","x":"World population: ~8.1 billion, projected to reach 9.7 billion by 2050, with most growth in Africa and Asia; India (≈1.67bn) overtakes China (≈1.30bn) as most populous by 2050; population is declining in 61 countries including China, Japan, Russia, and several European states."},{"t":"li","x":"U.S. population trends (context example from slides): growing, aging, and more diverse; ~338 million today; 65+ population growing sharply toward 2050."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Generational cohorts"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Cohort","Birth years","Broad characteristics"],["Baby Boomers","1946–1964","~76 million (US); large wave now reaching/past retirement age."],["Generation X","1965–1980","\"Baby bust\"; self-reliant; supportive of diversity."],["Generation Y (Millennials)","1981–1996 (please verify against original slide — one slide states 1983–1996)","Strong-willed, passionate, optimistic; technologically savvy; expect CSR/sustainability from brands."],["Generation Z","1997–2010","Broadest diversity to date; independent, hardworking, financially responsible, embrace diversity."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Population shifts and diversity"},{"t":"p","x":"U.S. population is shifting regionally toward the south and west, driven by three components: natural population change, state-to-state migration, and international migration (metropolitan area = 50,000+ people; micropolitan = 10,000–50,000). Ethnic and racial diversity is increasing, with major economic impact (e.g., Hispanic, African American, and Asian American consumer spending each in the trillions of dollars) — giving rise to multicultural marketing: combinations of the marketing mix reflecting the unique attitudes, ancestry, communication preferences, and lifestyles of different races."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Culture"},{"t":"p","x":"**Culture **is the set of values, ideas, and attitudes that are learned and shared among members of a group. Notable shifts include changing gender roles and more gender-neutral marketing, and rising sustainability and value consciousness (the concern for obtaining the best quality, features, and performance for a given price) — a large majority of shoppers now describe themselves as environmentally conscious."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Economic Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Macroeconomic conditions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Inflation: **rising production costs and prices (e.g., college tuition has risen far faster than family income)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Recession: **periods of declining economic activity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumer sentiment indices **(Consumer Confidence Index; Index of Consumer Sentiment) track consumer expectations about their own future financial situation — higher index = more favorable expectations, closely watched by marketers."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Consumer income (microeconomics)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Income concept","Definition"],["Gross income","Total money earned in one year by a person/household."],["Disposable income","Money left after paying taxes — used for necessities (food, housing, clothing, transportation)."],["Discretionary income","Money left after paying taxes AND necessities — the pool available for non-essential spending."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Illustrative consumer expenditure breakdown from the slides: ~13% of income on food, ~33% on housing, ~3% on clothing, ~25% on transportation and healthcare — the remainder is discretionary."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Technological Forces"},{"t":"li","x":"**E-commerce **has created new companies, disrupted others, and expanded value through convenience, choice, interactivity, and increased customer control."},{"t":"li","x":"**Internet of Things (IoT): **network of connectivity-enabled smart devices generating rich behavioral data for marketers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media: **digital technologies enabling creation/sharing of user-generated content; widely used by marketers for advertising."},{"t":"li","x":"**Artificial Intelligence (AI): **used for personalization, dynamic pricing, chatbots, and generative content (e.g., Coca-Cola's GenAI-developed \"Y3000\" flavor and campaign)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Big data & marketing analytics: **big data = extremely large data sets (social, transactional, voice/natural-language data) requiring sophisticated analysis; marketing analytics studies this data to evaluate performance via descriptive, diagnostic, and predictive analytics."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Competitive Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Forms of competition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Form","Number of sellers","Example"],["Pure competition","Many, similar/undifferentiated products","Agricultural commodities"],["Monopolistic competition","Many, substitutable products in a price range","Coffee, tea brands"],["Oligopoly","Few sellers control most sales","Telecom carriers"],["Pure monopoly","One seller","Public utilities"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Components of competition"},{"t":"p","x":"Firms must consider the factors that drive competitive intensity in an industry — this maps closely onto Porter's Five Forces logic:"},{"t":"li","x":"Barriers to entry — business practices or conditions that make it hard for new firms to enter."},{"t":"li","x":"Power of buyers and suppliers."},{"t":"li","x":"Existing competitors and substitute products."},{"t":"p","x":"(Context: small businesses are a major competitive force — 33+ million U.S. small businesses employ 46% of employees, generate 66% of new jobs, and represent 44% of GDP.)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Regulatory Forces"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Protecting competition"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Law","Year","Purpose"],["Sherman Antitrust Act","1890","Outlaws monopolistic practices and restraint of trade."],["Clayton Act","1914","Strengthens antitrust law; addresses specific anticompetitive practices (e.g., exclusive dealing)."],["Robinson–Patman Act","1936","Prohibits price discrimination that harms competition."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other legislative areas (know the categories, not every act)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Product-related: **Patent/Copyright law, Consumer Product Safety Act, labeling laws (e.g., Fair Packaging and Labeling Act), trademark law (Lanham Act)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Pricing & distribution: **laws against price fixing/discounting; distribution issues such as exclusive dealing, exclusive territories, and tying arrangements."},{"t":"li","x":"**Advertising & promotion: **FTC Act (1914); children's privacy (COPPA, 1998); anti-spam law (CAN-SPAM, 2004); telemarketing restrictions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-regulation: **industries police themselves (e.g., TV network guidelines on ads for children; the Better Business Bureau, a voluntary alliance promoting fair practices)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 3"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Oligopoly. Few sellers controlling the majority of sales is the defining feature of an oligopoly (e.g., T-Mobile/AT&T-style telecom markets)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Discretionary income. Disposable income = after taxes only; discretionary income = after taxes AND necessities — this is the pool available for optional purchases."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"Environmental scanning is the continual process of gathering information on events outside the organization to identify potential trends. Identifying a trend is only the first step; the marketer must also interpret/explain the trend — i.e., understand WHY it is happening and what it implies for the organization's customers, competitors, and marketing mix decisions — before it can be turned into a marketing action."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"Social — HK's aging population increasing demand for health-related products/services; Economic — rising cost of living/rents in HK squeezing discretionary spending; Technological — widespread adoption of e-payment (Octopus, FPS, WeChat Pay HK) reshaping retail and marketing; Competitive — the rise of mainland cross-border e-commerce (e.g., Taobao) intensifying price competition for HK retailers; Regulatory — HK's Personal Data (Privacy) Ordinance shaping how marketers can collect and use customer data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"The Sherman Antitrust Act (1890) outlaws monopolistic practices and restraints of trade to protect competitive markets; the Robinson-Patman Act (1936) specifically prohibits price discrimination between different buyers of the same goods when it harms competition. A large HK retail chain negotiating volume discounts from suppliers needs to be aware that heavily discriminatory pricing that disadvantages smaller competing retailers (in jurisdictions with similar rules) could expose the supplier — and potentially the retailer — to legal risk, so pricing terms must be defensible on objective grounds (e.g., genuine cost differences, volume economies) rather than arbitrary favoritism."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"Social — Australian consumers' growing interest in \"clean\"/science-backed skincare and sensitive-skin (barrier-repair) products, including for babies/infants (Anitch's product range). Economic — currency exchange rate (HKD/AUD) and Australian consumers' discretionary spending on premium personal-care products. Technological — the strength of e-commerce/D2C skincare distribution and social-commerce (Instagram/TikTok shopping) in Australia. Competitive — established local and global barrier-repair/dermo-cosmetic brands already present in Australian pharmacies and Priceline-style retailers. Regulatory — Australia's cosmetic/therapeutic goods regulations (TGA) and labelling requirements that an imported HK skincare brand must comply with before it can be legally sold."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 3"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A market with only a handful of large sellers who control most industry sales (e.g., telecom operators in Hong Kong) best describes:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Pure competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Oligopoly"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is money remaining after BOTH taxes AND necessities (food, housing, clothing, transportation) have been paid for?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Gross income"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Disposable income"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Discretionary income"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Net worth"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain what \"environmental scanning\" means and why identifying a trend is not, by itself, enough — what else must a marketer do with that trend?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name the five environmental forces and, for each one, give ONE original current example relevant to Hong Kong (not copied from the slides)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is the purpose of the Sherman Antitrust Act and the Robinson-Patman Act? Why would a large HK retail chain need to be aware of anti-price-discrimination rules when negotiating with suppliers?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Anitch Barrier Repair Body Cream enters Melbourne\n• (This mirrors your Group Project Option 3.) Anitch, a Hong Kong skincare brand whose R&D is backed by HKU medical/microbiology experts, plans to launch its Barrier Repair Body Cream in Melbourne, Australia."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Using the five environmental forces, identify ONE relevant trend or factor under each of Social, Economic, Technological, Competitive, and Regulatory forces that Anitch's marketing team should scan before entering the Melbourne market."}]},"5":{"title":"Understanding Consumer Behavior","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 4 · Learning outcome: Understand the stages of the purchase decision process and the factors influencing this process."},{"t":"p","x":"**Consumer behavior **consists of the actions a person takes in purchasing and using products/services, including the mental and social processes before and after those actions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Consumer Purchase Decision Process (5 stages)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stage","What happens","Key idea"],["1. Problem recognition","Perceiving a need","Triggered by a gap between the consumer's ideal and actual situation."],["2. Information search","Seeking value","Internal search (memory) and external search — personal sources (friends/family), public sources (reviews, media), marketer-dominated sources (ads, salespeople)."],["3. Alternative evaluation","Assessing value","Consumer forms evaluative criteria and narrows options to a consideration set (the acceptable brands actually compared)."],["4. Purchase decision","Buying value","Decide from whom to buy and when to buy."],["5. Postpurchase behavior","Realizing value","Satisfaction/dissatisfaction and possible cognitive dissonance (postpurchase psychological tension)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Postpurchase behavior — numbers worth remembering\n• Satisfied customers tell about 3 other people; dissatisfied customers tell about 9.\n• About 90% of dissatisfied customers will not buy the product again.\n• Cognitive dissonance = the anxiety/tension a buyer feels after a purchase, especially for high-involvement decisions.\n• Customer retention has outsized profit impact: e.g., a 5% increase in retention can raise profits by 70–80% (illustrative figure from course slides — company-specific, treat as an illustrative order of magnitude)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Consumer Involvement and Problem Solving"},{"t":"p","x":"**Involvement **is the personal, social, and economic significance of the purchase to the consumer. It drives which \"mode\" the 5-stage process runs in:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Problem-solving type","Involvement","Typical marketing strategy implication"],["Extended problem solving","High","Consumers seek lots of information; use comparative ads and personal selling; provide detailed product information."],["Limited problem solving","Medium","Some information search; moderate comparison of a few brands."],["Routine problem solving","Low","Little search — maintain product quality, avoid stockouts, use ads to reduce cognitive dissonance rather than to inform."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Situational Influences"},{"t":"li","x":"**Purchase task **— the reason behind the purchase (e.g., a gift vs. personal use)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social surroundings **— the presence and influence of other people."},{"t":"li","x":"**Physical surroundings **— store décor, music, crowding."},{"t":"li","x":"**Temporal effects **— time of day, time pressure/availability."},{"t":"li","x":"**Antecedent states **— the consumer's mood, circumstances, and available money at the moment of decision."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Consumer Touchpoints and Journey Maps"},{"t":"p","x":"**Consumer touchpoints **are a brand's points of contact with a consumer across the process (company channels, review/rating sites, other consumers, social media). A **consumer journey map **visually represents all touchpoints before, during, and after a purchase. AI/GenAI increasingly shapes this journey — smart devices help consumers research products, find deals, and resolve \"pain points\" faster and more efficiently."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Psychological Influences on Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Motivation — Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Level (bottom → top)","Type of need","Marketing example"],["1. Physiological","Food, water, shelter","Basic groceries, utilities."],["2. Safety","Security, protection","Insurance, home security systems."],["3. Social","Belonging, love, friendship","Social apps, community-building brands."],["4. Personal (esteem)","Status, recognition, achievement","Luxury goods, premium memberships."],["5. Self-actualization","Realizing one's full potential","Education, personal development, experiences."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Personality and perception"},{"t":"li","x":"**Personality **— a person's consistent behaviors/responses to recurring situations (traits such as assertiveness, extroversion, compliance, dominance); **self-concept **— how we see ourselves."},{"t":"li","x":"**Perception **— selecting, organizing, and interpreting information into a meaningful picture. The selective perception process filters what we notice: selective exposure (what we pay attention to), selective comprehension (how we interpret it), selective retention (what we remember)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Perceived risk **— anxiety felt because outcomes cannot be anticipated and may be negative; the greater the perceived risk, the more extensive the external search. Firms reduce it via seals of approval, endorsements, free trials/samples, extensive instructions, and warranties/guarantees."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learning"},{"t":"p","x":"**Learning **results from (1) repeated experience and (2) reasoning. Behavioral learning builds automatic responses through repeated exposure via four elements:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Element","Meaning"],["Drive","The need that stimulates action."],["Cue","A stimulus or symbol that triggers a response."],["Response","The action taken."],["Reinforcement","The reward for the action, strengthening the future response."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Stimulus generalization **— response to one stimulus is generalized to a similar stimulus (e.g., trusting a brand extension because you trust the parent brand)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stimulus discrimination **— ability to perceive differences among similar stimuli (e.g., distinguishing a genuine brand from a knock-off)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cognitive learning **— making connections between ideas without direct reinforcement."},{"t":"li","x":"**Brand loyalty **— a favorable attitude toward, and consistent repeat purchase of, a single brand over time."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Values, beliefs, and attitudes"},{"t":"p","x":"**Attitude **— a learned predisposition to respond favorably or unfavorably to an object, shaped by values and **beliefs **(a consumer's subjective perception of how a product performs on attributes, based on experience, advertising, and word of mouth). Marketers change attitudes through three approaches:"},{"t":"li","x":"Change beliefs about a brand's attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Change the perceived importance of attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Add new product attributes."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lifestyle and VALS"},{"t":"p","x":"Lifestyle describes how people spend their time and resources; lifestyle analysis (psychographics) helps target consumers. The VALS framework classifies consumers by primary motivation and resource level:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Primary motivation","Segments","Resource orientation"],["Ideals","Thinkers and Believers","Guided by knowledge and principles."],["Achievement","Achievers and Strivers","Guided by projecting success to others."],["Self-expression","Experiencers and Makers","Guided by desire for activity, variety, risk."]]},{"t":"p","x":"High-resource groups tend to be future-oriented; low-resource groups tend to focus on immediate/basic needs."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Sociocultural Influences on Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Personal influence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Opinion leaders **— individuals who exert direct/indirect social influence over others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Word of mouth **— highly powerful and can be positive or negative (\"buzz\"); underlies influencer marketing."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Reference groups"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Relationship to the group"],["Associative group","A group we already belong to."],["Aspiration group","A group we want to belong to."],["Dissociative group","A group we want to distance ourselves from."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**Brand community **— a specialized group of consumers with a structured set of relationships around a particular brand, its fellow customers, and the product in use."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social class"},{"t":"p","x":"Social class — relatively permanent, homogeneous divisions in society sharing similar values, interests, and behavior, determined by occupation, income source, and education. Three broad categories (each with subcategories): upper, middle, lower."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Family influence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Consumer socialization **— children learn to be consumers by interacting with adults during purchases and through their own experience."},{"t":"li","x":"**Family life cycle **— distinct phases a family progresses through, each with identifiable purchasing behaviors; note that \"traditional\" families are now a minority of U.S. households (~20%)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Decision-making styles: **spouse-dominant vs. joint decisions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Family member roles: **information gatherer, influencer, decision maker, purchaser, user — the same person can play more than one role, and the roles can belong to different family members for the same purchase."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Culture and subculture"},{"t":"p","x":"Culture — the set of values, ideas, and attitudes learned and shared among members of a group. Subcultures are subgroups within the larger national culture with their own unique values, ideas, and attitudes. The course slides illustrate this with three major U.S. subcultures (Hispanic/Latino, Black American, Asian American) and their distinct buying-pattern generalizations — useful as an illustration of the concept of multicultural marketing, though such broad generalizations should always be treated cautiously and updated with current local research."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 5"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Routine problem solving. Low-involvement, habitual repurchase without active comparison is the hallmark of routine problem solving."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Alternative evaluation. This is precisely where evaluative criteria are applied and a consideration set of acceptable brands is formed."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"Cognitive dissonance is the postpurchase psychological tension/anxiety a buyer feels after making a decision, especially when the purchase was important or involved trade-offs between attractive alternatives. To reduce it, a laptop company could send a reassuring post-purchase email highlighting the product's key strengths, offer a clear warranty/return policy, or provide onboarding support/tutorials that reinforce the buyer's confidence that they made the right choice."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"It appeals primarily to social needs (through the leaderboard/community and comparison with friends) and personal/esteem needs (through status from ranking well) rather than purely physiological needs — the core product (exercise tracking) touches physiological/safety (health), but the leaderboard feature is specifically designed to layer in social belonging and esteem/recognition on top of the base fitness function."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"Associative group — a group the consumer already belongs to, e.g., a student's own university class group buying matching class hoodies. Aspiration group — a group the consumer wants to join, e.g., an amateur runner buying the same shoe brand worn by elite marathoners they admire. Dissociative group — a group the consumer wants to distance from, e.g., a young professional avoiding a clothing brand strongly associated with a demographic or subculture they don't want to be identified with."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Problem recognition — Amy notices her running pace has stalled (gap between actual and desired performance); Information search — she asks friends (personal source) and watches YouTube reviews (public source); Alternative evaluation — she compares Apple Watch, Garmin, and Fitbit against criteria such as features/brand; Purchase decision — she decides to buy the Apple Watch, and decides where (mall) and when; Postpurchase behavior — not stated, but she may experience cognitive dissonance if the product underperforms versus the social expectations that drove the choice. (b) This resembles extended (or at least limited-to-extended) problem solving: it is a relatively costly, infrequent purchase, she actively searches for information from multiple sources, and she compares several brands before deciding. (c) Psychological influence — perception/social validation is intertwined with perceived risk reduction: comparing multiple sources (reviews, friends' experience) reduces the perceived risk of an unfamiliar, relatively expensive purchase. Sociocultural influence — reference group influence (aspiration/associative): Amy's decision is shaped by wanting to align with her friends and course-mates who already own Apple Watches, illustrating the power of reference groups and word of mouth on purchase decisions."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 5"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A consumer who buys the same brand of instant noodles every week without comparing alternatives is demonstrating:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Extended problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Limited problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Routine problem solving"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Cognitive dissonance"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which stage of the purchase decision process involves narrowing options down to a \"consideration set\" using evaluative criteria?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Problem recognition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Information search"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Alternative evaluation"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Postpurchase behavior"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Define cognitive dissonance and explain ONE concrete marketing action a company can take after the sale to reduce it for a high-involvement purchase (e.g., a laptop)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using Maslow's hierarchy, explain which need level a gym membership app with a social \"workout leaderboard\" feature is primarily appealing to, and justify your answer."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Distinguish between an associative, an aspiration, and a dissociative reference group, using one original brand example for each."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Buying a first smartwatch\n• Amy, a 21-year-old university student in Hong Kong, decides to buy her first smartwatch after noticing her running pace has stalled. She asks two friends who own Apple Watches and Garmins, reads reviews on YouTube, and compares an Apple Watch, a Garmin, and a Fitbit in a shopping mall before buying the Apple Watch because her friends and course-mates (whom she wants to fit in with) already use one."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Map Amy's behavior onto the five stages of the purchase decision process. (b) Which level of consumer involvement (extended/limited/routine) does this purchase most resemble, and why? (c) Identify ONE psychological influence and ONE sociocultural influence at work in Amy's decision, and explain each briefly."}]},"8":{"title":"Marketing Research: From Customer Insights to Actions","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 5 · Learning outcome: Identify the major purposes of marketing research, the major methods by which it is undertaken, and the challenges of organizing market research activities."},{"t":"p","x":"**Marketing research **is the process of defining a marketing problem and opportunity, systematically collecting and analyzing information, and recommending actions."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"The Five-Step Marketing Research Approach"},{"t":"p","x":"This is the single most important framework in this chapter — expect to be asked to apply it end-to-end."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 1 — Define the problem"},{"t":"li","x":"Set specific, measurable research objectives."},{"t":"li","x":"Choose the type of research needed:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Purpose"],["Exploratory research","Gathers preliminary information to help define the problem and suggest hypotheses — used when the issue is vague."],["Descriptive research","Describes market characteristics (who, what, where, when, how) — e.g., who buys what, how often."],["Causal research","Tests cause-and-effect relationships — e.g., \"does this price change CAUSE a change in sales?\""]]},{"t":"li","x":"Identify possible marketing actions that could result and define measures of success (criteria used to evaluate proposed solutions)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 2 — Develop the research plan"},{"t":"li","x":"Specify constraints (restrictions placed on potential solutions — budget, time, access)."},{"t":"li","x":"Identify what data are actually needed to support a marketing action."},{"t":"li","x":"Decide how to collect the data: concepts (ideas being tested), methods (data-collection approach), sampling (asking a subset/group for input), and statistical inference (generalizing sample results to the larger population)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 3 — Collect relevant information"},{"t":"p","x":"**Data **split into two broad types:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Definition","Examples"],["Secondary data","Facts/figures already recorded prior to this project.","Internal: marketing input data (budgets, sales-call reports), marketing outcome data (actual sales, customer communications). External: Census Bureau reports, trade association studies, business periodicals, syndicated panels (e.g., Nielsen TV ratings, J.D. Power)."],["Primary data","Facts/figures newly collected specifically for this project.","Observational data, questionnaire data, and other primary sources (social media, panels, experiments)."]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["Secondary vs. primary data — trade-offs\n• Secondary data — Advantages: time savings, inexpensive. Disadvantages: can be out of date, definitions/categories may not fit, may not be specific enough.\n• Primary data — Advantages: flexible, specific to the problem at hand. Disadvantages: costly, time-consuming."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Primary data — watching people (observational)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Mechanical methods: **e.g., Nielsen's People Meter, worn like a watch in sampled households, transmitting viewing data nightly."},{"t":"li","x":"**Personal methods: **mystery shoppers, direct observation, ethnographic research (observing consumers in their natural environment)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Neuromarketing: **technologies used to study brain responses to marketing stimuli."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Primary data — asking people (questionnaires)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Idea generation methods **(coming up with ideas): individual/depth interviews, focus groups (a facilitator guides ~8–12 consumers through ~10 guided questions in an informal, qualitative session; often used by brand marketers and sometimes quoted in advertising, e.g., \"99% of customers agreed...\")."},{"t":"li","x":"**Idea evaluation methods **(testing an idea): personal interview surveys, mail surveys, telephone interviews, online surveys, mall-intercept interviews."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Question formats (know these for survey-design questions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Format","Example use"],["Open-ended","\"What do you like about this product?\" — free text response."],["Closed-ended / dichotomous","Yes/No or two-option response."],["Multiple-choice","Choose one from several fixed options."],["Attitudinal","Rate agreement/feeling toward a statement."],["Semantic differential scale","Rate between two bipolar adjectives (e.g., Cheap … Expensive)."],["Likert scale","Rate agreement on a numeric scale (e.g., Strongly Disagree → Strongly Agree)."],["Media behavior","Which media/channels the respondent uses."],["Usage behavior","How often/how much the respondent uses the product."],["Demographic","Age, gender, income, etc."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other primary sources, big data, and analytics"},{"t":"li","x":"**Social media listening **can surface ideas for new products/services."},{"t":"li","x":"**Panel: **a sample of consumers measured repeatedly over time."},{"t":"li","x":"**Experiment: **manipulates an independent variable (the cause) to observe effects on a dependent variable (the result); **test markets **apply this in small real-world geographies before a full rollout."},{"t":"li","x":"**Big data **— extremely large data sets requiring sophisticated storage/analysis to reveal patterns; **data mining **examines large databases to find statistical relationships between purchasing patterns and marketing actions; **predictive modeling **uses data mining and probability analysis to forecast outcomes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Data visualization **presents analysis results for easy interpretation, feeding an \"intelligent enterprise\" that systematically converts data into decision-ready information."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 4 — Develop findings"},{"t":"p","x":"Analyze the collected data — e.g., using cross-tabulation (relating two variables, such as age of head-of-household against frequency of fast-food patronage) to surface patterns that explain WHY results look the way they do (not just WHAT the results are)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 5 — Take marketing action"},{"t":"li","x":"Make action recommendations based on the findings."},{"t":"li","x":"Implement the recommendations."},{"t":"li","x":"Evaluate the results — both the decision itself (did we choose correctly?) and the decision process (was our process for deciding sound?)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Sales Forecasting Techniques"},{"t":"p","x":"**Sales forecast **— the total sales of a product a firm expects to sell in a specified period, under specified environmental conditions and its own marketing efforts. Three main technique families:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Family","Technique","How it works"],["Judgments of the decision maker","Direct forecast","Estimate the value directly, without intervening steps."],["Judgments of the decision maker","Lost-horse forecast","Start from the last known value and list factors that could change it going forward."],["Surveys of knowledgeable groups","Survey of buyers' intentions","Ask prospective customers how likely they are to buy."],["Surveys of knowledgeable groups","Salesforce survey forecast","Ask the sales team to estimate likely sales."],["Statistical methods","Trend extrapolation (incl. linear trend extrapolation)","Extend a past pattern (e.g., a straight-line trend) into the future."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 8"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(C) Causal research. A controlled trial designed to test a cause-and-effect relationship (topping → spend) is the definition of causal research."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(C). A previously published government report is secondary data — it was collected before and for a different purpose. (A), (B), and (D) are all newly collected specifically for this firm's current needs, i.e., primary data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"A focus group is a qualitative method: a facilitator guides 8–12 consumers through open discussion, producing rich, in-depth but non-generalizable insights — useful early on, for idea generation or exploring WHY consumers feel a certain way. An online survey is typically quantitative: it reaches a much larger, more statistically representative sample with structured, closed-ended questions, producing generalizable numeric results (e.g., \"62% would buy this flavor\") but less depth of understanding. A marketer would use a focus group early (exploratory, idea generation) and an online survey later (idea evaluation, to quantify demand across a larger population)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"With no sales history, statistical trend extrapolation cannot be used (there is no past pattern to extend). The most appropriate family is surveys of knowledgeable groups — specifically a survey of buyers' intentions, asking a sample of prospective customers how likely they would be to buy the new snack, since this does not require historical company sales data."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Define the problem — set objectives and choose the research type; (2) Develop the research plan — specify constraints and data needed; (3) Collect relevant information — gather primary and/or secondary data; (4) Develop findings — analyze the data; (5) Take marketing action — recommend, implement, and evaluate."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Problem: uncertain demand and target age group for a new \"yuzu brown sugar\" flavor. Objective: estimate the % of existing customers who would purchase the new flavor at least once a month, broken down by age group. (b) Constraint: limited marketing research budget/time before the next menu review cycle. (c) Recommended method: an online or in-store intercept survey with a taste-test component — appropriate because it can reach a reasonably large, representative sample of current customers quickly and cheaply relative to a full focus-group program, while still capturing actual reaction to the product (via the taste test) rather than only stated intent. (d) Supportive finding example: \"45% of surveyed customers aged 18–34 say they would buy the flavor at least twice a month, versus 15% of customers aged 35+.\" (e) Marketing action: launch the flavor as a limited-time offer targeted at the 18–34 segment via social media promotion, then use actual sales data to decide on permanent addition to the menu."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 8"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm wants to test whether adding a new topping to a menu item CAUSES higher average spend per customer, by running a controlled trial in two comparable stores. This is an example of:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Exploratory research"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Descriptive research"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Causal research"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Secondary research"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which of the following is an example of SECONDARY data?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) A brand-new online survey the firm just designed and fielded"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) A focus group the firm just conducted"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A government census report published two years ago"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) A mystery-shopper visit the firm arranged this week"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain the difference between a focus group and an online survey as data-collection methods, including when a marketer would prefer one over the other."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A start-up has almost no sales history but wants to forecast next year's demand for a brand-new snack product. Which sales-forecasting technique family is most appropriate, and why? Name one specific technique from that family."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the five steps of the marketing research approach in order, in one line each."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Launching a new bubble-tea flavor\n• A Hong Kong bubble-tea chain wants to decide whether to permanently add a new \"yuzu brown sugar\" flavor to its menu. Management is unsure how big the potential demand is and which age group would like it most."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Design a short marketing research plan using the five-step approach: (a) state the problem and ONE research objective, (b) identify one relevant constraint, (c) recommend ONE primary data-collection method and justify it, (d) state what a supportive finding might look like, and (e) state ONE possible marketing action if the research confirms strong demand."}]},"9":{"title":"Market Segmentation, Targeting, and Positioning (STP)","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Week 6 · Learning outcome: Explain how attractive market segments are identified and products are positioned for competitive advantage."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Why this chapter matters for your Group Project\n• STP is the analytical backbone of the marketing-plan presentation you must deliver for the Group Project — segmentation, targeting, and a perceptual map / positioning statement are explicitly required deliverables."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Why Segment Markets?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Market segmentation **involves aggregating prospective buyers into groups (segments) that (1) have common needs and (2) will respond similarly to a marketing action. Firms then use **product differentiation **— distinct marketing mix actions — to help consumers see the product as different and better than competitors' offerings."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Three segmentation strategies"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Description","Example"],["One product, multiple segments","A single, largely unchanged product is marketed to several different segments.","Books and magazines."],["Multiple products, multiple segments","Different product variants are tailored to different segments.","Automobile model line-ups."],["Segments of one (mass customization)","Build-to-order only when there is an actual order.","Custom-configured products/services."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The segmentation trade-off"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organizational synergy **— a better-functioning organization results from serving multiple segments well."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cannibalization **— risk of a firm's new segment/offer stealing sales from its own existing offer."},{"t":"li","x":"The \"Tiffany/Walmart\" strategy — deliberately selling to both a high-end AND a low-end segment simultaneously, accepting the cannibalization risk for the reach it provides."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Five Steps in Segmenting and Targeting Markets"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 1 — Group potential buyers into segments"},{"t":"p","x":"Good segments should satisfy several criteria: simplicity and cost-effectiveness to identify; potential for increased profit; similarity of needs of buyers WITHIN a segment; difference of needs of buyers ACROSS segments; and the practical potential of a marketing action to actually reach the segment."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Basis (consumer markets)","What it captures","Example variables"],["Geographic","Where customers live or work","Country, region, city, climate"],["Demographic","Objective, measurable classification","Age, gender, income, education, family size"],["Psychographic","Subjective lifestyle/personality attributes","Values, activities, interests, opinions (VALS)"],["Behavioral","Observable actions","Usage rate, brand loyalty, occasion, benefits sought"]]},{"t":"table","rows":[["The 80/20 rule and usage rate\n• Usage rate = the quantity consumed or store visits during a specific period; frequency marketing focuses on rewarding usage rate.\n• 80/20 rule = roughly 80% of a firm's sales/profit typically come from 20% of its customers — a core justification for heavy-user targeting."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Organizational (B2B) markets are segmented similarly but with adapted variables: geographic (statistical area), demographic (by industry/NAICS code, by number of employees), and behavioral (usage rate). Marketers also build personas — fictional character descriptions that bring a target segment's data to life (\"a day in the life of...\")."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 2 — Group products to be sold into categories"},{"t":"p","x":"Group the firm's individual products/offerings into meaningful categories (e.g., a restaurant grouping menu items into breakfast, lunch, dinner, snacks) so they can be matched against market segments in the next step."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 3 — Develop a market-product grid and estimate market sizes"},{"t":"p","x":"**A market-product grid **is a framework relating market segments (rows) to products (columns), with each cell estimating the size of that segment's demand for that product (e.g., on a simple 0–3 scale from no market to large market). This is the analytical tool behind the Group Project's requirement to identify \"4 types of segmentation\" and connect them to specific product/marketing actions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 4 — Select target markets"},{"t":"p","x":"Two categories of criteria come into play: those used to divide the market into segments (above) and those used to actually pick which segment(s) to target:"},{"t":"li","x":"Market size"},{"t":"li","x":"Expected growth"},{"t":"li","x":"Competitive position"},{"t":"li","x":"Cost of reaching the segment"},{"t":"li","x":"Compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Step 5 — Take marketing actions to reach target markets"},{"t":"p","x":"Translate the chosen target segment(s) into concrete marketing-mix actions (e.g., specific promotions timed to a segment's behavior), while continuously watching competitor moves and market shifts. This is also where Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) becomes relevant — CLV represents the financial worth of a customer to a company over the life of the relationship, and helps justify how much a firm should be willing to spend to acquire/retain a given segment."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Positioning the Product"},{"t":"p","x":"**Product positioning **is the place a product occupies in consumers' minds, based on important attributes, relative to competing products. **Product repositioning **means deliberately changing that place in the consumer's mind."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Two approaches to positioning"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Approach","Logic"],["Head-to-head positioning","Compete directly against similar products on the same key attributes."],["Differentiation positioning","Seek a less-competitive niche by emphasizing attributes competitors don't own."]]},{"t":"p","x":"A positioning statement is derived from the company's customer value proposition and should clearly state the target segment, the category, and the key point(s) of difference."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Perceptual maps — how to build one"},{"t":"li","x":"Identify the important attributes for the product/brand class (choose two for a 2-D map — these become the X and Y axes)."},{"t":"li","x":"Discover how customers rate competing products/brands on those attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Discover where customers currently place the company's own product/brand on those attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"Decide whether/how to reposition the company's product in consumers' minds (e.g., moving it toward an attractive, less crowded space on the map)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Group Project reminder\n• Your Group Project explicitly requires a perceptual map — pick two attributes that are meaningful for your product/market (e.g., Price vs. Perceived Quality, or Traditional vs. Innovative), plot your brand and 2–4 key competitors, and use the map to justify your positioning statement."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Chapter 9"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(D) Behavioral. Usage rate/frequency of purchase is an observable behavior, the defining trait of behavioral segmentation."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(A) Head-to-head positioning. Competing on the SAME attributes for the SAME core segment as an established rival is the definition of head-to-head positioning (as opposed to seeking a distinct, less-contested space, which would be differentiation positioning)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"Cannibalization is the risk that a firm's own new product/segment/channel steals sales from its EXISTING product/segment/channel rather than generating fully incremental sales. Example: a premium restaurant group launching a budget fast-casual sub-brand risks cannibalizing its own premium restaurant's price-sensitive customers, who might trade down to the cheaper brand instead of buying fully new incremental volume — mirroring the classic \"Tiffany/Walmart\" trade-off of serving both a high-end and a low-end segment under one corporate umbrella."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Market size, (2) expected growth, (3) competitive position, (4) cost of reaching the segment, (5) compatibility with the organization's objectives and resources. Example explanation: \"cost of reaching the segment\" matters because even a large, fast-growing, attractive segment is not worth targeting if the media/channels needed to reach it are prohibitively expensive relative to the expected return."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"A market-product grid is a matrix that places market segments in rows and products/offerings in columns, with each cell estimating the size of demand that segment represents for that specific product. It helps answer the business question: \"Which combinations of WHO we sell to and WHAT we sell them represent the biggest, most attractive opportunities — and where should we focus (or not focus) our limited marketing resources?\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"(a) Demographic segment — affluent dual-income households with young children or elderly dependents in HK (household income above a set threshold), since they have both the financial means and a plausible functional need (childcare/eldercare support, household help) for a home robot. Psychographic segment — HK's \"early adopter\" tech-enthusiast segment: consumers who value innovation, status from owning cutting-edge technology, and convenience, regardless of household composition. (b) Example positioning statement: \"For busy, tech-forward Hong Kong households, Neo's Home Robot is the home robot that gives you back time and peace of mind, by reliably handling everyday household tasks — unlike ordinary smart-home gadgets, Neo is a genuine physical helper in your home.\" (c) Suggested axes: X = Price (Affordable ↔ Premium) and Y = Perceived Capability/Autonomy (Basic assistant ↔ Fully autonomous helper) — these two attributes are likely to be the main dimensions HK consumers use to compare a novel, high-involvement product category like home robots, letting the team visually show whether Neo should be positioned as a premium, highly capable option versus cheaper, more limited smart-home devices."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Chapter 9"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Segmenting customers by \"heavy users who buy 3+ times per week\" versus \"light users who buy once a month\" is an example of which segmentation basis?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Geographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Demographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Psychographic"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Behavioral"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A new energy-drink brand chooses to compete directly against Red Bull on the same \"high-energy, high-performance\" attributes valued by the same core segment. This is:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Head-to-head positioning"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Differentiation positioning"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Market development"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Product repositioning"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain \"cannibalization\" and give an original example of a brand risking cannibalization by serving two very different segments (a \"Tiffany/Walmart\"-style strategy)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the five criteria used to select which segments to actually target (Step 4), and briefly explain ONE of them."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"What is a market-product grid, and what specific business question does it help answer?"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6 (Applied mini-case — Group Project practice)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Mini-case: Positioning Neo's Home Robot for Hong Kong\n• Using the Group Project's Option 1 scenario: Neo's Home Robot (a general-purpose home humanoid robot) is to be introduced into the Hong Kong market."]]},{"t":"p","x":"(a) Propose ONE demographic and ONE psychographic segment that could be a viable target market in Hong Kong for this product, with a one-sentence rationale for each. (b) Write a short positioning statement for Neo's Home Robot aimed at your chosen target segment. (c) Suggest two attributes you would use as the X and Y axes of a perceptual map for this product category, and briefly justify your choice."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2205**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Marketing Management**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Complete Course Synthesis & Practice Exercises**"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Department of Marketing & International Business"},{"t":"p","x":"Based on: Kerin, Hartley, Lau & Rudelius — Marketing, 17th Edition (2025 Release, McGraw Hill)"},{"t":"p","x":"Covers Chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9 and Appendix A — as taught in Section 4, First Term 2026-27"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all Moodle course materials, with original revision notes and practice exercises"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"p","x":"This synthesis gathers every substantive piece of content shared on Moodle for BUS2205 Marketing Management (Section 4, First Term 2026-27), reorganized into clean revision notes, plus original practice exercises with full answer keys to help you assimilate the material for the mid-term test, the final exam, and your coursework."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Structure of this document"},{"t":"li","x":"Part 1 – Course Overview: course structure, assessment weighting, key dates, and a brief summary of the Individual Assignment and Group Project (context only, not exam content)."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 2 – Chapter-by-chapter revision notes, in the order taught: Ch.1 → Ch.2 → Ch.3 → Ch.5 → Ch.8 → Ch.9 → Appendix A. Each chapter ends with 4–6 original practice questions and a full answer key."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 3 – Exam-style review checklist across all chapters."},{"t":"li","x":"Part 4 – A mixed practice set (10 questions spanning multiple chapters, including one integrative mini-case) with a full answer key."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Important note on scope\n• Textbook Chapters 4, 6, and 7 are NOT included — they were not covered in the Week 1–6 slide decks shared on Moodle for this course and are not part of the mid-term coverage (Ch. 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9).\n• The mid-term test (26 Oct 2026) covers Textbook Chapters 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 and 9 only.\n• The Final Exam covers ALL course materials (all topics taught across the term, including Chapters 10-21 taught later, which are outside the scope of the Moodle files provided for this synthesis).\n• Wherever a fact could not be confirmed from the original slides (e.g., an ambiguous figure or a superseded statistic), it is flagged with \"(please verify against original slide)\"."]]},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 1 — Course Overview"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Information"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Detail"],["Course","BUS2205 Marketing Management — Section 4 (Tuesday)"],["Term","First Term, 2026–27"],["Department","Marketing & International Business, Lingnan University"],["Instructor","Prof. Aihua YAN (aihuayan@ln.edu.hk)"],["Credit hours","3 hours/week, one term (3 hrs class + 3 hrs personal study expected weekly)"],["Textbook","Kerin, Lau, Hartley & Rudelius (2025), Marketing, 17th ed., McGraw-Hill Education"],["Learning platform","McGraw-Hill Connect (self-learning platform; optional online quizzes)"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Description & Learning Outcomes"},{"t":"p","x":"This functional core course introduces the fundamental concepts of marketing and equips students with an integrated, strategic approach to developing a marketing plan, structured around the planning → implementation → control phases of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learning Outcomes (LOs)"},{"t":"li","x":"**LO1: **Apply marketing concepts and theories to situations and make decisions using a strategic marketing perspective."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO2: **Broaden managerial perspective of marketing from a business enterprise's point of view."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO3: **Improve analytical skills; think critically and creatively."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO4: **Present findings/recommendations concisely, convincingly, and logically in written work."},{"t":"li","x":"**LO5: **Make oral presentations with strong communication skills (vocabulary, visual aids, etc.)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assessment Weighting"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Assessment","Weight","Learning Outcomes Assessed"],["Group Project","20%","LO1, LO2, LO3, LO5"],["Mid-term Test (MCQ, 50 questions)","15%","LO1, LO2"],["Individual Written Assignment","15%","LO1, LO3, LO4"],["Final Exam (case + essay questions)","50%","LO1, LO2"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Dates"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Date","Event"],["1 Sep","Course overview; Chapter 1 – Understanding Marketing"],["8 Sep","Chapter 2 – Strategic Marketing Process"],["15 Sep","Chapter 3 – The Marketing Environment; project team formation"],["22 Sep","Chapter 5 – Consumer Behavior; Individual Assignment briefing"],["29 Sep","Chapter 8 – Marketing Research; Group Project briefing"],["6 Oct","Chapter 9 – Segmentation, Targeting & Positioning"],["13 & 20 Oct","Chapters 10–11 – Product Decisions (not covered in this document — see scope note)"],["26 Oct (Mon, 5–6pm, Room MBG22)","Mid-term Test — covers Ch. 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9"],["27 Oct","Chapter 15 – Channel Decisions"],["3 Nov","Chapters 18–20 – Integrated Marketing Communications"],["8 Nov 2026, 23:59","Individual Written Assignment deadline (via Turnitin/Moodle)"],["10 Nov","Chapters 13–14 – Pricing Decisions"],["17 & 24 Nov","Final project presentations / course review"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assignment Info (context only — not exam content)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Why this section is here\n• The two items below (Individual Assignment and Group Project) are summarized so you have the full course context in one document.\n• They are coursework deliverables, NOT topics tested on the mid-term or final exam — do not study them as \"content to memorize\", but do note the deadlines and deliverables."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Written Assignment (15% of grade)"},{"t":"p","x":"Brand: GODIVA (Hong Kong boutiques only). Acting as a marketing consultant, each student must visit one physical Godiva boutique in Hong Kong (not online/delivery, not outside HK) and, based on that visit plus outside research, propose at least two distinct strategies to extend Godiva's product life cycle in Hong Kong (e.g., modifying a product, changing served markets, or repositioning). Each strategy must be justified against four criteria: sufficient demand, fit with marketing objectives, company expertise/resources to implement, and ability to differentiate from competitors. Report: 800–1,000 words (in-store experience ≥100 words), submitted via Turnitin/Moodle by 23:59, 8 Nov 2026. Similarity threshold: 25%."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Group Project (20% of grade)"},{"t":"p","x":"Teams of 6–7 act as marketing consultants introducing either (a) a foreign product/service into the China/Hong Kong market, or (b) a China/Hong Kong product/service into a foreign market. Teams choose ONE of three assigned options for this term:"},{"t":"li","x":"Option 1: Neo's Home Robot (1X Technologies) — to be marketed in Hong Kong."},{"t":"li","x":"Option 2: Pop Mart's \"Pop Bakery\" concept — to be marketed in Paris, France."},{"t":"li","x":"Option 3: Anitch's Barrier Repair Body Cream (HK brand) — to be marketed in Melbourne, Australia."},{"t":"p","x":"Deliverables: a PowerPoint marketing plan (max 30 slides, max 50 words/slide) covering environmental scanning (PESTLE) and SWOT, 4 types of segmentation, target segment(s), positioning (with a perceptual map), and a full 4Ps marketing plan — plus a 30-second original video advertisement featuring all team members. A 25-minute team presentation (all members speaking) is delivered near the end of term. Both a peer evaluation and a Generative-AI declaration form are required from every student/team."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Academic integrity & AI use"},{"t":"li","x":"Plagiarism, collusion, and falsified data are treated as dishonest practice and can be referred to the Student Disciplinary Committee."},{"t":"li","x":"All written submissions go through Turnitin."},{"t":"li","x":"Any use of Generative AI tools must be declared on the official declaration form and must follow Lingnan University's GAI guidelines."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Appendix A — Building an Effective Marketing Plan"},{"t":"p","x":"Supplementary content · This appendix is directly useful for your Group Project, since it lays out the exact structure your team's marketing-plan presentation should follow."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Marketing Plans vs. Business Plans"},{"t":"li","x":"**Marketing plan **— a road map for the marketing actions of an organization for a specified future period (e.g., 1 or 5 years)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Business plan **— a road map for the ENTIRE organization over a specified future period (broader than marketing alone: also covers R&D, operations, organization-wide financials)."},{"t":"p","x":"What a plan should contain depends on (1) its target audience and purpose, (2) the kind/complexity of the organization, and (3) the industry. A plan aimed at an INTERNAL audience (to direct the firm) typically differs from one aimed at an EXTERNAL audience (to raise capital) — the external version adds a company description, organizational structure, and biographies of key personnel, since outside readers don't already know the firm."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The questions outside audiences (e.g., investors) always ask"},{"t":"li","x":"Is the marketing/business idea valid?"},{"t":"li","x":"Is the idea distinctive?"},{"t":"li","x":"Is there a real market for the offering?"},{"t":"li","x":"Are the financial projections realistic?"},{"t":"li","x":"Are the key personnel capable of executing the plan?"},{"t":"li","x":"Does the plan clearly describe how investors get their money back?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Writing and Style Guidelines"},{"t":"li","x":"Use a direct, professional style; be positive and specific."},{"t":"li","x":"Avoid superlatives (\"the best,\" \"revolutionary\") without evidence."},{"t":"li","x":"Use numbers for impact and bullet points for emphasis."},{"t":"li","x":"Use clear A-level and B-level headings so readers can navigate quickly."},{"t":"li","x":"Use visuals — photos, illustrations, graphs, and charts."},{"t":"li","x":"Aim for roughly 15–35 pages."},{"t":"li","x":"Use care in layout, design, and presentation, and cite credible sources of information/data."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Directly transferable to your Group Project slides\n• \"Number of words in each slide is limited to 50\" (your Group Project rule) echoes this chapter's advice to be concise, use bullets, and let numbers/visuals do the work rather than dense paragraphs."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Marketing Plan Outline (11 sections)"},{"t":"p","x":"The textbook illustrates this outline using a running example: Paradise Kitchens, Inc., maker of Howlin' Coyote Chili. The structure below is the one to use as your Group Project's mental checklist."},{"t":"table","rows":[["#","Section","What goes here"],["1","Table of Contents","Quick navigation by section/subsection."],["2","Executive Summary","The single most important element — \"sells\" the plan through clarity and brevity (often ~2 pages)."],["3","Company Description","Recent history and successes of the organization."],["4","Strategic Focus and Plan","Mission; Goals (financial & nonfinancial); Core competency and (sustainable) competitive advantage."],["5","Situation Analysis (SWOT)","Internal factors: Strengths & Weaknesses. External factors: social, competitive, technological, economic, legal/regulatory trends. Followed by finer-grained Industry Analysis, Competitor Analysis, Company Analysis, and Customer Analysis (incl. customer characteristics)."],["6","Market-Product Focus","Marketing & product objectives; Target markets; Points of difference; Positioning."],["7","Marketing Program","The 4Ps in detail: Product strategy, Price strategy, Promotion strategy (incl. sales promotion), Place (distribution) strategy."],["8","Financial Data and Projections","Past sales revenues; Five-year projections; Organization (org chart)."],["9","Implementation Plan","How the plan will actually be executed and rolled out over time (often with a schedule/Gantt-style chart)."],["10","Evaluation","How actual results will be compared with targets, and what contingency actions follow."],["11","Appendices","Supporting detail — e.g., biographical sketches of key personnel, detailed financial spreadsheets."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked example — SWOT analysis for Paradise Kitchens (Howlin' Coyote Chili)"},{"t":"p","x":"This condensed version of the textbook's worked example shows the level of specificity expected in a real plan's SWOT:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Category","Example points"],["Strengths (internal)","Experienced, entrepreneurial management; unique, high-quality/high-price product; excellent consumer acceptance in existing markets."],["Weaknesses (internal)","Small size limits options; no national awareness/distribution; heavy dependence on key employees; limited financial resources vs. giant competitors."],["Opportunities (external)","Fast-growing Southwestern/Mexican food category; large and growing Hispanic population; food-tech breakthroughs helping small producers compete."],["Threats (external)","Premium price may limit access to mass markets; product not patentable — larger competitors can imitate; more households eating out/ordering takeout instead of cooking."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Notice how each SWOT point is specific and evidence-based (not vague adjectives) — this is exactly the standard your Group Project's SWOT slide should meet."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Worked example — Points of Difference and Positioning"},{"t":"p","x":"Paradise Kitchens' points of difference: (1) unique taste + convenience combination no competitor matches, (2) alignment with the trend toward spicier foods, (3) distinctive premium packaging. Its positioning statement: positioned in consumers' minds as a very high-quality \"authentic Southwestern/Mexican tasting\" chili that is still quick and easy to prepare — i.e., it resolves the old trade-off where chili was either convenient OR tasty, but not both."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises — Appendix A"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1 (MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"Which section of a marketing plan is specifically designed to \"sell\" the plan to a reader through clarity and brevity, often before they read anything else in depth?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Implementation Plan"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Executive Summary"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Appendices"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Organization"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Name two elements that appear in a marketing plan aimed at an EXTERNAL audience (to raise capital) but would typically be unnecessary in a purely INTERNAL plan, and explain why."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3 (Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"List the four external SWOT categories (beyond simply \"opportunities and threats\") that the Paradise Kitchens example uses to organize external factors."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4 (Applied mini-case — Group Project practice)"},{"t":"p","x":"Using whichever Group Project option your own team has been assigned (Neo's Home Robot / Pop Bakery / Anitch Barrier Repair Body Cream), draft a one-paragraph Executive Summary (3–5 sentences) that a busy reader could use to understand your whole marketing plan at a glance. Then list, in order, the marketing-plan section headings (from the 11-section outline) you would use to structure your team's slide deck."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Appendix A"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Executive Summary. It is explicitly described as the single most important element in \"selling\" the plan through clarity and brevity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"(1) Company Description — external readers don't already know the firm's history/background, so it must be spelled out; internal readers already know this. (2) Biographies of key personnel / organizational structure — external investors need reassurance that capable people are running the venture, whereas internal readers already know the team. (Accept any two reasonable, correctly justified elements from the outline, e.g. organizational structure more broadly.)"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"Consumer/Social, Competitive, Technological, Economic, and Legal/Regulatory (five categories total, each split into opportunities and threats)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"p","x":"**Guidance (self-assessed — no single correct answer): **A strong Executive Summary states (1) what the product/service is and where it will be introduced, (2) the core target segment and positioning in one sentence, (3) the headline reason the opportunity is attractive (market size/trend), and (4) the essence of the 4Ps approach — all in plain, confident, non-superlative language. Section order to follow: Company/Product Description → Strategic Focus (objectives) → Situation Analysis (PESTLE/SWOT, Industry & Competitor Analysis) → Market-Product Focus (Segmentation, Targeting, Positioning + perceptual map) → Marketing Program (4Ps) → Implementation/Evaluation notes if time allows. This mirrors both the Appendix A outline and the Key Questions explicitly listed in your Group Project Guide."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 3 — Exam-Style Review Checklist (All Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this as a final self-test before the mid-term or final exam. For each item, ask yourself: \"Can I explain this out loud, from memory, with an example?\" If not, go back to that chapter's notes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 1 — Marketing Foundations"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Give the AMA definition of marketing and explain what \"exchange\" means."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 4 conditions required for an exchange/marketing to occur."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish needs vs. wants with an example."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 4 P's and the 5 uncontrollable environmental forces, and correctly sort a list of mixed examples into the two categories."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 4 eras of market orientation evolution (production → sales → marketing concept → customer relationship)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define market orientation and the marketing concept, and explain how they relate."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 4 types of utility with an example of each."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 2 — Strategy & the Strategic Marketing Process"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 3 levels of organizational strategy (corporate, SBU, functional)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 3 elements of a visionary organization's foundation (purpose, core values, mission/culture) and its direction (business, goals, KPIs)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Calculate dollar sales and dollar market share from given numbers."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Correctly classify a business into a BCG matrix quadrant (star/question mark/cash cow/dog) given its market share and market growth."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Apply the 4 diversification-analysis strategies (penetration, development ×2, diversification) to a scenario."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 3 planning-phase steps and the 4 implementation-phase components of the strategic marketing process."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain \"planning gap\" and the two possible responses to a deviation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 3 — The Marketing Environment"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define environmental scanning and explain why interpreting a trend matters as much as spotting it."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 5 environmental forces and give a current example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish gross, disposable, and discretionary income."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name and distinguish the 4 forms of competition (pure competition, monopolistic competition, oligopoly, pure monopoly)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 3 major pieces of regulatory legislation and what each protects (Sherman Act, Clayton Act, Robinson-Patman Act)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain self-regulation and give an example."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 5 — Consumer Behavior"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 stages of the purchase decision process, in order, with what happens at each stage."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish extended, limited, and routine problem solving, and the involvement level behind each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 situational influences."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain Maslow's hierarchy of needs with a marketing example at each level."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain selective perception (exposure/comprehension/retention) and 2 ways to reduce perceived risk."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the drive–cue–response–reinforcement model of behavioral learning."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish associative, aspiration, and dissociative reference groups."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the family decision-making roles (gatherer, influencer, decision maker, purchaser, user)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 8 — Marketing Research"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 steps of the marketing research approach, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish exploratory, descriptive, and causal research."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish primary vs. secondary data, with the advantages/disadvantages of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 4 primary data-collection methods (observational, focus group, survey types, experiment/panel)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish independent vs. dependent variable in an experiment."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name the 3 families of sales-forecasting technique and 1 specific technique from each."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Chapter 9 — Segmentation, Targeting & Positioning"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Define market segmentation and the 4 segmentation bases (geographic, demographic, psychographic, behavioral)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain the 80/20 rule and usage rate."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 steps in segmenting and targeting markets, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 5 criteria for selecting target markets."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain what a market-product grid shows and how to read one."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish head-to-head vs. differentiation positioning."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain how to build a perceptual map, step by step."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Appendix A — The Marketing Plan"},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Distinguish a marketing plan from a business plan."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  List the 11 sections of a marketing plan outline, roughly in order."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Explain how a plan for an external audience differs from one for an internal audience."},{"t":"p","x":"☐  Name at least 3 writing/style guidelines for a professional marketing plan."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Part 4 — Mixed Practice Set (Cross-Chapter)"},{"t":"p","x":"These questions mix multiple chapters, similar to the case-plus-essay format of the Final Exam. Answer key follows at the end of this section."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q1 (Ch.1, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm changing its packaging design is exercising control over which marketing mix element?"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Price"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Product"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Place"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Regulatory force"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q2 (Ch.2, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A firm's SBU has 8% share of a market growing at 12% per year, while its main rival has 35% share. Classify this SBU on the BCG matrix and explain your reasoning."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q3 (Ch.3, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A market with many sellers offering close substitutes that are NOT identical (e.g., independent coffee shops) best fits:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Pure competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) Oligopoly"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) Pure monopoly"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q4 (Ch.5, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A shopper feels regret shortly after buying an expensive winter coat online. Name this phenomenon and suggest one marketing action the retailer could take to address it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q5 (Ch.8, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A company mining its own loyalty-card transaction records to look for patterns is using:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) Primary data via a mail survey"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) Secondary, internal data"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A focus group"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) An experiment"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q6 (Ch.9, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"A skincare brand notices that 70% of its profit comes from just 15% of its customers, who buy every month. Name the principle this illustrates and explain one marketing action the brand could take in response."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q7 (Ch.2 + Ch.9, Short answer)"},{"t":"p","x":"Explain how \"market segmentation\" (Ch.9) fits into Step 2 of the strategic marketing process's planning phase (Ch.2) — i.e., why must a firm segment the market BEFORE it can design a marketing program?"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q8 (Ch.3 + Ch.1, MCQ)"},{"t":"p","x":"A new government data-privacy law forcing a retailer to change how it collects customer data for its loyalty app is an example of:"},{"t":"li","x":"(A) A controllable marketing-mix decision"},{"t":"li","x":"(B) An uncontrollable regulatory force"},{"t":"li","x":"(C) A behavioral segmentation basis"},{"t":"li","x":"(D) A form of competition"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Q9 — Integrative Mini-Case (SWOT + STP + Consumer Behavior)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Case: \"BrewLab\" specialty coffee — expansion decision\n• BrewLab is a small, HK-based specialty coffee roaster known among coffee enthusiasts for single-origin, ethically-sourced beans and a loyal Instagram following. It currently sells through one café in Sai Ying Pun and online direct-to-consumer subscriptions. Its founder is considering opening a second, larger café in a busy Central office district to capture office workers buying takeaway coffee.\n• BrewLab's coffee is more expensive than chain coffee (Pacific Coffee, Starbucks) but noticeably higher quality; the founder personally knows every regular customer at the Sai Ying Pun café. The team has limited capital and no experience running a high-volume takeaway operation."]]},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) SWOT (Ch.2): **Identify one strength, one weakness, one opportunity, and one threat for BrewLab's proposed Central expansion."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Segmentation & targeting (Ch.9): **Propose two segmentation bases (from geographic/demographic/psychographic/behavioral) that BrewLab should use to define its target segment in Central, and briefly justify each."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Positioning (Ch.9): **Should BrewLab use head-to-head or differentiation positioning against Starbucks/Pacific Coffee in Central? Justify your choice in 1–2 sentences."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Consumer behavior (Ch.5): **A Central office worker buying takeaway coffee every weekday morning is most likely engaging in which type of problem solving (extended/limited/routine)? What does this imply for BrewLab's in-store marketing tactics?"},{"t":"p","x":"**(e) Integration: **In 2–3 sentences, explain how your answers to (a)–(d) fit together into a single, coherent argument for (or against) the Central expansion."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Answer Key — Mixed Practice Set"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q1"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Product. Packaging design is a decision the firm makes and controls directly, making it part of the Product element of the marketing mix."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q2"},{"t":"a","x":"Question mark. The market growth rate (12%) is high, but the SBU's own market share (8%) is low relative to the rival's 35% — high growth + low relative share is the definition of a question mark, meaning the firm must decide whether to invest further to try to grow share (moving toward \"star\") or scale back."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q3"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Monopolistic competition. Many sellers, similar but not identical/substitutable products within a price range (the textbook's own example is coffee/tea) — this is monopolistic competition, not pure competition (which requires near-identical, commodity-like products)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q4"},{"t":"a","x":"This is cognitive dissonance (postpurchase psychological tension, especially likely for a higher-involvement/higher-price purchase). The retailer could send a reassuring confirmation email highlighting the coat's key benefits/reviews, offer a hassle-free return policy, or provide styling tips to reinforce the buyer's confidence in the purchase."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q5"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) Secondary, internal data. The loyalty-card data already exists inside the firm (an internal marketing outcome data source) and was collected before this specific analysis — that makes it secondary AND internal, even though data mining is being applied to it."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q6"},{"t":"a","x":"This illustrates the 80/20 rule (a small share of heavy users driving most profit) — closely related to usage-rate/behavioral segmentation. The brand should launch a loyalty/frequency-marketing program (e.g., a subscription or rewards tier) specifically to retain and further reward this high-usage-rate segment, since retaining these customers is far more profit-efficient than acquiring new, lower-value ones."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q7"},{"t":"a","x":"Step 2 of the planning phase (\"develop a market-product focus, customer value proposition, and goals\") explicitly requires market segmentation as its foundation: a firm cannot decide which customer value proposition and points of difference to build, nor design a coherent marketing program (Step 3), until it has first identified WHICH group(s) of customers — with common needs that respond similarly to marketing actions — it intends to serve. Segmentation therefore logically precedes and directly feeds the market-product focus step; without it, the subsequent marketing program has no clearly defined audience to be designed around."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q8"},{"t":"a","x":"(B) An uncontrollable regulatory force. A government-imposed privacy law is external to the firm and outside its control — a textbook example of a regulatory environmental force, distinct from a marketing-mix decision, a segmentation basis, or a competitive-structure concept."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Q9 — Integrative case answer key"},{"t":"p","x":"**(a) SWOT: **Strength — high perceived product quality and strong brand loyalty/community among coffee enthusiasts. Weakness — no experience running a high-volume takeaway operation, and limited capital to fund the expansion. Opportunity — large volume of office workers in Central who regularly buy takeaway coffee and increasingly value quality/ethical sourcing. Threat — intense, well-resourced competition from established chains (Starbucks, Pacific Coffee) with far greater scale, lower prices, and existing Central footprints."},{"t":"p","x":"**(b) Segmentation & targeting: **Behavioral segmentation — target \"heavy, habitual\" weekday coffee buyers (high usage rate, the 80/20-style core of any café's revenue), since they represent the most reliable, high-frequency revenue base in a business district. Psychographic segmentation — target quality-and-ethics-conscious professionals who are willing to pay a premium for better-sourced, better-tasting coffee (rather than competing purely on convenience/price), since this aligns with BrewLab's existing brand identity and is a segment less well served by the mass-market chains."},{"t":"p","x":"**(c) Positioning: **Differentiation positioning is the better fit: BrewLab cannot out-scale or out-price Starbucks/Pacific Coffee on convenience or cost, but it CAN own a distinct position around specialty quality, ethical sourcing, and an authentic brand story — attributes the mass chains do not credibly claim — making head-to-head competition on the chains' own terms (speed, ubiquity, low price) a weak strategy for a small, capital-constrained player."},{"t":"p","x":"**(d) Consumer behavior: **Most likely routine problem solving (or at most limited): buying a daily takeaway coffee is a low-involvement, high-frequency, habitual purchase for most office workers, who will not extensively research or compare each morning. This implies BrewLab's in-store tactics should focus on maintaining consistent product quality, avoiding stockouts/slow service (since routine buyers are highly sensitive to friction), and building habitual brand loyalty (e.g., a stamp/rewards card) rather than relying on heavy in-store persuasive information, which matters more for high-involvement purchases."},{"t":"p","x":"**(e) Integration: **Because BrewLab's core strength is quality/brand loyalty rather than scale, and because the target segment's daily coffee purchase is a low-involvement, habitual decision, the expansion is more likely to succeed if BrewLab pursues differentiation positioning around quality/ethics aimed at a psychographically-defined \"quality-conscious\" segment of habitual buyers — building fast, reliable, high-quality routine service and loyalty mechanics — rather than trying to out-compete the chains head-to-head on speed and price, which would expose exactly the weaknesses (limited capital, no high-volume experience) identified in the SWOT."}]},"BUS2105":{"file":"BUS2105_Microeconomics_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"What Is Economics?","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Scarcity","Our inability to satisfy all our wants; the fundamental problem that forces choice."],["Incentive","A reward that encourages an action, or a penalty that discourages one."],["Economics","The social science that studies the choices individuals, businesses, governments and societies make as they cope with scarcity, and the incentives that influence and reconcile those choices."],["Microeconomics","The study of choices individuals and businesses make, how those choices interact in markets, and the influence of governments."],["Macroeconomics","The study of the performance of national and global economies (e.g. unemployment, inflation, growth)."],["Factors of production","Productive resources: Land, Labor, Capital, Entrepreneurship."],["Human capital","The knowledge and skill people obtain from education, training and experience."],["Rational choice","A choice that compares costs and benefits and achieves the greatest benefit over cost for the chooser."],["Opportunity cost","The highest-valued alternative that must be given up to get something."],["Marginal benefit / marginal cost","The benefit / opportunity cost of one more (incremental) unit of an activity."],["Positive statement","A statement that can be tested against facts (\"what is\")."],["Normative statement","A statement that expresses an opinion and cannot be tested (\"what ought to be\")."],["Economic model","A simplified description of some aspect of the economic world, including only the features needed for the purpose at hand."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"1. The two big questions of economics"},{"t":"p","x":"• 1. What, how, and for whom are goods and services produced?"},{"t":"p","x":"• 2. When do choices made in self-interest also promote the social interest?"},{"t":"p","x":"The factors of production and what they earn:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","Payment earned"],["Land (natural resources)","Rent"],["Labor (work time and effort)","Wages"],["Capital (tools, machines, buildings)","Interest"],["Entrepreneurship (organizes the other three)","Profit"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Social interest has two dimensions: efficiency (resources used so that no one can be made better off without making someone else worse off) and fair shares (equity). Four themes illustrate tension between self-interest and social interest: globalization, information-age monopolies, climate change, and pandemics (e.g. Covid-19)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"2. The economic way of thinking — six key ideas"},{"t":"p","x":"**Important nuance: **Scarcity is not the same as poverty. Even someone with unlimited money still faces scarcity of time — this is why choice and opportunity cost apply to everyone."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"3. Economist as scientist and policy adviser"},{"t":"p","x":"Economists build models to make positive predictions (testable). Policy questions blend positive analysis (what will happen) with normative judgments (what should happen, i.e. the goal) — economics cannot choose the goal, but it can compare the marginal benefits and costs of alternative ways to reach a given goal."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Adventure-trip opportunity cost: A student forgoes a summer job paying $6,000 and free room/board at home to go on a trip costing $3,000 (travel/accommodation) + $200 (gasoline) + $1,400 (food)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Opportunity cost = **forgone income ($6,000) + direct trip costs ($3,000 + $200 + $1,400 = $4,600) = $10,600."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Which of the following is a microeconomic (rather than macroeconomic) question? (a) Why did the national unemployment rate rise to 5%? (b) Why did the price of coffee beans rise this year? (c) Why did GDP growth slow down? (d) Why did the central bank cut interest rates?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Which of the following is a normative statement? (a) An increase in university tuition decreases the quantity of students enrolling. (b) The government should provide free tuition to increase social equity. (c) When the price of textbooks rises, students buy fewer textbooks. (d) A 10% tax on soft drinks reduced sales by 8%."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Chloé can either work at a café for the summer, earning $5,000 over 10 weeks, or take an unpaid marketing internship. If she takes the internship, she will need to spend $600 on transport and $400 on business attire — costs she would not otherwise incur. What is the opportunity cost of taking the internship?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Match each factor of production to the income it earns: Land, Labor, Capital, Entrepreneurship ↔ Rent, Wages, Interest, Profit."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **A firm's marginal benefit (extra revenue) from producing one more unit is $50 and its marginal cost is $42. Should it produce that unit? At a higher output level, marginal benefit falls to $30 while marginal cost rises to $35 — should the firm produce that unit?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Explain, using the idea of scarcity and choice, why \"scarcity\" is not the same as \"poverty.\""},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(b) — microeconomics concerns choices of individual households, firms, and specific markets; (a), (c) and (d) are economy-wide aggregates studied in macroeconomics.\n**A2. **(b) — it contains the word \"should\" and expresses an opinion that cannot be tested against facts; the other three are testable positive statements.\n**A3. **Opportunity cost = forgone café earnings ($5,000) + extra internship costs ($600 + $400) = $6,000.\n**A4. **Land → Rent; Labor → Wages; Capital → Interest; Entrepreneurship → Profit.\n**A5. **First case: MB ($50) > MC ($42), so the firm should produce the unit (net gain $8). Second case: MB ($30) < MC ($35), so the firm should NOT produce that unit (net loss $5) — it should stop expanding output before that point.\n**A6. **Scarcity means wants always exceed the resources available to satisfy them — it exists for everyone, including the very wealthy, because time itself is always limited. Poverty is a state of having few resources relative to others. Even someone as rich as Bill Gates faces scarcity (of time, attention, etc.) and must make trade-offs, even though he is not poor."}]},"3":{"title":"Demand and Supply","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Competitive market","A market with many buyers and sellers, none of whom can influence the price."],["Quantity demanded","The amount consumers plan to buy at a given price in a given period."],["Law of demand","Other things being equal, the higher the price, the smaller the quantity demanded (and vice versa) — due to the substitution effect and income effect."],["Demand curve","The relationship between quantity demanded and price, other things constant; also a willingness-and-ability-to-pay (marginal benefit) curve."],["Change in demand","A shift of the whole demand curve, caused by a change in something other than the good's own price."],["Quantity supplied","The amount producers plan to sell at a given price in a given period."],["Law of supply","Other things being equal, the higher the price, the greater the quantity supplied — because marginal cost rises with output."],["Supply curve","The relationship between quantity supplied and price, other things constant; also a minimum-supply-price (marginal cost) curve."],["Equilibrium price / quantity","The price at which quantity demanded equals quantity supplied; the quantity bought and sold at that price."],["Substitute / Complement","A substitute can replace a good in use (demand for the good rises when the substitute's price rises); a complement is used together with it (demand falls when the complement's price rises)."],["Normal / Inferior good","Normal: demand rises as income rises. Inferior: demand falls as income rises."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six factors that shift demand"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six factors that shift supply"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 3.1–3.6 show the demand curve, a rightward shift from an income increase, the supply curve, and a rightward shift from a technology advance. A movement ALONG a curve is caused only by a change in the good's own price; a SHIFT of the curve is caused by any other relevant factor changing."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Market equilibrium and price adjustment"},{"t":"p","x":"At the equilibrium price, quantity demanded = quantity supplied. Below equilibrium price → shortage → price rises. Above equilibrium price → surplus → price falls."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 3.7–3.9 plot demand and supply curves crossing at equilibrium, then a rightward shift of demand raising both price and quantity, and a rightward shift of supply raising quantity but lowering price."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The four-case prediction table (both curves can shift)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Demand","Supply","Equilibrium Price","Equilibrium Quantity"],["Increase","No change","Rises","Rises"],["Decrease","No change","Falls","Falls"],["No change","Increase","Falls","Rises"],["No change","Decrease","Rises","Falls"],["Increase","Increase","Ambiguous","Rises"],["Decrease","Decrease","Ambiguous","Falls"],["Increase","Decrease","Rises","Ambiguous"],["Decrease","Increase","Falls","Ambiguous"]]}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Which of the following would shift the supply curve for smartphones rightward? (a) A rise in the price of smartphones. (b) A fall in the wages of factory workers making smartphones. (c) A rise in consumer income. (d) A rise in the price of a substitute in production."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Tea and coffee are substitutes. If the price of tea rises, what happens in the market for coffee?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **The demand and supply schedules for bubble tea (cups per day) are Qd = 1,400 − 200P and Qs = 200P − 200 (P in dollars). (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) At a price of $3, is there a shortage or surplus, and of how much? (c) At a price of $5, is there a shortage or surplus, and of how much?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **A health report causes demand for bubble tea to fall by 200 cups at every price (supply is unchanged from Q3). Find the new equilibrium price and quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **If both demand and supply for a good increase at the same time, what happens to the equilibrium quantity? What happens to the equilibrium price?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Classify each pair as substitutes, complements, or not a demand relationship: (a) butter and margarine; (b) printers and ink cartridges; (c) rice and rainfall in a rice-growing region."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(b) — a fall in an input price lowers production cost and increases supply (shifts right). (a) is a movement ALONG the curve, not a shift; (c) shifts demand, not supply; (d) would decrease supply of this good (producers switch toward the now more attractively priced substitute).\n**A2. **Coffee demand increases (shifts rightward) because tea and coffee are substitutes, so both the equilibrium price and equilibrium quantity of coffee rise.\n**A3. **(a) Set Qd = Qs: 1,400 − 200P = 200P − 200 → 1,600 = 400P → P = $4; Q = 1,400 − 200(4) = 600 cups/day. (b) At P = $3: Qd = 1,400 − 600 = 800; Qs = 200(3) − 200 = 400 → shortage of 400 cups (price will tend to rise toward $4). (c) At P = $5: Qd = 1,400 − 1,000 = 400; Qs = 200(5) − 200 = 800 → surplus of 400 cups (price will tend to fall toward $4).\n**A4. **New demand: Qd′ = 1,200 − 200P (shifted left by 200 at every price). Set equal to supply: 1,200 − 200P = 200P − 200 → 1,400 = 400P → P = $3.50; Q = 200(3.50) − 200 = 500 cups/day. (Equilibrium price falls from $4.00 to $3.50 and quantity falls from 600 to 500.)\n**A5. **Equilibrium quantity definitely increases (both shifts push quantity up). The equilibrium price change is ambiguous — it depends on which shift is larger, since the demand increase pushes price up while the supply increase pushes price down.\n**A6. **(a) Substitutes (close alternatives in consumption). (b) Complements (must be used together). (c) Not a demand-side relationship — rainfall affects the cost/availability of producing rice, so it is a supply-side (state of nature) factor, not a change in consumer demand."}]},"4":{"title":"Elasticity","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions and Formulas"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Price elasticity of demand (PED)","% change in quantity demanded ÷ % change in price (using the midpoint/average method); reported as an absolute value."],["Perfectly inelastic demand","PED = 0; vertical demand curve; quantity does not respond to price at all."],["Unit elastic demand","PED = 1; % change in Qd equals % change in P."],["Elastic demand","PED > 1; quantity is very responsive to price."],["Perfectly elastic demand","PED = ∞; horizontal demand curve."],["Total revenue test","If a price cut raises total revenue (P×Q), demand is elastic; if it lowers TR, demand is inelastic; if TR is unchanged, demand is unit elastic."],["Income elasticity of demand (YED)","% change in Qd ÷ % change in income. YED > 1: income-elastic normal good (luxury); 0 < YED < 1: income-inelastic normal good (necessity); YED < 0: inferior good."],["Cross elasticity of demand (XED)","% change in Qd of good A ÷ % change in price of good B. Positive → substitutes; negative → complements."],["Elasticity of supply","% change in quantity supplied ÷ % change in price; higher when resources are more easily substitutable and when more time has passed since the price change."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Midpoint (average) method"},{"t":"p","x":"Percentage changes are calculated on the AVERAGE of the initial and new values, so the elasticity is the same whether price rises or falls between the two points:"},{"t":"p","x":"PED = [ΔQ ÷ average Q] ÷ [ΔP ÷ average P]"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figure 4.1–4.3: the pizza demand curve example shows PED = 4 between $19.50–$20.50, falling toward the lower end of the curve — demand is elastic above the mid-point of a straight-line demand curve and inelastic below it; exactly unit elastic at the mid-point, where total revenue is maximized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Factors influencing PED"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Factors influencing elasticity of supply"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza price falls from $20.50 to $19.50 (average $20); quantity rises from 9 to 11 pizzas/hour (average 10)."},{"t":"p","x":"**%ΔQ = **(2/10) × 100 = 20%.  %ΔP = ($1/$20) × 100 = 5%.  PED = 20% ÷ 5% = 4 (highly elastic)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The average price of a bag of coffee falls from $12 to $10 (average $11) and the quantity demanded rises from 500 to 700 bags a week (average 600). Calculate the price elasticity of demand and classify it as elastic, inelastic, or unit elastic."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Using the numbers in Q1, calculate total revenue before and after the price change, and confirm your elasticity classification using the total revenue test."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **A consumer's monthly income rises from $3,000 to $3,600 (average $3,300), and their restaurant meals per month rise from 8 to 10 (average 9). Calculate the income elasticity of demand and classify the good."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **The price of butter rises from $4 to $5 a pack (average $4.50); as a result, the quantity demanded of margarine (a substitute) rises from 200 to 240 units (average 220). Calculate the cross elasticity of demand and interpret its sign."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Which good is likely to have the MOST inelastic demand: (a) a particular brand of soft drink; (b) insulin for a diabetic patient; (c) restaurant meals at one specific chain; (d) one specific laptop model?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **Elasticity of supply is likely to be HIGHER when: (a) resources needed are highly specialized; (b) the time frame considered is longer; (c) the good is a service that must be consumed instantly; (d) the firm cannot adjust its production process."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **%ΔQ = (700−500)/600 = 33.3%. %ΔP = (10−12)/11 = −18.2% (use magnitude 18.2%). PED = 33.3% ÷ 18.2% ≈ 1.83 → demand is ELASTIC (PED > 1).\n**A2. **TR before = $12 × 500 = $6,000. TR after = $10 × 700 = $7,000. Total revenue rose when price fell, which confirms elastic demand — consistent with PED ≈ 1.83 found in Q1.\n**A3. **%ΔQ = (10−8)/9 = 22.2%. %ΔY = (3,600−3,000)/3,300 = 18.2%. YED = 22.2% ÷ 18.2% ≈ 1.22. Since YED > 1, restaurant meals are income-elastic — a normal good behaving like a luxury for this consumer.\n**A4. **%ΔQ(margarine) = (240−200)/220 = 18.2%. %ΔP(butter) = (5−4)/4.5 = 22.2%. XED = 18.2% ÷ 22.2% ≈ 0.82. The positive sign confirms butter and margarine are substitutes.\n**A5. **(b) Insulin — a necessity with essentially no substitutes for a diabetic, so demand barely changes with price. The others have closer substitutes and are more elastic.\n**A6. **(b) — the longer the time frame, the more producers can adjust inputs and production methods, making supply more elastic. The other options describe conditions that make supply more INELASTIC."}]},"5":{"title":"Efficiency and Equity","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Resource allocation methods","Market price, command, majority rule, contest, first-come-first-served, lottery, personal characteristics, force — each suits different situations; none is universally best."],["Marginal benefit / demand curve","The demand curve is a marginal-benefit curve: willingness to pay for one more unit."],["Marginal cost / supply curve","The supply curve is a marginal-cost (minimum-supply-price) curve."],["Consumer surplus","Value received in excess of the price paid; area under the demand curve and above the price, up to quantity bought."],["Producer surplus","Amount received in excess of the minimum-supply price (cost); area below the price and above the supply curve, up to quantity sold."],["Efficient allocation","Occurs where marginal social benefit (MSB) = marginal social cost (MSC); total surplus (CS+PS) is maximized."],["Deadweight loss (DWL)","The decrease in total surplus that results from an inefficient (too small or too large) quantity."],["Market failure","Occurs when a market produces too little (underproduction) or too much (overproduction) relative to the efficient quantity."],["Utilitarianism","\"Greatest happiness for the greatest number\" — a fair-RESULT view (equality maximizes total benefit if marginal benefit of income diminishes)."],["Symmetry principle / fair rules","People in similar situations should be treated similarly; fairness is about equal opportunity via voluntary exchange under well-defined property rights (Nozick), not equal income."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Sources of market failure (leading to under- or overproduction)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figure 5.5–5.6: a demand-and-supply diagram with the competitive equilibrium at MSB = MSC. Restricting quantity below equilibrium creates a deadweight-loss triangle (underproduction); pushing quantity above equilibrium also creates a deadweight-loss triangle (overproduction), because in both cases MSB ≠ MSC over the affected units."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Pizza market: at $1/slice, Lisa buys 30 slices and Nick buys 10 (market demand = horizontal sum = 40 slices). Consumer surplus is the green triangle under demand and above $1, summed to 40 slices. At $15/pizza, Maria supplies 100 and Max supplies 50 (market supply = 150 pizzas); producer surplus is the blue triangle above the supply curve and below $15."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The demand and supply schedules for a good are Qd = 100 − 5P and Qs = 5P − 20. (a) Find the competitive equilibrium price and quantity. (b) Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium. (c) What is total surplus?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Suppose a quota restricts output of the good in Q1 to 30 units. Find (a) the price consumers are willing to pay for the 30th unit, (b) the marginal cost of producing the 30th unit, and (c) the resulting deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Resource allocation by \"first-come, first-served\" works best when: (a) the resource can serve many people at once; (b) the resource can serve only one person at a time in sequence; (c) there is no way to identify who values the resource most; (d) the government wants to redistribute wealth."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Using the same demand and supply as Q1, suppose a monopolist restricts output to 20 units (instead of the efficient 40). Is this over- or under-production? Calculate the resulting deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain the difference between the \"fair rules\" view (symmetry principle / Nozick) and the \"fair results\" view (utilitarianism / Rawls) of fairness, giving one example of a policy each view would evaluate differently."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **(a) Set 100 − 5P = 5P − 20 → 120 = 10P → P = $12; Q = 100 − 5(12) = 40 units. (b) Consumer surplus = ½ × (demand's price-intercept − equilibrium price) × Q = ½ × (20 − 12) × 40 = $160 (demand hits P-axis at P = $20 when Qd = 0). Producer surplus = ½ × (equilibrium price − supply's price-intercept) × Q = ½ × (12 − 4) × 40 = $160 (supply hits P-axis at P = $4 when Qs = 0). (c) Total surplus = $160 + $160 = $320.\n**A2. **(a) Demand price at Q = 30: 30 = 100 − 5P → P = $14 (buyers' marginal benefit). (b) Marginal cost at Q = 30: 30 = 5P − 20 → P = $10. (c) Deadweight loss = ½ × (40 − 30) × ($14 − $10) = ½ × 10 × 4 = $20.\n**A3. **(b) — e.g. a queue at a checkout or a single ticket window; the resource is used up sequentially by one customer at a time.\n**A4. **Underproduction (20 units is below the efficient 40). Demand price at Q = 20: 20 = 100 − 5P → P = $16. Marginal cost at Q = 20: 20 = 5P − 20 → P = $8. Deadweight loss = ½ × (40 − 20) × ($16 − $8) = ½ × 20 × 8 = $80.\n**A5. **The fair-RULES view judges a policy by whether it preserves voluntary exchange and property rights, regardless of the resulting income distribution — e.g. it would call a high inheritance tax unfair because it interferes with voluntarily-acquired property, even if it reduces inequality. The fair-RESULTS view (utilitarianism/Rawls) judges a policy by the distribution of outcomes it produces — e.g. it would favor progressive income redistribution because it raises the well-being of the worst-off, even though it requires compulsory transfers that the fair-rules view would call unfair."}]},"6":{"title":"Government Actions in Markets","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Price ceiling / rent ceiling","A regulated maximum price; below equilibrium it creates a shortage, increased search activity, and often a black market."],["Price floor / minimum wage","A regulated minimum price; above equilibrium it creates a surplus (unemployment in the labor market)."],["Tax incidence","The division of the burden of a tax between buyers and sellers — independent of who physically remits the tax."],["Tax wedge","The gap between the price buyers pay and the price sellers receive after a tax, equal to the tax per unit."],["Production quota","A government-imposed upper limit on the quantity a firm may produce."],["Subsidy","A payment from the government to a producer, which lowers the effective marginal cost and increases supply."],["Benefits principle (taxation)","People should pay taxes in proportion to the benefits they receive from government services."],["Ability-to-pay principle (taxation)","People should pay taxes according to how easily they can bear the burden — supports higher tax rates on higher incomes."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Tax incidence and elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 6.1–6.13: (i) a rent-ceiling diagram showing a housing shortage below equilibrium rent; (ii) a minimum-wage diagram showing a labor surplus (unemployment) above equilibrium wage; (iii) a tax-wedge diagram splitting the $3 cigarette tax between the price buyers pay ($8) and the price sellers receive ($5); (iv) production-quota and subsidy diagrams showing under- and over-production respectively; (v) an illegal-drug market diagram showing how penalties on buyers/sellers act like a tax, shifting demand/supply inward."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Cigarette tax: with no tax, equilibrium price = $6/pack, quantity = 350 million packs/year. A $3/pack tax is imposed. New equilibrium: buyers pay $8/pack, sellers receive $5/pack (an $8 − $5 = $3 wedge, matching the tax), and quantity falls to 325 million packs/year. Buyers bear $2 of the $3 tax and sellers bear $1, because demand for cigarettes is relatively more inelastic than supply."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **The equilibrium rent for an apartment is $1,500/month with 80,000 units rented. A rent ceiling is imposed at $1,200/month; at that price, quantity supplied is 60,000 and quantity demanded is 100,000. Is the ceiling binding? What is the size of the housing shortage?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **The demand for and supply of unskilled labor (in thousands of hours/week) are Qd = 1,300 − 50W and Qs = 50W − 300, where W is the hourly wage. (a) Find the equilibrium wage and quantity of labor hired. (b) The government sets a minimum wage of $18/hour. Find quantities demanded and supplied, and the resulting unemployment. (c) Calculate the deadweight loss created by the minimum wage."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **Using the cigarette-tax example above (no-tax equilibrium $6/pack, 350 million packs; $3 tax → buyers pay $8, sellers receive $5, quantity falls to 325 million): (a) state the buyers' and sellers' shares of the tax per pack; (b) calculate total tax revenue; (c) calculate the deadweight loss."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **If demand for a good is perfectly inelastic and supply is upward-sloping, who bears a tax placed on that good?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain, using the concepts of marginal social benefit (MSB) and marginal social cost (MSC), why a subsidy paid to farmers leads to overproduction and a deadweight loss."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Yes, binding, because $1,200 is below the $1,500 equilibrium rent. Shortage = quantity demanded − quantity supplied = 100,000 − 60,000 = 40,000 units.\n**A2. **(a) Set 1,300 − 50W = 50W − 300 → 1,600 = 100W → W = $16/hour; Q = 1,300 − 50(16) = 500 thousand hours/week. (b) At W = $18: Qd = 1,300 − 50(18) = 400 thousand hours; Qs = 50(18) − 300 = 600 thousand hours. Unemployment = 600 − 400 = 200 thousand hours/week. (c) At Q = 400 (thousand hrs), inverse demand price = (1,300 − 400)/50 = $18 (MSB); inverse supply price = (400 + 300)/50 = $14 (MSC). DWL = ½ × (500 − 400) × ($18 − $14) = ½ × 100 × 4 = 200 (thousand $) = $200,000/week.\n**A3. **(a) Buyers pay $2/pack more ($6→$8); sellers receive $1/pack less ($6→$5) — of the $3 tax, buyers bear two-thirds and sellers bear one-third, because demand is relatively less elastic than supply here. (b) Tax revenue = $3 × 325 million = $975 million/year. (c) DWL = ½ × $3 × (350 − 325) million = ½ × 3 × 25 million = $37.5 million/year.\n**A4. **Buyers bear the entire tax — with perfectly inelastic demand, quantity cannot change, so the price buyers pay rises by the full amount of the tax while the price sellers receive stays the same.\n**A5. **A subsidy lowers the effective marginal cost faced by farmers (MC − subsidy), so they expand output beyond the point where MSB = MSC. Beyond the efficient quantity, MSC (the true resource cost, ignoring the subsidy) exceeds MSB, so each additional unit produced destroys value rather than creating it. The subsidy causes overproduction, and the deadweight loss is the triangle of lost total surplus on units where MSC > MSB, financed at taxpayer expense."}]},"8":{"title":"Utility and Demand","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Budget line","Shows the limits to a consumer's affordable combinations of two goods, given income and prices."],["Total utility (TU)","The total benefit/satisfaction a person gets from consuming goods; generally rises with more consumption."],["Marginal utility (MU)","The change in total utility from a one-unit increase in consumption of a good."],["Diminishing marginal utility","MU decreases as the quantity consumed of a good increases (the general principle behind downward-sloping demand)."],["Marginal utility per dollar","MU of a good divided by its price (MU/P) — the \"bang per buck\" of the last dollar spent on that good."],["Utility-maximizing rule","Spend all income, and equalize MU per dollar across all goods: MUx/Px = MUy/Py."],["Consumer equilibrium","The affordable combination that maximizes total utility given prices and income."],["Paradox of value","Why an essential good (water) can be cheaper than a non-essential one (diamonds) — resolved by distinguishing total utility (large for water) from marginal utility (small for water, since so much is consumed) and by noting price reflects marginal, not total, utility."],["Behavioral economics","Studies how bounded rationality, bounded willpower, and bounded self-interest cause actual decisions to deviate from the standard rational-choice model."],["Endowment effect","The tendency to value something more highly simply because one owns it."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"How the utility-maximizing rule works"},{"t":"p","x":"Starting from any affordable combination: if MUx/Px > MUy/Py, the consumer gets more extra satisfaction per dollar from good x, so should buy more x and less y. As x increases its MU falls (diminishing MU); as y decreases its MU rises — this continues until MUx/Px = MUy/Py, the consumer equilibrium."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figure 8.3 (rows B, C, D of Lisa's just-affordable table): row B has too much soda relative to movies (MUsoda/Psoda < MUmovie/Pmovie); row D has too little soda (MUsoda/Psoda > MUmovie/Pmovie); row C is the equilibrium where the two ratios are equal."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Predictions from the theory"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (original numbers, same method as the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Ben has $32/month for e-books ($8 each) and coffee ($4/cup). Marginal utility: e-books 40, 32, 24, 16 (1st–4th); coffee 20, 16, 14, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4 (1st–8th)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Combination (E, C)","TU from e-books","TU from coffee","Total utility"],["0 e-books, 8 coffees","0","90","90"],["1 e-book, 6 coffees","40","80","120"],["2 e-books, 4 coffees","72","62","134 ← maximum"],["3 e-books, 2 coffees","96","36","132"],["4 e-books, 0 coffees","112","0","112"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Ben's consumer equilibrium is 2 e-books and 4 cups of coffee (total utility = 134, the maximum among affordable combinations). Check: at this point the next (3rd) e-book has MU = 24 → MU/P = 3; the last (4th) coffee has MU = 12 → MU/P = 3 — equal, confirming equilibrium."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Using Ben's data above: at the combination 1 e-book and 6 coffees, is Ben maximizing utility? Use marginal utility per dollar to explain, and state which direction he should adjust his spending."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Confirm the principle of diminishing marginal utility using Ben's coffee schedule (20, 16, 14, 12, 10, 8, 6, 4). Does it hold throughout?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **The paradox of value (water is cheap, diamonds are expensive, despite water being essential) is resolved by recognizing that: (a) water has higher total AND marginal utility than diamonds; (b) price reflects marginal utility, not total utility; (c) diamonds have more total utility than water; (d) diamonds are a public good."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Define behavioral economics' three \"bounds\" on rational choice (bounded rationality, bounded willpower, bounded self-interest) and give one everyday example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **If the price of a good falls so that MUgood/Pgood rises above MUother/Pother, a utility-maximizing consumer will: (a) buy less of the good until MU/P is re-equalized; (b) buy more of the good, which lowers its MU until equality is restored; (c) buy the same amount of both goods; (d) stop buying the other good entirely."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **No. The MU per dollar of the next (2nd) e-book = 32/8 = 4; the MU per dollar of the last (6th) coffee consumed = 8/4 = 2. Since 4 > 2, Ben gains more extra satisfaction per dollar from an additional e-book than from the coffee he is currently buying at the margin — he is not maximizing utility and should buy MORE e-books and LESS coffee (moving toward 2 e-books, 4 coffees, where MU/P is equalized).\n**A2. **Yes — MU falls continuously as quantity rises: 20 → 16 → 14 → 12 → 10 → 8 → 6 → 4, a strictly decreasing sequence with every additional cup, confirming diminishing marginal utility holds throughout this schedule.\n**A3. **(b) — Price is determined by marginal utility (the value of the LAST unit), not total utility. Water has huge total utility but low marginal utility because so much is consumed (its supply is abundant/elastic); diamonds have small total utility but high marginal utility because so little is consumed (supply is scarce/inelastic). At equilibrium, marginal utility per dollar is equalized across goods even though total utilities differ enormously.\n**A4. **Bounded rationality — the human brain has limited computing power, so people use rules of thumb instead of fully optimizing (e.g. picking the first \"good enough\" phone plan rather than comparing every option). Bounded willpower — people know a choice is against their long-term interest but do it anyway (e.g. snoozing the alarm despite planning an early start). Bounded self-interest — people sometimes act to help others at a personal cost (e.g. tipping generously at a restaurant they will never visit again).\n**A5. **(b) — the consumer increases quantity of the now cheaper/more attractive good; diminishing marginal utility means MU falls as more is bought, restoring MU/P equality across goods."}]},"9":{"title":"Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Budget equation","PxQx + PyQy = Y (expenditure = income)."],["Real income","Income expressed as a quantity of goods (the axis-intercept of the budget line)."],["Relative price","The price of one good divided by the price of another; equals the magnitude of the slope of the budget line."],["Indifference curve","A line joining all combinations of two goods among which the consumer is indifferent (equally satisfied)."],["Marginal rate of substitution (MRS)","The rate at which a person is willing to give up good Y to get one more unit of good X while remaining equally satisfied; equals the magnitude of the slope of the indifference curve."],["Diminishing MRS","As the quantity of X consumed rises, the consumer is willing to give up less and less Y for each additional unit of X (indifference curves are bowed toward the origin)."],["Best affordable point","On the budget line, on the highest attainable indifference curve, where MRS = relative price."],["Price effect","The total change in quantity consumed when a price changes = substitution effect + income effect."],["Substitution effect","The change in quantity bought from a price change alone, holding the consumer on the same indifference curve (always moves toward the now relatively cheaper good)."],["Income effect","The change in quantity bought from the resulting change in real income, prices held constant (positive for a normal good, negative for an inferior good)."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Budget line mechanics"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figure 9.1–9.2: Lisa's budget line for movies ($8) and soda ($4) with $40 income runs from 5 movies (0 soda) to 10 cases of soda (0 movies); a fall in income shifts it inward in parallel; a fall in the price of movies rotates it outward around the soda-axis intercept."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Substitution and income effects and the law of demand"},{"t":"p","x":"For a NORMAL good, a price fall triggers a substitution effect (always increases quantity bought of the now-cheaper good) AND a positive income effect (higher real income → buy more of a normal good) — both reinforce each other, guaranteeing the demand curve slopes down. For an INFERIOR good, the income effect is negative and works against the substitution effect; as long as the substitution effect dominates, demand still slopes down. (In the rare theoretical case where the negative income effect dominates — a \"Giffen good\" — demand would slope upward; this essentially never occurs in practice.)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Lisa's budget: $40 income, movie $8, soda $4 → budget line runs from (0 movies, 10 soda) to (5 movies, 0 soda). At point C (2 movies, 6 soda) her MRS = 2 (she'd give up 2 sodas per extra movie), matching the relative price PM/PS = 8/4 = 2 — this is her best affordable point."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **Maya has $60/week to spend on books (price $12) and movies (price $6). Write her budget equation (with movies M on the vertical axis and books B on the horizontal axis), and state the vertical intercept, horizontal intercept, and slope of her budget line."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **If the price of a book in Q1 falls to $6 (movies unchanged at $6), write the new budget equation and describe how the budget line changes (which intercept moves, and how the slope changes)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **At a point on Maya's indifference curve, she is willing to give up 8 movies to get 2 more books while remaining equally satisfied. What is her MRS of books for movies at that point? Using the ORIGINAL prices from Q1 (book $12, movie $6), is she at her best affordable point? If not, what should she do?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **Indifference curves for perfect complements (e.g. left shoes and right shoes) have what shape: (a) straight lines with constant slope; (b) L-shaped (right angles); (c) bowed toward the origin; (d) upward sloping?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Explain why, for a normal good, the substitution effect and income effect of a price decrease work in the SAME direction, and contrast this with an inferior good."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Budget equation: 12B + 6M = 60, i.e. M = 10 − 2B. Vertical intercept (B = 0): M = 10 movies (her real income in movies). Horizontal intercept (M = 0): B = 5 books. Slope = −2 (the relative price of a book in terms of movies, PB/PM = 12/6 = 2).\n**A2. **New equation: 6B + 6M = 60, i.e. M = 10 − B. The vertical intercept stays at M = 10 (unchanged, since the price of movies and income are unchanged). The horizontal intercept moves outward from B = 5 to B = 10. The slope becomes −1 (flatter than before) — the budget line rotates outward around the unchanged vertical intercept, becoming flatter because books are now relatively cheaper.\n**A3. **MRS = 8 movies ÷ 2 books = 4 (she is willing to give up 4 movies per additional book at this point). The relative price is PB/PM = 12/6 = 2. Since her MRS (4) exceeds the relative price (2), she values an additional book more than what the market requires her to sacrifice — she is NOT at her best affordable point and should buy MORE books and FEWER movies; as she does, diminishing MRS will lower her MRS toward 2, the point where MRS = relative price.\n**A4. **(b) — perfect complements are consumed only in fixed proportions, so the indifference curve is L-shaped: extra units of only one good add no extra utility without the matching quantity of the other.\n**A5. **For a normal good: the substitution effect always pushes the consumer toward the good whose relative price fell (this is universal for any price decrease). The income effect is positive for a normal good — a price fall raises real income, and a consumer with more real income buys more of a normal good — so it reinforces the substitution effect, guaranteeing quantity demanded rises (the law of demand). For an INFERIOR good, the income effect is negative (higher real income → LESS of the inferior good is bought), which works AGAINST the substitution effect. As long as the (positive) substitution effect is larger in magnitude than the (negative) income effect, demand still slopes down overall; only in the theoretical Giffen-good case would the negative income effect dominate and reverse the law of demand."}]},"11":{"title":"Output and Costs","notes":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Learning Objectives"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Key Definitions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Term","Definition"],["Short run","A time frame in which the quantity of at least one resource (typically the firm's plant/capital) is fixed."],["Long run","A time frame in which the quantities of ALL resources, including plant size, can be varied."],["Sunk cost","A cost already incurred that cannot be recovered; irrelevant to current decisions."],["Total product (TP)","Total output produced in a period, for a given quantity of the variable input (usually labor)."],["Marginal product (MP) of labor","Change in total product from one more unit of labor, other inputs constant."],["Average product (AP) of labor","Total product ÷ quantity of labor."],["Law of diminishing returns","As more of a variable input is used with a given quantity of fixed inputs, its marginal product eventually diminishes."],["Total fixed cost (TFC)","Cost of fixed inputs; does not change with output."],["Total variable cost (TVC)","Cost of variable inputs; rises with output."],["Total cost (TC)","TC = TFC + TVC."],["Marginal cost (MC)","Increase in TC from a one-unit increase in output."],["Average fixed cost (AFC) / Average variable cost (AVC) / Average total cost (ATC)","TFC/Q, TVC/Q, TC/Q respectively. ATC = AFC + AVC."],["Economies of scale","Long-run average cost (LRAC) falls as output (and plant size) increases."],["Diseconomies of scale","LRAC rises as output increases."],["Constant returns to scale","LRAC stays constant as output increases."],["Minimum efficient scale (MES)","The smallest output at which LRAC reaches its minimum."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Concepts Explained"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Key relationships to remember"},{"t":"p","x":"**Diagram in original slides: **Figures 11.1–11.9: (i) TP curve rising and flattening (like a PPF); (ii) MP curve rising then falling, with bars whose heights are the marginal product of each successive worker; (iii) U-shaped AVC and ATC curves with the MC curve cutting through both minimum points; (iv) the LRAC envelope curve built from several short-run ATC curves (one per plant size), each tangent to LRAC at one output level, illustrating economies of scale (downward-sloping section), constant returns (flat section) and diseconomies of scale (upward-sloping section)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Worked Example (from the slides)"},{"t":"p","x":"Sweater knitting: a firm choosing between 1, 2, 3 or 4 knitting machines to produce 13 sweaters/day finds ATC = $7.69 (1 machine), $6.80 (2 machines), $7.69 (3 machines), $9.50 (4 machines). The least-cost plant for 13 sweaters/day is 2 machines ($6.80) — this point lies on the long-run average cost curve."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **A firm's total product schedule is: Labor (workers) 0,1,2,3,4,5,6 → Total product 0, 8, 20, 30, 36, 40, 42. Compute marginal product (MP) and average product (AP) at each labor level. At which labor level do increasing marginal returns end and diminishing marginal returns begin?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **Using the total product schedule above, suppose the daily wage is $50/worker (the only variable cost) and total fixed cost is $100/day. (a) Complete a table of TVC, TC, AFC, AVC, ATC, and MC at each output level (8, 20, 30, 36, 40, 42 units). (b) At what output is AVC lowest? (c) At what output is ATC lowest? (d) Confirm that MC is below ATC while ATC is falling and above ATC while ATC is rising."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **A firm's average total cost is falling as output increases. This implies that: (a) marginal cost must be falling too; (b) marginal cost is below average total cost; (c) marginal cost is above average total cost; (d) marginal cost equals average fixed cost."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **A firm can build a small, medium, or large plant. The short-run ATC for producing 200 units/day is: Small = $25, Medium = $18, Large = $30. Which plant should the firm use to produce 200 units in the long run? What does the pattern of these three values suggest about economies/diseconomies of scale around this output?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **Define minimum efficient scale and explain its significance for how many firms can efficiently operate in an industry."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **MP: L1 = 8, L2 = 12, L3 = 10, L4 = 6, L5 = 4, L6 = 2. AP: L1 = 8.0, L2 = 10.0, L3 = 10.0, L4 = 9.0, L5 = 8.0, L6 = 7.0. Marginal product RISES from L1 (8) to L2 (12) — increasing marginal returns — then FALLS from L3 onward (12→10→6→4→2) — diminishing marginal returns begin at the 3rd worker.\n**A2. **(a) TVC = $50 × L, TC = TVC + $100:  Q=8 (L=1): TVC=$50, TC=$150, AFC=$12.50, AVC=$6.25, ATC=$18.75.  Q=20 (L=2): TVC=$100, TC=$200, AFC=$5.00, AVC=$5.00, ATC=$10.00.  Q=30 (L=3): TVC=$150, TC=$250, AFC=$3.33, AVC=$5.00, ATC=$8.33.  Q=36 (L=4): TVC=$200, TC=$300, AFC=$2.78, AVC=$5.56, ATC=$8.33.  Q=40 (L=5): TVC=$250, TC=$350, AFC=$2.50, AVC=$6.25, ATC=$8.75.  Q=42 (L=6): TVC=$300, TC=$400, AFC=$2.38, AVC=$7.14, ATC=$9.52.  Marginal cost (ΔTC/ΔQ between successive labor levels): 0→8: $6.25; 8→20: $4.17; 20→30: $5.00; 30→36: $8.33; 36→40: $12.50; 40→42: $25.00. (b) AVC is lowest ($5.00) at Q = 20 and Q = 30 (tied — a plateau, typical with discrete data). (c) ATC is lowest ($8.33) at Q = 30 and Q = 36 (tied). (d) Over 8→20 (MC=$4.17) ATC falls from $18.75 to $10.00: MC < ATC ✓. Around Q=30–36 (MC=$8.33) ATC is at its $8.33 minimum: MC ≈ ATC ✓. Over 36→40 (MC=$12.50) ATC rises from $8.33 to $8.75: MC > ATC ✓ — confirming the MC/ATC crossing rule.\n**A3. **(b) — while ATC is falling, MC must be pulling it down from below, exactly like a new test score below your GPA average pulls the average down.\n**A4. **Use the Medium plant ($18/unit, the lowest ATC for 200 units) — this point lies on the LRAC curve. Since ATC rises again with the Large plant ($30), the firm has moved from economies of scale (Small→Medium, ATC falls) into diseconomies of scale (Medium→Large, ATC rises) around this output level; the Medium plant is at or near the minimum efficient scale for this output.\n**A5. **Minimum efficient scale (MES) is the smallest output level at which long-run average cost reaches its minimum. If MES is LARGE relative to total market demand, the market can support only a few large, low-cost firms (tending toward oligopoly or natural monopoly). If MES is SMALL relative to market demand, many small firms can each operate at the lowest attainable cost, supporting a competitive market structure with many firms."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2105**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Microeconomics for Business**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Workbook"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University · First Term, 2026–27 · Section 7 (Dr. Albert Wong)"},{"t":"p","x":"Textbook: Parkin, Economics, 14th Global Edition (Pearson / MyLab Economics)"},{"t":"p","x":"Chapters covered: 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11"},{"t":"p","x":"Compiled from all lecture materials shared on Moodle, with original practice exercises and fully worked answer keys."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Table of Contents"},{"t":"p","x":"(Right-click and choose \"Update Field\" in Word to populate/refresh this list.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Information"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Item","Details"],["Course","BUS 2105 Microeconomics for Business (Section 7), 2026–27 First Term"],["Instructor","Prof. Wong Man Chung, Albert — SEK101/2, Tel. 2616 7852, albertwong@ln.edu.hk"],["Textbook","Parkin, Michael. Economics, 14th Global Edition (2022) + MyLab Economics with e-text"],["MyLab Course ID (Section L4 & L7)","lingnan52752"],["Course description","Study of the choices individuals and firms make and how these choices interact in markets; characteristics of different market structures; economic environment of business."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assessment Breakdown"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Component","Weight"],["Final Examination","50%"],["Midterm Test (in-class, MC, covers Ch. 1, 3–6, 8, 9)","20%"],["MyLab Economics assignments*","10%"],["Group Debate","15%"],["Participation","5%"]]},{"t":"p","x":"*If a student does not use MyLab Economics, the midterm weight becomes 30% instead."},{"t":"p","x":"Midterm test: Monday 2 November 2026, in class, 40 MC questions, 1 hour, covering Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Course Schedule (as shared on Moodle)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Week / Date","Topic","Reading"],["7 Sep","Introduction","Ch. 1"],["14 Sep","Demand and Supply","Ch. 3"],["21 Sep","Elasticity","Ch. 4"],["28 Sep","Efficiency, Equity","Ch. 5"],["5 Oct","Government Actions in Markets; Utility and Demand","Ch. 6, Ch. 8"],["12 Oct","Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices","Ch. 9"],["19 Oct","Chung Yeung Festival — no class","—"],["26 Oct","Output and Costs","Ch. 11"],["2 Nov","Midterm Test; Perfect Competition (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 12"],["9 Nov","Monopoly (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 13"],["16 Nov","Monopolistic Competition (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 14"],["23 Nov","Oligopoly / Course Review (not in this synthesis — slides not shared)","Ch. 15"],["30 Nov","Debate","—"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: Chapters 2, 7, 10, 12, 13, 14 and 15 were not among the files shared on Moodle for this synthesis and are therefore not covered in the chapter notes below. This document covers exactly the eight chapters for which lecture slides were provided: Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 and 11."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"MyLab Economics — Quick Registration Note"},{"t":"p","x":"MyLab Economics is Pearson's online homework/quiz platform used for 10% of the course grade (one quiz per chapter; best 8 of 12 scores count). To register: go to https://mlm.pearson.com/global, enter the Course ID for your section (L4/L7 = lingnan52752), create or sign in to a Pearson account, and redeem your access code (from the textbook bundle). Use Chrome for best results, with pop-ups and cookies enabled. Full step-by-step screenshots are in the separate \"MyLab Econ Student Registration\" file on Moodle — this synthesis does not reproduce them since they contain no economics content to revise."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Exam-Style Review Checklist (All Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this list to self-test before the midterm (Ch. 1, 3–6, 8, 9) and the final (all chapters). Tick each item only once you can explain it out loud without notes."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 1 — What is Economics?"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define scarcity, incentive, microeconomics, macroeconomics."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 4 factors of production and what each earns (rent/wages/interest/profit)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish positive vs normative statements with an example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain rational choice and the MB vs MC decision rule."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain opportunity cost, including correctly excluding sunk costs."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 3 — Demand and Supply"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the law of demand and the law of supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List all 6 demand shifters and all 6 supply shifters."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish a movement along a curve from a shift of the curve."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Solve algebraically for equilibrium price and quantity from linear Qd/Qs equations."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Predict price and quantity changes for all 4 basic and 4 combined shift scenarios."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 4 — Elasticity"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate PED, YED, and XED using the midpoint (average) method."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Classify demand as elastic / unit elastic / inelastic / perfectly elastic or inelastic."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Apply the total revenue test correctly (link price change, elasticity, and TR)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 3 determinants of PED and the 2 determinants of elasticity of supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Interpret the sign of income elasticity (normal vs inferior) and cross elasticity (substitute vs complement)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 5 — Efficiency and Equity"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the 8 resource allocation methods and when each works best."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why the demand curve is a marginal benefit curve and the supply curve is a marginal cost curve."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus from linear demand/supply."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the efficiency condition (MSB = MSC) and calculate deadweight loss for under- and over-production."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ List the sources of market failure and contrast the fair-rules view with the fair-results view."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 6 — Government Actions in Markets"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why a binding price ceiling creates a shortage, and a binding price floor creates a surplus."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain the tax wedge and calculate the buyer's and seller's share of a per-unit tax."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain how relative elasticities of demand and supply determine tax incidence."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Calculate tax revenue and deadweight loss from a per-unit tax."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish the effects of a production quota (underproduction) from a subsidy (overproduction)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 8 — Utility and Demand"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define total utility and marginal utility, and state the principle of diminishing marginal utility."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Apply the utility-maximizing rule (spend all income; equalize MU per dollar across goods)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Solve a consumer-choice problem using a table of just-affordable combinations."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain the paradox of value using total vs marginal utility."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Name the three \"bounds\" on rational choice from behavioral economics."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 9 — Possibilities, Preferences, and Choices"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Write and interpret a budget equation; identify intercepts and slope (relative price)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain how a price change rotates, and an income change shifts, the budget line."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Define MRS and diminishing MRS, and state the 3 conditions for the best affordable point."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Decompose a price effect into substitution effect and income effect."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain why demand slopes down for a normal good and (usually) for an inferior good."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Ch. 11 — Output and Costs"},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Distinguish the short run from the long run, and identify a sunk cost."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Derive and relate TP, MP, and AP; state the law of diminishing returns."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Derive TFC, TVC, TC, AFC, AVC, ATC, and MC from a cost/output schedule."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ State the MC–AVC–ATC relationship (crossing at each average's minimum)."},{"t":"p","x":"☐ Explain economies of scale, diseconomies of scale, and minimum efficient scale using the LRAC curve."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Set (Spanning Multiple Chapters)"},{"t":"p","x":"A cumulative-style set similar in spirit to the midterm/final. Mixes conceptual, MCQ and quantitative questions across Chapters 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9 and 11."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Practice Questions"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q1. **[Ch.1] Diego is deciding whether to attend a weekend conference. The conference fee is $150. If he attends, he will miss a day of freelance work worth $300. He has already paid a non-refundable $50 deposit for a photography workshop that overlaps with the conference (he loses this $50 regardless of his choice). What is Diego's opportunity cost of attending the conference?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q2. **[Ch.3] The market for umbrellas has Qd = 500 − 20P and Qs = 20P − 100. (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) An unusually rainy season increases demand by 200 units at every price. Find the new equilibrium price and quantity."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q3. **[Ch.4] A cinema cuts ticket prices from $15 to $13 (average $14), and weekly attendance rises from 2,000 to 2,400 (average 2,200). (a) Calculate the price elasticity of demand. (b) What happens to total revenue? (c) Is demand elastic or inelastic over this range?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q4. **[Ch.5] Demand for a good is Qd = 200 − 4P and supply is Qs = 6P − 50. (a) Find the equilibrium price and quantity. (b) Calculate consumer surplus and producer surplus at equilibrium."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q5. **[Ch.6] Before any tax, the equilibrium price of a bottle of wine is $20 with 100,000 bottles sold monthly. A $5/bottle excise tax is imposed. After the tax, consumers pay $23/bottle and quantity falls to 90,000. (a) How much of the tax do consumers pay vs. producers? (b) What is monthly tax revenue? (c) What is the deadweight loss?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q6. **[Ch.8] Priya has $18 to spend on tea ($3/cup) and pastries ($3 each). Her marginal utility schedule (1st–6th unit) is: Tea: 15, 12, 9, 6, 3, 0. Pastries: 18, 15, 12, 9, 6, 3. Find her utility-maximizing combination(s)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q7. **[Ch.9] Sam has $40/week to spend on video games (price $10) and books (price $5). Write his budget equation with games (G) on the vertical axis and books (B) on the horizontal axis, and state the opportunity cost of one more book in terms of games."},{"t":"p","x":"**Q8. **[Ch.11] A firm has total fixed cost of $200. At Q = 50 units, total variable cost is $300. At Q = 100 units, total variable cost is $500. (a) Compute average total cost (ATC) at Q = 50 and Q = 100. (b) Compute the average marginal cost over this range. (c) Is average variable cost (AVC) rising or falling over this range, and is this consistent with your marginal cost answer?"},{"t":"p","x":"**Q9. **[Ch.5 + Ch.6] A city imposes a rent ceiling below the market-clearing rent. Using the concepts of consumer surplus, producer surplus, and deadweight loss, explain why this policy is inefficient even though some renters clearly benefit from paying a lower rent."},{"t":"a","x":"**A1. **Opportunity cost = conference fee ($150) + forgone freelance earnings ($300) = $450. The $50 workshop deposit is a SUNK COST — it is lost either way and does not belong in the opportunity-cost calculation.\n**A2. **(a) 500 − 20P = 20P − 100 → 600 = 40P → P = $15; Q = 500 − 20(15) = 200 units. (b) New demand: Qd′ = 700 − 20P. Set 700 − 20P = 20P − 100 → 800 = 40P → P = $20; Q = 20(20) − 100 = 300 units. (Price rises from $15 to $20 and quantity rises from 200 to 300.)\n**A3. **(a) %ΔQ = (2,400−2,000)/2,200 = 18.2%. %ΔP = (13−15)/14 = −14.3% (magnitude 14.3%). PED = 18.2%/14.3% ≈ 1.27. (b) TR before = $15×2,000 = $30,000; TR after = $13×2,400 = $31,200 — total revenue RISES when price falls. (c) Demand is ELASTIC (PED > 1), consistent with revenue rising after the price cut.\n**A4. **(a) 200 − 4P = 6P − 50 → 250 = 10P → P = $25; Q = 200 − 4(25) = 100 units. (b) Demand's price-intercept (Q=0): 4P=200 → P=$50; CS = ½×(50−25)×100 = $1,250. Supply's price-intercept (Q=0): 6P=50 → P≈$8.33; PS = ½×(25−8.33)×100 ≈ $833.33. Total surplus ≈ $2,083.33.\n**A5. **(a) Consumers pay $3/bottle more ($20→$23); producers receive $2/bottle less ($20→$18, since $23−$5 tax=$18). (b) Tax revenue = $5 × 90,000 = $450,000/month. (c) Deadweight loss = ½ × $5 × (100,000−90,000) = ½×5×10,000 = $25,000/month.\n**A6. **Since tea and pastries have the same price ($3), just-affordable combinations satisfy T + P = 6. Cumulative utility: Tea (0,15,27,36,42,45,45 for 0–6 cups), Pastry (0,18,33,45,54,60,63 for 0–6). Combination totals: (0,6)=63; (1,5)=75; (2,4)=81; (3,3)=81; (4,2)=75; (5,1)=63; (6,0)=45. Maximum total utility = 81, achieved at EITHER 2 tea + 4 pastries OR 3 tea + 3 pastries — a tie, because with equal prices the crossover happens exactly at the boundary between these two combinations (the marginal utilities of the 3rd tea and 4th pastry are both 9, equal to each other).\n**A7. **Budget equation: 10G + 5B = 40 → G = 4 − 0.5B. Vertical intercept: G = 4 games (if B = 0). Horizontal intercept: B = 8 books (if G = 0). The opportunity cost of one more book = 0.5 games (the relative price PB/PG = 5/10 = 0.5, the magnitude of the budget line's slope).\n**A8. **(a) At Q=50: TC = $200+$300 = $500, ATC = $500/50 = $10. At Q=100: TC = $200+$500 = $700, ATC = $700/100 = $7. (b) Average MC = ΔTVC/ΔQ = ($500−$300)/(100−50) = $200/50 = $4/unit. (c) AVC(50) = $300/50 = $6; AVC(100) = $500/100 = $5 — AVC is FALLING over this range, which is consistent with theory because MC ($4) is below both AVC values, pulling the average down.\n**A9. **A rent ceiling below equilibrium restricts the quantity of housing supplied below the efficient quantity, so marginal social benefit (MSB) exceeds marginal social cost (MSC) on the units no longer supplied — this is underproduction. The consumer surplus of the few tenants who obtain housing at the lower regulated rent may rise, but producer surplus (landlords) shrinks substantially, and the units that are no longer built or rented represent a pure deadweight loss (lost surplus that no one captures) — compounded further by the real resource costs of increased search activity and black-market activity. Because total surplus (CS+PS) falls even though a subset of renters individually gains, the policy is inefficient overall, even though it may still be judged \"fair\" or \"unfair\" on separate equity grounds."}]},"BUS2202":{"file":"BUS2202_Organisational_Behaviour_Synthese_et_Exercices.docx","mtime":"2026-10-11","lessons":{"1":{"title":"Introduction to the Field of Organisational Behaviour","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 1   |   Scheduled: 7 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is Organisational Behaviour?"},{"t":"li","x":"**Organisational behaviour (OB): **the study of what people think, feel and do in and around organisations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Organisations: **groups of people who work interdependently toward some purpose."},{"t":"p","x":"OB matters for individuals (helps you understand and predict workplace events, and get things done by influencing/coordinating with others — employers rate OB skills as highly important) and for organisations (OB theory improves organisational effectiveness)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Organisational Effectiveness"},{"t":"p","x":"An ideal state in which the organisation:"},{"t":"li","x":"Has a good fit with its external environment — organisations are \"open systems\" that take in inputs and transform them into outputs."},{"t":"li","x":"Effectively transforms inputs into outputs, especially through its human capital."},{"t":"li","x":"Satisfies the needs of its key stakeholders."},{"t":"li","x":"**Human capital: **the knowledge, skills, abilities, creative thinking and other valued resources employees bring — essential for survival, difficult to find/copy, and hard to replace with technology."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stakeholders: **any entity that affects or is affected by the firm’s objectives and actions; firms must manage conflicting stakeholder interests with limited resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR): **activities intended to benefit society/environment beyond the firm’s immediate financial or legal obligations — the \"triple bottom line\" of economic, social and environmental performance."},{"t":"li","x":"**Integrative Model of OB: **the course’s big-picture framework, showing how the environment and organisational context flow into individual and team inputs and processes (e.g., MARS, perceptions, teams, leadership — the topics of Lessons 1-10), which in turn drive individual, team and organisational-level outcomes (performance, effectiveness). Every later chapter in this course \"plugs into\" this overall model at the individual, team, or organisational level of analysis."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Five Anchors of OB Knowledge"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Anchor","Meaning"],["Systematic research","OB knowledge is built on rigorous research; organisations should practice evidence-based management."],["Practical orientation","OB theories must be usable in practice — application is the true measure of a theory’s impact."],["Multidisciplinary","Many OB concepts are borrowed from psychology, sociology, economics, etc., while OB also develops its own theories."],["Contingency","The same action can have different consequences in different situations — the situation must be diagnosed before choosing an action."],["Multiple levels of analysis","OB topics are usually relevant at the individual, team and organisational levels."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Emerging Workplace Trends"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the Workplace"},{"t":"li","x":"AI = technologies that autonomously simulate human intelligence/cognitive capabilities (predictive, generative, and — potentially — artificial general intelligence)."},{"t":"li","x":"Risks: incomplete/flawed input data causing \"hallucinations\"; error amplification when AI is trained on its own outputs (\"AI poisons itself\"); imperfect prompting produces flawed output."},{"t":"li","x":"Increasingly relevant to OB topics such as decision making, job design and team dynamics."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Inclusive Workplace and Diversity"},{"t":"li","x":"**Surface-level diversity: **observable demographic/physiological differences."},{"t":"li","x":"**Deep-level diversity: **psychological differences (values, beliefs, personality) — includes generational diversity, which is more about life stage than generational cohort."},{"t":"li","x":"**Benefits: **better decisions, attitudes and team performance, more creativity, better representation of community needs, moral/legal imperative."},{"t":"li","x":"**Challenges: **teams take longer to become effective, and diversity can bring more dysfunctional conflict, lower information sharing and lower morale if not managed well."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Work-Life Integration, Remote/Hybrid Work, Employment Relationships"},{"t":"li","x":"**Work-life integration: **being effectively engaged in work and nonwork roles with low role conflict; depleting resources in one role starves the others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Remote work: **performing the job away from the organisation’s premises. Benefits: better work-life integration, lower turnover, higher productivity, environmental and cost benefits. Risks: social isolation, less informal communication, lower team cohesion, weaker organisational culture."},{"t":"li","x":"**Hybrid work: **mixing home and office work; can create tension between employees and executives over expectations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Three employment relationships: **direct employment (higher work quality/innovation/agility, but risk of lower satisfaction among indirect workers on mixed teams); indirect employment (outsourced/agency — generally lower job satisfaction, weaker social networks, ambiguous manager discretion); contract employment (worker serves a client through their own firm)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. The MARS Model of Individual Behaviour and Performance"},{"t":"p","x":"Four factors directly determine individual behaviour and performance — remembered as MARS:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","Definition","Key point"],["Motivation","Internal forces affecting direction, intensity and persistence of voluntary effort.","Determines whether/how hard someone tries."],["Ability","Aptitudes (natural talents) and learned capabilities needed to complete a task.","Improved via person-job matching: selecting, developing, or redesigning jobs."],["Role perceptions","Understanding of the job duties expected of us (tasks, priorities, preferred behaviours/procedures).","Clear role perceptions improve performance, coordination and motivation."],["Situational factors","Context beyond the person’s immediate control.","Can be a constraint, a facilitator, or a cue for behaviour."]]},{"t":"p","x":"All four MARS factors must be present for good performance — a deficiency in any one of them will constrain behaviour and results, regardless of how strong the others are."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Five Types of Individual Behaviour in Organisations"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Task performance: **voluntary, goal-directed behaviours — proficient (performing known tasks well), adaptive (adjusting to change), and proactive (self-initiated improvement)."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Organisational citizenship behaviours (OCBs): **cooperative, helpful behaviours that support the work context beyond formal job duties; directed at individuals and/or the organisation; some are discretionary, others near-implicit job requirements; can have negative side effects (e.g., overload) if excessive."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Counterproductive work behaviours: **voluntary behaviours that may harm the organisation (e.g., theft, harassment, sabotage)."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Joining and staying with the organisation: **relates to recruitment/retention — skills shortages and high turnover are ongoing challenges."},{"t":"li","x":"**5. Maintaining work attendance: **absenteeism (driven mainly by situational factors and motivation) and presenteeism (attending work while significantly unwell/impaired, reducing effective capacity)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: According to the MARS model, which factor refers to an employee’s understanding of what tasks they are accountable for and which behaviours are preferred?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Motivation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Ability"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Role perceptions"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Situational factors"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A completely remote software company with no head office, whose staff work from home worldwide, is an example of which emerging workplace trend?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Hybrid work"},{"t":"p","x":"b) A fully distributed/remote organisation"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Indirect employment"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Presenteeism"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain, using the \"open systems\" idea, why organisational effectiveness requires more than just producing outputs efficiently."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish surface-level diversity from deep-level diversity, and give one workplace example of each."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A talented graphic designer at a marketing agency consistently misses deadlines. She clearly wants to do well and has the right design skills, but she says she is \"never quite sure what the client actually wants approved by when.\" Using the MARS model, diagnose which factor is most likely deficient here, and recommend one concrete fix."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Role perceptions — the scenario describes understanding of tasks, priorities and preferred behaviours, which is the definition of role perceptions in the MARS model.\n**2. **b) A fully distributed/remote organisation (like Emsisoft in the slides) — the whole company operates with no physical head office; this differs from hybrid work, which mixes home and office time for the same employees.\n**3. **Being an \"open system\" means an organisation depends on a good fit with its external environment (getting the right inputs, meeting stakeholder/market needs) in addition to internally converting inputs to outputs. A firm can be internally efficient yet still fail if it is poorly aligned with its environment or does not satisfy key stakeholders — so effectiveness = good external fit + efficient transformation + stakeholder satisfaction, not efficiency alone.\n**4. **Surface-level diversity refers to observable, demographic or physiological differences (e.g., age, gender, ethnicity, visible disability). Deep-level diversity refers to psychological differences that are less visible (e.g., values, personality, attitudes, beliefs) — for example, two employees of the same age and gender (similar surface-level) might hold very different work values (different deep-level diversity).\n**5. **The most likely deficiency is role perceptions: she has ability (design skill) and apparently motivation (wants to do well), but lacks clarity on tasks/priorities/expected approval process (\"never sure what... approved by when\"). Fix: clarify role perceptions directly — e.g., a written client-approval workflow/checklist specifying who signs off, by when, and in what format, agreed with her manager and the client-facing team, so expectations and priorities are explicit rather than assumed."}]},"2":{"title":"Individual Behaviour, Personality and Values","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 2   |   Scheduled: 14 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Personality"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **a relatively enduring pattern of thoughts, emotions and behaviours that characterizes a person, plus the psychological processes behind them. Personality traits are categories of behaviour tendencies that are evident across situations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Nature vs. nurture: **heredity explains roughly 50% of behavioural tendencies (nature); the rest is shaped by socialization and learning (nurture). Personality stabilizes in young adulthood as self-concept and executive function (self-regulation) mature, though some traits can still change over the life course."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Big Five (Five-Factor) Personality Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Factor","High scorers tend to be...","Workplace relevance"],["Openness to experience","Imaginative, curious, unconventional","May predict creative performance"],["Conscientiousness","Careful, disciplined, organized, achievement-oriented","Best overall predictor of job performance across jobs; weak predictor of adaptive/proactive performance"],["Extraversion","Outgoing, talkative, sociable, assertive","Associated with effective leaders and salespeople"],["Agreeableness","Trusting, cooperative, compassionate","Predicts team-member and customer-service performance; weaker predictor of proficient/proactive performance"],["Neuroticism (emotional stability)","Anxious, insecure (low stability) vs. calm, resilient (high stability)","Low neuroticism linked to better stress tolerance"]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Issues with the Big Five model: **"},{"t":"li","x":"Higher scores are not always \"better\" (e.g., extreme conscientiousness can reduce flexibility)."},{"t":"li","x":"Narrower facets within a factor (e.g., assertiveness within extraversion) sometimes predict better than the broad factor."},{"t":"li","x":"Personality is not perfectly static over time."},{"t":"li","x":"The five-factor model does not capture every perspective on personality (e.g., the Dark Triad and Jungian/MBTI types only partially overlap with it)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"MBTI (Myers-Briggs Type Indicator) / Jungian Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"The most widely used personality test and the most widely studied measure of cognitive style."},{"t":"li","x":"Takes a neutral view of results (no \"good\"/\"bad\" type) and improves self-awareness and mutual understanding."},{"t":"li","x":"Poor at predicting job performance, effective leadership, or team development — caution is advised before using it to select job applicants."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Dark Triad of Personality"},{"t":"p","x":"Three overlapping but distinct \"dark side\" personality traits, discussed alongside leadership because they help explain toxic leadership and workplace misconduct."},{"t":"li","x":"**Machiavellianism: **strong motivation to get what one wants at others’ expense; believes deceit is a natural/acceptable tool; enjoys misleading, outwitting or controlling others; rarely trusts or empathizes with coworkers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Narcissism: **an obsessive belief in one’s own superiority and entitlement, an excessive need for attention/admiration, and intense envy of others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Psychopathy: **ruthless, manipulative \"social predators\"; the \"mask of psychopathy\" is superficial charm hiding selfish self-promotion; impulsive, antisocial, sometimes fraudulent, thrill-seeking behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"**Workplace consequences: **more organisational politics (influence tactics for personal gain), higher risk of serious white-collar crime, workplace aggression/bullying (linked to psychopathy), poorer decision-making, and typically less effective team behaviour — though the Dark Triad can occasionally correlate with some positive outcomes (e.g., short-term charm, risk-taking), the overall workplace effect is negative."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Values in the Workplace"},{"t":"li","x":"**Values: **stable, evaluative beliefs that guide our preferences and define what is right/wrong or good/bad — what we feel we \"ought\" to do; organized into a value system (a personal hierarchy of values)."},{"t":"p","x":"Compared with personality, values are more evaluative (not just descriptive), can conflict with each other, and are shaped more by nurture than nature."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Schwartz’s Values Model"},{"t":"p","x":"57 values grouped into 10 categories, and further into 4 broader quadrants:"},{"t":"li","x":"Openness to change — motivated to pursue new/innovative ways."},{"t":"li","x":"Conservation — motivated to preserve the status quo/tradition."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-enhancement — motivated by self-interest."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-transcendence — motivated to promote the welfare of others and nature."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"How Values Influence Decisions and Behaviour"},{"t":"li","x":"They affect the relative attractiveness of different choices."},{"t":"li","x":"They frame how we perceive situations."},{"t":"li","x":"They motivate us to act consistently with our self-concept and public image."},{"t":"p","x":"**When values fail to predict behaviour**"},{"t":"li","x":"Situational barriers or lack of opportunity."},{"t":"li","x":"Counter-motivational forces (competing pressures)."},{"t":"li","x":"Lack of values awareness — values are abstract and easy to overlook during routine behaviour."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Values Congruence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the similarity between a person’s value hierarchy and that of another source (a team, or the organisation)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team values congruence: **linked to higher team cohesion and performance."},{"t":"li","x":"**Person-organisation values congruence: **linked to higher job satisfaction, loyalty and organisational citizenship, and lower stress and turnover."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Ethical Values and Behaviour"},{"t":"li","x":"**Ethics: **the study of moral principles/values and whether actions/outcomes are right/wrong, good/bad."},{"t":"p","x":"Four ethical principles:"},{"t":"li","x":"Utilitarianism — the greatest good for the greatest number."},{"t":"li","x":"Individual rights — everyone holds the same natural rights."},{"t":"li","x":"Distributive justice — benefits and burdens should be shared equally or proportionally."},{"t":"li","x":"Ethic of care — a moral obligation to help others."},{"t":"li","x":"**Moral intensity: **the degree an issue demands ethical consideration — higher when consequences are large, there’s wide agreement the outcome is good/bad, the outcome is probable, and many people are affected."},{"t":"li","x":"**Moral sensitivity: **a person’s ability to detect and judge the importance of a moral dilemma — higher with more expertise/knowledge of norms, relevant past experience, empathy, a self-view as an ethical person, and mindfulness."},{"t":"li","x":"**Supporting ethical behaviour: **a corporate code of ethics, educating/testing employees’ ethical knowledge, systems for reporting/investigating wrongdoing, and an ethical culture with ethical leadership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Values Across Cultures (Cross-Cultural Dimensions)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Dimension","High end","Low end / contrast"],["Individualism","Values personal freedom, self-sufficiency, being recognized for unique qualities (e.g., USA)","Low individualism societies value group ties more (contrast with collectivism)"],["Collectivism","Values group membership and harmonious relationships within the group (e.g., China/Taiwan)","Low collectivism (e.g., some individualist Western societies)"],["Power distance","Accepts unequal power, obedience to authority, formal rules (e.g., China, India)","Expects relatively equal power sharing, interdependent boss relationship (e.g., Sweden, Denmark)"],["Uncertainty avoidance","Feels threatened by ambiguity; values structure and direct communication (e.g., Greece, Belgium)","Tolerates ambiguity and uncertainty (e.g., Singapore, Denmark)"],["Achievement vs. nurturing orientation","Assertiveness, competitiveness, materialism (e.g., Japan, USA)","Values relationships and human interaction (e.g., Netherlands, Sweden)"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Note: individualism and collectivism are conceptually distinct (not simply opposite ends of one scale) — a culture/person can, in principle, score independently on each."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: Which of the Big Five personality factors is generally the strongest overall predictor of job performance across most types of jobs?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Openness to experience"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Extraversion"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Conscientiousness"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Agreeableness"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: An employee who takes pleasure in outwitting and controlling coworkers, believes deceit is an acceptable tool to get ahead, and rarely trusts others, best illustrates which Dark Triad trait?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Narcissism"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Psychopathy"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Machiavellianism"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Neuroticism"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain why values are described as \"more evaluative than descriptive\" compared with personality, and why this matters for predicting workplace behaviour."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using Hofstede-style cultural values, explain why a manager from a high power-distance culture might struggle to motivate employees using a highly participative decision-making style in a country with much lower power distance (or vice versa)."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A new hire tells his manager during onboarding, \"I really value work-life balance and creative freedom,\" but three months in he is consistently the first to arrive, stays late without being asked, and volunteers for structured, high-pressure client-facing projects. Using the \"values don’t always predict behaviour\" concepts, give two plausible explanations for this apparent values-behaviour gap."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Conscientiousness — it is described in the slides as a weak predictor only of adaptive/proactive performance, but it is the best-established overall predictor of general task performance across most job types.\n**2. **c) Machiavellianism — the specific combination of strategic deceit-as-acceptable, pleasure in manipulation, and low trust/empathy toward coworkers is the defining pattern of Machiavellianism, distinct from narcissism (entitlement/need for admiration) and psychopathy (impulsive, antisocial, superficially charming).\n**3. **Personality traits describe how someone typically behaves (\"what is\"), while values are evaluative — they define what someone believes ought to be done (right/wrong, good/bad) and guide preferences. This matters because two people can share the same value (e.g., \"honesty\") yet act differently depending on situational opportunity, competing motivations, or whether the value is salient/consciously activated in that moment — values are a weaker, more conditional predictor of specific behaviour than trait-behaviour links tend to be.\n**4. **In a high power-distance culture, employees are socialized to expect and be comfortable with unequal power, deference to authority and top-down decisions; being suddenly asked to freely voice opinions or share decision authority can feel uncomfortable, risky, or even like a sign of a weak/incompetent leader. Conversely, in a low power-distance culture, employees expect relatively equal power sharing and an interdependent (not purely dependent) relationship with their boss, so a highly directive, non-participative style can feel demotivating or be seen as illegitimate. Either mismatch between the leader’s style and the culture’s power-distance norms can undermine motivation — this is the same logic behind path-goal theory’s cultural contingencies covered in the leadership lesson.\n**5. **Two plausible explanations: (1) Situational factors/lack of opportunity — e.g., a genuinely demanding early-career workload, an under-resourced team, or a manager who implicitly rewards visibility, may be overriding his stated values as situational barriers. (2) Impression management / self-concept motivation early in a new job — he may be acting to build a strong first impression or prove commitment (consistent with self-enhancement/self-verification motives), with the values-consistent behaviour expected to reappear once he is established. (A third acceptable answer: low values awareness — values are abstract, and in the day-to-day routine of a new job, habitual/reactive behaviour can simply override an abstract stated value that is not consciously \"front of mind.\")"}]},"3":{"title":"Perception and Learning in Organisations","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 3   |   Scheduled: 21 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Self-Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **our self-beliefs and self-evaluations, comparing our current (perceived) self with our desired (ideal) self, at three levels: individual, relational and collective."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Three Cs of Self-Concept"},{"t":"li","x":"**Complexity: **the number of distinct, important identities a person perceives in themselves; higher when the selves are separate/distinct rather than similar. High complexity = more adaptive, more diverse networks, but more stressful; low complexity = fewer identities to manage, more resources per identity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Consistency: **how compatible one’s multiple self-views are with each other and with one’s actual attributes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Clarity: **how clearly, confidently and stably the self-concept is defined; increases with age, self-reflection and consistency. High clarity is linked to better performance, leadership and career development, and less threat from conflict — though extremely high clarity can cause role inflexibility."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Self-Evaluation and Self-Regulation Processes"},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-enhancement: **the drive to see and present oneself positively (competent, ethical, valued); linked to better mental/physical health and higher \"can-do\" motivation, but also riskier decisions, inflated sense of personal causation, and slower recognition of one’s own mistakes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-verification: **the motivation to confirm and maintain our existing self-concept; we communicate our self-view to others, seek confirming feedback, and tend to dismiss feedback that contradicts our self-concept."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-evaluation: **covers self-esteem (liking/respecting/being satisfied with oneself — high self-esteem = less swayed by others, more persistent, clearer thinking), self-efficacy (belief we can successfully perform a specific task — a MARS-model \"ability\" input), and locus of control (general belief about how much control we have over life events — an internal locus of control is linked to higher self-evaluation)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social Self and Social Identity Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"We balance two opposing needs: to be distinctive/unique (personal identity) and to belong/be included with others (social identity). We define ourselves partly through the groups we identify with — especially groups we are easily identified with, that carry high status, or where we hold minority status in a given situation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Perceptual Process"},{"t":"li","x":"**Perception: **the process of receiving information about, and making sense of, the world around us."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Selective Attention"},{"t":"p","x":"We select some sensory information and ignore the rest, influenced by characteristics of both the perceiver and the object; selected information is tagged with emotional markers. Biases include assumptions/expectations and confirmation bias (favouring information that fits what we already believe)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Perceptual Organization and Interpretation"},{"t":"li","x":"Categorical thinking — organizing people/things into categories to reduce complexity."},{"t":"li","x":"Perceptual grouping principles — similarity/proximity, closure (filling in missing pieces), and perceiving trends."},{"t":"li","x":"Mental models — knowledge structures (visual \"maps\" or cause-effect \"relational\" models) that help us describe, explain and predict the world; the risk is that mental models make it hard to see the world differently, so we must actively question them."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Perceptual Biases and Problems"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Stereotyping"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **assigning traits to people based on their membership in a social category — stereotypes may contain a \"kernel of truth\" but are embellished/distorted."},{"t":"li","x":"**Why it happens: **categorical thinking, the drive to comprehend/predict others’ behaviour, and support for self-enhancement/social identity, reinforced through three sub-processes: categorization (sorting people into groups), homogenization (assuming similarity within a group, difference between groups), and differentiation (favouring one’s own group)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Problems: **overgeneralization, stereotype threat, and it is a foundation of systemic/intentional discrimination. Stereotype activation is hard to prevent, but its application can be minimized."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Attribution Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the process of deciding whether an observed behaviour/event is caused mainly by internal factors (the person) or external factors (the situation, beyond the person’s control)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-serving bias: **attributing our own failures to external causes and our successes to internal causes (a product of self-enhancement)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Fundamental attribution error (correspondence bias): **the tendency to over-emphasize internal causes when explaining other people’s behaviour, because we find it harder to see the external/situational forces acting on them (a fairly modest-sized error, but still commonly discussed)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Self-Fulfilling Prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"A cycle: (1) a supervisor forms expectations about an employee; (2) those expectations affect the supervisor’s own behaviour toward the employee; (3) the supervisor’s behaviour affects the employee’s ability/motivation (self-confidence); (4) the employee’s behaviour becomes more consistent with the supervisor’s original expectations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Strongest: **at the start of a relationship, when several people share the same expectation, and when the employee has low prior achievement."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing it: **awareness (limited benefit on its own), a supportive/learning organisational culture, and hiring supervisors who are inherently optimistic about staff."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Other Perceptual Effects"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Effect","Description"],["Halo effect","A general impression from one trait colours our perception of a person’s other, unrelated traits."],["False-consensus effect","Overestimating how much others share our own beliefs, values or traits."],["Primacy effect","Quickly forming an opinion of someone based on the first information received — hard to change afterward."],["Recency effect","The most recent information dominates our overall perception/judgement."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Improving Perceptions"},{"t":"li","x":"**Awareness of biases: **a starting point, but on its own gives only limited bias reduction and can even reinforce stereotypes."},{"t":"li","x":"**Improving self-awareness: **e.g., the Implicit Association Test, and the Johari Window (a 2x2 model of self/others’ awareness: open, blind, hidden and unknown areas) to expand mutual understanding."},{"t":"li","x":"**Meaningful interaction: **based on the \"contact hypothesis\" — people working together on valued, shared activities reduces perceptual bias of one another and builds empathy (understanding and being sensitive to others’ feelings, thoughts and situations)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Global Mindset"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the ability to adopt a global perspective, empathize and act effectively across cultures, process complex information about novel environments, and develop new, multilevel mental models."},{"t":"li","x":"**Developing it: **begins with self-awareness, comparing your own mental models against those from other cultures/regions, and building knowledge of other people/cultures — ideally through direct immersion (e.g., an exchange programme)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A manager rates an employee highly on \"leadership potential\" mainly because the employee is very articulate in meetings, and then (without further evidence) assumes the same person must also be highly organized and technically skilled. This is best explained by:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Self-fulfilling prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"d) False-consensus effect"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: According to attribution theory, when we explain our own failures by pointing to bad luck or an unfair boss, but explain our own successes by pointing to our talent and hard work, we are showing:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Self-serving bias"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Stereotype threat"},{"t":"p","x":"d) The halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between the fundamental attribution error and self-serving bias, using one workplace example for each."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using the Johari Window and the contact hypothesis, explain two concrete things an organisation could do to reduce stereotyping between two departments that rarely interact."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A new supervisor is told, before ever meeting her team, that \"the night-shift team is difficult and unmotivated.\" Within a few weeks, the night-shift team’s performance and engagement have genuinely declined. Using the self-fulfilling prophecy model, explain step-by-step how this could have happened, and suggest one action that could have prevented it."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Halo effect — a single positive trait (articulateness) is being used to infer unrelated positive traits (organization, technical skill) without direct evidence.\n**2. **b) Self-serving bias — internal attribution for success, external attribution for failure, specifically about one’s own behaviour/outcomes (this is distinct from the fundamental attribution error, which is about how we judge other people’s behaviour).\n**3. **Fundamental attribution error: judging other people’s behaviour by over-emphasizing internal/personal causes and under-weighting situational causes — e.g., assuming a colleague who missed a deadline is simply \"lazy,\" without considering that they were pulled onto an urgent client fire drill. Self-serving bias: how we explain our own behaviour/outcomes, attributing our own failures to external causes and our own successes to internal causes — e.g., a salesperson who hits target says \"I worked hard and built great client relationships\" (internal) but who misses target says \"the market was terrible this quarter\" (external).\n**4. **Two acceptable actions: (1) Run a joint, shared-goal project or workshop that puts members of both departments together on a valued task (contact hypothesis) — genuine cooperative interaction toward a common outcome reduces perceptual bias and builds empathy between the groups. (2) Use a structured Johari Window exercise in a cross-department session, where each side shares how they see themselves and receives feedback on their \"blind spot\" from the other department — this expands the shared \"open\" area of mutual understanding and can surface and correct inaccurate stereotypes on both sides.\n**5. **Step-by-step: (1) The new supervisor forms a negative expectation about the team before even meeting them, based only on hearsay. (2) This expectation shapes her own behaviour toward the team — e.g., less trust, more controlling supervision, less positive feedback, possibly less delegation. (3) Her behaviour affects the team’s actual motivation and confidence (they may feel micromanaged, undervalued or mistrusted). (4) Over time the team’s engagement and performance genuinely decline, appearing to \"confirm\" the original expectation, even though the expectation itself helped cause the outcome. Prevention: the supervisor (or whoever briefed her) should avoid transmitting unverified second-hand expectations before first-hand contact, and instead approach the team with a deliberately positive-but-realistic expectation and gather her own direct evidence before forming judgements — consistent with the guidance that leaders should maintain positive, realistic expectations toward all employees."}]},"4":{"title":"Workplace Emotions, Attitudes and Stress","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 4   |   Scheduled: 28 Sep 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Emotions vs. Attitudes"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Attitudes","Emotions"],["Nature","Cluster of beliefs, feelings and behavioural intentions","Experiences tied to a specific attitude object"],["Basis","Judgments involving conscious reasoning","Operate as events, often nonconscious"],["Duration","More stable over time","Brief episodes"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Emotions-Attitudes-Behaviour Model"},{"t":"p","x":"Beliefs (established perceptions about the attitude object, e.g., \"this job involves long hours\") lead to feelings (conscious positive/negative evaluations formed from the valence of those beliefs), which lead to behavioural intentions (motivation to act), which are the best predictor of actual behaviour. However, the links between each stage are only \"contingencies\" — not guarantees — because personality, past experience, and situational constraints can break the chain at any step (e.g., two people share a feeling but form different intentions; or share an intention but only one has the situational freedom to act on it)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Cognitive Dissonance"},{"t":"p","x":"An emotional experience caused by a perceived inconsistency among our beliefs, feelings and behaviour — it threatens our self-image as a rational person and motivates us toward consistency. Because it is hard to undo behaviour already taken, we typically resolve dissonance by changing our beliefs/feelings about the attitude object, or by recalling previous decisions consistent with the current one."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Emotional Labor and Emotional Intelligence"},{"t":"li","x":"**Emotional labor: **the effort, planning and control needed to display organisationally desired emotions during interpersonal interactions. It is most stressful when the required display differs sharply from what is actually felt, or contradicts one’s self-concept. Emotional display norms also vary across cultures (e.g., Russian employees report more stress from being expected to smile at customers, since smiling carries a different cultural meaning there)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Surface acting: **consciously faking the expected emotion through verbal/nonverbal behaviour — stressful and hard to sustain."},{"t":"li","x":"**Deep acting: **regulating one’s actual felt emotions (changing/modifying the situation, suppressing or amplifying emotion, shifting attention, or reframing the situation) so the displayed emotion becomes more genuine."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Emotional Intelligence (EI) Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["","Self","Others"],["Recognition of emotions","Awareness of own emotions","Awareness of others’ emotions"],["Regulation of emotions","Management of own emotions","Management of others’ emotions"]]},{"t":"p","x":"EI outcomes: better teamwork, better handling of emotional labor, more effective leadership, better decisions involving others, and a more positive mindset for creativity. EI can be developed through training, coaching, practice and feedback, and tends to increase with age."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Workplace Attitudes: Job Satisfaction and Commitment"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"EVLN: Responses to Job Dissatisfaction"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Response","Meaning"],["Exit","Leaving the situation — quitting, transferring, being absent."],["Voice","Trying to change the situation — problem-solving, complaining constructively."],["Loyalty","Patiently waiting for the situation to improve."],["Neglect","Reducing effort/quality, increasing absenteeism."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Job satisfaction and performance: **the relationship is only moderately positive, because general attitudes are poor predictors of specific behaviours, employees often have limited control over their own performance, and causation can run the other way — good performance (if rewarded) increases satisfaction, rather than the reverse."},{"t":"li","x":"**Service profit chain model: **explains why job satisfaction raises customer satisfaction and profit — via (1) employee emotions transferring to customer emotions, and (2) more satisfied, experienced employees delivering better service."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisational Commitment"},{"t":"li","x":"**Affective commitment: **emotional attachment to and identification with the organisation — linked to lower turnover and higher motivation/OCB. Built through organisational justice and support, shared values, trust, organisational comprehension (understanding how the firm works), and employee involvement/psychological ownership."},{"t":"li","x":"**Continuance commitment: **a calculative attachment — staying because leaving would be costly or because there are no good alternatives; associated with lower turnover but also lower performance, OCB and cooperation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Normative commitment: **a felt obligation/moral duty to stay, based on the norm of reciprocity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Workplace Stress"},{"t":"li","x":"**Stress: **an adaptive response to a situation perceived as challenging or threatening to our well-being; it prepares us to adapt to a hostile environment. Stress can be \"eustress\" (a positive, motivating challenge) or \"distress\" (negative strain)."},{"t":"li","x":"**General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS): **the body’s stress response unfolds in three stages — alarm reaction, resistance, and (if the stressor persists) exhaustion."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Main Workplace Stressors"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stressor","Description"],["Organizational constraints","Lack of resources needed to do the job — indirectly threatens rewards and status (\"situation\" in the MARS model)."],["Interpersonal conflict","Others’ behaviour interferes with our goals or feels threatening; includes workplace harassment such as bullying and sexual harassment."],["Work overload","More hours and/or more intensive work than can reasonably be sustained."],["Low task control","Lack of control over how and when tasks are performed; more stressful as personal responsibility for outcomes increases."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Differences in Stress"},{"t":"p","x":"People experience less stress, or fewer negative stress outcomes, when they have better physical health/lifestyle, appropriate coping strategies, a personality profile of lower neuroticism and higher extraversion, and a positive self-concept."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Managing Work-Related Stress — Five Strategies"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Remove the stressor: **e.g., person-job matching, reducing noise, a complaint system, giving employees more control over their work, work-life integration practices."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Withdraw from the stressor: **permanent (e.g., transfer) or temporary (e.g., vacation) solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Change stress perceptions: **e.g., strengthening a positive self-concept, personal goal setting, using humour."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Control stress consequences: **e.g., exercise, meditation, wellness programmes, Employee Assistance Programmes (EAPs)."},{"t":"li","x":"**5. Receive social support: **emotional and informational support from others (supervisors, coworkers, family)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **This chapter connects directly to the Individual Case assignment (Janet Wong, stress management) summarized in the Course Overview — the four stressors and five stress-management strategies above are the core theory to apply in that report."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: An employee genuinely believes long working hours are \"part of ambition and drive,\" but after months of 70-hour weeks he privately starts to resent the job and feels physically exhausted, yet keeps telling colleagues \"I love the hustle.\" This best illustrates:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The self-fulfilling prophecy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cognitive dissonance"},{"t":"p","x":"c) The halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A flight attendant who does not personally feel cheerful but consciously forces a smile and an upbeat tone with every passenger, without changing how she actually feels inside, is engaging in:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Deep acting"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Surface acting"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Emotional intelligence regulation of others"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Affective commitment"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Using the EVLN model, describe what \"neglect\" looks like in practice, and explain why it is often harder for a manager to detect early than \"voice\" or \"exit.\""},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain why job satisfaction and job performance are only moderately correlated, giving two of the three reasons discussed in class."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A customer-service team member has recently been given a heavier caseload (work overload), has very little say in how she schedules her calls (low task control), and has started missing minor errors and calling in sick more often. Using stress theory, identify which two of the four main workplace stressors are most clearly present, and recommend one management action from each of two different stress-management strategy categories to help her."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Cognitive dissonance — there is a perceived inconsistency between his behaviour/feelings (resentment, exhaustion) and his stated belief/public identity (\"I love the hustle\"); rather than changing the behaviour (reducing hours), he is likely to keep reinforcing the consonant belief to reduce the emotional discomfort.\n**2. **b) Surface acting — she is faking the expected emotional display (smiling, upbeat tone) without regulating or changing her actual inner feelings, which is the definition of surface acting (and the more stressful of the two acting strategies).\n**3. **Neglect means quietly reducing work effort or quality and/or increasing absenteeism, rather than actively complaining (voice) or leaving (exit). It is harder to detect early because it is passive and low-visibility — there is no single triggering event or conversation for a manager to notice, and small drops in effort or slightly more sick days can easily be attributed to other causes (workload, personal life) rather than recognized as a dissatisfaction signal, unlike a formal complaint (voice) or a resignation (exit), which are unmistakable events.\n**4. **Any two of: (1) General attitudes (like overall job satisfaction) are poor predictors of specific behaviours (like a particular performance outcome) — attitudes and behaviours are best matched when both are measured at a similarly specific/general level. (2) Employees often have limited personal control over their performance (e.g., depends on equipment, teammates, market conditions), which weakens the link from how they feel to what they can actually produce. (3) The causal arrow may run in reverse — good performance, when it is recognized and rewarded, increases satisfaction — but if performance is not rewarded, this reverse loop does not strengthen the correlation.\n**5. **Stressors present: Work overload (heavier caseload) and Low task control (little say in scheduling calls). Recommended actions — one per category: (1) Remove the stressor: redistribute part of her caseload or give her more autonomy over how she sequences/schedules her calls (directly reduces overload and increases task control). (2) Control stress consequences / receive social support: offer access to the company’s wellness programme or EAP, and ensure her supervisor checks in supportively and offers informational support (e.g., coaching on prioritization) rather than simply monitoring the missed errors and sick days."}]},"5":{"title":"Motivation","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 5   |   Scheduled: 5 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Motivation and Engagement Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Employee motivation: **the forces within a person that affect the direction, intensity and persistence of effort for voluntary behaviour (same \"M\" as in the MARS model)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Employee engagement: **an employee’s emotional and cognitive motivation — a focused, intense, persistent and purposive effort toward work-related goals."},{"t":"li","x":"**Why motivation is harder today: **a multigenerational, multicultural workforce; rapid workplace change; and less motivation achievable simply through direct supervision."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Drives, Needs and Four-Drive Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Drives: **hardwired, innate, universal brain activity that corrects deficiencies and produces emotions that energize action (\"prime movers\" of behaviour)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Needs: **goal-directed forces we experience, channeling our emotions toward specific goals shaped by self-concept, social norms and experience."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four-Drive Theory (Lawrence & Nohria)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Drive","Meaning"],["Drive to acquire","Seek, acquire, control and retain objects or experiences."],["Drive to bond","Form social relationships and mutual caring commitments with others."],["Drive to comprehend","Satisfy curiosity; know and understand ourselves and our environment."],["Drive to defend","Protect ourselves physically and socially."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Mechanism: drives determine which emotions get attached to incoming information; emotions become conscious when strong enough or when drives conflict; our mental skill set then uses social norms, personal values and experience to convert these drive-based emotions into goal-directed choices and effort."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Content Theories of Motivation"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Maslow’s Needs Hierarchy Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Five (of seven) needs are arranged in a hierarchy (physiological, safety, belongingness/love, esteem, self-actualization); the lowest unmet need dominates until satisfied, then the next-higher need becomes the top motivator. The model lacks strong empirical support — its main problem is that people do not actually have a universal, identical needs hierarchy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Maslow’s contributions despite the weak evidence: **a holistic perspective (study needs together, not in isolation), a humanistic perspective (social dynamics, not just instinct, shape motivation), and a positive perspective (emphasis on self-actualization/growth, foundational to \"positive OB\")."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Intrinsic vs. Extrinsic Motivation"},{"t":"li","x":"**Intrinsic motivation: **seeking fulfilment from the activity itself, not as a means to another outcome — anchored in innate drives for competence and autonomy."},{"t":"li","x":"**Extrinsic motivation: **being motivated by an instrumental outcome (pay, recognition) that indirectly fulfils needs. Extrinsic rewards can sometimes undermine intrinsic motivation, but the research shows this effect is minimal and often absent."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Learned Needs Theory (McClelland)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for achievement (nAch): **wants to accomplish challenging goals, values clear feedback, prefers moderate-risk tasks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for affiliation (nAff): **seeks approval from others, tends to conform, avoids conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"**Need for power (nPow): **seeks power/influence for social or personal purposes."},{"t":"p","x":"These needs are \"learned\" — they can be strengthened or weakened through self-concept, social norms and experience, and training can shift a person’s need strength through reinforcement."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Process Theories of Motivation"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Expectancy Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Motivation = a function of three beliefs, multiplied together:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Link","Belief","How to increase it"],["Effort → Performance (E-to-P)","My effort will lead to the required performance level","Train/select for fit; provide time, resources, coaching and behavioural modeling to build self-efficacy"],["Performance → Outcome (P-to-O)","My performance will lead to a specific outcome/reward","Measure performance accurately; clearly explain the reward link; show real examples of others being rewarded for performance"],["Outcome valence","That outcome/reward is something I actually value","Ensure rewards are genuinely valued; individualize rewards; minimize \"countervalent\" (negatively valued) side-effects"]]},{"t":"p","x":"Because the three components multiply, a \"zero\" on any one of them (e.g., a reward the employee does not value at all) drives overall motivation toward zero, regardless of how strong the other two beliefs are."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organizational Behaviour Modification (OB Mod) — the A-B-Cs"},{"t":"p","x":"Antecedent (a cue that triggers behaviour) → Behavior → Consequence (what follows the behaviour and shapes whether it recurs)."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Consequence type","What happens","Effect on behaviour"],["Positive reinforcement","A desired consequence is introduced after the behaviour","Behaviour increases / is maintained"],["Negative reinforcement","An undesired consequence is removed after the behaviour","Behaviour increases"],["Punishment","An undesired consequence is introduced after the behaviour","Behaviour decreases"],["Extinction","A previously reinforcing consequence is withheld","Behaviour decreases (fades out)"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Social Cognitive Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"Learning from consequences — observing what happens to others and anticipating similar consequences for ourselves in other situations."},{"t":"li","x":"Behaviour modeling — learning by observing and imitating others’ behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-regulation — purposive action: setting our own goals/standards, anticipating consequences, and self-reinforcing our own behaviour."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Goal Setting, Feedback and Coaching"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Effective Goal-Setting Features"},{"t":"p","x":"Specific, Measurable, Achievable (yet challenging), Relevant (within the employee’s control), Time-framed, Exciting (builds real commitment, not just compliance), Reviewed (feedback/recognition on progress)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Characteristics of Effective Feedback"},{"t":"p","x":"Specific (identifiable behaviours/outcomes), Relevant (within the employee’s control), Timely, Credible (from a knowledgeable, unbiased source), and Sufficiently Frequent (more for learners; otherwise matched to the task cycle)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Strengths-Based Coaching"},{"t":"p","x":"Maximizes potential by focusing on strengths, not weaknesses: the employee identifies an area of strength, the coach helps them discover how to leverage it, and they discuss situational barriers and solutions together — motivating because people prefer feedback on their strengths, and core traits/interests tend to stabilize in adulthood."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Organizational Justice and Equity Theory"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Three Types of Organizational Justice"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Focus"],["Distributive justice","Fairness of the outcomes themselves (based on equality, equity, or need)."],["Procedural justice","Fairness of the decision-making process used to reach the outcome."],["Interactional justice","Fairness/respect in how people are treated during the process."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Equity Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"People compare their own outcome-to-input ratio with a comparison other’s ratio. Perceived inequity (under- or over-reward) creates tension that motivates people to restore balance by: reducing their own inputs, increasing their own outcomes, increasing the other’s inputs, reducing the other’s outcomes, changing their perceptions (cognitively re-evaluating), changing their comparison other, or leaving the field (e.g., quitting, transferring)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Procedural Justice Rules (selected)**"},{"t":"li","x":"Decision makers have no self-interest and set aside restrictive doctrines."},{"t":"li","x":"Decisions use complete, accurate information and consider all affected groups."},{"t":"li","x":"Criteria/procedures are applied consistently and ethically."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees can voice evidence/opinions, and questionable decisions can be appealed."},{"t":"p","x":"**Interactional Justice Rules**"},{"t":"li","x":"Employees are treated politely and with respect."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees receive thorough, well-justified explanations."},{"t":"li","x":"Employees receive honest, candid and timely information."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: An employee believes that no matter how hard she works, her performance will never actually be measured accurately or rewarded by her manager. According to expectancy theory, which link is broken?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Effort-to-performance (E-to-P) expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancy"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Outcome valence"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A manager removes an annoying, repetitive email alert every time an employee submits her report on time, which increases the employee’s tendency to submit reports on time going forward. This is an example of:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Positive reinforcement"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Punishment"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Negative reinforcement"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Extinction"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Using equity theory, list three different ways an employee who feels under-rewarded relative to a coworker might try to restore equity, other than quitting."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain why Maslow’s needs hierarchy is considered to lack strong empirical support, and name one lasting contribution it still made to the study of motivation."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A junior analyst has strong technical skills and clearly wants to do well (high E-to-P belief — he is confident his effort will produce good work), and he knows that strong performance reviews lead to bonuses at his firm (strong P-to-O belief). However, he tells his mentor \"I honestly don’t care about the bonus — what I want is more interesting, varied project work, and nobody’s offering me that.\" Using expectancy theory, diagnose the motivational gap and recommend a fix consistent with the theory."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancy — her belief is specifically that good performance will not be measured or linked to a reward, which is a breakdown in the performance→outcome link, not in whether her effort produces performance (E-to-P) or whether she values the reward (valence).\n**2. **c) Negative reinforcement — an aversive stimulus (the annoying alert) is removed contingent on the desired behaviour (on-time submission), which increases that behaviour. This differs from positive reinforcement, where something desirable would be added, not an unpleasant stimulus taken away.\n**3. **Any three of: reduce her own inputs (e.g., put in less effort/time); increase her own outcomes (e.g., ask for a raise, take more perks); try to increase the coworker’s inputs (e.g., expect them to take on more work); try to reduce the coworker’s outcomes; cognitively change her perception of the situation (re-evaluate what \"fair\" means here, downplay the comparison); or change her comparison other (compare herself to a different, less favourably-treated colleague instead).\n**4. **Maslow’s theory lacks strong empirical support mainly because it assumes a single, universal hierarchy of needs and a strict \"one need dominates until satisfied, then the next takes over\" progression — but research shows people differ substantially in their personal needs hierarchies, so the model does not hold up as a precise, testable framework. Lasting contributions (any one): its holistic perspective (studying multiple needs together rather than in isolation), its humanistic perspective (recognizing social dynamics, not just instinct, shape motivation), or its positive perspective (highlighting self-actualization/growth needs, which helped found \"positive OB\").\n**5. **The motivational gap is in outcome valence: his E-to-P and P-to-O beliefs are both strong, but the specific outcome on offer (a bonus) has low personal valence for him — he simply does not value it much, so overall motivation toward \"chasing the bonus\" stays low even though the other two expectancy components are intact (recall the three components multiply, so a low-valence outcome caps the overall effect). Fix, consistent with expectancy theory’s guidance to \"individualize rewards\" and \"ensure rewards are valued\": his manager should offer an outcome he genuinely values instead — e.g., link strong performance reviews to a say in choosing more varied/interesting project assignments, rather than (or in addition to) a cash bonus."}]},"6":{"title":"Team Dynamics","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 8   |   Scheduled: 12 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. What Is a Team?"},{"t":"p","x":"Five defining features: (1) two or more people; (2) exist to fulfil a purpose; (3) interdependence and need for collaboration; (4) mutual accountability — members influence each other; (5) members perceive themselves to be a team."},{"t":"li","x":"**Types of teams vary by: **permanence (how long the team exists), skill diversity (variety of member skills/knowledge), and authority dispersion (how decision-making is distributed)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Informal Groups"},{"t":"p","x":"Groups that exist primarily for the benefit of their members, driven by the innate drive to bond, social identity, goal accomplishment, and emotional support — informal groups can also benefit the organisation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Advantages and Challenges of Teams"},{"t":"li","x":"**Advantages: **better decisions/products, better information sharing and coordination, higher motivation (fulfils the drive to bond, creates felt obligation to the group), mutual accountability (members monitor each other), and coworkers act as moving performance benchmarks."},{"t":"li","x":"**Challenges: **process losses, and social loafing (individuals exert less effort in a group than they would alone)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Social Loafing — Causes and Remedies**"},{"t":"li","x":"**More likely when: **individual performance is hidden/indistinguishable, the work itself is low in intrinsic motivation, due to certain individual characteristics, or when team dynamics undermine motivation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing it: **form smaller teams, specialize tasks, measure individual performance, increase job enrichment, and raise awareness of social loafing and team obligations."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. The Team Effectiveness Model"},{"t":"p","x":"Team effectiveness is shaped by the organisational/team environment, team design, and team processes."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisation and Team Environment"},{"t":"p","x":"Reward systems, organisational structure, communication systems, organisational leadership, and physical space (e.g., shared \"obeya\" collaboration rooms) all shape how well a team can function."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Design: Task, Size and Composition"},{"t":"li","x":"**Best task characteristics for teams: **complex tasks that can be divided into specialized roles, well-structured tasks (low variability, high analyzability), and high task interdependence."},{"t":"p","x":"**Levels of Task Interdependence**"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Description","Example"],["Pooled interdependence","Each member’s output is combined, with minimal direct interaction between members.","Several people sharing one photocopier"],["Sequential interdependence","One member’s output becomes the next member’s input, in one direction.","An assembly line"],["Reciprocal interdependence","Work flows back and forth between members in both directions.","A medical team treating a patient"]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Team size: **smaller teams generally perform better (less process loss, more individual engagement, faster development) — but the team must still be large enough to complete the task."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team composition: **members must engage in both task work (behaviours that complete the assigned task) and teamwork (the \"5 Cs\" behaviours that support the team as a unit — e.g., coordinating, communicating, cooperating)."},{"t":"p","x":"**Diversity in Team Composition**"},{"t":"li","x":"**Advantages: **multiple perspectives on problems/alternatives, a broader knowledge base, and better representation of constituents."},{"t":"li","x":"**Disadvantages: **slower team development, and greater susceptibility to \"faultlines\" (subgroup splits along visible demographic or value lines)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Team Processes"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Stages of Team Development"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Stage","What happens"],["Forming","Members learn about each other and evaluate their membership in the team."],["Storming","Conflict emerges; members act proactively and compete for roles/influence."],["Norming","Roles become established; the team builds consensus around objectives and a shared mental model."],["Performing","Efficient coordination, high cooperation and trust, strong commitment to team goals, identification with the team."],["Adjourning","The team disbands; focus shifts from task to relationships (wrapping up, closure)."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Two central underlying processes across these stages: developing team identification, and developing shared team mental models and coordinating routines."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Roles and Team Building"},{"t":"li","x":"**Team roles: **expected, purposive behaviour patterns with perceived value to the team/organisation, attached to one or more members, acquired formally or informally; divided into task-work roles and team-work roles."},{"t":"li","x":"**Team building activities: **goal setting, problem-solving, role clarification, and interpersonal relations work — effective only under specific supportive conditions."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Norms"},{"t":"p","x":"Informal rules and shared expectations that regulate behaviour, developing through initial team experiences, critical events in the team’s history, and the values/experience members bring. Preventing or changing dysfunctional norms: state desired norms explicitly when forming the team, select members with compatible values, openly discuss counter-productive norms, use team-based rewards to counter bad norms, or (as a last resort) disband a team with entrenched dysfunctional norms."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Team Cohesion"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the degree of attraction members feel toward the team and their motivation to remain in it."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stronger/faster with: **higher member similarity, smaller team size, regular/frequent interaction, somewhat difficult entry, higher team success, and more external competition/challenge."},{"t":"li","x":"**Cohesion boosts performance when: **task interdependence is high and team norms align with organisational objectives — but high cohesion combined with norms that are NOT aligned with organisational goals can actually hurt performance (e.g., a very close-knit team can collectively agree to cut corners)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Trust in Teams"},{"t":"p","x":"Three levels, from lowest to highest: calculus-based trust (based on consistent rewards/punishments), knowledge-based trust (based on predictability from experience with the person), and identification-based trust (based on mutual understanding/shared identity). \"Swift trust\" is an initial moderate-to-high trust extended to new teammates just because they joined the team."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Self-Directed and Remote Teams"},{"t":"li","x":"**Self-directed teams: **cross-functional groups organised around whole work processes, with autonomy over task decisions. Success factors: responsibility for an entire work process, high interdependence within the team, low interdependence with other teams, autonomy to organise/coordinate work, and a work site/technology that supports team communication and job enrichment."},{"t":"li","x":"**Remote (virtual/distributed) teams: **vary by geographic dispersion and how much members work apart. Success factors: strong use of the \"5 Cs\" teamwork behaviours, freedom to choose from a toolkit of communication channels, fairly high task structure, and some opportunities to meet face-to-face."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Team Decision Making"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Four Constraints on Team Decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"Time constraints — production blocking (only one person can effectively speak/contribute at a time) and coordination difficulty."},{"t":"li","x":"Evaluation apprehension — reluctance to voice unusual ideas due to self-presentation concerns."},{"t":"li","x":"Peer pressure to conform — suppresses dissenting opinions."},{"t":"li","x":"Overconfidence (inflated team efficacy) — caused by self-enhancement, cohesion, external threats, and mutually reinforcing beliefs."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Guidelines for Better Team Decisions"},{"t":"li","x":"Build in checks and balances so no single member dominates."},{"t":"li","x":"Maintain an optimal (not oversized) team size."},{"t":"li","x":"Encourage confidence, but stay alert to overconfidence."},{"t":"li","x":"Foster norms that encourage critical thinking."},{"t":"li","x":"Develop and maintain psychological safety."},{"t":"li","x":"Use team structures that specifically encourage creativity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Creative Team Structures"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Structure","Description"],["Brainstorming","Rules: speak freely, no criticism, generate as many ideas as possible, build on others’ ideas. Works best with skilled facilitators, confident members and psychological safety; success = idea quality, not quantity. Limited by production blocking and conformity/fixation effects."],["Brainwriting","Brainstorming done individually/in writing rather than through conversation — reduces production blocking."],["Electronic brainstorming","Brainwriting supported by technology — reduces production blocking, evaluation apprehension and conformity even further."],["Nominal group technique","Brainwriting combined with a subsequent verbal discussion stage."]]}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A hospital surgical team, where information and adjustments flow back and forth constantly between the surgeon, anaesthetist and nurses during an operation, best illustrates which type of task interdependence?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Pooled interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Sequential interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Reciprocal interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Independent tasks"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: A close-knit team has very high cohesion, but its shared informal norm is \"we don’t report each other’s mistakes to management.\" What is the most accurate conclusion?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) High cohesion always improves performance regardless of norms"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cohesion improves performance only when team norms align with organisational goals — here they do not, so performance/quality may suffer"},{"t":"p","x":"c) This is an example of social loafing"},{"t":"p","x":"d) This is an example of swift trust"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Name and briefly describe the five stages of team development, in order."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Explain two of the four constraints on effective team decision-making, and for each, name one structural remedy (e.g., a creative-team structure) that helps address it."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly formed venture team (four co-founder friends) has just moved from a period of open disagreement and jockeying for roles into a phase where responsibilities are becoming clearer and the group is building a shared understanding of how to work together. (a) Which two stages of team development does this describe (the one just completed and the one now beginning)? (b) Suggest one concrete team-building action appropriate for consolidating the new stage."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) Reciprocal interdependence — work and information flow back and forth in both directions between team members in real time, unlike the one-directional flow of sequential interdependence (assembly line) or the minimal-interaction pooled interdependence (shared photocopier).\n**2. **b) Cohesion improves performance specifically when task interdependence is high and team norms are consistent with organisational objectives; here the norm actively undermines an organisational goal (accountability/quality), so despite high cohesion, performance or compliance outcomes are likely to suffer rather than improve.\n**3. **Forming (members learn about each other, evaluate membership) → Storming (conflict emerges, members compete for roles) → Norming (roles settle, consensus builds around objectives and a shared mental model) → Performing (efficient, cooperative, high-trust coordination, strong commitment) → Adjourning (the team disbands, focus shifts from task to relationships).\n**4. **Any two, e.g.: (1) Time constraints/production blocking — remedy: brainwriting or electronic brainstorming, which let members contribute simultaneously in writing instead of waiting to speak. (2) Evaluation apprehension — remedy: electronic brainstorming (anonymity reduces fear of judgement) or strong brainstorming ground rules (\"no criticism\") enforced by a skilled facilitator. (3) Peer pressure to conform — remedy: nominal group technique, which separates individual idea generation (in writing, before group discussion) from the group conformity pressure of live debate. (4) Overconfidence/inflated team efficacy — remedy: building in checks and balances/critical-thinking norms so no single narrative dominates unchecked.\n**5. **(a) The team has just completed Storming (open disagreement, competing for roles) and is now entering Norming (roles clarifying, building a shared team mental model and consensus on objectives). (b) A concrete team-building action: a facilitated role-clarification session where each founder explicitly states their intended responsibilities and decision rights, and the group agrees and documents them together — this is exactly the \"role clarification\" type of team building that helps consolidate the Norming stage (this maps onto the actual exam-style question on this same case in the Chapter 8 slides)."}]},"7":{"title":"Leadership in Organisational Settings","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 12   |   Scheduled: 26 Oct 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Leadership Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership: **the ability to influence, motivate and enable others to contribute toward the effectiveness of the organisations of which they are members. Leaders influence and motivate others, and act as enablers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Shared leadership: **the view that leadership is a role, not a position held by one person — employees lead each other (e.g., championing new ideas). It flourishes where formal leaders are willing to delegate power, the culture is collaborative (not competitive), and employees have developed effective influence skills."},{"t":"p","x":"Four main perspectives of leadership covered in this chapter: transformational, managerial, implicit leadership, and leader attributes."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Elements of Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Develop and communicate a strategic vision: **an aspirational, higher-purpose future state — linked to personal values, challenging, abstract, and unifying. Communicated with symbols, metaphors, framing, and delivered with humility, sincerity and passion."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Model the vision: **the leader \"walks the talk\" — their own behaviour symbolizes and demonstrates the vision, which both legitimizes the vision and builds employee trust in the leader."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Encourage experimentation: **encourage employees to question current practices and support a learning orientation."},{"t":"li","x":"**4. Build commitment to the vision: **built cumulatively through communicating, modeling and encouraging experimentation, and reinforced through rewards, recognition and celebration."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Charisma vs. Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Charisma: **a personal trait that produces referent power and tends to create dependent followers."},{"t":"li","x":"**Transformational leadership: **a set of behaviours that engage followers through persuasion and trust, empowering (rather than creating dependent) followers. There is a risk that leaders become \"intoxicated\" by their own charisma."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Evaluating Transformational Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Strengths: **linked to higher satisfaction, commitment, performance, OCBs, better decisions and more creativity."},{"t":"li","x":"**Limitations: **circular logic (it is sometimes defined by its own effects on employees), mixed models that blur leader traits with leader behaviours (confusing cause and effect), and uncertainty about whether it is a truly universal theory across cultures (some specific elements may fit some cultures better than others)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Managerial Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Daily activities that support and guide employees’ performance/well-being to sustain current objectives and practices — assumes a relatively stable environment and is micro-focused, whereas transformational leadership assumes a dynamic environment and is macro-focused. Managerial leadership is \"doing things right\"; transformational leadership is \"doing the right things.\" The two are interdependent, not substitutes for each other."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Task-Oriented vs. People-Oriented Behaviours"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Task-oriented behaviours","People-oriented behaviours"],["Assign work, clarify responsibilities, set goals/deadlines, give feedback, establish procedures, plan future work.","Show concern for employee needs, make the workplace pleasant, recognize contributions, listen to employees."]]},{"t":"p","x":"Both styles are necessary, but they have different effects on outcomes; most effective leaders use both, situationally."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Servant Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Leadership focused on serving followers’ need fulfilment, personal development and growth — selfless, egalitarian, humble, nurturing, empathetic, and ethical. Characteristics: a natural calling to serve others; a humble, egalitarian, accepting relationship style; and ethical decisions/actions. (Note: it is essentially an extension/variation of people-oriented leadership.)"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Contingency Perspectives: Path-Goal Theory and Substitutes"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Path-Goal Leadership Model"},{"t":"p","x":"A contingency model: leaders vary their style to clarify performance-to-outcome (P-to-O) expectancies, influence outcome valences, and facilitate goal achievement. Four styles:"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Style","What it looks like","Best when..."],["Directive","Clarifies goals, methods, and performance standards (= task-oriented).","Employees are inexperienced/unskilled; task is nonroutine/ambiguous; employees have an external locus of control; team norms are dysfunctional."],["Supportive","Provides psychological support (= people-oriented).","Employees are low-skilled (combined with directive); team cohesion is low; employees have an external locus of control."],["Participative","Involves employees in decisions.","Employees have an internal locus of control; task is nonroutine."],["Achievement-oriented","Sets challenging goals and shows confidence employees can reach them.","Employees have an internal locus of control and are skilled/experienced."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Leadership Substitutes Theory"},{"t":"p","x":"Contingencies that limit a leader’s influence or make a particular style unnecessary — e.g., strong training/experience can substitute for task-oriented leadership; a highly cohesive team can substitute for supportive leadership. Evidence: substitutes help, but they do not completely replace the need for real leadership."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Implicit Leadership Perspective"},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership prototypes: **our preconceived beliefs about the features/behaviours of an effective leader; we evaluate leaders more favourably when they fit our prototype."},{"t":"li","x":"**Romance of leadership effect: **the tendency to distort/inflate a leader’s perceived effect on organisational success — driven by our need to simplify complex explanations and our need for a sense of situational control."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Personal Attributes of Effective Leaders and Authentic Leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"Eight commonly identified attributes: personality (especially extraversion and conscientiousness, though other Big Five factors also matter), self-concept (complex, internally consistent, clear self-view as a leader, positive self-evaluation), leadership motivation (strong need for socialized power), drive (moderately high need for achievement, inquisitiveness, boldness), integrity (truthfulness, consistent words/actions, applying one’s values), knowledge of the business, cognitive and practical intelligence, and emotional intelligence."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Authentic Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Know yourself: **self-reflection, seeking feedback from trusted sources, understanding your inner purpose."},{"t":"li","x":"**Be yourself: **developing your own style, self-discipline anchored in personal values, maintaining a strong, positive self-evaluation."},{"t":"p","x":"**Limitations of the Leader-Attributes Perspective**"},{"t":"li","x":"Treats leadership as a universal formula, when effective combinations of attributes can differ."},{"t":"li","x":"Different combinations of attributes may work equally well."},{"t":"li","x":"Views leadership as residing purely \"within the person,\" when it is arguably relational."},{"t":"li","x":"The attributes-effectiveness link is muddied by implicit leadership (prototype fit)."},{"t":"li","x":"Attributes indicate leadership potential only, not guaranteed performance."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Cultural and Gender Issues in Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"**Culture: **societal values/practices shape a leader’s own values and decisions, and shape followers’ prototypes of what an effective leader looks like. Some styles appear near-universal (e.g., \"charismatic visionary\"); others vary — e.g., participative leadership is seen as more characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures than in high power-distance ones."},{"t":"li","x":"**Gender: **male and female leaders show broadly similar levels of task- and people-oriented leadership; women tend to use more participative leadership. Gender stereotypes and leader prototypes distort followers’ evaluations — women are evaluated more negatively when applying directive/autocratic styles, yet rated more favourably than men on emerging leadership qualities such as coaching and teamwork."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Dark Side of Leadership — Cross-Reference to the Dark Triad (Chapter 2)"},{"t":"p","x":"This lecture also recapped the Dark Triad of personality (Machiavellianism, narcissism, psychopathy — see Lesson 2 for full definitions) because these traits help explain destructive or toxic leadership: a leader high in narcissism may pursue a compelling \"vision\" mainly for self-glorification rather than genuine follower benefit (undermining authentic transformational leadership); Machiavellian or psychopathic tendencies in a leader are linked to more organisational politics, unethical influence tactics, and poorer team outcomes, even when such leaders can appear charismatic in the short term."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"8. Case Study: \"The Real Leadership Lessons of Steve Jobs\" (Isaacson, HBR 2012)"},{"t":"p","x":"Summary: Isaacson argues that the real lessons from Steve Jobs come from what he actually built (Apple, Pixar, and the transformation of computing, music, phones, tablets, retail and publishing), not merely from his famously abrasive personality. He distills Jobs’s approach into repeated behaviours: an obsessive focus (cutting product lines to a handful of priorities); relentless simplification (of design and user interfaces); taking end-to-end responsibility for the whole user experience; leapfrogging competitors instead of just catching up; prioritizing great products over short-term profit; trusting instinct/intuition over focus groups; a \"reality distortion field\" that pushed teams to achieve what they thought was impossible; deliberately shaping first impressions (\"impute\"); relentless perfectionism (delaying launches until a design felt right); demanding only \"A player\" talent; insisting on face-to-face collaboration over slides and email; holding both big-picture vision and granular detail simultaneously; fusing the humanities with technology; and staying \"hungry and foolish\" (a nonconformist, restless drive to keep disrupting)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Linking the Case to Leadership Theory (2-3 concepts)"},{"t":"li","x":"**1. Transformational leadership: **Jobs repeatedly built and communicated an aspirational, higher-purpose vision (e.g., \"insanely great\" products, \"put a dent in the universe\"), modeled that vision through his own obsessive standards (\"walking the talk\" on quality/simplicity), and pushed relentless experimentation (delaying the iPhone and iPad launches to get the design right) — a textbook illustration of the \"develop/communicate vision, model the vision, encourage experimentation\" elements, though the case itself frames the question \"Is Steve Jobs charismatic or transformational?\" as genuinely debatable."},{"t":"li","x":"**2. Charisma vs. transformational leadership (and its risks): **Jobs’s \"reality distortion field\" is a clear example of referent power/charisma producing highly dependent, intensely loyal followers who would push past what they believed possible — illustrating both the potential upside of charisma (extraordinary achievement, e.g., Gorilla Glass in under six months) and the theoretical risk flagged in the slides that leaders can become \"intoxicated\" by their own charisma, and that charismatic influence can shade into control rather than empowerment."},{"t":"li","x":"**3. Personal attributes of effective leaders (drive, self-concept, knowledge of the business): **Jobs displays an extreme version of \"drive\" (boldness, action-orientation, relentless achievement need), a very strong, internally consistent self-concept/self-view as a leader-innovator (\"that’s not who I am\"), and deep knowledge of the business/product across both the big picture and fine details — while also showing the limitation that attributes indicate potential, not guaranteed universal effectiveness: his \"tough on people\" management style is explicitly debated in the case as something that worked in his specific context, but that other CEOs who copy the harshness without his ability to also inspire genuine loyalty \"make a dangerous mistake.\""}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"9. Complementary Practice Exercises (New Questions)"},{"t":"p","x":"These are original questions (not from Moodle), in addition to the official quiz reproduced in Section 10 below."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A startup founder tells the team \"we are going to change how people commute forever,\" works alongside engineers late into the night on the smallest UI details, and publicly celebrates any team member who challenges the current product roadmap with a better idea. Which perspective of leadership does this best illustrate?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Managerial leadership only"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Transformational leadership (vision + modeling + encouraging experimentation)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Leadership substitutes theory"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Implicit leadership only"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: According to path-goal theory, which leadership style pairing is most appropriate for a highly skilled, experienced team with an internal locus of control who wants to be stretched further?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Achievement-oriented (and participative)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive only"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive only"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between managerial leadership and transformational leadership, and why the slides describe them as \"interdependent\" rather than substitutes for one another."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: What is \"leadership substitutes theory,\" and give one workplace example of a factor that could substitute for supportive leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly promoted team lead has excellent technical knowledge and a clear personal vision for improving the product, but her team says she \"micromanages every decision and never lets us push back.\" Using authentic leadership and the leader-attributes perspective, identify what seems to be missing, and recommend one concrete change."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Transformational leadership — the vision statement (\"change how people commute forever\"), personally modeling high standards, and celebrating challenges to the status quo map directly onto the three elements: develop/communicate a vision, model the vision, and encourage experimentation.\n**2. **b) Achievement-oriented (often combined with participative) — path-goal theory recommends achievement-oriented and participative styles for skilled, experienced employees with an internal locus of control, since directive leadership would be seen as excessive, unwanted control for this group.\n**3. **Managerial leadership is the daily, micro-focused work of supporting current performance and well-being in a relatively stable environment (\"doing things right\"); transformational leadership is the macro-focused work of communicating and driving a shift toward a new future state in a dynamic environment (\"doing the right things\"). They are interdependent because an organisation needs both a compelling future vision (transformational) and disciplined day-to-day execution and support (managerial) — a leader who is purely visionary but never manages daily operations well (or vice versa) will struggle to sustain performance.\n**4. **Leadership substitutes theory identifies contingencies that either limit how much a leader can actually influence outcomes, or make a specific leadership style unnecessary. Example: a highly cohesive team with strong mutual social support can substitute for supportive leadership, because members already provide each other the psychological support a leader would otherwise need to supply.\n**5. **What seems to be missing is the \"be yourself\"/self-discipline balance of authentic leadership, combined with an over-reliance on directive control rather than participative/achievement-oriented leadership suited to a presumably capable team; from the leader-attributes perspective, she may have strong knowledge/drive but is not yet demonstrating the relational, empowering side of effective leadership (also connects to servant/shared leadership — trusting the team to lead in areas of their own expertise). Concrete change: deliberately delegate one or two recurring decisions fully to the team, explicitly inviting dissent/challenge in the next meeting (moving toward a more participative or achievement-oriented style), and seek trusted feedback on how her behaviour is being perceived."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"10. Official MCQ Quiz — Chapter 12 Leadership (with Answers)"},{"t":"p","x":"Reproduced from the course-provided quiz bank. Use this to self-test recall of exact terminology before the exam."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"True / False"},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 1. **The directive style is unnecessary when employees have routine and simple tasks."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 2. **One concern of path-goal theory is that it assumes leaders can change managerial styles to fit the situation."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 3. **Research indicates that male and female leaders widely differ in their levels of task-oriented and people-oriented leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 4. **Charismatic visionary is a universally recognized leadership category."},{"t":"p","x":"**T/F 5. **According to path-goal leadership theory, directive leadership should never be applied to inexperienced employees."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Multiple Choice"},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Leaders who create, communicate, and model a shared vision for the team are using the ________ perspective."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Transformational leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Path-goal leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Managerial leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Substitute leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **________ leadership is mostly a variation of people-oriented leadership."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Path-goal"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Shared"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Managerial"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Transformational"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Servant"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Which of the following leadership theories or perspectives explicitly includes the participative and the directive styles?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Competency (trait) perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Transformational leadership theory"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Path-goal theory"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Managerial leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **________ exists when employees persuade coworkers to try out a new work activity or introduce new technology."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) The romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Shared leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Directive leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **The \"romance of leadership\" is important for which of the following approaches?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Transformational leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Managerial leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Implicit leadership perspective"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Contingency perspective of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Servant perspective of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**11. **Which of these concepts discusses the tendency of employees to overestimate the leader’s influence on organizational outcomes?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Path-goal leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Transactional leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Managerial leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**12. **How do leadership prototypes develop?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Defined by organizational policy"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Defined by leaders"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Through culture, as all cultures share a universal prototype"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Through the romance of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Through socialization within families and society"},{"t":"p","x":"**13. **Why do people tend to evaluate female leaders slightly less favorably than male leaders?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Females tend to adopt a directive style of leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) People have more trust in authoritarian leadership than participative leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) People tend to rely on gender stereotypes and prototypes of leaders"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Females tend to use one leadership style, whereas effective leaders use many styles"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Females are more people-oriented than task-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"**14. **Which of the following statements about culture and leadership is true?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Participative leadership is perceived as characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"b) There are no similarities and only differences in leadership across cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Transformational leadership theory explains differences in leadership practices across cultures"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Employees in high power-distance cultures prefer a participative leadership style"},{"t":"p","x":"e) People’s expectations from their leaders are universal and do not differ across countries"},{"t":"p","x":"**15. **How do females differ from males in their use of leadership styles?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Females tend to use more of the people-oriented leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Females have much lower interpersonal skills than males"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Females tend to use more of the task-oriented leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Females tend to use more of the participative leadership style than do males"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Females tend to use directive styles of leadership in organizations"},{"t":"p","x":"**16. **How do cultural values relate to leader behavior?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Leaders define cultural values"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Cultural values have no impact on a leader’s actions"},{"t":"p","x":"c) There is a universal set of cultural values all leaders need to adopt"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Cultural values have no impact on leader behaviors"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Culture shapes the leader’s values"},{"t":"p","x":"**17. **Studies have found that females, when compared to males, are evaluated negatively as leaders when they:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Empower employees"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Use a participative style"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Use a directive style"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Use coaching"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Behave according to their followers’ expectations"},{"t":"p","x":"**18. **________ is a leadership style identified in path-goal theory?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Servant leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Transformational leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Implicit leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Authentic leadership"},{"t":"p","x":"**19. **Which of the following leadership styles in path-goal theory is the same as task-oriented leadership?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Collaborative"},{"t":"p","x":"**20. **Which of the following leadership styles in path-goal theory is the same as people-oriented leadership?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Telling"},{"t":"p","x":"**21. **According to path-goal theory, a combination of ________ leadership is best for employees who are (or perceive themselves to be) inexperienced and unskilled."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Participative and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive and achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"**22. **People with an external locus of control tend to be more satisfied with ________ leadership styles."},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Participative and directive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Supportive and achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Supportive and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"**23. **When team cohesiveness is low, which of the following leadership styles should be used?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Directive"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented"},{"t":"p","x":"e) Authoritative"},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Short Answer (Official Quiz)"},{"t":"p","x":"**SA1. **Briefly describe the implicit leadership theory."},{"t":"p","x":"**SA2. **Is the following statement accurate? Why or why not? \"Females are less effective than males in leadership positions because they tend to rely too much on the supportive style of leadership.\""},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**True/False:**\n**1. **TRUE — the directive style is ineffective (unnecessary) with routine/simple tasks because the manager’s guidance serves no purpose and can be seen as unnecessarily close control.\n**2. **TRUE — a real concern with path-goal theory is that it assumes leaders can readily switch styles to fit the situation, when in reality this takes considerable effort because leaders tend to prefer one style consistent with their own personality/values.\n**3. **FALSE — field studies generally find male and female leaders do not differ much in task-oriented or people-oriented leadership levels.\n**4. **TRUE — \"charismatic visionary\" (visionary, inspirational, high performance orientation, integrity, decisiveness) is recognized across cultures by middle managers worldwide as characteristic of effective leaders.\n**5. **FALSE — a combination of directive and supportive leadership is actually recommended for inexperienced/unskilled employees; directive leadership is only detrimental for skilled, experienced employees (too much supervisory control).\n**Multiple Choice:**\n**6. **a) Transformational leadership — creating, communicating and modeling a shared vision is the defining behaviour set of transformational leaders.\n**7. **e) Servant leadership — it is essentially an extension/variation of people-oriented leadership, defined as serving others’ need fulfilment, development and growth.\n**8. **d) Path-goal theory — its four styles explicitly include directive, supportive, participative and achievement-oriented leadership.\n**9. **c) Shared leadership — exists when employees persuade coworkers to try new activities/technology, or support each other through influence/OCBs.\n**10. **c) Implicit leadership perspective — the \"romance of leadership\" (inflating perceived leader influence on outcomes) is one of its two core components, alongside leadership prototypes.\n**11. **a) The romance of leadership — this is precisely the tendency to inflate a leader’s perceived effect on organisational success.\n**12. **e) Through socialization within the family and society — leadership prototypes are learned expectations built up from early life experience, not imposed by policy.\n**13. **c) People tend to rely on gender stereotypes and prototypes of leaders — this shapes (distorts) evaluation of female leaders independent of their actual competence.\n**14. **a) Participative leadership is perceived as characteristic of effective leadership in low power-distance cultures (but less so in high power-distance cultures).\n**15. **d) Females tend to use more of the participative leadership style than males (linked in the slides to socialization toward being more egalitarian/less status-oriented).\n**16. **e) Culture shapes the leader’s values and norms, which in turn shape the leader’s decisions/actions, and also shapes followers’ expectations of leaders.\n**17. **c) Use a directive style — studies find females are evaluated negatively when they apply a directive/autocratic style, because this conflicts with followers’ more participative, communal prototype of a female leader, even though the same behaviour is judged less harshly when a male leader does it.\n**18. **a) Achievement-oriented leadership — one of the four path-goal styles (directive, supportive, participative, achievement-oriented).\n**19. **a) Directive — directive leadership is the same as task-oriented leadership (clarifying goals, methods, and performance standards).\n**20. **b) Supportive — supportive leadership is the same as people-oriented leadership (providing psychological support).\n**21. **c) Directive and supportive — directive leadership gives task information, supportive leadership helps them cope with uncertainty, which together best fit inexperienced/unskilled employees.\n**22. **c) Directive and supportive — people with an external locus of control believe outcomes depend on luck/fate rather than their own effort, so they respond better to more directive/supportive guidance.\n**23. **b) Supportive — when team cohesion is low, leaders should use the supportive style to help build morale and cohesion.\n**Short Answer:**\n**SA1. **The implicit leadership perspective explains that followers’ own perceptions shape how \"effective\" a leader is judged to be. It has two components: (1) leadership prototypes — preconceived beliefs about the features/behaviours of an effective leader, developed through socialization within family and society, which shape our willingness to accept someone as a leader; and (2) the romance of leadership — followers tend to inflate a leader’s perceived influence on organisational success, partly because it simplifies complex events, and partly (in many cultures) because people prefer to believe outcomes are driven by people rather than uncontrollable forces.\n**SA2. **The statement is false. Research indicates females are about as effective as males in leadership roles (so the premise that they are \"less effective\" is incorrect), and females do not, in fact, disproportionately rely on the supportive style — they tend to adopt more of a participative style than male leaders. Females are often rated higher than males on emerging leadership qualities like coaching, teamwork and empowerment, but they can still be evaluated unfairly (negatively) when applying more directive/autocratic styles, because of gender stereotypes and follower leader-prototypes — so any leadership effectiveness gap is more about biased perception than actual differences in ability or style."}]},"8":{"title":"Power and Influence in the Workplace","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 10   |   Scheduled: 2 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. The Meaning of Power"},{"t":"li","x":"**Power: **the capacity of a person, team or organisation to influence others — a potential to change attitudes/behaviour, not necessarily an actual attempt to do so. It depends on the target’s perception that the power-holder controls a valuable resource, requires a minimum level of trust between both parties, and involves unequal dependence between the parties."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Five Sources of Power"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source","Description"],["Legitimate power","Agreement that people in specific positions can request certain behaviours from others; bounded by each follower’s \"zone of indifference\" (their willingness to defer to authority in a given domain); connected to the norm of reciprocity and to information control (legitimate power lets you distribute/gatekeep information)."],["Reward power","Control over rewards valued by others, or the ability to remove negative sanctions."],["Coercive power","The ability to apply punishment."],["Expert power","The capacity to influence others through valued knowledge or skill; risk of \"mindless deference\" to expertise. A key form is managing uncertainty — preventing, forecasting, or absorbing environmental changes for the organisation."],["Referent power","The capacity to influence others through identification with, and respect for, the power-holder; associated with charisma."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Contingencies of Power"},{"t":"li","x":"**Nonsubstitutability: **power increases when a resource has few/no other sources and few/no substitutes; can be increased by differentiating the resource or controlling access to it."},{"t":"li","x":"**Centrality: **how interdependent others are with the power-holder — how many people, and how quickly, are affected by that person’s actions/resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Visibility: **being known as the holder of a valued resource — built through social interaction and visible \"power symbols.\""},{"t":"li","x":"**Discretion: **freedom to exercise independent judgement — formal rules reduce discretion and, with it, power."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Power Through Social Networks"},{"t":"li","x":"**Social networks: **connect people through interdependence, serving instrumental (task-related) and expressive (emotional/social) functions; cultural variants exist (e.g., \"guanxi\" in Chinese business networks)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Social capital: **the goodwill (mutual support, trust, reciprocity, coordination) that lets people in a network actually share knowledge, opportunities and other resources."},{"t":"li","x":"**Strong ties: **close-knit relationships — offer resources quickly and plentifully, but the resources tend to be less unique (everyone in a tight circle already has similar information)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Weak ties: **acquaintances — offer more unique resources, but more slowly."},{"t":"li","x":"**Network centrality: **a person’s overall importance in a network, based on betweenness (sitting between other people/connecting them), degree centrality (sheer number of connections), and closeness (strength of connections)."},{"t":"p","x":"Networks provide three power resources: information (expert power), visibility, and referent power."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Consequences of Power"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type of power experience","Typical effect"],["Feeling empowered (power over oneself / freedom from others’ influence)","Higher motivation, but can bring less mindful thinking, less empathy, and more reliance on stereotypes."],["Having power over others","Tends to produce a sense of duty/responsibility, less stereotyping, more empathy, and more mindfulness of how one’s actions affect others."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Influencing Others — Eight Influence Tactics"},{"t":"p","x":"Influence: any behaviour that attempts to alter another person’s attitudes or behaviour, applying one or more power bases, conditional on the power contingencies above. Influence is essential to coordination, and is central to the very definition of leadership."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Tactic","Description"],["Silent authority","The mere presence or request of the power-holder influences behaviour (subtle use of legitimate power)."],["Assertiveness","\"Vocal authority\" — reminding, checking, or applying pressure (legitimate/coercive power)."],["Information control","Manipulating others’ access to information to shape their attitudes/behaviour."],["Coalition formation","Pooling members’ resources and power to influence others; symbolizes the issue as legitimate/important and adds influence via social identity."],["Upward appeal","Claiming (or showing evidence of) support from higher authority."],["Persuasion","Logical arguments and/or emotional appeals; effectiveness depends on the persuader, the message, the channel, and the audience."],["Impression management","Actively shaping others’ perceptions of us — includes self-presentation/personal branding and ingratiation (building liking/perceived similarity)."],["Exchange","Trading resources for a desired behaviour — central to negotiation, reciprocity, and social networks."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Contingencies of Influence"},{"t":"li","x":"\"Soft\" tactics (e.g., persuasion) are generally more acceptable than \"hard\" tactics (e.g., assertiveness)."},{"t":"li","x":"The best tactic depends on the influencer’s power base (e.g., experts succeed with persuasion; strong legitimate power holders succeed with silent authority)."},{"t":"li","x":"It also depends on organisational position (e.g., being too assertive with one’s boss can carry consequences) and on personal, organisational and cultural values (e.g., ingratiation is more common in the US than in Hong Kong, possibly linked to power distance)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Organisational Politics"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **using influence tactics for personal gain at the perceived expense of others or the organisation — generally has negative, not beneficial, consequences."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing organisational politics: **provide sufficient resources, clarify resource-allocation rules, manage change effectively, and actively discourage political behaviour."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A finance manager is highly valued because she is the only person in the company who fully understands the firm’s complex tax structure, and no one else has time to learn it. This mainly illustrates which power contingency?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Visibility"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Nonsubstitutability"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Discretion"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Centrality"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: An employee mentions in a meeting, \"the VP already agrees with this plan, she told me so yesterday,\" in order to get buy-in from a hesitant colleague. This is an example of which influence tactic?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Coalition formation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Upward appeal"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Silent authority"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Exchange"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain the difference between \"strong ties\" and \"weak ties\" in a social network, and why weak ties are often described as valuable for finding a new job."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish \"feeling empowered\" from \"having power over others\" in terms of their typical psychological and behavioural consequences."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A newly hired specialist has deep technical expertise that nobody else in the department has (expert power), but she has almost no visibility — most senior leaders don’t know her by name, and she rarely interacts outside her immediate team. Using the power contingencies framework, explain why her actual influence in the organisation may be lower than her expertise alone would suggest, and recommend one action to increase her power."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Nonsubstitutability — her power comes from being the only source of a needed, non-substitutable resource (specialized tax knowledge), not from being widely known (visibility) or having discretion/centrality per se.\n**2. **b) Upward appeal — claiming or showing evidence of support from a higher authority (the VP) to influence the colleague’s decision.\n**3. **Strong ties are close-knit relationships (e.g., close colleagues, family) that provide resources quickly and abundantly, but because everyone in a tight circle tends to share similar information, the resources are less unique. Weak ties are more distant acquaintances who provide resources more slowly, but because they move in different circles, they are more likely to offer genuinely new, unique information — such as a job opening you would never hear about through your close inner circle. This is why weak ties are valuable for job searching: they act as bridges to information you don’t already have.\n**4. **\"Feeling empowered\" (power over oneself, freedom from others’ influence) tends to raise motivation but can reduce mindful thinking and empathy and increase reliance on stereotypes about others. \"Having power over others,\" by contrast, tends to create a sense of duty/responsibility and is linked to less stereotyping, more empathy, and more mindfulness of how one’s actions affect other people — i.e., power directed at others, when experienced as a responsibility, tends to produce more careful, other-aware behaviour than power experienced purely as personal freedom.\n**5. **Even strong expert power delivers limited real influence if the contingencies of power are not also present: without visibility (being known as the holder of that expertise) and without centrality (being connected to, and depended on by, many people across the organisation), her expert power stays \"dormant\" because decision-makers simply don’t know to draw on it or trust it. Recommended action: deliberately build visibility and network centrality — e.g., presenting her findings at cross-departmental meetings, contributing to visible cross-functional projects, or building an internal professional profile/personal brand — so that her expertise becomes known and accessible (also connecting expertise to information/referent power through the social network)."}]},"9":{"title":"Conflict and Negotiation in the Workplace","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 11   |   Scheduled: 9 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Is Conflict Good or Bad?"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Negative outcomes","Positive outcomes"],["Lower performance; higher stress, dissatisfaction, turnover; less information sharing/coordination; more organisational politics; wasted resources; weaker team cohesion (conflict within the team)","Better decision making; tests the logic of arguments; questions assumptions; generates creative thinking; more responsiveness to a changing environment; stronger team cohesion (conflict with an outside opponent)"]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Task vs. Relationship Conflict"},{"t":"li","x":"**Task (constructive) conflict: **focuses on the quality of ideas/arguments (logic, evidence, assumptions), shows respect for all participants, and avoids attacking the opponent’s competence or authority."},{"t":"li","x":"**Relationship conflict: **focuses on the opponent’s character, tries to win by undermining their credibility, and relies on status/assertiveness — dysfunctional because it triggers defense mechanisms, reduces trust, and reduces motivation to communicate, escalating the conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"**Minimizing relationship conflict during task conflict: **emotional intelligence (regulating emotions, reframing hostility), team development (mutual understanding, cohesion motivating de-escalation), and psychological safety norms (safe to disagree constructively, with respect and open-mindedness)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. The Conflict Process and Its Structural Sources"},{"t":"p","x":"The conflict process model traces how sources of conflict lead to perceptions/emotions, then to manifest behaviour, then to outcomes/resolution attempts."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Six Structural Sources of Conflict"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Source","Description"],["Incompatible goals","One party’s goals appear to interfere with another’s."],["Differentiation","Different training, values, beliefs and experience between parties (e.g., generational diversity)."],["Interdependence","All conflict requires interdependence; conflict risk rises as interdependence increases."],["Scarce resources","Creates direct competition for the resource."],["Ambiguous rules","Creates uncertainty and opens the door to office politics."],["Communication problems","Poorly stated messages escalate conflict; conflict itself causes more emotive messaging, and reduced communication then amplifies the conflict further."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Five Interpersonal Conflict-Handling Styles"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Style","Best used when...","Main risk"],["Problem solving","Interests are not perfectly opposed; parties trust each other/are open; the issue is complex.","Takes time; sharing information can increase the other party’s power."],["Forcing","A quick resolution is required; your position has stronger logical/moral grounds; the other party would otherwise exploit cooperation.","Relationship damage; harms long-term relations."],["Avoiding","The conflict is emotionally charged (relationship conflict); parties want to preserve harmony; resolution costs exceed its benefits.","The conflict remains unresolved, causing ongoing frustration/uncertainty."],["Yielding","The issue matters less to you than the other party; your position is logically weaker; the other party has much more power; harmony matters.","Raises the other party’s future expectations."],["Compromising","A single issue with opposing interests; parties lack time/trust for problem solving; roughly equal power; harmony matters.","Can produce a sub-optimal outcome where a larger mutual gain was possible."]]},{"t":"li","x":"**Cultural/gender differences: **collectivist cultures tend to show more avoidance; men tend to use more forcing; women tend to use somewhat more avoiding (especially female managers), problem solving, compromising and yielding — often linked to differing motivations/expectations around protecting relationships."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Structural Approaches to Conflict Management"},{"t":"li","x":"Emphasize superordinate goals — focus on shared objectives, which reduces goal incompatibility and may reduce differentiation."},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce differentiation — create common experiences (meaningful interaction, staff rotation, building a strong shared culture)."},{"t":"li","x":"Improve communication and understanding — e.g., Johari Window and contact-hypothesis-based activities (only effective once differentiation has been reduced first)."},{"t":"li","x":"Reduce interdependence — use buffers (e.g., inventory), use integrator roles, or combine jobs into one."},{"t":"li","x":"Increase resources — weigh the cost of extra resources against the cost of ongoing conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"Clarify rules/procedures — establish clear rules, roles, responsibilities and schedules."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Negotiation"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **trying to resolve divergent goals by redefining the terms of interdependence between parties."},{"t":"li","x":"**Distributive approach: **a win-lose orientation, most common with a single item to resolve."},{"t":"li","x":"**Integrative (mutual gains) approach: **a win-win orientation, works better with multiple issues of different value to each party (allowing trade-offs)."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"BATNA and Power in Negotiation"},{"t":"p","x":"BATNA = Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement — it represents your estimated cost of walking away. Having more than one BATNA increases your negotiating power. Negotiation power overall rises with favourable sources and contingencies of power (Chapter 10), which also tend to improve your BATNA."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"The Negotiation Process"},{"t":"li","x":"**Gather information: **listen closely, ask questions, watch nonverbal cues, summarize the other party’s statements, and share your own reactions — the goal is to discover the needs behind their stated positions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Manage concessions: **make fewer, smaller, clearly labeled concessions; explicitly ask for reciprocity; use multi-issue offers rather than resolving one issue at a time."},{"t":"li","x":"**Manage time and relationships: **be aware of deadline effects, exploding offers and escalating time commitments; build trustworthiness by finding common ground, managing first impressions, signalling trustworthiness, and using emotional intelligence."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Negotiation Setting"},{"t":"li","x":"Location — easier to negotiate on your own turf."},{"t":"li","x":"Physical setting — seating, spacing, and formality all matter."},{"t":"li","x":"Audience — negotiators become more competitive and make fewer concessions when being watched."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Gender and Negotiation"},{"t":"p","x":"Compared with men, women in negotiations tend to set lower target points and accept offers closer to their resistance point, rely less on alternatives to improve outcomes, avoid initiating negotiation (accepting first offers more often), receive more deceitful tactics from counterparts, and be judged less favourably when they do use effective, assertive negotiation tactics. With training and experience, women negotiate about as well as men."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Third-Party Dispute Resolution"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Type","Key features"],["Inquisition","The third party (often a manager) controls both the investigation process and the final decision. Popular with managers (fits a decision-oriented role) but usually the least effective method, and can conflict with procedural justice principles."],["Mediation","The third party controls the process but not the outcome, helping the parties reach their own solution. Generally has the highest potential satisfaction with both process and outcome."],["Arbitration","The third party controls the outcome via predetermined rules/processes (used when mediation fails). Provides procedural justice because rules and process are set in advance."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: Two departments are fighting over the same limited annual training budget, with no clear rule for how it should be split. Which two structural sources of conflict are most clearly present?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Differentiation and interdependence"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Scarce resources and ambiguous rules"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Communication problems and incompatible goals"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Interdependence and communication problems"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: In a negotiation, a job candidate says, \"I have two other strong offers on the table,\" giving her more confidence to hold firm on salary. This illustrates the importance of:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) The bargaining zone model"},{"t":"p","x":"b) BATNA (and having more than one)"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Distributive justice"},{"t":"p","x":"d) The conflict process model"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain why \"avoiding\" is sometimes the appropriate conflict-handling style, giving one situation where it would be the best choice and one risk of overusing it."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Distinguish mediation from arbitration, and explain why mediation typically produces higher satisfaction with the outcome than arbitration or inquisition."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: Two team members are in an escalating dispute over how to approach a shared project. What started as a disagreement about the best method (task conflict) has turned personal — they are now openly questioning each other’s competence and credibility in front of the team (relationship conflict). As their manager, using conflict theory, describe two specific actions you would take to de-escalate the relationship conflict while still allowing the useful task conflict (differing ideas about method) to continue productively."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Scarce resources and ambiguous rules — a limited shared budget is a classic scarce-resource conflict, and the absence of a clear allocation rule directly matches the \"ambiguous rules\" structural source.\n**2. **b) BATNA (having more than one) — her alternative offers represent her Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement; having multiple strong BATNAs increases her negotiating power and confidence to hold her position.\n**3. **Avoiding is appropriate when the conflict is emotionally charged (relationship conflict), when parties want to preserve harmony, or when the cost of resolving the issue outweighs the benefit — e.g., a minor stylistic disagreement between two colleagues who otherwise work well together might best be left alone rather than escalated. The risk of overusing it is that the underlying issue remains unresolved, which can cause ongoing frustration, uncertainty, and (if used repeatedly on substantive issues) allow real problems to fester.\n**4. **In mediation, the third party controls the process (e.g., structuring the conversation, ensuring both sides are heard) but the parties themselves control the outcome/solution. In arbitration, the third party controls the actual outcome, based on predetermined rules. Mediation typically produces the highest satisfaction because the parties retain ownership of the solution (voice and self-determination, consistent with procedural justice), whereas arbitration and especially inquisition impose an outcome on the parties, reducing their sense of control and voice even if the process itself feels reasonably fair.\n**5. **Two acceptable actions: (1) Explicitly separate the person from the problem — redirect the conversation back to the specific task/method being debated, and set (or reinforce) a team norm of psychological safety that disagreement about ideas is welcome but attacking a colleague’s competence or character is not; this uses \"structural approaches to conflict management\" (clarifying norms/rules) and protects task conflict while suppressing relationship conflict. (2) Act as a mediator (not an arbitrator) in a private conversation with both parties — control the process (structured turn-taking, active listening, summarizing) without dictating the final method decision, so both retain ownership of the eventual solution; this both rebuilds trust (reducing relationship conflict) and keeps the substantive method discussion (task conflict) alive."}]},"10":{"title":"Culture and Organisational Change","notes":[{"t":"p","x":"Textbook reference: Chapter 14 (+ Chapter 15, pp. 215-218, 220-221)   |   Scheduled: 16 Nov 2026"},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"1. Organisational Culture Defined"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the values and assumptions shared within an organisation, providing direction toward the \"right way\" of doing things — often described as the company’s (largely invisible) \"DNA,\" a powerful template for employee behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"**The \"iceberg\" idea: **visible artifacts sit above the surface, while the deeper values and shared assumptions that actually drive behaviour sit unseen below it — you must look at multiple artifacts to accurately decipher a culture, since culture is often fuzzy, fragmented into subcultures, and lives within individuals rather than as one single, unified entity."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Organisational Culture Profile — Content Dimensions"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Dimension","Characteristics"],["Innovation","Experimenting, opportunity-seeking, risk-taking, few rules."],["Stability","Predictability, security, rule-oriented."],["Respect for people","Fairness, tolerance."],["Outcome orientation","Action-oriented, high expectations, results-oriented."],["Attention to detail","Precise, analytic."],["Team orientation","Collaboration, people-oriented."],["Aggressiveness","Competitive, low emphasis on social responsibility."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Subcultures"},{"t":"li","x":"**Dominant culture: **the values/assumptions shared most consistently and widely across the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Subcultures: **exist throughout the organisation; countercultures are subcultures that oppose parts of the dominant culture — they serve two useful functions: surveillance/critical review, and acting as a source of emerging (future) values."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"2. Culture Artifacts"},{"t":"p","x":"Observable symbols/signs that maintain and transmit culture — you typically need several artifacts together to accurately decipher a company’s real culture."},{"t":"li","x":"**Stories and legends: **social prescriptions of desired (or dysfunctional) behaviour; most effective when they describe real people, are believed to be true, are known throughout the organisation, and are prescriptive."},{"t":"li","x":"**Language: **how employees address each other/outsiders, express emotion and describe stakeholders; leaders use language to anchor or shift culture; language also differentiates subcultures."},{"t":"li","x":"**Rituals: **programmed routines (e.g., how visitors are greeted)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Ceremonies: **planned activities for an audience (e.g., award ceremonies)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Physical structures/symbols: **building design, office layout, furniture and other physical cues that both shape and reflect culture."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"3. Culture Strength and Effectiveness"},{"t":"li","x":"**Culture strength: **how widely and deeply employees hold the organisation’s dominant values/assumptions. In strong cultures, most employees understand and embrace the dominant values, these are institutionalized through well-established artifacts, and the culture is long-lasting (often traceable to the founder)."},{"t":"p","x":"Three functions of a strong culture: (1) control system — an \"automatic pilot\" guiding decisions/behaviour; (2) social glue — bonds people together, fulfilling the need for social identity; (3) sense-making — helps employees understand what is expected of them (role clarity). Culture also shapes ethical conduct."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Contingencies of Culture Strength"},{"t":"li","x":"Culture content must be aligned with the environment — a misaligned culture guides the wrong decisions for stakeholder relations."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture strength should not reach the level of a cult — cults lock people into rigid mental models and suppress dissenting subcultures."},{"t":"li","x":"The culture should be an adaptive culture — externally focused, supporting continuous improvement and a learning orientation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"4. Merging Organisational Cultures"},{"t":"li","x":"**Bicultural audit: **part of merger due diligence — (1) identify cultural differences between the merging firms; (2) analyze which differences will cause conflict and which provide common ground; (3) identify strategies and action plans to bridge the two cultures."},{"t":"table","rows":[["Strategy","Description","Best when..."],["Assimilation","The acquired firm’s staff embrace the acquiring firm’s culture.","The acquired culture is weak/dysfunctional and the acquiring culture is strong and successful. (Rare.)"],["Deculturation","The acquiring firm imposes its culture and practices.","The acquired firm’s culture clearly does not work; often meets employee resistance."],["Integration","A composite culture preserves the best of both.","Both firms have relatively weak cultures, values overlap, or staff are motivated to adopt new shared values. (Slow, potentially risky.)"],["Separation","Each firm keeps its own culture/practices.","The merging firms are in unrelated industries or different countries."]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"5. Changing and Strengthening Organisational Culture"},{"t":"li","x":"Actions of founders/leaders — a founder’s values/personality shape early culture; transformational leaders can reshape culture during organisational change."},{"t":"li","x":"Aligning artifacts — since artifacts keep culture \"in place,\" changing them (stories, symbols, language) helps change the culture itself."},{"t":"li","x":"Introducing culturally consistent rewards — rewards are powerful artifacts that reinforce desired values."},{"t":"li","x":"Supporting workforce stability and communication — it takes time to build shared understanding of a culture, and strong cultures depend on frequent, open communication."},{"t":"li","x":"Attracting, selecting and socializing employees — via the Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) framework and formal socialization practices."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) Theory"},{"t":"li","x":"**Attraction: **applicants self-select toward organisations whose values feel compatible with their own."},{"t":"li","x":"**Selection: **firms select applicants whose values seem compatible with the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Attrition: **employees whose values are incompatible eventually quit or are removed."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"6. Organisational Socialization"},{"t":"li","x":"**Definition: **the process by which individuals learn the values, expected behaviours and social knowledge needed to take on their role in the organisation."},{"t":"li","x":"**Learning process: **newcomers build organisational comprehension — learning expectations, power dynamics, culture and jargon — and form social relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"**Adjustment process: **newcomers adapt to the new work setting, develop new work roles/social identity, and adopt new team norms/behaviours; those with more diverse prior work experience tend to adjust better."},{"t":"li","x":"**Psychological contracts: **an individual’s beliefs about the terms of a reciprocal exchange with the organisation — transactional contracts are short-term, well-defined economic exchanges; relational contracts are long-term attachments with a broad range of mutual obligations."},{"t":"li","x":"**Improving socialization: **realistic job previews (RJPs — giving a balanced mix of positive and negative information about the job/context before hiring), and socialization agents such as supervisors (technical information, feedback, job duties) and co-workers (role models, ideally accessible, tolerant and supportive)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"7. Organisational Change (Chapter 15 — Supplementary Content)"},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **The slide deck for Chapter 15 (Organisational Change) was not among the Moodle files available for this synthesis, even though the exam-revision summary explicitly lists Lewin’s force field analysis, why people resist change, and the unfreezing-changing-refreezing stages as examinable. The notes below use standard, well-established OB theory on this exact topic so your coverage is complete — please verify the precise terminology, examples and any extra detail against your own lecture slides once you can access them."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lewin’s Force Field Analysis"},{"t":"p","x":"Kurt Lewin proposed that, at any point, an organisation is in a state of relative equilibrium held in place by two opposing sets of forces:"},{"t":"li","x":"**Driving forces: **pressures pushing the organisation toward change (e.g., new technology, competitive threats, leadership pressure, new regulations)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Restraining forces: **pressures maintaining the status quo (e.g., habit, fear, vested interests, group norms, structural inertia)."},{"t":"p","x":"Change happens when driving forces outweigh restraining forces. Simply increasing driving forces can raise tension and resistance; the model suggests it is often more effective (and more sustainable) to reduce restraining forces as well as, or instead of, only pushing harder."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Lewin’s Three-Step Change Model"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Step","What happens"],["1. Unfreezing","Creating the motivation and readiness for change — building dissatisfaction with the current state, communicating why change is needed, and reducing restraining forces."],["2. Changing (moving)","Actually implementing the new attitudes, behaviours, processes or structures — often through training, new routines, and role modeling by leaders."],["3. Refreezing","Stabilizing the new state so it becomes the new normal — reinforcing new behaviours through rewards, aligned artifacts/culture, and consistent reinforcement, so the organisation does not drift back to the old way."]]},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Why People Resist Change (Standard Reasons)"},{"t":"li","x":"Fear of the unknown / uncertainty about how change will personally affect them."},{"t":"li","x":"Loss of something valued — status, power, job security, established relationships, or a sense of competence and mastery (\"saving face\" — not wanting to look incompetent under new methods)."},{"t":"li","x":"Breaking established habits and routines, which feel effortful and uncomfortable."},{"t":"li","x":"Lack of trust in leadership, or poor/insufficient communication about the reasons for and nature of the change."},{"t":"li","x":"Disruption to social relationships and existing team dynamics."},{"t":"li","x":"A genuine (not merely self-interested) belief that the proposed change is the wrong direction."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"General Strategies for Managing Resistance to Change"},{"t":"li","x":"Communication and education — explain the need for and nature of the change clearly and early."},{"t":"li","x":"Participation and involvement — let affected employees help shape the change, increasing buy-in and voice."},{"t":"li","x":"Facilitation and support — training, counselling, and resources to ease the transition."},{"t":"li","x":"Negotiation and agreement — where some parties will clearly lose out, negotiate to offset their losses."},{"t":"li","x":"Coercion — used sparingly, when speed is essential and other approaches have failed; carries relationship risk."},{"t":"p","x":"This connects directly back to organisational culture: successful, lasting organisational change usually requires \"unfreezing\" old cultural assumptions and then \"refreezing\" new ones using the same culture-change levers covered in Section 5 above (leader actions, aligned artifacts, rewards, and socialization)."}],"ex":[{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Complementary Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"New, original questions to help you self-test this lesson (not from the official slides)."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A company’s founder personally tells a well-known internal story, in every new-hire orientation, about the time she refunded an angry customer out of her own pocket before the company had a formal refund policy. This is primarily an example of which type of culture artifact?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) A ritual"},{"t":"p","x":"b) A ceremony"},{"t":"p","x":"c) A story/legend"},{"t":"p","x":"d) A physical symbol"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: Two companies with very different, incompatible national cultures merge, but the acquiring firm decides to let each unit keep running under its own existing culture and management practices. This is an example of which culture-merging strategy?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Assimilation"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Deculturation"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Integration"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Separation"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **Short answer: Explain, using Lewin’s force field analysis, why simply \"pushing harder\" for change (increasing driving forces) without addressing restraining forces can sometimes backfire."},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **Short answer: Using the Attraction-Selection-Attrition (ASA) framework, explain how a strong culture can become self-reinforcing over time, for better or worse."},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Applied scenario: A company is rolling out a new company-wide software system. Several long-tenured employees are quietly avoiding training sessions and continuing to use the old manual process \"just in case,\" even though management has announced the change. Using Lewin’s model and the standard reasons for resisting change, diagnose what stage of change this suggests the organisation is stuck at, identify the most likely reason(s) for resistance, and recommend one action for each of the unfreezing and refreezing stages."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **c) A story/legend — it describes a real person, is presented as true, is known throughout the organisation (told at every orientation), and is prescriptive (it signals desired customer-first behaviour) — matching all four features of an effective story/legend.\n**2. **d) Separation — each firm keeps its own corporate culture and practices, which the slides identify as the best approach when merging firms are in unrelated industries or very different countries/cultures.\n**3. **Force field analysis models an organisation as held in equilibrium by driving forces (for change) and restraining forces (against change). Simply increasing driving forces (e.g., more pressure, mandates, deadlines) without reducing restraining forces (fear, habit, distrust, loss of status) raises overall tension in the system: employees may comply superficially while resistance and anxiety build \"underground,\" or resistance may intensify to counterbalance the added pressure, producing more conflict and less durable change than an approach that also actively reduces restraining forces (e.g., addressing fears, building trust, easing the habit-breaking process).\n**4. **Under ASA, applicants are attracted to organisations whose values feel compatible with their own (Attraction), firms tend to hire people who fit the existing culture (Selection), and employees whose values clash with the culture tend to leave or be pushed out over time (Attrition). Because all three forces point the same direction, they compound: the culture becomes progressively more homogeneous and strongly held over time. This is positive when the culture is genuinely adaptive and ethical (reinforcing a healthy strong culture), but it can also entrench a dysfunctional or overly homogeneous culture (e.g., filtering out genuinely useful diversity of thought), since ASA does not discriminate between \"good\" and \"bad\" cultural content — it just reinforces whatever content is already dominant.\n**5. **This organisation appears stuck at the unfreezing stage — the announcement alone has not created enough real motivation/readiness for change, since restraining forces (habit, likely some fear of looking incompetent with the new system, and possibly distrust that the change is really permanent) are still stronger than the driving forces for these employees. Likely resistance reasons: breaking established habits/routines, and fear of the unknown or loss of a sense of competence/mastery (\"saving face\") with the unfamiliar new system. Recommended actions: Unfreezing — more direct communication of why the change matters (e.g., linking it to real business risks of the old process) plus reducing the specific fear driving resistance (e.g., reassurance that mistakes during the learning period are expected and won’t be penalized); Refreezing — once employees do adopt the new system, reinforce and lock in the new behaviour by removing access to the old manual process, updating official procedures/artifacts to reference only the new system, and rewarding/recognizing early adopters so the new way becomes the visible norm rather than something that can quietly slide back."}]}},"extras":[{"t":"p","x":"**BUS2202**"},{"t":"p","x":"**Organisational Behaviour**"},{"t":"p","x":"Complete Revision Synthesis & Practice Exercises"},{"t":"p","x":"Lingnan University — Term 1, AY2026-27"},{"t":"p","x":"All Moodle content for Lessons 1-10 (Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10, 11, 12, 14), synthesized and cross-checked against the instructor’s exam revision summary, with original practice exercises per lesson and a mixed final review set."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"How to Use This Document"},{"t":"p","x":"This synthesis gathers every substantive piece of course content posted on Moodle for BUS2202 Organisational Behaviour and organizes it lesson by lesson, in the order actually taught (Lessons 1-10, following the official course schedule). For each lesson you will find: (1) clean revision notes covering every theory, model and concept from the slides, and (2) a \"Complementary Practice Exercises\" section with brand-new self-test questions and a full answer key. The document ends with a cross-lesson exam review checklist and a mixed practice set."},{"t":"li","x":"**Course admin (this section): **syllabus highlights, full schedule, and a short, clearly-labeled summary of the Individual Assignment and Group Presentation — for context only, not exam content."},{"t":"li","x":"**Lessons 1-10: **revision notes + 5 practice questions with answer key each."},{"t":"li","x":"**Leadership (Lesson 7): **also includes the official MCQ quiz (with answers) and a summary of the Steve Jobs case study linked to leadership theory."},{"t":"li","x":"**End of document: **exam-style review checklist across all lessons + one mixed practice set (10 questions) with full answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **One topic listed in the instructor’s exam revision summary — Chapter 15, Organisational Change (Lewin’s force field analysis, unfreezing/changing/refreezing, resistance to change) — was not among the 18 files extracted from Moodle for this course. It has been added at the end of Lesson 10 using standard, correct OB theory so your coverage is complete; please verify the specifics (e.g., exact terminology used in your lecture) against the original slide deck once you can access it."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Course Overview (Admin Information)"},{"t":"p","x":"The material below is course administration, drawn from the syllabus, rubrics, schedule and assignment briefs. It is summarized here for context and is clearly separated from the examinable content that starts at Lesson 1."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Syllabus Highlights"},{"t":"li","x":"**Course: **BUS2202 Organisational Behaviour, 3 credits, 3-hour lecture per week, Functional Core course."},{"t":"li","x":"**Course description: **the study of what people think, feel and do in organisations, and how this affects organisational performance; aims to give students conceptual tools to work more effectively in the workplace."},{"t":"li","x":"**Learning outcomes (LOs): **"},{"t":"li","x":"LO1 — Explain and discuss concepts/theories of individual and organisational behaviour and their workplace impacts."},{"t":"li","x":"LO2 — Apply OB knowledge to diagnose work-related issues and develop solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"LO3 — Demonstrate critical thinking on behaviour-related problems."},{"t":"li","x":"LO4 — Develop effective teamwork skills in a business context."},{"t":"li","x":"**Indicative content (4 blocks): **Introduction to OB; Individual Behaviour and Processes (values/ethics, personality, perception/learning, emotions/attitudes, job satisfaction, motivation, stress); Group Processes (team effectiveness, conflict, power/politics, leadership); Organisational Processes (culture, organisational change)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Required textbook: **McShane, S.L. & Von Glinow, M.A., Organizational Behavior (9th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2020) — the individual assignment brief references the newer 10th ed., Organizational Behavior: Emerging Knowledge, Global Reality (2024)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Assessment weighting: **50% continuous assessment (25% group/team work, 15% individual case, 10% individual class participation) + 50% final examination."},{"t":"li","x":"**Academic integrity: **Turnitin submission required for written work; GAI (generative AI) use must be declared per Lingnan’s GAI guidelines; plagiarism and fabricated references are treated as serious academic offences (fabricated/falsified references can result in a grade of zero)."},{"t":"li","x":"**Workload expectation: **about 9 hours/week total (3 contact + 6 personal study)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Full Course Schedule (Term 1, AY2026-27: 1 Sep - 30 Nov 2026)"},{"t":"table","rows":[["Lesson","Date","Topic","Chapter","Notes"],["1","7 Sep","Introduction to the Field of OB","Ch 1","Ice-breaking, course overview"],["2","14 Sep","Individual Behaviour, Personality and Values","Ch 2","Recap of assessments; group exercises"],["3","21 Sep","Perception and Learning in Organisations","Ch 3","Perception/learning psychology tests"],["4","28 Sep","Workplace Emotions, Attitudes and Stress","Ch 4","Submit group members’ name list"],["5","5 Oct","Motivation","Ch 5","Case discussion; individual assignment checkpoint"],["6","12 Oct","Team Dynamics","Ch 8","Case discussion"],["—","19 Oct","Holiday — No class","—","Individual Assignment due 20 Oct, 23:59 (15%)"],["7","26 Oct","Leadership in Organisational Settings","Ch 12","Submit group topic (1-page PPT)"],["8","2 Nov","Power and Influence","Ch 10","In-class exercises"],["9","9 Nov","Conflict and Negotiation","Ch 11","In-class exercises"],["10","16 Nov","Culture and Organisational Change","Ch 14 & 15","In-class exercises"],["11","23 Nov","Group Presentation","—","25% of grade"],["12","30 Nov","Course Summary and Exam Revision","—","Instructor’s topic-by-chapter summary"],["Exam","8-12 & 14-19 Dec","Final Examination","All","50% of grade"]]},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Assignment Info (Context Only — Not Exam Content)"},{"t":"p","x":"The two items below are continuous-assessment deliverables, summarized briefly so you know what is expected alongside your revision. They are not part of the examinable theory content."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Individual Case Report — Stress Management (15%)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Case: **\"The Human Element: The Case of Senior Project Manager, Janet Wong\" — a senior project manager overriding an AI risk-monitoring system’s warnings under fatigue, leading to a costly production incident, while also neglecting family responsibilities."},{"t":"li","x":"**Task: **a 1,400-word report (incl. references) answering four questions (25 marks each): (1) causes of Janet’s stress with case evidence; (2) work and personal consequences of that stress; (3) whether other team members would react the same way to similar stressors (individual differences); (4) what Janet and her supervisor could have done differently, including around the human-in-the-loop AI interventions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Practical requirements: **submit via Turnitin by 23:59, 20 Oct 2026; English quality counts for 10% of the mark; GAI use must be declared (GAI content should not exceed 20%, similarity not exceed 10%, excluding references); late submission = 10% deduction per day; fabricated/falsified references = automatic zero."},{"t":"p","x":"**Gap note: **This case maps directly onto the Chapter 4 stress content (stressors, individual differences in stress, stress management strategies) covered in Lesson 4 of this document — revisit that section when preparing the report."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Group Presentation — OB/HR Issue Analysis (25%)"},{"t":"li","x":"**Format: **groups of 4-5 members; 15-minute oral presentation + 5-minute Q&A (20 minutes total); hardcopy PowerPoint (in notes form) submitted in Lecture 12; no written report required."},{"t":"li","x":"**Task: **identify and critically analyse a real, recent OB/HR issue related to Employee Motivation, Team Dynamics, or Leadership, applying course theories/concepts (not just describing the case), evaluate the effectiveness of actions taken, and recommend solutions."},{"t":"li","x":"**Process: **submit a one-page PowerPoint project proposal (title, issue, area of investigation) before the group consultation (before Lecture 7); presentation structure = cover page, introduction, analysis, recommendations, conclusion, APA references."},{"t":"li","x":"**Assessment criteria: **comprehension/relevance/accuracy of content, evidence of research, application of OB/HR theory, critical evaluation, logical flow, creativity/feasibility of recommendations, presentation style, referencing."},{"t":"li","x":"**Other rules: **peer evaluation adjusts individual marks for uneven contribution; Turnitin required; GAI use must be declared and cited; English quality assessed separately."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Rubric Snapshot (For Awareness)"},{"t":"p","x":"All four rubrics (class participation, individual case, group presentation, final exam) score on a similar structure: understanding of concepts, problem diagnosis / stakeholder analysis, application of concepts to the problem, clarity/logic of expression, and English quality. The final exam rubric weighs concept understanding, problem diagnosis, stakeholder analysis and application of theory at 20% each, plus 20% for clear/logical/accurate expression — i.e., knowing the theory is necessary but not sufficient: you must also apply it precisely to the scenario given and reference multiple stakeholder perspectives."},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Final Exam Review Checklist (All Lessons)"},{"t":"p","x":"Use this as a self-test: for each item, check that you can define it, explain it in your own words, and apply it to a short scenario. This checklist is cross-checked against the instructor’s \"Exam Revision — Summary of key topics by Chapter\" so every listed exam topic is represented somewhere in this document."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 1 — Introduction to OB"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of OB and of an organisation; organisational effectiveness (open systems, human capital, stakeholders)."},{"t":"li","x":"The Integrative Model of OB (big-picture framework)."},{"t":"li","x":"The five OB anchors (systematic research, practical orientation, multidisciplinary, contingency, multiple levels of analysis)."},{"t":"li","x":"Emerging workplace trends: AI, inclusive workplace/diversity, work-life integration, remote/hybrid work, employment relationships."},{"t":"li","x":"The MARS model (Motivation, Ability, Role perceptions, Situational factors) and the five types of individual behaviour (task performance, OCBs, counterproductive behaviour, joining/staying, attendance)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 2 — Personality and Values"},{"t":"li","x":"Big Five personality factors and their workplace links; issues with the model."},{"t":"li","x":"MBTI/Jungian theory — strengths and (important) limitations."},{"t":"li","x":"The Dark Triad: Machiavellianism, narcissism, psychopathy, and workplace consequences."},{"t":"li","x":"Schwartz’s values model; values congruence; when values fail to predict behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Four ethical principles; moral intensity vs. moral sensitivity; supporting ethical behaviour."},{"t":"li","x":"Cross-cultural values: individualism, collectivism, power distance, uncertainty avoidance, achievement-nurturing."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 3 — Perception and Learning"},{"t":"li","x":"Self-concept: the 3 Cs (complexity, consistency, clarity); self-enhancement, self-verification, self-evaluation (self-esteem, self-efficacy, locus of control); social self/social identity theory."},{"t":"li","x":"The perceptual process: selective attention, perceptual organization, mental models."},{"t":"li","x":"Stereotyping (categorization, homogenization, differentiation); attribution theory (self-serving bias, fundamental attribution error); self-fulfilling prophecy; halo, false-consensus, primacy and recency effects."},{"t":"li","x":"Improving perceptions: awareness, self-awareness (Johari Window), meaningful interaction/contact hypothesis, empathy."},{"t":"li","x":"Global mindset."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 4 — Emotions, Attitudes and Stress"},{"t":"li","x":"Attitudes vs. emotions; the beliefs → feelings → behavioural intentions → behaviour model; cognitive dissonance."},{"t":"li","x":"Emotional labor (surface vs. deep acting); the Emotional Intelligence model (recognition/regulation x self/others)."},{"t":"li","x":"EVLN model of dissatisfaction; job satisfaction-performance link; the service profit chain; affective/continuance/normative commitment."},{"t":"li","x":"Stress definition, GAS; four major stressors (constraints, interpersonal conflict, overload, low task control); individual differences in stress; five stress-management strategies."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 5 — Motivation"},{"t":"li","x":"Four-drive theory; Maslow’s needs hierarchy (and its weaknesses/contributions); intrinsic vs. extrinsic motivation; learned needs theory (nAch, nAff, nPow)."},{"t":"li","x":"Expectancy theory (E-to-P, P-to-O, valence) and its practical levers."},{"t":"li","x":"OB Mod / A-B-Cs (positive/negative reinforcement, punishment, extinction); social cognitive theory (modeling, self-regulation)."},{"t":"li","x":"Effective goal setting and feedback; strengths-based coaching."},{"t":"li","x":"Organizational justice (distributive, procedural, interactional); equity theory and ways to correct perceived inequity."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 6 — Team Dynamics"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of a team; informal groups; advantages/challenges of teams (incl. social loafing and remedies)."},{"t":"li","x":"Team effectiveness model: organisation/team environment, task interdependence types (pooled/sequential/reciprocal), team size, team composition/diversity."},{"t":"li","x":"Stages of team development (forming-storming-norming-performing-adjourning); team roles, norms, cohesion, trust (calculus/knowledge/identification-based, swift trust)."},{"t":"li","x":"Self-directed teams and remote teams — success factors."},{"t":"li","x":"Team decision-making constraints and remedies; creative structures (brainstorming, brainwriting, electronic brainstorming, nominal group technique)."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 7 — Leadership"},{"t":"li","x":"Leadership definition; shared leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Transformational leadership (4 elements) vs. charisma; managerial leadership vs. transformational; task- vs. people-oriented; servant leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Path-goal theory (4 styles + contingencies); leadership substitutes theory."},{"t":"li","x":"Implicit leadership (prototypes + romance of leadership)."},{"t":"li","x":"8 personal attributes of effective leaders; authentic leadership; limitations of the attributes perspective."},{"t":"li","x":"Cultural and gender issues in leadership; Dark Triad and destructive leadership."},{"t":"li","x":"Steve Jobs case — link to transformational leadership, charisma, and leader attributes; official MCQ quiz content."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 8 — Power and Influence"},{"t":"li","x":"Definition of power; five sources (legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent)."},{"t":"li","x":"Four power contingencies (nonsubstitutability, centrality, visibility, discretion); consequences of power (empowerment vs. power-over)."},{"t":"li","x":"Social networks: social capital, strong/weak ties, network centrality (betweenness, degree, closeness), guanxi."},{"t":"li","x":"Eight influence tactics; contingencies of influence (soft vs. hard tactics)."},{"t":"li","x":"Organisational politics and how to minimize it."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 9 — Conflict and Negotiation"},{"t":"li","x":"Positive/negative outcomes of conflict; task vs. relationship conflict and how to minimize the latter."},{"t":"li","x":"The conflict process model; six structural sources of conflict."},{"t":"li","x":"Five conflict-handling styles and their contingencies; cultural/gender differences."},{"t":"li","x":"Six structural approaches to conflict management; three types of third-party intervention (inquisition, mediation, arbitration)."},{"t":"li","x":"Distributive vs. integrative negotiation; BATNA; the negotiation process (information, concessions, time/relationships); negotiation setting; gender and negotiation."},{"t":"h","l":2,"x":"Lesson 10 — Culture and Organisational Change"},{"t":"li","x":"Elements of culture (the \"iceberg\"); organisational culture profile dimensions; subcultures/countercultures."},{"t":"li","x":"Culture artifacts (stories, language/rituals/ceremonies, physical structures); culture strength and its three functions; contingencies of culture strength."},{"t":"li","x":"Four strategies for merging cultures (assimilation, deculturation, integration, separation) and the bicultural audit."},{"t":"li","x":"Five strategies for changing/strengthening culture; ASA theory; organisational socialization (learning/adjustment, psychological contracts, RJPs, socialization agents)."},{"t":"li","x":"Lewin’s force field analysis; unfreezing-changing-refreezing; reasons people resist change; strategies for managing resistance. (Supplementary — verify against original Ch.15 slide.)"},{"t":"h","l":1,"x":"Mixed Practice Exam Set (All Lessons)"},{"t":"p","x":"10 original questions spanning multiple lessons, in a style similar to the final exam (mix of MCQ, short-answer, and applied/case questions). Attempt all questions before checking the answer key."},{"t":"p","x":"**1. **MCQ: A manager assumes a new hire from a well-regarded university must automatically be an excellent performer, and rates his (still unobserved) work more favourably than it deserves. Which concept from Lesson 3 best explains this?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Fundamental attribution error"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Halo effect"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Equity theory"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Social loafing"},{"t":"p","x":"**2. **MCQ: Which combination of path-goal leadership styles (Lesson 7) is generally recommended for employees who are inexperienced and unskilled?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Achievement-oriented and participative"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Directive and supportive"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Participative only"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Achievement-oriented only"},{"t":"p","x":"**3. **MCQ: According to expectancy theory (Lesson 5), if an employee is fully confident her effort will lead to strong performance, and she knows strong performance is measured and rewarded, but she genuinely does not care about the specific reward on offer, her overall motivation will be:"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Very high, since two of three components are strong"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Low, because the components multiply and a near-zero valence drags down the whole expectancy chain"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Unaffected by valence, since it only matters for extrinsic rewards"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Determined only by her locus of control"},{"t":"p","x":"**4. **MCQ: A team that has just resolved its early competition for roles and is now building consensus on objectives and a shared way of working, but is not yet performing at peak efficiency, is in which stage of team development (Lesson 6)?"},{"t":"p","x":"a) Forming"},{"t":"p","x":"b) Storming"},{"t":"p","x":"c) Norming"},{"t":"p","x":"d) Performing"},{"t":"p","x":"**5. **Short answer: A company wants to reduce dysfunctional relationship conflict on its cross-functional teams (Lesson 9) while still encouraging healthy task conflict. Name two concrete practices it could adopt, drawing on both the conflict lesson and the team dynamics lesson."},{"t":"p","x":"**6. **Short answer: Using both the power (Lesson 8) and culture (Lesson 10) lessons, explain how a strong organisational culture can itself function as a form of legitimate power over employees, even without any single manager exercising direct authority."},{"t":"p","x":"**7. **Short answer: A long-serving employee scores very high on organisational commitment, but exit interviews later reveal she stayed mainly \"because I’d lose my pension and couldn’t easily find another job at my age\" rather than because she loved the company. Which type of organisational commitment (Lesson 4) does this describe, and why does this type typically NOT predict strong performance or organisational citizenship behaviour?"},{"t":"p","x":"**8. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 4, 5 and 9): A high-performing employee has recently become irritable, is missing small details in his work, and has twice raised his voice at a colleague during a routine planning meeting — a level of task disagreement that used to stay perfectly professional between them. He mentions in passing that his workload has \"basically doubled\" since a teammate left three months ago and was never replaced. (a) Using stress theory, identify the most likely primary stressor. (b) Using motivation theory, explain one likely secondary effect on his motivation (pick one theory and justify). (c) Using conflict theory, classify what is now happening with his colleague and recommend one action to prevent it from escalating into full relationship conflict."},{"t":"p","x":"**9. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 6 and 7): A newly appointed team leader inherited a team that already had strong informal norms and high cohesion before she arrived, but those norms currently tolerate cutting corners on quality checks. Using team dynamics and leadership theory, recommend a specific leadership approach (naming a theory/style) and a specific team-level action to shift the team’s norms without destroying its existing cohesion."},{"t":"p","x":"**10. **Applied scenario (integrates Lessons 2, 3 and 8): A senior employee with high Machiavellian tendencies has built an extensive internal network and is skilled at persuasion and impression management, but is known to selectively share information (\"information control\") to keep colleagues dependent on him. (a) Name the specific power source(s) and influence tactic(s) most clearly at play. (b) Explain, using attribution theory, why colleagues might be slow to correctly identify what he is doing. (c) Suggest one structural/organisational response (not just \"tell him to stop\") that would reduce his ability to keep using this tactic."},{"t":"h","l":3,"x":"Answer Key with Reasoning"},{"t":"a","x":"**1. **b) Halo effect — one favourable trait/cue (a prestigious university) is being used to infer unrelated positive qualities (performance) without direct evidence; this is distinct from the fundamental attribution error, which concerns explaining someone’s behaviour by internal vs. external causes.\n**2. **b) Directive and supportive — directive leadership provides needed task guidance, and supportive leadership helps inexperienced/unskilled employees cope with the uncertainty of the unfamiliar task; achievement-oriented and participative styles fit skilled, internally-motivated employees instead.\n**3. **b) Low — because expectancy theory’s three components (E-to-P, P-to-O, valence) combine multiplicatively, not additively, a valence close to zero pulls overall motivation toward zero even when the other two beliefs are strong.\n**4. **c) Norming — competition for roles (a storming behaviour) has been resolved, and the team is now building shared consensus/mental models, but has not yet reached the smooth, high-trust efficiency of performing.\n**5. **Any two reasonable practices, e.g.: (1) Establish explicit psychological-safety team norms at formation (Lesson 6) that separate critiquing ideas from critiquing people, reinforced by the team leader modeling this distinction. (2) Use a structured third-party approach — e.g., a peer facilitator/mediator (Lesson 9) for recurring cross-functional disagreements, so disputes are processed through a process that controls the conversation (mediation) rather than escalating into personal attacks. (3) Build emotional intelligence and team development/mutual understanding early (e.g., a kickoff session where members share working styles), which the conflict lesson identifies as reducing relationship conflict during task conflict.\n**6. **A strong organisational culture functions like a form of legitimate/normative power because widely shared values and assumptions (Lesson 10) become an internalized \"automatic pilot\" that guides what employees believe is the appropriate way to behave — similar to how legitimate power works through people’s agreement that certain requests/expectations are appropriate to follow (Lesson 8), except here the \"authority\" is the collective culture and its artifacts (stories, rituals, language) rather than a specific person’s position. Because the control is largely self-imposed and reinforced through social identity (\"social glue\") and sense-making, it can be even more powerful and pervasive than a single manager’s direct legitimate or reward power, since employees are, in effect, both the source and the target of the influence.\n**7. **This is continuance commitment — a calculative attachment based on the (high) cost of leaving (losing her pension) and a perceived lack of good alternatives, rather than affective commitment (genuine emotional attachment) or normative commitment (felt moral obligation). Continuance commitment does not predict strong performance or OCB because the employee’s motivation to stay is not connected to caring about the organisation’s success — she is present because leaving is costly, not because she is emotionally invested in going \"above and beyond,\" so her discretionary effort and citizenship behaviour remain low even though her tenure/loyalty (in the narrow sense of \"stays a long time\") looks strong.\n**8. **(a) Primary stressor: work overload (his workload effectively doubled with no replacement hire) — this is one of the four major workplace stressors. (b) One acceptable answer: under expectancy theory, sustained overload likely erodes his effort-to-performance (E-to-P) expectancy — he may increasingly doubt that his effort can still produce the standard of work he used to deliver, given an unsustainable workload, which would reduce his overall motivation and could explain the missed details (alternatively, equity theory is also acceptable: he may perceive his input/outcome ratio has worsened — much higher inputs for the same outcomes/reward — creating inequity tension that shows up as reduced effort or irritability). (c) This looks like an early sign of relationship conflict developing out of what was previously professional task conflict (raised voice during a routine disagreement is a relationship-conflict warning sign, not just task disagreement). Recommended action: the manager should intervene early and privately — address the underlying stressor (redistribute workload or get approval to backfill the missing role) while also having a supportive, non-judgemental conversation with him about the recent friction, to prevent the (stress-driven) irritability from further damaging the working relationship with his colleague.\n**9. **Leadership approach: the situation calls for a directive element of path-goal leadership (clarifying that the quality-check standard is non-negotiable and setting clear expectations), rather than a purely supportive or laissez-faire style, since the existing team norm is actively counter-productive; this can be paired with elements of transformational leadership (clearly articulating why quality matters — connecting it to a larger shared purpose/vision the team can rally around, rather than simply issuing a rule). Team-level action: rather than disbanding the team (an extreme last resort) or ignoring the cohesion, the leader should explicitly and openly discuss the counter-productive norm with the team (a specific technique from the Lesson 6 norms content), invite the team’s own input on a better process, and introduce a team-based reward or recognition tied to quality outcomes — this leverages the team’s existing high cohesion in service of the new norm rather than fighting against it.\n**10. **(a) Power sources: expert power (network/persuasion skill) and especially legitimate/gatekept information control combined with referent power built through impression management; the influence tactics at play are clearly information control and impression management (and likely coalition-building, given his extensive network). (b) Attribution theory explanation: colleagues are likely committing something close to a fundamental attribution error in reverse — because he is skilled at impression management, they may attribute his helpfulness/charm to genuine positive character (an internal, dispositional attribution: \"he’s just a well-connected, helpful person\") rather than correctly attributing his selective information-sharing to a strategic, self-interested tactic; his persuasive skill actively shapes their perception, making the real (situational/strategic) cause harder to see. (c) Structural response: reduce his structural monopoly on information rather than relying on confronting him directly — e.g., implement shared documentation/knowledge-management systems so that critical information is not exclusively channeled through him, and formally distribute the information-brokering role across more than one person; this directly reduces his nonsubstitutability and discretion (two power contingencies from Lesson 8) without needing to rely on changing his personality."}]}}};
